{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' cores/bench/LESSONS.md && rg -n -i '\\\"category\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"micro_opt\\\"|micro_opt|forward|hazard|load-use|regfile|bypass|write-first|stall' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r1s0 (improvement, +8.33%): RV32M multiply variants should share a sign-selected 33x33 product and select the required 32-bit half, since parallel signed/unsigned multiplier cones waste LUTs even though each instruction consumes only one result.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r1s2 (regression, -10.84%): A combinational BTFNT predictor that derives the PC update from imem_data puts instruction fetch, branch decode, immediate extraction, and target addition on the IF-to-PC feedback path, so it must be pipelined or decoupled when its Fmax loss exceeds CoreMark's saved redirect bubbles.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r2s0 (regression, +20.73%): Replacing a shared 33x33 signed multiplier with a 32x32 unsigned product can regress when MULH/MULHSU require chained conditional 32-bit high-half corrections, because the added subtractor network outweighs any saved multiplier logic on the CoreMark-critical ALU path.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r2s1 (improvement, +32.31%): Moving DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU into a blocking iterative unit removes wide divide/remainder cones from the common EX path, and CoreMark's sparse division use lets the Fmax gain outweigh the rare multi-cycle front-end stalls.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r2s2 (regression, +0.69%): Removing imem_data from branch prediction is insufficient when a combinational PC-indexed BTB tag match, counter read, and next-PC mux remain on the fetch feedback path; CoreMark's saved loop-back bubbles did not amortize that added front-end cost.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r3s0 (regression, -27.02%): Registering a blocking 33x33 multiplier without splitting its arithmetic only moves the full-cycle product into a new register-to-register cone while adding EX ownership and interlock logic, so it cannot deliver the divide unit's Fmax benefit without real pipelining or iteration.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r3s1 (broken, n/a): A sequential fetch FIFO must index its storage procedurally with runtime read/write pointers; generate-block hierarchy and prefixes are elaboration-time constructs and reject dynamic queue indices before synthesis.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r4s0 (broken, n/a): Decode-qualified load-use detection must classify every architectural source in the same valid/timing domain as operand capture, because suppressing a real dependency lets stale register data reach EX and is caught by RVFI register checks.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r4s1 (regression, -18.56%): A four-entry procedural IF queue can lose CoreMark fitness when its pointer/count, dequeue arbitration, and redirect-flush control cost more than the transient imem stalls that prefetch can cover, so front-end decoupling needs measured slack rather than assumed stall hiding.\n1:{\"id\": \"baseline-bench-2d60805\", \"title\": \"Baseline retest for cores/bench\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 282.82, \"delta_pct\": 0.0, \"vs_baseline\": 0.0, \"fmax_mhz\": 127.03, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 9563, \"ff\": 1866, \"seeds\": [128.72, 127.03, 123.02], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T00:46:51.300788Z\", \"round_id\": 0, \"slot\": 0}\n2:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Share RV32 multiply datapath\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current ALU computes three distinct 64-bit products on every ALU\\nevaluation, although an instruction can consume only one result. This is a\\nhigh-LUT combinational cone in a design already near its LUT target and\\nbelow its 135 MHz Fmax target.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current ALU computes three distinct 64-bit products on every ALU\\nevaluation, although an instruction can consume only one result. This is a\\nhigh-LUT combinational cone in a design already near its LUT target and\\nbelow its 135 MHz Fmax target.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the unconditional signed-signed, unsigned-unsigned, and\\nsigned-unsigned product expressions with one shared 33x33 unsigned modular\\nmultiplier. Select the operands' sign extension from the decoded multiply\\noperation, then select bits [31:0] for MUL or [63:32] for MULH, MULHU, and\\nMULHSU. The low 64 bits of the 33-bit two's-complement product preserve all\\nRV32M result definitions, while removing two large parallel multiplier\\ncones. Keep the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS expressions unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement one opcode-selected 33x33 multiplier and route all four RV32M multiply variants through it, preserving the ALTOPS branch and division semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Extend multiply tests with signed/unsigned boundary and randomized vectors that prove the shared product's high and low halves match RV32M semantics.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement one opcode-selected 33x33 multiplier and route all four RV32M multiply variants through it, preserving the ALTOPS branch and division semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Extend multiply tests with signed/unsigned boundary and randomized vectors that prove the shared product's high and low halves match RV32M semantics.\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 306.38, \"delta_pct\": 8.33, \"vs_baseline\": 8.33, \"fmax_mhz\": 137.61, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 9816, \"ff\": 1867, \"seeds\": [136.3, 149.12, 137.61], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T00:55:35.641639Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r1s0 (improvement, +8.33%): RV32M multiply variants should share a sign-selected 33x33 product and select the required 32-bit half, since parallel signed/unsigned multiplier cones waste LUTs even though each instruction consumes only one result.\"}\n3:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Decode-stage branch resolution\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The five-stage pipe resolves every conditional branch in EX, so a taken\\nloop branch discards two fetched/decode-path cycles. CoreMark spends much\\nof its time in short counted loops, making this fixed control penalty a\\nmeaningful limit on iterations per cycle under the same bus-stall model.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The five-stage pipe resolves every conditional branch in EX, so a taken\\nloop branch discards two fetched/decode-path cycles. CoreMark spends much\\nof its time in short counted loops, making this fixed control penalty a\\nmeaningful limit on iterations per cycle under the same bus-stall model.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Restructure the front end so conditional branches resolve in decode using\\nthe register-file read values and explicit bypasses from EX/MEM and WB.\\nRedirect the PC and squash only the fetched wrong-path instruction in the\\nsame decode cycle; retain EX resolution for JALR and preserve a targeted\\nload-to-branch interlock until load data is available. This removes one\\nrecovery cycle for the common backward conditional branch while retaining\\nsingle-retire RVFI ordering and the existing memory interface.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 7, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\