 ALTOPS behavior for formal builds.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/divider.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a signed/unsigned restoring iterative divider with start, busy, done, quotient, remainder, and RV32M divide-by-zero/overflow handling.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Dispatch DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU to the divider, hold their EX/MEM advance until completion, and route the selected result into the normal writeback and RVFI payload.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a divide-busy interlock that freezes younger work without duplicating retirement or disturbing memory-stall behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the divider request/completion handshake into execute and global pipeline stall control.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Retain fast-ALU checks and add directed RV32M divide/remainder corner cases covering signs, zero divisors, and INT_MIN divided by minus one.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove real combinational divide and remainder datapaths from the fast ALU while retaining the ALTOPS behavior for formal builds.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/divider.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a signed/unsigned restoring iterative divider with start, busy, done, quotient, remainder, and RV32M divide-by-zero/overflow handling.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Dispatch DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU to the divider, hold their EX/MEM advance until completion, and route the selected result into the normal writeback and RVFI payload.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a divide-busy interlock that freezes younger work without duplicating retirement or disturbing memory-stall behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the divider request/completion handshake into execute and global pipeline stall control.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Retain fast-ALU checks and add directed RV32M divide/remainder corner cases covering signs, zero divisors, and INT_MIN divided by minus one.\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 405.36, \"delta_pct\": 32.31, \"vs_baseline\": 43.33, \"fmax_mhz\": 182.08, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491817, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5681, \"ff\": 2226, \"seeds\": [193.35, 179.08, 182.08], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T01:11:12.351940Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r2s1 (improvement, +32.31%): Moving DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU into a blocking iterative unit removes wide divide/remainder cones from the common EX path, and CoreMark's sparse division use lets the Fmax gain outweigh the rare multi-cycle front-end stalls.\"}\n9:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"Decoupled sequential fetch queue\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current IF stage couples every instruction-bus ready pulse directly to\\nPC advance, so a random imem stall immediately freezes the entire pipeline.\\nCoreMark also has load-use, data-memory, and occasional divide stalls during\\nwhich sequential instruction bandwidth is otherwise unused.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current IF stage couples every instruction-bus ready pulse directly to\\nPC advance, so a random imem stall immediately freezes the entire pipeline.\\nCoreMark also has load-use, data-memory, and occasional divide stalls during\\nwhich sequential instruction bandwidth is otherwise unused.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the single combinational fetch payload with a small sequential\\ninstruction FIFO and an independent fetch PC. While decode or execute is\\nstalled, the front end continues to fill unused FIFO entries whenever imem\\nis ready; while imem is temporarily not ready, decode consumes buffered\\ninstructions. A redirect atomically invalidates queued wrong-path entries\\nand restarts fetching at the resolved target. This preserves the existing\\nEX-stage branch decision and single-retire RVFI boundary while converting\\navailable slack into tolerance of the benchmark's random instruction stalls.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the fetch-queue payload type and any queue-depth constants needed by the decoupled front end.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the direct PC-to-ID payload with a small sequential prefetch FIFO, separate fetch/consume pointers, ready-qualified fills, and redirect invalidation.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire decode-consume backpressure and existing redirect control to the queued fetch interface without changing the top-level contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate decode availability from raw imem readiness so a queued instruction can issue during an imem stall while preserving load, dmem, and divider interlocks.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed queue tests for sequential fill/drain, imem backpressure masking, and redirect flushing of wrong-path entries.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the fetch-queue payload type and any queue-depth constants needed by the decoupled front end.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the direct PC-to-ID payload with a small sequential prefetch FIFO, separate fetch/consume pointers, ready-qualified fills, and redirect invalidation.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire decode-consume backpressure and existing redirect control to the queued fetch interface without changing the top-level contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate decode availability from raw imem readiness so a queued instruction can issue during an imem stall while preserving load, dmem, and divider interlocks.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed queue tests for sequential fill/drain, imem backpressure masking, and redirect flushing of wrong-path entries.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"build_failed: yosys synth: cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv:62: ERROR: Index in generate block prefix syntax is not constant!\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 3, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r3s1 (broken, n/a): A sequential fetch FIFO must index its storage procedurally with runtime read/write pointers; generate-block hierarchy and prefixes are elaboration-time constructs and reject dynamic queue indices before synthesis.\"}\n12:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r4s1\", \"title\": \"Procedural decoupled instruction queue\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Random instruction-memory backpressure currently freezes the PC and decode\\npath immediately, even when a prior fetch could have supplied useful work.\\nThe CoreMark evaluation injects instruction stalls, while load-use, data-memory,\\nand divide waits create opportunities to prefetch sequential instructions.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Random instruction-memory backpressure currently freezes the PC and decode\\npath immediately, even when a prior fetch could have supplied useful work.\\nThe CoreMark evaluation injects instruction stalls, while load-use, data-memory,\\nand divide waits create opportunities to prefetch sequential instructions.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the direct combinational IF-to-ID path with a four-entry instruction\\nqueue driven by a separate fetch PC. Implement its storage as a normal packed\\narray indexed procedurally by runtime read/write pointers, never as generated\\nhierarchy. A ready-qualified fill may run while downstream is interlocked, and\\ndecode consumes an already buffered instruction during temporary imem stalls.\\nOn an EX redirect, atomically discard all queued entries, reset the pointers\\nand count, and restart the fetch PC at the architectural target; no pre-redirect\\ninstruction may reach ID. This converts available front-end slack into\\ninstruction-stall coverage without putting prediction logic on the PC feedback\\npath or changing the single-retire RVFI boundary.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 9, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Define a fetch-packet bundle carrying PC and instruction, plus queue-size and pointer-width constants.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the direct fetch payload with a four-entry procedural storage array, runtime read/write pointers, occupancy counter, ready-qualified fill, decode dequeue, and redirect flush.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate raw instruction-bus readiness from decode availability so a nonempty queue can feed ID during an imem stall while preserving memory, load-use, and divider interlocks.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire decode-consume permission and queue-empty state through the front end while retaining EX redirect priority and the nret=1 external contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed tests for runtime pointer wraparound, simultaneous dequeue/fill, imem-stall masking, downstream interlocks, and redirect invalidation with PC/RVFI ordering checks.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 9, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Define a fetch-packet bundle carrying PC and instruction, plus queue-size and pointer-width constants.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the direct fetch payload with a four-entry procedural storage array, runtime read/write pointers, occupancy counter, ready-qualified fill, decode dequeue, and redirect flush.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate raw instruction-bus readiness from decode availability so a nonempty queue can feed ID during an imem stall while preserving memory, load-use, and divider interlocks.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire decode-consume permission and queue-empty state through the front end while retaining EX redirect priority and the nret=1 external contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed tests for runtime pointer wraparound, simultaneous dequeue/fill, imem-stall masking, downstream interlocks, and redirect invalidation with PC/RVFI ordering checks.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 330.11, \"delta_pct\": -18.56, \"vs_baseline\": 16.72, \"fmax_mhz\": 155.06, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4697199, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5854, \"ff\": 2299, \"seeds\": [146.35, 158.43, 155.06], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T01:50:36.434439Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 4, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r4s1 (regression, -18.56%): A four-entry procedural IF queue can lose CoreMark fitness when its pointer/count, dequeue arbitration, and redirect-flush control cost more than the transient imem stalls that prefetch can cover, so front-end decoupling needs measured slack rather than assumed stall hiding.\"}\n15:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r5s1\", \"title\": \"Cold iterative high-half multiply unit\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The shared signed 33x33 multiplier is present on every EX-stage cycle even\\nthough CoreMark primarily needs the low-half MUL result. The signed and\\nunsigned high-half variants are substantially colder compiler operations,\\nyet their operand-selection and wide-product cone remain on the common\\nALU timing path after divide/remainder have already been isolated.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The shared signed 33x33 multiplier is present on every EX-stage cycle even\\nthough CoreMark primarily needs the low-half MUL result. The signed and\\nunsigned high-half variants are substantially colder compiler operations,\\nyet their operand-selection and wide-product cone remain on the common\\nALU timing path after divide/remainder have already been isolated.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep architectural MUL as a one-cycle unsigned low-32-bit product, whose\\nresult is independent of operand signedness, and dispatch MULH, MULHU, and\\nMULHSU to a blocking iterative high-product unit. The unit snapshots its\\noperands and signedness, performs a shift/add product over multiple cycles,\\nthen injects the required high half through the normal EX/MEM path while\\nyounger issue is interlocked. This removes the 33-bit signed product and\\nhigh-half selection logic from every ordinary EX cycle; the occasional\\nmulti-cycle high multiply should be amortized by the improved Fmax.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Make the fast MUL path a low-half unsigned product and remove real high-half multiply selection from the common combinational ALU cone, while retaining the matching ALTOPS behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mulhi_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a signedness-aware iterative high-half multiplier with start/busy/done/result handshake and ALTOPS-compatible formal behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Dispatch MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU to the new unit, snapshot forwarded operand metadata, and insert its completed result into EX/MEM exactly once.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the long-operation interlock so a high-half multiply freezes younger issue without replaying or duplicating retirements.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the high-half-multiply busy indication into the existing global execute-stall control without changing the nret=1 interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Keep exact low-half MUL checks and add directed MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU signedness and edge-case vectors through the iterative execution path.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Make the fast MUL path a low-half unsigned product and remove real high-half multiply selection from the common combinational ALU cone, while retaining the matching ALTOPS behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mulhi_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a signedness-aware iterative high-half multiplier with start/busy/done/result handshake and ALTOPS-compatible formal behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Dispatch MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU to the new unit, snapshot forwarded operand metadata, and insert its completed result into EX/MEM exactly once.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the long-operation interlock so a high-half multiply freezes younger issue without replaying or duplicating retirements.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the high-half-multiply busy indication into the existing global execute-stall control without changing the nret=1 interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Keep exact low-half MUL checks and add directed MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU signedness and edge-case vectors through the iterative execution path.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 349.82, \"delta_pct\": -13.7, \"vs_baseline\": 23.69, \"fmax_mhz\": 157.13, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491735, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 6385, \"ff\": 2585, \"seeds\": [148.17, 157.13, 160.15], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T02:11:27.653699Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 5}\n18:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r6s1\", \"title\": \"Ordered one-entry store buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The random dmem-ready deassertions currently hold the entire pipeline for\\nevery store, even though a store has no destination register and younger\\nnon-memory instructions cannot observe its completion. CoreMark performs\\nfrequent stores in its list and matrix kernels, so these otherwise avoidable\\nfull-pipeline waits lower useful instruction throughput.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The random dmem-ready deassertions currently hold the entire pipeline for\\nevery store, even though a store has no destination register and younger\\nnon-memory instructions cannot observe its completion. CoreMark performs\\nfrequent stores in its list and matrix kernels, so these otherwise avoidable\\nfull-pipeline waits lower useful instruction throughput.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Restructure the memory side around a one-entry ordered store buffer. A store\\nentering MEM snapshots its aligned address, replicated write data, and byte\\nmask and may retire once accepted into the buffer; the buffer alone drives\\nthe dmem write request until io_dmemReady acknowledges it. While that pending\\nwrite drains, younger non-memory instructions continue through the pipe. A\\nload, a second store when the entry is occupied, and any operation requiring\\nmemory ordering interlock until the entry has drained, so the externally\\nvisible memory order and RVFI store payload remain architectural. This avoids\\nthe queue and predictor front-end costs that regressed, while directly\\ncovering a distinct source of the benchmark's injected dmem stalls.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a one-entry store-buffer state machine that snapshots stores, drives pending write requests, accepts a store without waiting for dmemReady, and preserves its RVFI memory fields at retirement.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the packed store-buffer request payload and explicit memory-issue status signals needed to distinguish buffered stores from ordered loads.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the blanket dmem stall with ordering-aware interlocks: permit non-memory work behind a pending buffered store, but hold loads and a second store until the buffer drains.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire memory-stage buffer occupancy and memory-order backpressure into pipeline control without changing the nret=1 external interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_mem_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed tests for delayed store acknowledgment, continued non-memory retirement, store-to-load ordering, consecutive stores, byte masks, and RVFI store payload stability.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a one-entry store-buffer state machine that snapshots stores, drives pending write requests, accepts a store without waiting for dmemReady, and preserves its RVFI memory fields at retirement.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the packed store-buffer request payload and explicit memory-issue status signals needed to distinguish buffered stores from ordered loads.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the blanket dmem stall with ordering-aware interlocks: permit non-memory work behind a pending buffered store, but hold loads and a second store until the buffer drains.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire memory-stage buffer occupancy and memory-order backpressure into pipeline control without changing the nret=1 external interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_mem_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed tests for delayed store acknowledgment, continued non-memory retirement, store-to-load ordering, consecutive stores, byte masks, and RVFI store payload stability.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"formal_failed: insn_lb_ch0\\nne_0: ##   0:00:24  Status: failed\\nSBY 23:20:34 [insn_sh_ch0] engine_0: finished (returncode=1)\\nSBY 23:20:34 [insn_sh_ch0] engine_0: Status returned by engine: FAIL\\nSBY 23:20:34 [insn_sh_ch0] summary: Elapsed clock time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:25 (25)\\nSBY 23:20:34 [insn_sh_ch0] summary: Elapsed process time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:21 (21)\\nSBY 23:20:34 [insn_sh_ch0] summary: engine_0 (smtbmc bitwuzla) returned FAIL\\nSBY 23:20:34 [insn_sh_ch0] summary: counterexample trace: insn_sh_ch0/engine_0/trace.vcd\\nSBY 23:20:34 [insn_sh_ch0] summary:   failed assertion rvfi_testbench._witness_.check_flatten_checker_inst__assert_rvfi_insn_check_sv_167_102 at rvfi_insn_check.sv:167.7-167.29 step 20\\nSBY 23:20:34 [insn_sh_ch0] summary:   failed assertion rvfi_testbench._witness_.check_flatten_checker_inst__assert_rvfi_insn_check_sv_187_132 at rvfi_insn_check.sv:187.9-187.64 step 20\\nSBY 23:20:34 [insn_sh_ch0] DONE (FAIL, rc=0)\\n\\nFormal: 44 passed, 9 failed\\nFailed: insn_lb_ch0 insn_lbu_ch0 insn_lh_ch0 insn_lhu_ch0 insn_lw_ch0 insn_sb_ch0 insn_sh_ch0 insn_sw_ch0 reg_ch0\\n\\n--- insn_lb_ch0/logfile.txt (last 30 lines) ---\\nSBY 23:19:59 [insn_lb_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 7..\\nSBY 23:19:59 [insn_lb_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 8..\\nSBY 23:19:59 [insn_lb_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 9..\\nSBY 23:19:59 [insn_lb_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 10..\\nSBY 23:19:59 [insn_lb_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 11..\\nSBY 23:19:59 [insn_lb_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 12..\\nSBY 23:19:59 [insn_lb_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 1