ion redirect cost on nearly every iteration.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The five-stage core resolves every conditional branch in EX and normally\\ncontinues fetching PC+4. CoreMark spends substantial time in counted loops,\\nso its backward conditional branches are taken repeatedly and pay the\\ntwo-younger-instruction redirect cost on nearly every iteration.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a combinational backward-taken, forward-not-taken (BTFNT) predictor in\\nIF. For a valid BRANCH encoding with a negative B immediate, fetch will\\nselect the statically computed target instead of PC+4 and carry the\\npredicted next PC with the instruction. EX will compare that prediction with\\nits resolved next PC, redirect and squash only on disagreement. This removes\\nthe recurring loop-back recovery bubbles without adding a RAM-based BTB or a\\npredictor-table timing path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Recognize negative-offset conditional branches, form the B-immediate target, and advance the fetch PC to that predicted target.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry predicted-taken and predicted-next-PC metadata through the pipeline bundles to branch resolution.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch IF predictor metadata alongside decoded instruction state.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare resolved branch direction and target against prediction, issuing a redirect only for a misprediction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the misprediction signal to flush only wrong-path work while preserving correctly predicted control-flow throughput.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire prediction metadata and misprediction recovery between fetch, execute, and hazard control.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed checks for backward-taken prediction, forward/not-taken fall-through, and final loop-exit recovery.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Recognize negative-offset conditional branches, form the B-immediate target, and advance the fetch PC to that predicted target.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry predicted-taken and predicted-next-PC metadata through the pipeline bundles to branch resolution.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch IF predictor metadata alongside decoded instruction state.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare resolved branch direction and target against prediction, issuing a redirect only for a misprediction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the misprediction signal to flush only wrong-path work while preserving correctly predicted control-flow throughput.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire prediction metadata and misprediction recovery between fetch, execute, and hazard control.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed checks for backward-taken prediction, forward/not-taken fall-through, and final loop-exit recovery.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 252.17, \"delta_pct\": -10.84, \"vs_baseline\": -10.84, \"fmax_mhz\": 108.67, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4309463, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 9601, \"ff\": 1897, \"seeds\": [108.67, 115.59, 101.69], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T00:58:34.960871Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r1s2 (regression, -10.84%): A combinational BTFNT predictor that derives the PC update from imem_data puts instruction fetch, branch decode, immediate extraction, and target addition on the IF-to-PC feedback path, so it must be pipelined or decoupled when its Fmax loss exceeds CoreMark's saved redirect bubbles.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl-5-{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-001-r2s0\", \"title\": \"Unsigned multiplier with signed corrections\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current shared multiplier still uses a 33x33 signed product to cover\\nall RV32M multiply signedness variants. The high-half forms can instead\\nshare a narrower unsigned 32x32 product, with signedness represented by\\nsimple 32-bit correction terms. This should reduce the multiply cone on a\\ncore whose recent fitness gain came from multiplier datapath simplification.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current shared multiplier still uses a 33x33 signed product to cover\\nall RV32M multiply signedness variants. The high-half forms can instead\\nshare a narrower unsigned 32x32 product, with signedness represented by\\nsimple 32-bit correction terms. This should reduce the multiply cone on a\\ncore whose recent fitness gain came from multiplier datapath simplification.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the sign-selected 33x33 multiplier with one unsigned 32x32 product.\\nUse its low half directly for MUL and its high half for MULHU. For MULH,\\nsubtract operand b from the unsigned high half when a is negative and\\nsubtract operand a when b is negative; for MULHSU subtract b when a is\\nnegative. These modulo-2^32 corrections exactly implement the signed\\nproducts while avoiding sign-extension bits in the multiplier. Preserve the\\nRISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS operations and all division/reminder behavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement a shared unsigned 32x32 product and RV32M signed-high correction network, retaining existing ALTOPS and division logic.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed and randomized boundary vectors for MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU to validate the correction identities.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement a shared unsigned 32x32 product and RV32M signed-high correction network, retaining existing ALTOPS and division logic.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed and randomized boundary vectors for MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU to validate the correction identities.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 369.9, \"delta_pct\": 20.73, \"vs_baseline\": 30.79, \"fmax_mhz\": 166.14, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10476, \"ff\": 1875, \"seeds\": [170.44, 145.92, 166.14], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T01:09:09.962940Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r2s0 (regression, +20.73%): Replacing a shared 33x33 signed multiplier with a 32x32 unsigned product can regress when MULH/MULHSU require chained conditional 32-bit high-half corrections, because the added subtractor network outweighs any saved multiplier logic on the CoreMark-critical ALU path.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl-6-{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Decoupled iterative divide unit\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The EX ALU currently instantiates combinational DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU\\nalongside every normal integer operation. Those wide divide/remainder cones\\nconsume LUTs and can constrain the common EX result path despite CoreMark\\nspending almost all of its cycles on ordinary RV32I operations and multiplies.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The EX ALU currently instantiates combinational DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU\\nalongside every normal integer operation. Those wide divide/remainder cones\\nconsume LUTs and can constrain the common EX result path despite CoreMark\\nspending almost all of its cycles on ordinary RV32I operations and multiplies.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the combinational RV32M divide/remainder implementation with a\\nblocking iterative divide unit that accepts an EX-stage divide instruction,\\nholds front-end issue while it runs, then injects the correctly signed\\nquotient or remainder into the existing EX/MEM path. Keep add/logic/shift\\nand the shared multiplier entirely on the one-cycle EX path. This structural\\nseparation should shorten normal-cycle timing enough to raise Fmax, while\\nthe rare multi-cycle divide latency has little CoreMark IPC cost.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove real combinational divide and remainder datapaths from the fast ALU while retaining the ALTOPS behavior for formal builds.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/divider.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a signed/unsigned restoring iterative divider with start, busy, done, quotient, remainder, and RV32M divide-by-zero/overflow handling.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Dispatch DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU to the divider, hold their EX/MEM advance until completion, and route the selected result into the normal writeback and RVFI payload.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a divide-busy interlock that freezes younger work without duplicating retirement or disturbing memory-stall behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the divider request/completion handshake into execute and global pipeline stall control.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Retain fast-ALU checks and add directed RV32M divide/remainder corner cases covering signs, zero divisors, and INT_MIN divided by minus one.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove real combinational divide and remainder datapaths from the fast ALU while retaining the ALTOPS behavior for formal builds.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/divider.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a signed/unsigned restoring iterative divider with start, busy, done, quotient, remainder, and RV32M divide-by-zero/overflow handling.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Dispatch DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU to the divider, hold their EX/MEM advance until completion, and route the selected result into the normal writeback and RVFI payload.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a divide-busy interlock that freezes younger work without duplicating retirement or disturbing memory-stall behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the divider request/completion handshake into execute and global pipeline stall control.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Retain fast-ALU checks and add directed RV32M divide/remainder corner cases covering signs, zero divisors, and INT_MIN divided by minus one.\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 405.36, \"delta_pct\": 32.31, \"vs_baseline\": 43.33, \"fmax_mhz\": 182.08, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491817, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5681, \"ff\": 2226, \"seeds\": [193.35, 179.08, 182.08], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T01:11:12.351940Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r2s1 (improvement, +32.31%): Moving DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU into a blocking iterative unit removes wide divide/remainder cones from the common EX path, and CoreMark's sparse division use lets the Fmax gain outweigh the rare multi-cycle front-end stalls.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:7:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-003-r2s2\", \"title\": \"PC-indexed dynamic loop predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark repeatedly executes a small set of conditional loop branches. The\\nprevious static predictor saved cycles but placed instruction-memory data,\\nbranch decode, immediate construction, and target addition on the fetch\\nPC feedback path, causing an unacceptable Fmax regression.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark repeatedly executes a small set of conditional loop branches. The\\nprevious static predictor saved cycles but placed instruction-memory data,\\nbranch decode, immediate construction, and target addition on the fetch\\nPC feedback path, causing an unacceptable Fmax regression.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small direct-mapped branch target buffer with a two-bit saturating\\ncounter per entry. Fetch indexes it solely with the registered PC; a valid\\ntag match and taken counter select a previously learned target, otherwise\\nfetch falls through. EX continues to resolve every branch and updates the\\nentry with the resolved direction and target, then redirects only on a\\npredicted-next-PC mismatch. This warms up after one taken loop edge while\\navoiding any io_imemData-to-PC combinational dependency, preserving timing\\nbetter than BTFNT while removing recurring loop-back recovery bubbles.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small PC-indexed direct-mapped BTB with tags, branch targets, valid bits, and two-bit saturating direction counters; update it only from resolved valid conditional branches.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add predicted-next-PC metadata to the fetch/decode and downstream pipeline bundles so EX can compare prediction with the architectural resolved PC.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select PC+4 or the predictor target from registered-PC predictor outputs without decoding or using io_imemData on the PC feedback path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch the fetch prediction metadata with each decoded instruction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare the resolved branch next PC with the carried predicted next PC, emit a redirect only on mismatch, and expose resolved-branch training information.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Treat a prediction mismatch as the control recovery event while retaining existing load-use and memory-backpressure priorities.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate and wire the predictor, EX training path, prediction metadata, and misprediction recovery without changing the nret=1 top-level contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed warm-up, repeated taken-loop, final not-taken exit, alias/tag-miss, and bus-stall tests that check PC/RVFI control-flow correctness.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small PC-indexed direct-mapped BTB with tags, branch targets, valid bits, and two-bit saturating direction counters; update it only from resolved valid conditional branches.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add predicted-next-PC metadata to the fetch/decode and downstream pipeline bundles so EX can compare prediction with the architectural resolved PC.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select PC+4 or the predictor target from registered-PC predictor outputs without decoding or using io_imemData on the PC feedback path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch the fetch prediction metadata with each decoded instruction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare the resolved branch next PC with the carried predicted next PC, emit a redirect only on mismatch, and expose resolved-branch training information.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Treat a prediction mismatch as the control recovery event while retaining existing load-use and memory-backpressure priorities.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate and wire the predictor, EX training path, prediction metadata, and misprediction recovery without changing the nret=1 top-level contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed warm-up, repeated taken-loop, final not-taken exit, alias/tag-miss, and bus-stall tests that check PC/RVFI control-flow correctness.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 308.5, \"delta_pct\": 0.69, \"vs_baseline\": 9.08, \"fmax_mhz\": 131.46, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4261280, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 12633, \"ff\": 2881, \"seeds\": [131.46, 142.49, 120.55], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T01:18:31.997287Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r2s2 (regression, +0.69%): Removing imem_data from branch prediction is insufficient when a combinational PC-indexed BTB tag match, counter read, and next-PC mux remain on the fetch feedback path; CoreMark's saved loop-back bubbles did not amortize that added front-end cost.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:8:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-001-r3s0\", \"title\": \"Registered shared multiplier path\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The iterative divider removed a cold but very wide arithmetic cone from the\\ncommon EX path and delivered the current best Fmax. The remaining shared\\n33x33 multiplier is still evaluated in the combinational ALU path for every\\ncycle, so its forwarding-to-result cone can now dominate timing even when\\nthe retiring instruction is ordinary RV32I work.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The iterative divider removed a cold but very wide arithmetic cone from the\\ncommon EX path and delivered the current best Fmax. The remaining shared\\n33x33 multiplier is still evaluated in the combinational ALU path for every\\ncycle, so its forwarding-to-result cone can now dominate timing even when\\nthe retiring instruction is ordinary RV32I work.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Preserve the validated sign-selected 33x33 multiply arithmetic, but move it\\ninto a small registered blocking multiplier unit. On an RV32M multiply,\\nEX snapshots the fully forwarded operands and operation, stalls younger\\nissue while the unit produces the selected half, then injects one normal\\nEX/MEM result. Ordinary add, logic, shift, address, and branch operations\\nbypass the multiplier entirely. This trades a short fixed latency only for\\nmultiply instructions for a shorter and less congested common EX timing\\npath, analogous to the successful divide isolation without using the\\nregressive unsigned correction network.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Keep only non-multiply fast ALU operations on the combinational EX result path and retain ALTOPS-compatible operation definitions.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mul_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a registered shared 33x33 sign-selected RV32M multiplier with start, busy, done, and selected-result handshake signals.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Dispatch MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU using snapshotted forwarded operands, hold their EX advance until completion, and inject the completed result into the existing EX/MEM and RVFI flow.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a multiplier-busy interlock with the same priority discipline as the divide and memory-stall controls.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire multiplier busy status into the pipeline hazard control without changing the nret=1 top-level interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_mul_unit.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed and randomized signedness, high-half, zero, and boundary tests for the registered multiplier handshake and RV32M results.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Keep only non-multiply fast ALU operations on the combinational EX result path and retain ALTOPS-compatible operation definitions.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mul_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a registered shared 33x33 sign-selected RV32M multiplier with start, busy, done, and selected-result handshake signals.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Dispatch MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU using snapshotted forwarded operands, hold their EX advance until completion, and inject the completed result into the existing EX/MEM and RVFI flow.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a multiplier-busy interlock with the same priority discipline as the divide and memory-stall controls.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire multiplier busy status into the pipeline hazard control without changing the nret=1 top-level interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_mul_unit.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed and randomized signedness, high-half, zero, and boundary tests for the registered multiplier handshake and RV32M results.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 295.83, \"delta_pct\": -27.02, \"vs_baseline\": 4.6, \"fmax_mhz\": 138.47, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4680666, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5962, \"ff\": 2425, \"seeds\": [138.47, 140.39, 129.3], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T01:35:15.695432Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 3, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r3s0 (regression, -27.02%): Registering a blocking 33x33 multiplier without splitting its arithmetic only moves the full-cycle product into a new register-to-register cone while adding EX ownership and interlock logic, so it cannot deliver the divide unit's Fmax benefit without real pipelining or iteration.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:9:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"Decoupled sequential fetch queue\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current IF stage couples every instruction-bus ready pulse directly to\\nPC advance, so a random imem stall immediately freezes the entire pipeline.\\nCoreMark also has load-use, data-memory, and occasional divide stalls during\\nwhich sequential instruction bandwidth is otherwise unused.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current IF stage couples every instruction-bus ready pulse directly to\\nPC advance, so a random imem stall immediately freezes the entire pipeline.\\nCoreMark also has load-use, data-memory, and occasional divide stalls during\\nwhich sequential instruction bandwidth is otherwise unused.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the single combinational fetch payload with a small sequential\\ninstruction FIFO and an independent fetch PC. While decode or execute is\\nstalled, the front end continues to fill unused FIFO entries whenever imem\\nis ready; while imem is temporarily not ready, decode consumes buffered\\ninstructions. A redirect atomically invalidates queued wrong-path entries\\nand restarts fetching at the resolved target. This preserves the existing\\nEX-stage branch decision and single-retire RVFI boundary while converting\\navailable slack into tolerance of the benchmark's random instruction stalls.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the fetch-queue payload type and any queue-depth constants needed by the decoupled front end.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the direct PC-to-ID payload with a small sequential prefetch FIFO, separate fetch/consume pointers, ready-qualified fills, and redirect invalidation.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire decode-consume backpressure and existing redirect control to the queued fetch interface without changing the top-level contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate decode availability from raw imem readiness so a queued instruction can issue during an imem stall while preserving load, dmem, and divider interlocks.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed queue tests for sequential fill/drain, imem backpressure masking, and redirect flushing of wrong-path entries.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the fetch-queue payload type and any queue-depth constants needed by the decoupled front end.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the direct PC-to-ID payload with a small sequential prefetch FIFO, separate fetch/consume pointers, ready-qualified fills, and redirect invalidation.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire decode-consume backpressure and existing redirect control to the queued fetch interface without changing the top-level contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate decode availability from raw imem readiness so a queued instruction can issue during an imem stall while preserving load, dmem, and divider interlocks.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed queue tests for sequential fill/drain, imem backpressure masking, and redirect flushing of wrong-path entries.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"build_failed: yosys synth: cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv:62: ERROR: Index in generate block prefix syntax is not constant!\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 3, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r3s1 (broken, n/a): A sequential fetch FIFO must index its storage procedurally with runtime read/write pointers; generate-block hierarchy and prefixes are elaboration-time constructs and reject dynamic queue indices before synthesis.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:10:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-003-r3s2\", \"title\": \"Lookahead registered BTB predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark's repeated loop-closing branches can recover IPC, but both earlier\\npredictors regressed because their lookup logic was directly on the PC\\nfeedback path. A useful predictor must retain a short fetch path while\\nmaking its target available before the loop branch is consumed.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark's repeated loop-closing branches can recover IPC, but both earlier\\npredictors regressed because their lookup logic was directly on the PC\\nfeedback path. A useful predictor must retain a short fetch path while\\nmaking its target available before the loop branch is consumed.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Query a small tagged BTB one sequential instruction ahead, using fetch PC+4,\\nand register the hit, direction, target, and queried PC. On the following\\ncycle that registered response belongs to the current sequentially fetched\\ninstruction; if it is a taken branch prediction, IF advances to the saved\\ntarget while still delivering the branch itself to ID. Carry the predicted\\nnext PC to EX and redirect only when architectural resolution disagrees.\\nThis predicts hot loop-closing branches without imem-data decode, BTB tag\\ncompare, counter read, or target selection on the PC-to-PC timing path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small tagged, direct-mapped two-bit-counter BTB whose lookup response is registered from a PC+4 lookahead query and whose entries train only on resolved valid conditional branches.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add predicted-next-PC metadata to the IF/ID and downstream payloads so execution can verify the fetch decision precisely.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Issue the sequential PC+4 predictor query and select the next fetch PC from the registered response when it matches the current fetch PC, without flushing the correctly delivered predicted branch.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch the fetch packet's predicted next PC with the decoded instruction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare resolved control-flow next PC against the carried prediction, emit redirects only for mismatches, and expose valid conditional-branch training fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Use prediction mismatch as the control flush event while preserving existing load-use, divide, and bus-stall priority rules.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate and wire the registered lookahead predictor, predictor metadata, branch training, and mismatch recovery without changing the nret=1 top-level contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed tests for sequential lookahead warm-up, repeated taken loops, final not-taken exits, target-entry misses after redirects, aliases, and bus-stall/RVFI correctness.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small tagged, direct-mapped two-bit-counter BTB whose lookup response is registered from a PC+4 lookahead query and whose entries train only on resolved valid conditional branches.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add predicted-next-PC metadata to the IF/ID and downstream payloads so execution can verify the fetch decision precisely.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Issue the sequential PC+4 predictor query and select the next fetch PC from the registered response when it matches the current fetch PC, without flushing the correctly delivered predicted branch.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch the fetch packet's predicted next PC with the decoded instruction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare resolved control-flow next PC against the carried prediction, emit redirects only for mismatches, and expose valid conditional-branch training fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Use prediction mismatch as the control flush event while preserving existing load-use, divide, and bus-stall priority rules.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate and wire the registered lookahead predictor, predictor metadata, branch training, and mismatch recovery without changing the nret=1 top-level contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed tests for sequential lookahead warm-up, repeated taken loops, final not-taken exits, target-entry misses after redirects, aliases, and bus-stall/RVFI correctness.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"formal_failed: insn_beq_ch0\\n32:10 [insn_bltu_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:05:53  Writing trace to Yosys witness file: engine_0/trace.yw\\nSBY 22:32:10 [insn_bltu_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:05:53  Status: failed\\nSBY 22:32:10 [insn_bltu_ch0] engine_0: finished (returncode=1)\\nSBY 22:32:10 [insn_bltu_ch0] engine_0: Status returned by engine: FAIL\\nSBY 22:32:10 [insn_bltu_ch0] summary: Elapsed clock time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:05:53 (353)\\nSBY 22:32:10 [insn_bltu_ch0] summary: Elapsed process time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:04:53 (293)\\nSBY 22:32:10 [insn_bltu_ch0] summary: engine_0 (smtbmc bitwuzla) returned FAIL\\nSBY 22:32:10 [insn_bltu_ch0] summary: counterexample trace: insn_bltu_ch0/engine_0/trace.vcd\\nSBY 22:32:10 [insn_bltu_ch0] summary:   failed assertion rvfi_testbench._witness_.check_flatten_checker_inst__assert_rvfi_insn_check_sv_198_152 at rvfi_insn_chec