{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/LESSONS.md && jq -r 'select(.category == \\\"predictor\\\") | [.id, .title, .outcome, (.fitness // \\\"n/a\\\" | tostring), (.delta_pct // \\\"n/a\\\" | tostring), (.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis | gsub(\\\"\\\\n\\\"; \\\" \\\"))] | @tsv' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl && test \"'! -e cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260710-003-r9s2.yaml && echo hypothesis-id-available'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/LESSONS.md && jq -r 'select(.category == \\\"predictor\\\") | [.id, .title, .outcome, (.fitness // \\\"n/a\\\" | tostring), (.delta_pct // \\\"n/a\\\" | tostring), (.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis | gsub(\\\"\\\\n\\\"; \\\" \\\"))] | @tsv' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl && test \"'! -e cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260710-003-r9s2.yaml && echo hypothesis-id-available'","aggregated_output":"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r1s0 (improvement, +8.33%): RV32M multiply variants should share a sign-selected 33x33 product and select the required 32-bit half, since parallel signed/unsigned multiplier cones waste LUTs even though each instruction consumes only one result.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r1s2 (regression, -10.84%): A combinational BTFNT predictor that derives the PC update from imem_data puts instruction fetch, branch decode, immediate extraction, and target addition on the IF-to-PC feedback path, so it must be pipelined or decoupled when its Fmax loss exceeds CoreMark's saved redirect bubbles.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r2s0 (regression, +20.73%): Replacing a shared 33x33 signed multiplier with a 32x32 unsigned product can regress when MULH/MULHSU require chained conditional 32-bit high-half corrections, because the added subtractor network outweighs any saved multiplier logic on the CoreMark-critical ALU path.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r2s1 (improvement, +32.31%): Moving DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU into a blocking iterative unit removes wide divide/remainder cones from the common EX path, and CoreMark's sparse division use lets the Fmax gain outweigh the rare multi-cycle front-end stalls.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r2s2 (regression, +0.69%): Removing imem_data from branch prediction is insufficient when a combinational PC-indexed BTB tag match, counter read, and next-PC mux remain on the fetch feedback path; CoreMark's saved loop-back bubbles did not amortize that added front-end cost.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r3s0 (regression, -27.02%): Registering a blocking 33x33 multiplier without splitting its arithmetic only moves the full-cycle product into a new register-to-register cone while adding EX ownership and interlock logic, so it cannot deliver the divide unit's Fmax benefit without real pipelining or iteration.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r3s1 (broken, n/a): A sequential fetch FIFO must index its storage procedurally with runtime read/write pointers; generate-block hierarchy and prefixes are elaboration-time constructs and reject dynamic queue indices before synthesis.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r4s0 (broken, n/a): Decode-qualified load-use detection must classify every architectural source in the same valid/timing domain as operand capture, because suppressing a real dependency lets stale register data reach EX and is caught by RVFI register checks.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r4s1 (regression, -18.56%): A four-entry procedural IF queue can lose CoreMark fitness when its pointer/count, dequeue arbitration, and redirect-flush control cost more than the transient imem stalls that prefetch can cover, so front-end decoupling needs measured slack rather than assumed stall hiding.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r5s2 (broken, n/a): A registered lookahead BTB must treat its response PC and predicted next PC as a fetch-acceptance transaction, invalidating or holding them consistently across redirects and stalls, or stale predictions can misassociate with conditional branches and violate RVFI control-flow checks.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r6s0 (improvement, +1.51%): Encoding BEQ/BNE and signed/unsigned relational branches from one 33-bit subtract's zero, sign, and borrow flags shares a carry-chain-friendly EX cone, modestly improving CoreMark loop-branch timing without changing redirect behavior.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r6s1 (broken, n/a): A write-behind store buffer must make buffer admission, external write acknowledgement, and RVFI retirement one ordered transaction boundary, since retiring a store while its request can outlive or be overwritten by younger MEM traffic violates architectural load/store ordering and register-state checks.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r6s2 (broken, n/a): A PC-only branch predictor must validate the fetched instruction's branch identity or rely on immutable instruction memory, because formal's per-cycle symbolic imem can reuse a trained PC for a non-branch and make the predictor skip its required PC+4.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r7s0 (improvement, +4.61%): On an FPGA MEM path, replacing variable 32-bit load shifts and shifted store masks with fixed addr-indexed byte/halfword lane muxes removes a barrel-mux cone after dmem and improves Fmax when alignment trapping already restricts halfword selection.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r7s1 (regression, -11.37%): Zero-wait MEM-to-EX forwarding creates a dmem-return-to-load-format-to-operand-mux-to-ALU critical path, so CoreMark's saved load-use bubbles can be outweighed by Fmax loss unless the load response is timing-isolated.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r8s2 (broken, n/a): A signature-mismatched conditional-branch prediction must redirect to the resolved PC+4 even when the branch is not taken, because treating it as a cold not-taken branch without recovery leaves the already-applied stale taken target on the fetch path and violates RVFI PC-forward progress.\nhyp-20260710-003-r1s2\tBackward-taken fetch branch prediction\tregression\t252.17\t-10.84\tAdd a combinational backward-taken, forward-not-taken (BTFNT) predictor in IF. For a valid BRANCH encoding with a negative B immediate, fetch will select the statically computed target instead of PC+4 and carry the predicted next PC with the instruction. EX will compare that prediction with its resolved next PC, redirect and squash only on disagreement. This removes the recurring loop-back recovery bubbles without adding a RAM-based BTB or a predictor-table timing path. \nhyp-20260710-003-r2s2\tPC-indexed dynamic loop predictor\tregression\t308.5\t0.69\tAdd a small direct-mapped branch target buffer with a two-bit saturating counter per entry. Fetch indexes it solely with the registered PC; a valid tag match and taken counter select a previously learned target, otherwise fetch falls through. EX continues to resolve every branch and updates the entry with the resolved direction and target, then redirects only on a predicted-next-PC mismatch. This warms up after one taken loop edge while avoiding any io_imemData-to-PC combinational dependency, preserving timing better than BTFNT while removing recurring loop-back recovery bubbles. \nhyp-20260710-003-r3s2\tLookahead registered BTB predictor\tbroken\tn/a\tn/a\tQuery a small tagged BTB one sequential instruction ahead, using fetch PC+4, and register the hit, direction, target, and queried PC. On the following cycle that registered response belongs to the current sequentially fetched instruction; if it is a taken branch prediction, IF advances to the saved target while still delivering the branch itself to ID. Carry the predicted next PC to EX and redirect only when architectural resolution disagrees. This predicts hot loop-closing branches without imem-data decode, BTB tag compare, counter read, or target selection on the PC-to-PC timing path. \nhyp-20260710-003-r4s2\tReturn-address-stack fetch predictor\tregression\t364.95\t-9.97\tAdd a shallow committed return-address stack and predict only canonical returns (`jalr x0, x1/x5, 0`) in IF. The prediction is just an instruction field match and a mux of the registered RAS top, avoiding branch-immediate extraction, target addition, tags, and counter RAM on the PC path. Train pushes and pops only when the corresponding JAL/JALR has resolved in EX; carry the predicted next PC to EX and retain existing redirect recovery for any mismatch. Correct returns therefore avoid the existing two younger wrong-path instructions without changing conditional-branch behavior. \nhyp-20260710-003-r5s2\tValidated lookahead BTB predictor\tbroken\tn/a\tn/a\tImplement a small direct-mapped conditional-branch BTB whose lookup is made for sequential PC+4 and registered before it can influence fetch. A response may redirect only when its recorded query PC exactly equals the current fetch PC; otherwise fetch deterministically falls through and invalidates the stale response. EX trains only resolved valid branches and carries the exact predicted next PC for mismatch recovery. Redirects, instruction stalls, and downstream holds invalidate or preserve the response according to whether the matching fetched instruction was actually accepted. This removes hot loop recovery bubbles while keeping tag comparison, counter read, and target selection out of the PC-to-PC feedback path. \nhyp-20260710-003-r6s2\tOne-entry loop target predictor\tbroken\tn/a\tn/a\tAdd a one-entry last-resolved conditional-branch predictor. It stores only the PC, resolved target, and last direction of the most recently resolved valid conditional branch; fetch predicts taken only when the registered PC exactly matches that entry and the stored direction is taken. This replaces a table lookup, tag array, and saturating-counter logic with a single equality check and target mux, while preserving EX as the sole architectural resolver. The fetched instruction carries an explicit predicted-next-PC transaction; EX redirects only on a mismatch and trains the entry only when that branch actually advances, with redirect and all front-end holds keeping prediction metadata paired with its accepted instruction. \nhyp-20260710-003-r7s2\tSpeculation-safe loop target predictor\tregression\t167.49\t-59.3\tAdd a one-entry last-resolved conditional-branch predictor, but make its prediction a transaction carried by every fetched instruction rather than a branch-only promise. The entry stores branch PC, target, and last direction; its small registered-PC equality and target mux remain the only logic added to fetch. IF/ID and ID/EX carry predicted_next_pc for all accepted fetches. In EX, compare that value against the architectural next PC for every valid instruction: non-control instructions resolve to PC+4, branches and jumps resolve normally. Any mismatch redirects and flushes, including a predictor hit whose symbolic imem word is no longer a branch. Train the entry only when a valid resolved conditional branch advances from EX, and bind prediction metadata to its fetch PC across imem/dmem stalls and redirects. This retains the timing advantage of a one-entry PC-only predictor while making all stale or aliased hits recoverable under RVFI. \nhyp-20260710-003-r8s2\tSignature-qualified loop predictor\tbroken\tn/a\tn/a\tAdd a one-entry last-taken conditional-branch predictor that stores branch PC, target, direction, and the resolved instruction word as a signature. IF uses only registered PC equality, direction, and target muxing to select the speculative successor; the instruction signature is carried as sideband validation metadata and is not placed in the PC-select cone. EX retains architectural branch resolution and redirects only for a predicted control instruction whose resolved successor differs, or for a predicted hit that resolves as a non-branch (including a symbolic-imem signature mismatch), explicitly restoring PC+4. This avoids assuming PC identity proves instruction identity, while replacing the prior universal 32-bit next-PC compare with narrow prediction-valid/control-qualified recovery. \nhypothesis-id-available\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
