{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260710-003-r9s2.yaml && git status --short --untracked-files=all | rg 'hyp-20260710-003-r9s2' || true\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260710-003-r9s2.yaml && git status --short --untracked-files=all | rg 'hyp-20260710-003-r9s2' || true\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20260710-003-r9s2\ntitle: \"Epoch-bound registered loop predictor\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  CoreMark repeatedly executes hot backward conditional branches, but every\n  previous direct fetch predictor either lengthened the PC feedback path or\n  let a registered prediction become detached from the instruction it was\n  meant to accelerate under stalls and redirects.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a one-entry last-taken conditional-branch predictor whose lookup is\n  issued only for the sequential candidate PC+4 and whose response is\n  registered at the same edge that advances the fetch PC to that candidate.\n  The response carries its queried PC and a fetch epoch; it may select its\n  stored target only for that exact next accepted fetch, then is consumed.\n  Redirects increment the epoch and invalidate the response, while imem,\n  dmem, and divide holds retain or discard it according to whether its query\n  PC was actually accepted. EX carries the prediction transaction with every\n  fetched instruction and compares the architectural successor for every\n  valid instruction, recovering to PC+4 for symbolic-imem non-branch alias\n  hits as well as normal direction/target mispredictions. This saves one\n  hot-loop recovery cycle without putting BTB lookup, target selection, or\n  instruction decode in the PC-to-PC combinational path.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/lookahead_loop_predictor.sv\n    description: \"Implement a resettable one-entry last-taken branch record plus a registered PC+4 lookup response tagged with query PC and fetch epoch.\"\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add accepted-fetch prediction transaction fields, including queried PC, predicted successor, valid bit, and epoch, to the pipeline payloads.\"\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Capture a registered lookahead response only on a sequential fetch acceptance, consume it only for its exact tagged PC and epoch, and prioritize older EX recovery.\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Latch the predictor transaction atomically with the fetched instruction so ordinary holds and flushes cannot detach metadata.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Compare each valid instruction's architectural successor against its carried prediction, recover every mismatch including non-branch alias hits, and emit advance-qualified branch training.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Define fetch-acceptance and redirect/hold precedence so predictor response creation, consumption, and invalidation match pipeline progress.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire predictor lookup, epoch management, accepted-fetch metadata, EX recovery, and branch training while preserving the nret=1 top-level RVFI contract.\"\n  - file: test/test_predictor.py\n    description: \"Add directed warm-up, repeated-loop, final-exit, redirect, imem/dmem-stall, divide-hold, and symbolic-PC-alias recovery tests with RVFI PC-order checks.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
