ent/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_6-terra-rep1/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20260710-002-r9s1/formal/last_run-1816.log\\nPer-check logs in: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_6-terra-rep1/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20260710-002-r9s1/formal/riscv-formal/cores/bench-1816/checks/<check>/logfile.txt\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 9, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r9s1 (broken, n/a): A decoupled in-order completion queue must make dequeue an atomic retirement event for RVFI payload/order, register-file writeback, and PC-forward state, because separate retire and commit views can misalign architectural instruction state across queue transitions.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:28:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-003-r9s2\", \"title\": \"Epoch-bound registered loop predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark repeatedly executes hot backward conditional branches, but every\\nprevious direct fetch predictor either lengthened the PC feedback path or\\nlet a registered prediction become detached from the instruction it was\\nmeant to accelerate under stalls and redirects.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark repeatedly executes hot backward conditional branches, but every\\nprevious direct fetch predictor either lengthened the PC feedback path or\\nlet a registered prediction become detached from the instruction it was\\nmeant to accelerate under stalls and redirects.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry last-taken conditional-branch predictor whose lookup is\\nissued only for the sequential candidate PC+4 and whose response is\\nregistered at the same edge that advances the fetch PC to that candidate.\\nThe response carries its queried PC and a fetch epoch; it may select its\\nstored target only for that exact next accepted fetch, then is consumed.\\nRedirects increment the epoch and invalidate the response, while imem,\\ndmem, and divide holds retain or discard it according to whether its query\\nPC was actually accepted. EX carries the prediction transaction with every\\nfetched instruction and compares the architectural successor for every\\nvalid instruction, recovering to PC+4 for symbolic-imem non-branch alias\\nhits as well as normal direction/target mispredictions. This saves one\\nhot-loop recovery cycle without putting BTB lookup, target selection, or\\ninstruction decode in the PC-to-PC combinational path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/lookahead_loop_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement a resettable one-entry last-taken branch record plus a registered PC+4 lookup response tagged with query PC and fetch epoch.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add accepted-fetch prediction transaction fields, including queried PC, predicted successor, valid bit, and epoch, to the pipeline payloads.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Capture a registered lookahead response only on a sequential fetch acceptance, consume it only for its exact tagged PC and epoch, and prioritize older EX recovery.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch the predictor transaction atomically with the fetched instruction so ordinary holds and flushes cannot detach metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare each valid instruction's architectural successor against its carried prediction, recover every mismatch including non-branch alias hits, and emit advance-qualified branch training.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Define fetch-acceptance and redirect/hold precedence so predictor response creation, consumption, and invalidation match pipeline progress.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire predictor lookup, epoch management, accepted-fetch metadata, EX recovery, and branch training while preserving the nret=1 top-level RVFI contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed warm-up, repeated-loop, final-exit, redirect, imem/dmem-stall, divide-hold, and symbolic-PC-alias recovery tests with RVFI PC-order checks.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/lookahead_loop_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement a resettable one-entry last-taken branch record plus a registered PC+4 lookup response tagged with query PC and fetch epoch.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add accepted-fetch prediction transaction fields, including queried PC, predicted successor, valid bit, and epoch, to the pipeline payloads.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Capture a registered lookahead response only on a sequential fetch acceptance, consume it only for its exact tagged PC and epoch, and prioritize older EX recovery.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch the predictor transaction atomically with the fetched instruction so ordinary holds and flushes cannot detach metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare each valid instruction's architectural successor against its carried prediction, recover every mismatch including non-branch alias hits, and emit advance-qualified branch training.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Define fetch-acceptance and redirect/hold precedence so predictor response creation, consumption, and invalidation match pipeline progress.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire predictor lookup, epoch management, accepted-fetch metadata, EX recovery, and branch training while preserving the nret=1 top-level RVFI contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed warm-up, repeated-loop, final-exit, redirect, imem/dmem-stall, divide-hold, and symbolic-PC-alias recovery tests with RVFI PC-order checks.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 343.92, \"delta_pct\": -20.1, \"vs_baseline\": 21.6, \"fmax_mhz\": 148.59, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4320468, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5975, \"ff\": 2379, \"seeds\": [156.74, 148.59, 142.33], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T02:57:47.515089Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 9, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260710-003-r9s2 (regression, -20.10%): An epoch-safe registered loop predictor can still lose badly when every EX instruction computes and 32-bit-compares an architectural successor against carried prediction metadata, because that recovery logic extends the hot EX redirect cone more than CoreMark amortizes the saved loop-back bubble.\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/LESSONS.md && jq -r 'select(.category == \\\"micro_opt\\\") | [.id,.title,.outcome,(.fitness // \\\"n/a\\\"),(.delta_pct // \\\"n/a\\\"),(.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis // \\\"\\\")] | @tsv' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/LESSONS.md && jq -r 'select(.category == \\\"micro_opt\\\") | [.id,.title,.outcome,(.fitness // \\\"n/a\\\"),(.delta_pct // \\\"n/a\\\"),(.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis // \\\"\\\")] | @tsv' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r1s0 (improvement, +8.33%): RV32M multiply variants should share a sign-selected 33x33 product and select the required 32-bit half, since parallel signed/unsigned multiplier cones waste LUTs even though each instruction consumes only one result.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r1s2 (regression, -10.84%): A combinational BTFNT predictor that derives the PC update from imem_data puts instruction fetch, branch decode, immediate extraction, and target addition on the IF-to-PC feedback path, so it must be pipelined or decoupled when its Fmax loss exceeds CoreMark's saved redirect bubbles.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r2s0 (regression, +20.73%): Replacing a shared 33x33 signed multiplier with a 32x32 unsigned product can regress when MULH/MULHSU require chained conditional 32-bit high-half corrections, because the added subtractor network outweighs any saved multiplier logic on the CoreMark-critical ALU path.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r2s1 (improvement, +32.31%): Moving DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU into a blocking iterative unit removes wide divide/remainder cones from the common EX path, and CoreMark's sparse division use lets the Fmax gain outweigh the rare multi-cycle front-end stalls.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r2s2 (regression, +0.69%): Removing imem_data from branch prediction is insufficient when a combinational PC-indexed BTB tag match, counter read, and next-PC mux remain on the fetch feedback path; CoreMark's saved loop-back bubbles did not amortize that added front-end cost.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r3s0 (regression, -27.02%): Registering a blocking 33x33 multiplier without splitting its arithmetic only moves the full-cycle product into a new register-to-register cone while adding EX ownership and interlock logic, so it cannot deliver the divide unit's Fmax benefit without real pipelining or iteration.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r3s1 (broken, n/a): A sequential fetch FIFO must index its storage procedurally with runtime read/write pointers; generate-block hierarchy and prefixes are elaboration-time constructs and reject dynamic queue indices before synthesis.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r4s0 (broken, n/a): Decode-qualified load-use detection must classify every architectural source in the same valid/timing domain as operand capture, because suppressing a real dependency lets stale register data reach EX and is caught by RVFI register checks.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r4s1 (regression, -18.56%): A four-entry procedural IF queue can lose CoreMark fitness when its pointer/count, dequeue arbitration, and redirect-flush control cost more than the transient imem stalls that prefetch can cover, so front-end decoupling needs measured slack rather than assumed stall hiding.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r5s2 (broken, n/a): A registered lookahead BTB must treat its response PC and predicted next PC as a fetch-acceptance transaction, invalidating or holding them consistently across redirects and stalls, or stale predictions can misassociate with conditional branches and violate RVFI control-flow checks.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r6s0 (improvement, +1.51%): Encoding BEQ/BNE and signed/unsigned relational branches from one 33-bit subtract's zero, sign, and borrow flags shares a carry-chain-friendly EX cone, modestly improving CoreMark loop-branch timing without changing redirect behavior.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r6s1 (broken, n/a): A write-behind store buffer must make buffer admission, external write acknowledgement, and RVFI retirement one ordered transaction boundary, since retiring a store while its request can outlive or be overwritten by younger MEM traffic violates architectural load/store ordering and register-state checks.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r6s2 (broken, n/a): A PC-only branch predictor must validate the fetched instruction's branch identity or rely on immutable instruction memory, because formal's per-cycle symbolic imem can reuse a trained PC for a non-branch and make the predictor skip its required PC+4.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r7s0 (improvement, +4.61%): On an FPGA MEM path, replacing variable 32-bit load shifts and shifted store masks with fixed addr-indexed byte/halfword lane muxes removes a barrel-mux cone after dmem and improves Fmax when alignment trapping already restricts halfword selection.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r7s1 (regression, -11.37%): Zero-wait MEM-to-EX forwarding creates a dmem-return-to-load-format-to-operand-mux-to-ALU critical path, so CoreMark's saved load-use bubbles can be outweighed by Fmax loss unless the load response is timing-isolated.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r8s2 (broken, n/a): A signature-mismatched conditional-branch prediction must redirect to the resolved PC+4 even when the branch is not taken, because treating it as a cold not-taken branch without recovery leaves the already-applied stale taken target on the fetch path and violates RVFI PC-forward progress.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r9s1 (broken, n/a): A decoupled in-order completion queue must make dequeue an atomic retirement event for RVFI payload/order, register-file writeback, and PC-forward state, because separate retire and commit views can misalign architectural instruction state across queue transitions.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260710-003-r9s2 (regression, -20.10%): An epoch-safe registered loop predictor can still lose badly when every EX instruction computes and 32-bit-compares an architectural successor against carried prediction metadata, because that recovery logic extends the hot EX redirect cone more than CoreMark amortizes the saved loop-back bubble.\nbaseline-bench-2d60805\tBaseline retest for cores/bench\timprovement\t282.82\t0.0\t\nhyp-20260710-001-r1s0\tShare RV32 multiply datapath\timprovement\t306.38\t8.33\tReplace the unconditional signed-signed, unsigned-unsigned, and\\nsigned-unsigned product expressions with one shared 33x33 unsigned modular\\nmultiplier. Select the operands' sign extension from the decoded multiply\\noperation, then select bits [31:0] for MUL or [63:32] for MULH, MULHU, and\\nMULHSU. The low 64 bits of the 33-bit two's-complement product preserve all\\nRV32M result definitions, while removing two large parallel multiplier\\ncones. Keep the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS expressions unchanged.\\n\nhyp-20260710-001-r2s0\tUnsigned multiplier with signed corrections\tregression\t369.9\t20.73\tReplace the sign-selected 33x33 multiplier with one unsigned 32x32 product.\\nUse its low half directly for MUL and its high half for MULHU. For MULH,\\nsubtract operand b from the unsigned high half when a is negative and\\nsubtract operand a when b is negative; for MULHSU subtract b when a is\\nnegative. These modulo-2^32 corrections exactly implement the signed\\nproducts while avoiding sign-extension bits in the multiplier. Preserve the\\nRISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS operations and all division/reminder behavior.\\n\nhyp-20260710-001-r3s0\tRegistered shared multiplier path\tregression\t295.83\t-27.02\tPreserve the validated sign-selected 33x33 multiply arithmetic, but move it\\ninto a small registered blocking multiplier unit. On an RV32M multiply,\\nEX snapshots the fully forwarded operands and operation, stalls younger\\nissue while the unit produces the selected half, then injects one normal\\nEX/MEM result. Ordinary add, logic, shift, address, and branch operations\\nbypass the multiplier entirely. This trades a short fixed latency only for\\nmultiply instructions for a shorter and less congested common EX timing\\npath, analogous to the successful divide isolation without using the\\nregressive unsigned correction network.\\n\nhyp-20260710-001-r4s0\tDecode-aware load-use interlock\tbroken\tn/a\tn/a\tMake the load-use hazard detector aware of whether the IF/ID opcode actually\\nconsumes rs1 and/or rs2. Preserve the one-cycle interlock for real dependent\\nconsumers, while suppressing it for operand-immediate ALU operations, loads,\\nJAL, LUI, AUIPC, FENCE, EBREAK, and illegal encodings whose bit fields are\\nnot architectural sources. This removes false bubbles without changing the\\nPC, memory, RVFI, or arithmetic critical paths.\\n\nhyp-20260710-001-r5s0\tExhaustive source-aware load interlock\tbroken\tn/a\tn/a\tReplace the raw-field load-use comparison with an exhaustive, opcode-local\\nsource-use table in the hazard unit. Classify rs1 as live only for OP,\\nOP-IMM, LOAD, STORE, BRANCH, and JALR, and rs2 only for OP, STORE, and\\nBRANCH; gate the corresponding comparisons with these bits and IF/ID valid.\\nKeep every true one-cycle load dependency stalled, including all RV32M OP\\noperations, branches, stores, and JALR. This removes false bubbles without\\nadding work to the fetch or EX arithmetic timing paths.\\n\nhyp-20260710-001-r6s0\tShared branch subtract flags\timprovement\t411.5\t1.51\tReplace the six separately expressed branch comparisons with one 33-bit\\nsubtract/flag network in EX. Derive equality from the difference, signed less-\\nthan from operand signs plus the difference sign, and unsigned less-than from\\nthe subtraction borrow; select the requested BEQ/BNE/BLT/BGE/BLTU/BGEU result.\\nThis should map to one carry-chain-friendly arithmetic cone instead of parallel\\ncomparator logic while retaining the existing EX-stage redirect timing, stalls,\\nand RVFI PC behavior.\\n\nhyp-20260710-001-r7s0\tFixed lane memory extraction\timprovement\t430.45\t4.61\tReplace variable-shift based load extraction and variable-shift byte-mask\\nformation with fixed, address-indexed lane muxes. Select byte data with a\\nfour-arm addr[1:0] case, select aligned halfword data with addr[1], and form\\nSB/SH masks from the same fixed cases. Preserve the existing misalignment\\ntrap and dmem gating exactly, keep word loads/stores direct, and retain all\\nRVFI memory payload semantics. This removes a general 32-bit barrel-shift\\nnetwork from the dmem-return path without changing pipeline timing or IPC.\\n\nhyp-20260710-001-r8s0\tExact source-aware load interlock\tbroken\tn/a\tn/a\tDerive explicit uses-rs1 and uses-rs2 signals from the validated decode of\\nthe IF/ID instruction, and gate the existing load-use comparisons with those\\nsignals and IF/ID validity. Classify every architectural source exactly:\\nOP, STORE, and BRANCH use both; OP-IMM, LOAD, and JALR use rs1 only; all\\nremaining legal instructions and traps use neither. Keep the existing\\none-cycle stall for every true dependency, so the MEM/WB forwarding boundary\\nand the dmem-to-EX timing path remain unchanged while false interlocks no\\nlonger cost CoreMark issue cycles.\\n\nhyp-20260710-001-r9s0\tPrecompute PC-relative control targets\tregression\t419.9\t-2.45\tForm and register pc + immediate in ID together with the existing immediate\\nand instruction payload, then use that registered value in EX for branch\\ntargets and direct-JAL targets. Keep JALR's forwarded rs1 + immediate\\ncalculation, branch predicate, redirect timing, misalignment detection, and\\nRVFI pc_wdata semantics unchanged. This relocates a 32-bit adder from the\\nbranch decision/redirect path into the earlier ID-to-ID/EX path, where it is\\nindependent of forwarding and branch comparison, reducing the critical\\ncontrol-flow cone while preserving the pipeline's current IPC.\\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
