the common EX result path after\\nthe divider was isolated. Prior work showed that merely registering this\\ncone adds control cost without shortening it; a real arithmetic split is\\nneeded to convert multiplier timing pressure into usable FPGA Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the one-cycle 33x33 signed multiplier with a blocking, two-stage\\npartial-product unit. Stage one captures forwarded operands and computes\\nfour 16/17-bit partial products; stage two recombines their shifted sum,\\nselects the RV32M high or low word, and inserts the result once into the\\nexisting EX/MEM path. Hold younger issue only for the added multiply stage,\\nwhile older MEM/WB retirement continues normally. This removes the wide\\nmultiplier from the normal ALU cone and should raise Fmax enough to offset\\nCoreMark's sparse one-cycle multiply interlocks.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the real 33x33 product from the fast ALU and retain only non-multiply operations plus matching RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mul_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a two-stage signedness-selected partial-product multiplier with operand/result registers, RV32M high/low selection, and a start/busy/done handshake.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Dispatch all MUL-family operations to the new unit, snapshot forwarded sources and instruction metadata, and emit the completed result into EX/MEM exactly once.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the long-operation interlock for the multiplier's two-stage lifecycle while preserving load-use, redirect, divide, and dmem-stall priorities.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire multiplier busy/completion control into execute and global stall signals without changing the nret=1 interface or RVFI retirement boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Add exact RV32M multiply high/low corner and randomized vectors, including dependent consumers and consecutive multiply retirement ordering.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the real 33x33 product from the fast ALU and retain only non-multiply operations plus matching RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mul_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a two-stage signedness-selected partial-product multiplier with operand/result registers, RV32M high/low selection, and a start/busy/done handshake.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Dispatch all MUL-family operations to the new unit, snapshot forwarded sources and instruction metadata, and emit the completed result into EX/MEM exactly once.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the long-operation interlock for the multiplier's two-stage lifecycle while preserving load-use, redirect, divide, and dmem-stall priorities.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire multiplier busy/completion control into execute and global stall signals without changing the nret=1 interface or RVFI retirement boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Add exact RV32M multiply high/low corner and randomized vectors, including dependent consumers and consecutive multiply retirement ordering.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 355.48, \"delta_pct\": -17.42, \"vs_baseline\": 25.69, \"fmax_mhz\": 166.39, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4680666, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 6224, \"ff\": 2556, \"seeds\": [177.3, 166.39, 160.15], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T03:22:53.733150Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 11, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r11s1 (regression, -17.42%): Splitting RV32M multiplication into parallel partial products and a blocking recombination stage can still regress CoreMark when the extra EX interlock and wide shifted-sum reduction cost more than removing the monolithic multiplier from the normal ALU cone, especially for sparse multiplies.\"}\ncores/bench/test/test_alu.py:162:    \"\"\"Exercise every sign mode through the shared multiplier datapath.\"\"\"\nbaseline-bench-2d60805 improvement Baseline retest for cores/bench :: null\nhyp-20260710-001-r1s0 improvement Share RV32 multiply datapath :: Replace the unconditional signed-signed, unsigned-unsigned, and\nsigned-unsigned product expressions with one shared 33x33 unsigned modular\nmultiplier. Select the operands' sign extension from the decoded multiply\noperation, then select bits [31:0] for MUL or [63:32] for MULH, MULHU, and\nMULHSU. The low 64 bits of the 33-bit two's-complement product preserve all\nRV32M result definitions, while removing two large parallel multiplier\ncones. Keep the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS expressions unchanged.\n\nhyp-20260710-001-r2s0 regression Unsigned multiplier with signed corrections :: Replace the sign-selected 33x33 multiplier with one unsigned 32x32 product.\nUse its low half directly for MUL and its high half for MULHU. For MULH,\nsubtract operand b from the unsigned high half when a is negative and\nsubtract operand a when b is negative; for MULHSU subtract b when a is\nnegative. These modulo-2^32 corrections exactly implement the signed\nproducts while avoiding sign-extension bits in the multiplier. Preserve the\nRISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS operations and all division/reminder behavior.\n\nhyp-20260710-001-r3s0 regression Registered shared multiplier path :: Preserve the validated sign-selected 33x33 multiply arithmetic, but move it\ninto a small registered blocking multiplier unit. On an RV32M multiply,\nEX snapshots the fully forwarded operands and operation, stalls younger\nissue while the unit produces the selected half, then injects one normal\nEX/MEM result. Ordinary add, logic, shift, address, and branch operations\nbypass the multiplier entirely. This trades a short fixed latency only for\nmultiply instructions for a shorter and less congested common EX timing\npath, analogous to the successful divide isolation without using the\nregressive unsigned correction network.\n\nhyp-20260710-001-r4s0 broken Decode-aware load-use interlock :: Make the load-use hazard detector aware of whether the IF/ID opcode actually\nconsumes rs1 and/or rs2. Preserve the one-cycle interlock for real dependent\nconsumers, while suppressing it for operand-immediate ALU operations, loads,\nJAL, LUI, AUIPC, FENCE, EBREAK, and illegal encodings whose bit fields are\nnot architectural sources. This removes false bubbles without changing the\nPC, memory, RVFI, or arithmetic critical paths.\n\nhyp-20260710-001-r5s0 broken Exhaustive source-aware load interlock :: Replace the raw-field load-use comparison with an exhaustive, opcode-local\nsource-use table in the hazard unit. Classify rs1 as live only for OP,\nOP-IMM, LOAD, STORE, BRANCH, and JALR, and rs2 only for OP, STORE, and\nBRANCH; gate the corresponding comparisons with these bits and IF/ID valid.\nKeep every true one-cycle load dependency stalled, including all RV32M OP\noperations, branches, stores, and JALR. This removes false bubbles without\nadding work to the fetch or EX arithmetic timing paths.\n\nhyp-20260710-001-r6s0 improvement Shared branch subtract flags :: Replace the six separately expressed branch comparisons with one 33-bit\nsubtract/flag network in EX. Derive equality from the difference, signed less-\nthan from operand signs plus the difference sign, and unsigned less-than from\nthe subtraction borrow; select the requested BEQ/BNE/BLT/BGE/BLTU/BGEU result.\nThis should map to one carry-chain-friendly arithmetic cone instead of parallel\ncomparator logic while retaining the existing EX-stage redirect timing, stalls,\nand RVFI PC behavior.\n\nhyp-20260710-001-r7s0 improvement Fixed lane memory extraction :: Replace variable-shift based load extraction and variable-shift byte-mask\nformation with fixed, address-indexed lane muxes. Select byte data with a\nfour-arm addr[1:0] case, select aligned halfword data with addr[1], and form\nSB/SH masks from the same fixed cases. Preserve the existing misalignment\ntrap and dmem gating exactly, keep word loads/stores direct, and retain all\nRVFI memory payload semantics. This removes a general 32-bit barrel-shift\nnetwork from the dmem-return path without changing pipeline timing or IPC.\n\nhyp-20260710-001-r8s0 broken Exact source-aware load interlock :: Derive explicit uses-rs1 and uses-rs2 signals from the validated decode of\nthe IF/ID instruction, and gate the existing load-use comparisons with those\nsignals and IF/ID validity. Classify every architectural source exactly:\nOP, STORE, and BRANCH use both; OP-IMM, LOAD, and JALR use rs1 only; all\nremaining legal instructions and traps use neither. Keep the existing\none-cycle stall for every true dependency, so the MEM/WB forwarding boundary\nand the dmem-to-EX timing path remain unchanged while false interlocks no\nlonger cost CoreMark issue cycles.\n\nhyp-20260710-001-r9s0 regression Precompute PC-relative control targets :: Form and register pc + immediate in ID together with the existing immediate\nand instruction payload, then use that registered value in EX for branch\ntargets and direct-JAL targets. Keep JALR's forwarded rs1 + immediate\ncalculation, branch predicate, redirect timing, misalignment detection, and\nRVFI pc_wdata semantics unchanged. This relocates a 32-bit adder from the\nbranch decision/redirect path into the earlier ID-to-ID/EX path, where it is\nindependent of forwarding and branch comparison, reducing the critical\ncontrol-flow cone while preserving the pipeline's current IPC.\n\nhyp-20260711-001-r10s0 regression Share ALU barrel shifter :: Replace the three variable-shift expressions with one right-shift barrel\ndatapath. For SLL, reverse the operand bits before the shared logical\nright shift and reverse the result afterward; for SRL and SRA, feed the\noperand directly and select zero or sign fill. Bit reversal is pure wiring,\nso this removes duplicate wide shift-mux cones while preserving one-cycle\nALU latency, forwarding, branch timing, and every RV32I shift result.\n\nhyp-20260711-001-r11s0 broken Decoder-owned load-use source masks :: Make the validated decoder the single source of uses_rs1 and uses_rs2\nmetadata, then carry those bits with the IF/ID payload to gate the existing\nload-use comparisons. Classify OP, STORE, and BRANCH as two-source;\nOP-IMM, LOAD, and JALR as rs1-only; and JAL, LUI, AUIPC, FENCE, and EBREAK\nas source-free. Illegal or unrecognized encodings remain conservatively\ntwo-source so the interlock cannot weaken trap-path operand capture. This\nremoves only false bubbles while retaining the current pipeline, forwarding,\nmemory timing, RVFI payload, and all true load dependencies.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
