arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_6-terra-rep1/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20260710-001-r8s0/formal/last_run-51008.log\\nPer-check logs in: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_6-terra-rep1/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20260710-001-r8s0/formal/riscv-formal/cores/bench-51008/checks/<check>/logfile.txt\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 8}\n29:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r10s0\", \"title\": \"Share ALU barrel shifter\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The ALU currently describes independent variable left, logical-right, and\\narithmetic-right shifts. These wide mux networks consume LUTs even though\\nan instruction selects only one shift result, and they sit in the common\\nsingle-cycle EX datapath used by CoreMark integer code.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The ALU currently describes independent variable left, logical-right, and\\narithmetic-right shifts. These wide mux networks consume LUTs even though\\nan instruction selects only one shift result, and they sit in the common\\nsingle-cycle EX datapath used by CoreMark integer code.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the three variable-shift expressions with one right-shift barrel\\ndatapath. For SLL, reverse the operand bits before the shared logical\\nright shift and reverse the result afterward; for SRL and SRA, feed the\\noperand directly and select zero or sign fill. Bit reversal is pure wiring,\\nso this removes duplicate wide shift-mux cones while preserving one-cycle\\nALU latency, forwarding, branch timing, and every RV32I shift result.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement a shared right-shift barrel datapath with wire-only bit reversal for SLL and selectable sign extension for SRA, preserving all non-shift and ALTOPS behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Add exhaustive shift-count boundary vectors and randomized SLL/SRL/SRA checks, including sign-bit and all-ones operands, against RV32I reference results.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement a shared right-shift barrel datapath with wire-only bit reversal for SLL and selectable sign extension for SRA, preserving all non-shift and ALTOPS behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Add exhaustive shift-count boundary vectors and randomized SLL/SRL/SRA checks, including sign-bit and all-ones operands, against RV32I reference results.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 412.88, \"delta_pct\": -4.08, \"vs_baseline\": 45.99, \"fmax_mhz\": 185.46, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491817, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5597, \"ff\": 2217, \"seeds\": [188.29, 173.88, 185.46], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T03:10:19.961425Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 10, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r10s0 (regression, -4.08%): Sharing RV32I shifts through a 33-bit arithmetic-right barrel with pre/post bit-reversal can hurt CoreMark because the direction/sign-control muxing and reversal routing extend the hot EX shift-result path more than the removed parallel shift cones save.\"}\n35:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r12s0\", \"title\": \"Validity-masked LUTRAM register file\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The 32 by 32 register file currently resets every data bit, forcing a\\nresettable flip-flop implementation and placing a large asynchronous read\\nmux on the decode-to-execute path. Tang Nano LUTRAM can implement the data\\narray more compactly when the array itself is not reset.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The 32 by 32 register file currently resets every data bit, forcing a\\nresettable flip-flop implementation and placing a large asynchronous read\\nmux on the decode-to-execute path. Tang Nano LUTRAM can implement the data\\narray more compactly when the array itself is not reset.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep architectural reset semantics with a resettable 32-bit valid bitmap,\\nwhile leaving the 32-bit register-data array unreset and marking it for\\ndistributed RAM inference. Reads from invalid entries return zero, and a\\nsame-cycle write remains the highest-priority bypass, so x0, reset, and\\nwrite-first behavior remain identical. This removes reset fanout and should\\nmap the two asynchronous read ports to a shallower LUTRAM implementation,\\nimproving Fmax without adding pipeline latency or changing RVFI state.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/reg_file.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace resettable register-data flops with a distributed-RAM-friendly unreset array plus resettable valid bitmap, retaining x0 masking and write-first dual-read semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_reg_file.py\", \"description\": \"Add reset-after-write, invalid-entry zero-read, and same-cycle write-first tests to prove the valid-mask implementation preserves architectural register-file behavior.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/reg_file.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace resettable register-data flops with a distributed-RAM-friendly unreset array plus resettable valid bitmap, retaining x0 masking and write-first dual-read semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_reg_file.py\", \"description\": \"Add reset-after-write, invalid-entry zero-read, and same-cycle write-first tests to prove the valid-mask implementation preserves architectural register-file behavior.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 377.26, \"delta_pct\": -12.36, \"vs_baseline\": 33.39, \"fmax_mhz\": 169.46, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491817, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 3415, \"ff\": 1426, \"seeds\": [180.28, 157.06, 169.46], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T03:30:04.131083Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 12, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r12s0 (regression, -12.36%): Replacing resettable register-file data with a valid-masked LUTRAM array can hurt Fmax when the valid-bitmap lookup and invalid-to-zero mux are added to each asynchronous decode read port, outweighing any reset-fanout or storage-mapping savings.\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"jq -r 'select(.id == \\\"hyp-20260710-001-r9s0\\\" or .id == \\\"hyp-20260711-001-r11s0\\\" or .id == \\\"hyp-20260710-001-r4s0\\\") | \\\"ID: \\\\(.id)\\\\nTITLE: \\\\(.title)\\\\nHYP: \\\\(.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis)\\\\nNOTES: \\\\(.implementation_notes)\\\\nOUTCOME: \\\\(.outcome) \\\\(.fitness // \\\"n/a\\\")\\\\n\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl && find cores/bench/test -maxdepth 1 -type f -printf '%f\\\\n' | sort && git status --short\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"jq -r 'select(.id == \\\"hyp-20260710-001-r9s0\\\" or .id == \\\"hyp-20260711-001-r11s0\\\" or .id == \\\"hyp-20260710-001-r4s0\\\") | \\\"ID: \\\\(.id)\\\\nTITLE: \\\\(.title)\\\\nHYP: \\\\(.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis)\\\\nNOTES: \\\\(.implementation_notes)\\\\nOUTCOME: \\\\(.outcome) \\\\(.fitness // \\\"n/a\\\")\\\\n\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl && find cores/bench/test -maxdepth 1 -type f -printf '%f\\\\n' | sort && git status --short\"","aggregated_output":"ID: hyp-20260710-001-r4s0\nTITLE: Decode-aware load-use interlock\nHYP: Make the load-use hazard detector aware of whether the IF/ID opcode actually\nconsumes rs1 and/or rs2. Preserve the one-cycle interlock for real dependent\nconsumers, while suppressing it for operand-immediate ALU operations, loads,\nJAL, LUI, AUIPC, FENCE, EBREAK, and illegal encodings whose bit fields are\nnot architectural sources. This removes false bubbles without changing the\nPC, memory, RVFI, or arithmetic critical paths.\n\nNOTES: null\nOUTCOME: broken n/a\n\nID: hyp-20260710-001-r9s0\nTITLE: Precompute PC-relative control targets\nHYP: Form and register pc + immediate in ID together with the existing immediate\nand instruction payload, then use that registered value in EX for branch\ntargets and direct-JAL targets. Keep JALR's forwarded rs1 + immediate\ncalculation, branch predicate, redirect timing, misalignment detection, and\nRVFI pc_wdata semantics unchanged. This relocates a 32-bit adder from the\nbranch decision/redirect path into the earlier ID-to-ID/EX path, where it is\nindependent of forwarding and branch comparison, reducing the critical\ncontrol-flow cone while preserving the pipeline's current IPC.\n\nNOTES: \nOUTCOME: regression 419.9\n\nID: hyp-20260711-001-r11s0\nTITLE: Decoder-owned load-use source masks\nHYP: Make the validated decoder the single source of uses_rs1 and uses_rs2\nmetadata, then carry those bits with the IF/ID payload to gate the existing\nload-use comparisons. Classify OP, STORE, and BRANCH as two-source;\nOP-IMM, LOAD, and JALR as rs1-only; and JAL, LUI, AUIPC, FENCE, and EBREAK\nas source-free. Illegal or unrecognized encodings remain conservatively\ntwo-source so the interlock cannot weaken trap-path operand capture. This\nremoves only false bubbles while retaining the current pipeline, forwarding,\nmemory timing, RVFI payload, and all true load dependencies.\n\nNOTES: null\nOUTCOME: broken n/a\n\nfind: -printf: unknown primary or operator\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
