{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n -i 'dsp|MULT18|MULT9|\\\\\"'$mul|mul.*primitive|use_dsp'\"' . --glob '\"'!cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'\"' --glob '\"'!**/.git/**'\"' | head -n 160 && rg -n 'synth_gowin|synth.*gowin|gowin' fpga Makefile cores/bench -g '*.tcl' -g '*.mk' -g '*.sh'\"","aggregated_output":"./cores/neorv32/rtl/core/neorv32_prim.vhd:303:    if rising_edge(clk_i) then -- no reset to improve DSP mapping\n./cores/neorv32/rtl/core/neorv32_cpu.vhd:63:    CPU_FAST_MUL_EN     : boolean;                        -- use DSPs for M extension's multiplier\n./cores/neorv32/rtl/core/neorv32_cpu.vhd:423:    FAST_MUL_EN      => CPU_FAST_MUL_EN,  -- use DSPs for M extension's multiplier\n./cores/neorv32/rtl/core/neorv32_cpu_decompressor.vhd:336:          when others => -- \"001\"/\"101\": C.FLDSP / C.LQSP, C.FSDSP / C.SQSP -> illegal\n./cores/neorv32/rtl/core/neorv32_top.vhd:69:    CPU_FAST_MUL_EN     : boolean                        := false;         -- use DSPs for M extension's multiplier\n./cores/neorv32/rtl/core/neorv32_cpu_alu_muldiv.vhd:5:-- Multiplications can be mapped to DSP blocks (faster!) when FAST_MUL_EN = true.   --\n./cores/neorv32/rtl/core/neorv32_cpu_alu_muldiv.vhd:25:    FAST_MUL_EN : boolean; -- use DSPs for faster multiplication\n./cores/ibex/rtl/ibex_counter.sv:8:  // the output is hard-wired to 0. This is required to allow Xilinx DSP inference to work\n./cores/ibex/rtl/ibex_counter.sv:9:  // correctly. When `ProvideValUpd` is set no DSPs are inferred.\n./cores/ibex/rtl/ibex_counter.sv:54:  // On Xilinx FPGAs, 48-bit DSPs are available that can be used for the\n./cores/ibex/rtl/ibex_counter.sv:56:  // UseDsp is on purpose int as with string Xilinx throws an error for the\n./cores/ibex/rtl/ibex_counter.sv:57:  // use_dsp pragma.\n./cores/ibex/rtl/ibex_counter.sv:58:  localparam int UseDsp = CounterWidth < 49 ? \"yes\" : \"no\";\n./cores/ibex/rtl/ibex_counter.sv:59:  (* use_dsp = UseDsp *) logic [CounterWidth-1:0] counter_q;\n./cores/ibex/rtl/ibex_counter.sv:61:  localparam int UseDsp = \"no\";\n./cores/ibex/rtl/ibex_counter.sv:65:  if (UseDsp == \"yes\") begin : g_cnt_dsp\n./cores/ibex/rtl/ibex_counter.sv:66:    // Use sync. reset for DSP.\n./cores/ibex/rtl/ibex_counter.sv:74:  end else begin : g_cnt_no_dsp\n./cores/neorv32/rtl/core/neorv32_cpu_alu_fpu.vhd:12:-- * Multiplication (FMUL instruction) will infer DSP blocks (if available).        --\n./cores/neorv32/rtl/core/neorv32_cpu_alu.vhd:39:    FAST_MUL_EN      : boolean; -- use DSPs for M extension's multiplier\n./cores/neorv32/rtl/core/neorv32_cpu_alu.vhd:169:      FAST_MUL_EN => FAST_MUL_EN, -- use DSPs for faster multiplication\n./bench/programs/coremark/README.md:338:* BF533 with VDSP50 \n./bench/gpt-5_5_medium/rep2/agent.log:4:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778696146832,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1dd72e607ffeznJ60Rvr51ABAB\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"grep\",\"callID\":\"call_z4mACB06Eh1AYijmkU1lPEEL\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"pattern\":\"category: micro_opt|micro_opt|hypothesis|title|fitness|outcome\",\"path\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_5_medium-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\",\"include\":\"log.jsonl\"},\"output\":\"Found 28 matches\\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_5_medium-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:\\n  Line 1: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"baseline-bench-5327bba\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Baseline retest for cores/bench\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"improvement\\\", \\\"fitness\\\": 282.82, \\\"delta_pct\\\": 0.0, \\\"vs_baseline\\\": 0.0, \\\"fmax_mhz\\\": 127.03, \\\"ipc_coremark\\\": 2e-06, \\\"cycles\\\": 4491485, \\\"iterations\\\": 10, \\\"lut4\\\": 9563, \\\"ff\\\": 1866, \\\"seeds\\\": [128.72, 127.03, 123.02], \\\"formal_passed\\\": true, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": true, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"implementation_notes\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-05-13T13:08:01.185076Z\\\", \\\"round_id\\\": 0, \\\"slot\\\": 0}\\n\\n  Line 2: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r1s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Multicycle RV32M arithmetic unit\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current ALU computes every multiply and divide result combinationally in\\\\nthe EX stage, including signed and unsigned 64-bit products plus divider and\\\\nremainder operators. That wide arithmetic cone is a likely Fmax limiter on\\\\nTang Nano 20K, while CoreMark's hot path should not need single-cycle DIV/REM\\\\nthroughput. Trading rare M-extension latency for a shorter common EX critical\\\\npath should improve iterations/second through higher clock frequency.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current ALU computes every multiply and divide result combinationally in\\\\nthe EX stage, including signed and unsigned 64-bit products plus divider and\\\\nremainder operators. That wide arithmetic cone is a likely Fmax limiter on\\\\nTang Nano 20K, while CoreMark's hot path should not need single-cycle DIV/REM\\\\nthroughput. Trading rare M-extension latency for a shorter common EX critical\\\\npath should improve iterations/second through higher clock frequency.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Split RV32M operations out of the always-combinational ALU path behind a\\\\nsmall multicycle M-unit handshake in EX. Keep ADD/SUB/logic/branch/address\\\\noperations purely combinational, but when the decoded alu_op is MUL/DIV/REM,\\\\nhold ID/EX and EX/MEM until the iterative arithmetic result is ready, then\\\\nretire normally through the existing MEM/WB and RVFI path. This should remove\\\\ndivider and large multiplier logic from the ordinary EX-stage timing path;\\\\nthe added stalls only affect uncommon M instructions and should be outweighed\\\\nby improved Fmax on the FPGA fitness metric.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 6, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/alu.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Restrict the combinational ALU to fast RV32I operations and expose only lightweight M-op selection or helper logic needed by the new M-unit.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a multicycl...\\n  Line 3: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r1s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Resolve control flow in ID\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"CoreMark spends a significant fraction of its bracketed runtime in tight loops and conditional control flow. The current five-stage pipeline resolves branches and jumps in EX, then flushes both IF and ID, so every taken branch or jump pays roughly a two-cycle redirect penalty. Reducing that penalty improves iterations/cycle without increasing retirement width or touching the external NRET=1 contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"CoreMark spends a significant fraction of its bracketed runtime in tight loops and conditional control flow. The current five-stage pipeline resolves branches and jumps in EX, then flushes both IF and ID, so every taken branch or jump pays roughly a two-cycle redirect penalty. Reducing that penalty improves iterations/cycle without increasing retirement width or touching the external NRET=1 contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Move branch and JAL/JALR target/condition resolution from EX into the decode stage, with decode-stage operand forwarding from EX/MEM and MEM/WB for branch compares and JALR base operands. Keep the existing EX-stage trap/metadata path as the retirement source, but let ID drive redirect one cycle earlier when operands are available; stall only for unresolved load-to-branch/JALR dependencies. This should cut the common taken-control penalty from two bubbles to one while preserving RVFI order, trap reporting, and single-channel retirement.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 8, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Wire ID-stage early redirect signals into the IF and hazard path while preserving the existing NRET=1 RVFI port set.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add early branch/JAL/JALR resolution using decoded control, immediates, regfile operands, and forwarded compare/base operands.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/forward_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend forwarding ...\\n  Line 4: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r1s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Static BTFNT branch predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current five-stage core always fetches sequentially and resolves every branch or jump in EX, so each taken loop branch pays a two-instruction front-end flush. CoreMark has tight loops and many backward branches, making a small static predictor likely to improve iterations per cycle without adding much FPGA area or hurting the ALU critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current five-stage core always fetches sequentially and resolves every branch or jump in EX, so each taken loop branch pays a two-instruction front-end flush. CoreMark has tight loops and many backward branches, making a small static predictor likely to improve iterations per cycle without adding much FPGA area or hurting the ALU critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add an IF-stage static backward-taken/forward-not-taken predictor using the fetched instruction bits to compute B-type targets for negative branch immediates, and optionally early-predict JAL targets because they are unconditional PC-relative redirects. Carry the predicted next PC through IF/ID and ID/EX so EX redirects only when the resolved next PC differs from the prediction; mispredicted forward-taken branches, not-taken backward branches, and JALR still recover through the existing flush path. This should remove most loop-closing branch bubbles while preserving the single-retire RVFI contract because retirement remains unchanged at MEM/WB.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 8, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend IF/ID and ID/EX pipeline bundles with the predicted next PC needed for EX-stage mispredict detection.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Implement static BTFNT next-PC selection for backward conditional branches and early PC-relative JAL prediction from fetched instruction bits.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Lat...\\n  Line 5: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r2s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Simplify MEM load lane muxes\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"After the successful M-unit split, the remaining always-active timing paths are more likely to be ordinary RV32I datapath logic than rare multiply/divide arithmetic. The MEM stage currently uses variable shifts for load extraction and shifted masks for byte lanes, which can synthesize into wider mux/barrel-shifter logic on the FPGA load-to-WB path. CoreMark performs many byte, halfword, and word accesses, so a smaller MEM-stage cone should improve Fmax without changing CPI.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"After the successful M-unit split, the remaining always-active timing paths are more likely to be ordinary RV32I datapath logic than rare multiply/divide arithmetic. The MEM stage currently uses variable shifts for load extraction and shifted masks for byte lanes, which can synthesize into wider mux/barrel-shifter logic on the FPGA load-to-WB path. CoreMark performs many byte, halfword, and word accesses, so a smaller MEM-stage cone should improve Fmax without changing CPI.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Rewrite the MEM-stage byte-lane handling as explicit case muxes keyed by addr[1:0] instead of expressions like dmem_rdata >> (addr[1:0] * 8) and mask << addr[1:0]. Keep the same misalignment trap behavior, RVFI aligned mem_addr reporting, replicated store data convention, and MEM/WB retirement boundary. This should reduce LUT depth and routing on the common load-data path while preserving the existing five-stage control flow and single-retire NRET=1 contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace variable load-data shifts and shifted byte masks with explicit addr[1:0] case muxes for LB/LBU/LH/LHU/LW and store masks.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace variable load...\\n  Line 6: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r2s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Add fetch skid buffer\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current front end has no real IF/ID storage: the IF payload is combinational from the current PC and imem data, and an imem-not-ready cycle injects a bubble directly into decode. With the tournament cosim using roughly 22% random instruction stalls, CoreMark loses many cycles even when the back end is already stalled by load-use, dmem, or the multicycle M unit. A small fetch buffer is a structural way to recover iterations/cycle without revisiting the prior broken ID-stage branch-resolution path or adding fetch-path target adders that hurt Fmax.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current front end has no real IF/ID storage: the IF payload is combinational from the current PC and imem data, and an imem-not-ready cycle injects a bubble directly into decode. With the tournament cosim using roughly 22% random instruction stalls, CoreMark loses many cycles even when the back end is already stalled by load-use, dmem, or the multicycle M unit. A small fetch buffer is a structural way to recover iterations/cycle without revisiting the prior broken ID-stage branch-resolution path or adding fetch-path target adders that hurt Fmax.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Insert a one- or two-entry instruction skid buffer between IF and ID. The PC/imem side should continue fetching sequential instructions whenever the buffer has space, while the ID side consumes one buffered instruction only when ID/EX is ready. On redirect, flush the buffer and restart fetch at the EX-resolved target; on imem stalls, hold the outstanding fetch instead of emitting a synthetic NOP into decode. This should overlap random iStall cycles with existing back-end stalls and reduce front-end bubbles while preserving single-retire RVFI ordering and the existing EX-stage control-flow correctness.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 7, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"descr...\\n  Line 7: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r2s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Tiny return-address stack predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The prior static BTFNT predictor targeted loop branches but put branch immediate decode\\\\nand target adders directly on the IF PC-update path, regressing the CoreMark fitness\\\\ndespite saving some bubbles. CoreMark still has frequent function calls and returns,\\\\nand the current core pays the same EX-stage redirect penalty for every JALR return.\\\\nA tiny return predictor can attack those bubbles without adding branch-target arithmetic\\\\nto fetch.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The prior static BTFNT predictor targeted loop branches but put branch immediate decode\\\\nand target adders directly on the IF PC-update path, regressing the CoreMark fitness\\\\ndespite saving some bubbles. CoreMark still has frequent function calls and returns,\\\\nand the current core pays the same EX-stage redirect penalty for every JALR return.\\\\nA tiny return predictor can attack those bubbles without adding branch-target arithmetic\\\\nto fetch.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a small 4-entry return-address stack that predicts only canonical returns\\\\n(JALR x0, 0(x1/x5)) in IF. Push pc+4 for link-register calls when they resolve,\\\\npop/predict the stack top for returns, and carry the predicted next PC through the\\\\npipeline so EX redirects only on mismatch. This keeps ordinary branch fetch as pc+4,\\\\navoids B-type immediate decode and target adders in the IF critical path, and should\\\\nrecover part of the call/return control-flow penalty with modest LUT/FF cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add predicted-next-PC and return-predicted metadata to IF/ID, ID/EX, and EX/MEM bundles for mispredict detection and RAS bookkeeping.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Detect canonical return encodings in the fetched instruction and select the RAS top as the next PC only when the stack is nonempty....\\n  Line 8: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r3s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Source-aware load-use stalls\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current load-use hazard detector compares a load destination against the\\\\nraw rs1 and rs2 bitfields of the following instruction, regardless of whether\\\\nthat instruction actually consumes those operands. For I-type immediates,\\\\nloads, JALR, LUI, AUIPC, JAL, FENCE, and EBREAK, some of those fields are\\\\nimmediates or destination bits rather than true sources. Any accidental match\\\\nwith the preceding load rd inserts an unnecessary bubble, directly reducing\\\\nCoreMark iterations per cycle without improving correctness.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current load-use hazard detector compares a load destination against the\\\\nraw rs1 and rs2 bitfields of the following instruction, regardless of whether\\\\nthat instruction actually consumes those operands. For I-type immediates,\\\\nloads, JALR, LUI, AUIPC, JAL, FENCE, and EBREAK, some of those fields are\\\\nimmediates or destination bits rather than true sources. Any accidental match\\\\nwith the preceding load rd inserts an unnecessary bubble, directly reducing\\\\nCoreMark iterations per cycle without improving correctness.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Make the load-use interlock source-aware by qualifying the rd comparisons with\\\\nsimple decoded uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 signals for the instruction currently in\\\\nIF/ID. Keep the existing one-cycle stall for real load consumers, but avoid\\\\nstalling on unused source fields such as OP-IMM rs2 bits, U/J-type operand\\\\nfields, and SYSTEM/FENCE encodings. This should improve CPI under CoreMark's\\\\nload-heavy code while adding only small opcode decode logic off the main ALU\\\\nand memory datapaths, so Fmax should be largely preserved.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Derive or wire IF/ID source-use qualifiers into the hazard unit without changing the NRET=1 top-level RVFI interface.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl...\\n  Line 9: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r3s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Load-use MEM bypass\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The previous structural attempts spent LUTs and timing on front-end control-flow machinery, and the log shows those changes either broke correctness or regressed fitness. The current five-stage core still has the classic one-cycle load-use interlock even though the benchmark memory is zero-wait in FPGA and only randomly stalled in cosim. CoreMark has frequent dependent loads through pointer-heavy list, matrix, and state code, so removing this bubble can improve iterations per cycle without adding predictor target adders to the fetch critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The previous structural attempts spent LUTs and timing on front-end control-flow machinery, and the log shows those changes either broke correctness or regressed fitness. The current five-stage core still has the classic one-cycle load-use interlock even though the benchmark memory is zero-wait in FPGA and only randomly stalled in cosim. CoreMark has frequent dependent loads through pointer-heavy list, matrix, and state code, so removing this bubble can improve iterations per cycle without adding predictor target adders to the fetch critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a MEM-to-EX load-data bypass path that lets an instruction immediately behind a load proceed into EX when the load is in MEM and dmem is ready. Compute the existing load extraction result combinationally in MEM, expose it to the forwarding network, and add a forwarding select for EX operands that chooses the current MEM load result over the older EX/MEM ALU address. Keep the load-use stall only when the load will not have valid data that cycle, such as a dmem stall or a misaligned/trapping load. This structural bypass should remove many single-cycle bubbles while preserving the NRET=1 retirement boundary and RVFI order at MEM/WB.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 6, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\"...\\n  Line 10: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r3s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Decode-stage JAL predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The prior predictor attempts regressed because they placed branch target decode,\\\\ntarget adders, return detection, and prediction muxes directly on the IF PC path.\\\\nCoreMark still pays the existing EX redirect penalty for every unconditional JAL,\\\\nand JAL targets are PC-relative with no register dependency or branch condition.\\\\nPredicting only JAL in decode should recover one control-flow bubble for calls and\\\\nlong jumps while avoiding the risky fetch-path predictor logic that hurt Fmax.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The prior predictor attempts regressed because they placed branch target decode,\\\\ntarget adders, return detection, and prediction muxes directly on the IF PC path.\\\\nCoreMark still pays the existing EX redirect penalty for every unconditional JAL,\\\\nand JAL targets are PC-relative with no register dependency or branch condition.\\\\nPredicting only JAL in decode should recover one control-flow bubble for calls and\\\\nlong jumps while avoiding the risky fetch-path predictor logic that hurt Fmax.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a narrow decode-stage always-taken predictor for legal JAL instructions only.\\\\nID computes pc+imm for JAL, redirects fetch one cycle before EX would, and records\\\\nthe predicted next PC in the ID/EX bundle. EX then suppresses the normal JAL redirect\\\\nwhen the resolved jump target matches the predicted target, but retains the existing\\\\nEX recovery path for conditional branches, JALR, illegal instructions, and any target\\\\nmismatch. Because the predictor excludes branches and returns, it needs no B-type\\\\nimmediate decode in IF, no RAS state, and no operand forwarding, making it a smaller\\\\npredictor angle with a better chance of improving CoreMark iterations/cycle without\\\\nsacrificing the current high Fmax.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add p...\\n  Line 11: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r4s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Source-qualified load interlock\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current load-use detector compares the preceding load rd against both raw\\\\nsource bitfields of the following instruction, even when those bits are not\\\\narchitecturally consumed. CoreMark has many loads and immediate/control\\\\ninstructions, so accidental rd matches can inject bubbles that reduce\\\\niterations per cycle without protecting any real dependency.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current load-use detector compares the preceding load rd against both raw\\\\nsource bitfields of the following instruction, even when those bits are not\\\\narchitecturally consumed. CoreMark has many loads and immediate/control\\\\ninstructions, so accidental rd matches can inject bubbles that reduce\\\\niterations per cycle without protecting any real dependency.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a tiny source-use decode for the IF/ID instruction and qualify the existing\\\\nload-use interlock with uses_rs1 and uses_rs2. This is a narrower retry of the\\\\nearlier source-aware load-use idea: do not mask or reinterpret RVFI operand\\\\nreporting, and do not add MEM bypass selects or fetch prediction paths. The\\\\ncore should keep the one-cycle stall for true load consumers while avoiding\\\\nfalse stalls on OP-IMM rs2 fields, U/J-type immediates, FENCE, SYSTEM/EBREAK,\\\\nand other instructions that do not read both source registers.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Derive simple IF/ID uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 qualifiers from opcode/funct class and pass them only to the hazard unit.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Gate load-use rd comparisons with the source-use qualifiers while preserving existing dmem, imem, redirect, and M-unit stall priority.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Derive simple IF/ID use...\\n  Line 12: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r4s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Register IF/ID fetch boundary\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current front end feeds imem_data directly through decode, immediate\\\\ngeneration, regfile address decode, hazard checks, and into the ID/EX\\\\nregister in a single cycle. Prior structural attempts tried to hide stalls or\\\\nreduce bubbles with added prediction and bypass control, but those changes\\\\nregressed by lengthening already sensitive mux/control paths. A real IF/ID\\\\nregister is a simpler structural retiming that targets Fmax directly.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current front end feeds imem_data directly through decode, immediate\\\\ngeneration, regfile address decode, hazard checks, and into the ID/EX\\\\nregister in a single cycle. Prior structural attempts tried to hide stalls or\\\\nreduce bubbles with added prediction and bypass control, but those changes\\\\nregressed by lengthening already sensitive mux/control paths. A real IF/ID\\\\nregister is a simpler structural retiming that targets Fmax directly.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Insert a proper IF/ID pipeline register inside the fetch stage, so fetched\\\\ninstruction, PC, and valid are captured before decode sees them. The PC still\\\\nadvances only when the downstream ID stage can accept the entry, and redirect\\\\nflushes the registered IF/ID payload before wrong-path state can retire. This\\\\nadds one front-end cycle and may slightly increase taken-control penalty, but\\\\nit removes the imem-to-decoder combinational path and should improve FPGA\\\\nFmax enough to raise CoreMark iter/sec without changing the NRET=1 retirement\\\\ncontract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Keep the existing IF/ID bundle shape, but document and use it as a registered pipeline boundary rather than a combinational fetch payload.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add an IF/ID register for pc, instruction, and valid; hold it on deco...\\n  Line 13: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r4s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Tiny resolved-target BTB\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current core resolves all taken branches and jumps in EX, so tight CoreMark loops pay a two-bubble redirect penalty. Prior predictor attempts regressed because they decoded immediates, computed targets, or managed return-stack state on the front-end critical path. A small BTB trained from already-resolved EX redirects can target the same loop bubbles while avoiding fetch-stage target arithmetic.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current core resolves all taken branches and jumps in EX, so tight CoreMark loops pay a two-bubble redirect penalty. Prior predictor attempts regressed because they decoded immediates, computed targets, or managed return-stack state on the front-end critical path. A small BTB trained from already-resolved EX redirects can target the same loop bubbles while avoiding fetch-stage target arithmetic.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a tiny direct-mapped branch target buffer that stores resolved taken conditional-branch targets, preferably only backward loop branches, along with a compact PC tag and a 2-bit confidence counter. IF predicts to the stored target on a tag hit with a taken counter, otherwise it keeps the existing pc+4 path; EX updates the BTB after branch resolution and flushes on any predicted/resolved next-PC mismatch. Because the target is stored from the prior execution, the fetch path adds only a small tag compare and next-PC mux instead of B/J immediate decode and target adders, which should recover loop-closing branch bubbles with less Fmax damage than the previous predictor attempts.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Carry predicted next-PC metadata through IF/ID and ID/EX so EX can distinguish correct BTB predictions from mispredictions without changing the NRET=1 RVFI interface.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Imple...\\n  Line 14: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r5s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Drop regfile reset fanout\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current register file resets all 32 architectural registers with a for-loop,\\\\nwhich forces reset muxing or reset routing onto the whole small RF array. After\\\\nthe M-unit split, remaining FPGA Fmax is likely limited by ordinary datapath\\\\nand control fanout rather than rare arithmetic. CoreMark initializes the state\\\\nit uses, x0 is already hardwired on reads, and RVFI correctness depends on\\\\nretired architectural behavior rather than power-on values of unused registers.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current register file resets all 32 architectural registers with a for-loop,\\\\nwhich forces reset muxing or reset routing onto the whole small RF array. After\\\\nthe M-unit split, remaining FPGA Fmax is likely limited by ordinary datapath\\\\nand control fanout rather than rare arithmetic. CoreMark initializes the state\\\\nit uses, x0 is already hardwired on reads, and RVFI correctness depends on\\\\nretired architectural behavior rather than power-on values of unused registers.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Remove the explicit reset clear from the integer register file and keep only\\\\nthe synchronous write path for nonzero destination registers. Reads of x0 stay\\\\nforced to zero, same-cycle write-first bypass stays unchanged, and reset of the\\\\npipeline control/valid bits still prevents spurious retirement after reset.\\\\nThis should reduce reset fanout and per-register muxing in a timing-sensitive,\\\\nalways-active block without changing CPI, branch behavior, memory behavior, or\\\\nthe NRET=1 RVFI interface. The change is deliberately narrower than prior\\\\nfailed micro-opts: it does not touch hazard detection, RVFI operand reporting,\\\\nMEM load extraction, or any front-end prediction path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/reg_file.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove the reset-time loop that clears all 32 registers while preser...\\n  Line 15: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r5s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Ordered one-entry store buffer\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent structural attempts spent timing and LUTs on the front end or on load\\\\nbypass paths, and those changes either regressed fitness or broke stall\\\\nhandling. The current core still freezes the entire pipeline whenever a store\\\\nreaches MEM and dmem_ready is low, even though independent ALU, branch, and\\\\nregister-only instructions behind that store could execute while the store is\\\\nwaiting. CoreMark has frequent data stores and the apples-to-apples cosim\\\\ninjects random dmem stalls, so store backpressure is a promising structural\\\\ntarget that avoids fetch-path target adders and load-result critical muxes.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent structural attempts spent timing and LUTs on the front end or on load\\\\nbypass paths, and those changes either regressed fitness or broke stall\\\\nhandling. The current core still freezes the entire pipeline whenever a store\\\\nreaches MEM and dmem_ready is low, even though independent ALU, branch, and\\\\nregister-only instructions behind that store could execute while the store is\\\\nwaiting. CoreMark has frequent data stores and the apples-to-apples cosim\\\\ninjects random dmem stalls, so store backpressure is a promising structural\\\\ntarget that avoids fetch-path target adders and load-result critical muxes.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a single-entry ordered store buffer at the MEM boundary. A regular data\\\\nstore can retire into the buffer with its address, byte mask, write data, and\\\\nRVFI metadata captured, allowing the front of the pipeline to keep executing\\\\nnon-memory instructions while the buffer drives dmem until dmem_ready accepts\\\\nthe write. Preserve precise in-order behavior by stalling any younger load,\\\\nstore, MMIO access, FENCE, EBREAK, or illegal/trapping instruction until the\\\\nbuffer drains; MMIO marker/UART stores should bypass the buffer and keep the\\\\nexisting blocking behavior so CoreMark timing brackets re...\\n  Line 16: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r5s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Single-entry loop target predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current core fetches sequentially and pays the existing EX-stage redirect\\\\npenalty on every taken loop-closing branch. Earlier predictor attempts\\\\nregressed because they placed immediate decode, target adders, BTB arrays,\\\\ncounters, or RAS control on the fetch path. A predictor for this slot should\\\\ntherefore be much narrower: reuse targets already resolved in EX and add the\\\\nsmallest possible fetch-side structure.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current core fetches sequentially and pays the existing EX-stage redirect\\\\npenalty on every taken loop-closing branch. Earlier predictor attempts\\\\nregressed because they placed immediate decode, target adders, BTB arrays,\\\\ncounters, or RAS control on the fetch path. A predictor for this slot should\\\\ntherefore be much narrower: reuse targets already resolved in EX and add the\\\\nsmallest possible fetch-side structure.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a single-entry resolved-loop target predictor trained only by taken\\\\nbackward conditional branches. The entry stores one branch PC and its resolved\\\\ntarget; IF predicts taken only when the current PC equals that stored PC,\\\\notherwise it keeps the existing pc+4 behavior. Carry predicted-taken and\\\\npredicted-next-PC metadata to EX so the normal redirect path fires only on a\\\\nmismatch, and invalidate the entry when that same branch resolves not-taken\\\\nat loop exit. This should recover repeated inner-loop branch bubbles in\\\\nCoreMark while avoiding the fetch target adders, multi-entry tag arrays,\\\\nconfidence counters, and return-stack machinery that hurt prior predictors.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add minimal predicted-taken and predicted-next-PC metadata to IF/ID and ID/EX bundles for EX-stage mismatch detection.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Implement ...\\n  Line 17: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r6s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Prune dead EX/MEM branch metadata\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core has already won most of its fitness from removing broad\\\\ntiming/fanout costs, while recent attempts that add prediction, buffering,\\\\nbypassing, or memory-ordering control have regressed. The EX/MEM bundle still\\\\ncarries branch_taken and branch_target fields that are assigned every cycle\\\\nbut are not consumed by MEM, WB, RVFI, hazard, or forwarding logic. Those 33\\\\ndead flops and their input adders/mux routes sit in an always-active pipeline\\\\nboundary, so pruning them is a low-risk micro-optimization for LUT/FF count\\\\nand routing delay without changing CPI.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core has already won most of its fitness from removing broad\\\\ntiming/fanout costs, while recent attempts that add prediction, buffering,\\\\nbypassing, or memory-ordering control have regressed. The EX/MEM bundle still\\\\ncarries branch_taken and branch_target fields that are assigned every cycle\\\\nbut are not consumed by MEM, WB, RVFI, hazard, or forwarding logic. Those 33\\\\ndead flops and their input adders/mux routes sit in an always-active pipeline\\\\nboundary, so pruning them is a low-risk micro-optimization for LUT/FF count\\\\nand routing delay without changing CPI.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Remove the unused branch_taken and branch_target fields from ex_mem_t and stop\\\\nregistering them in ex_stage. Keep redirect and pc_next computation exactly as\\\\nthey are today, because pc_next is the RVFI-visible resolved next PC and the\\\\nredirect output is still the only live control-flow signal. This should trim\\\\ndead sequential state and reduce EX-stage fanout/routing pressure while\\\\npreserving the NRET=1 RVFI contract, trap behavior, memory behavior, and all\\\\nexisting stall/flush priorities.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 1, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove unused branch_taken and...\\n  Line 18: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r6s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Pipeline EX operand retiming\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent structural attempts that added fetch prediction, skid buffering, load bypassing,\\\\nor store buffering all paid too much control-path cost. The current core's remaining\\\\nhigh-fanout structural path is more basic: ID/EX values pass through forwarding muxes,\\\\nALU operand selection, ALU or branch compare/target logic, and into EX/MEM plus the\\\\nredirect path in one cycle. Retiming that boundary can target Fmax without adding a\\\\npredictor, memory ordering structure, or RVFI contract change.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent structural attempts that added fetch prediction, skid buffering, load bypassing,\\\\nor store buffering all paid too much control-path cost. The current core's remaining\\\\nhigh-fanout structural path is more basic: ID/EX values pass through forwarding muxes,\\\\nALU operand selection, ALU or branch compare/target logic, and into EX/MEM plus the\\\\nredirect path in one cycle. Retiming that boundary can target Fmax without adding a\\\\npredictor, memory ordering structure, or RVFI contract change.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Split the existing EX stage into a lightweight EX1 operand/metadata register and an EX2\\\\nexecute/redirect stage. EX1 captures the forwarded rs1/rs2 values, selected ALU operands,\\\\ndecoded control, immediate, PC, and RVFI source data; EX2 performs the ALU operation,\\\\nbranch/jump resolution, M-unit handshake, and drives the existing EX/MEM payload. Add the\\\\nminimal hazard/forwarding adjustments needed for the extra execute stage, preferring a\\\\none-cycle interlock on immediately dependent ALU consumers over wide new bypass muxes if\\\\nthat keeps timing short. This structural retiming should shorten the common forwarding-to-\\\\nALU-to-EX/MEM cone and may raise Tang Nano 20K Fmax enough to offset a small CPI cost,\\\\nwhile preserving NRET=1 retirement at MEM/WB and all RVFI ordering/trap behavior.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\"...\\n  Line 19: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r6s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Single-entry JAL target predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts regressed because they put immediate decode, target\\\\nadders, multi-entry tags, counters, or return-stack control on the fetch path.\\\\nCoreMark still pays the EX redirect penalty for repeated unconditional calls\\\\nand jumps, but JAL has no direction ambiguity once a target is known. A very\\\\nsmall learned JAL target entry can attack only that control-flow cost while\\\\navoiding conditional branch and return prediction complexity.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts regressed because they put immediate decode, target\\\\nadders, multi-entry tags, counters, or return-stack control on the fetch path.\\\\nCoreMark still pays the EX redirect penalty for repeated unconditional calls\\\\nand jumps, but JAL has no direction ambiguity once a target is known. A very\\\\nsmall learned JAL target entry can attack only that control-flow cost while\\\\navoiding conditional branch and return prediction complexity.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a single-entry predictor trained only by legal resolved JAL instructions\\\\nin EX. The entry stores the exact JAL PC and resolved target; IF predicts to\\\\nthe stored target only when the current PC equals that entry, otherwise it\\\\nkeeps the existing pc+4 path. Carry only predicted-valid and predicted-next-PC\\\\nmetadata to EX so the normal redirect path is suppressed on an exact correct\\\\nprediction and used for all misses or mismatches. This should recover repeated\\\\ncall/jump bubbles in CoreMark without fetch-side immediate decode, B-type\\\\nbranch prediction, return-stack state, confidence counters, or multi-entry BTB\\\\nrouting that hurt previous predictor hypotheses.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add minimal predicted-valid and predicted-next-PC fields to IF/ID and ID/EX bundles for EX-stage JAL prediction validation....\\n  Line 20: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r7s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Iterative M divider\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The largest proven fitness gain for this core came from moving RV32M arithmetic\\\\nout of the ordinary EX ALU path, but the current m_unit still contains wide\\\\ncombinational DIV/REM operators behind a one-cycle result register. Even if\\\\nthose operations are rare in CoreMark, the FPGA still has to place and time\\\\nthe divider/remainder cone, so it can cap Fmax or perturb routing for the hot\\\\nRV32I pipeline.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The largest proven fitness gain for this core came from moving RV32M arithmetic\\\\nout of the ordinary EX ALU path, but the current m_unit still contains wide\\\\ncombinational DIV/REM operators behind a one-cycle result register. Even if\\\\nthose operations are rare in CoreMark, the FPGA still has to place and time\\\\nthe divider/remainder cone, so it can cap Fmax or perturb routing for the hot\\\\nRV32I pipeline.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the one-cycle DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU implementation in m_unit with a small\\\\niterative restoring divider that runs only after a divide or remainder starts.\\\\nKeep MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU on the existing short registered path so any\\\\nmultiply use does not pay a 32-cycle penalty, and keep the existing EX-stage\\\\nhold/valid handshake as the only pipeline interface. This should remove the\\\\nexpensive combinational divider/remainder logic from the FPGA timing closure\\\\nproblem while adding CPI only for uncommon divide/remainder instructions.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/m_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Implement DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU with an iterative signed/unsigned divider state machine while preserving ALTOPS behavior under RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Adjust the existing M-unit busy/valid handshake only as needed so EX holds until multi-cycle DIV/REM completion and still clears completed results exactly once.\\\"}]}, \\\"expec...\\n  Line 21: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r7s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Register dmem stall boundary\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent structural attempts added front-end prediction, fetch buffering, load\\\\nbypassing, store buffering, or a deeper EX pipe, and each paid too much in\\\\nmux/control cost or correctness complexity. A smaller structural target is\\\\nthe current dmem stall path: io_dmemReady fans combinationally through the\\\\nhazard unit into PC hold, ID hold, EX/MEM hold, and MEM/WB valid clearing in\\\\nthe same cycle. CoreMark's apples-to-apples mode injects random dmem stalls,\\\\nso this control path is active during evaluation, and reducing its fanout may\\\\nimprove Fmax without changing the visible memory ordering contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent structural attempts added front-end prediction, fetch buffering, load\\\\nbypassing, store buffering, or a deeper EX pipe, and each paid too much in\\\\nmux/control cost or correctness complexity. A smaller structural target is\\\\nthe current dmem stall path: io_dmemReady fans combinationally through the\\\\nhazard unit into PC hold, ID hold, EX/MEM hold, and MEM/WB valid clearing in\\\\nthe same cycle. CoreMark's apples-to-apples mode injects random dmem stalls,\\\\nso this control path is active during evaluation, and reducing its fanout may\\\\nimprove Fmax without changing the visible memory ordering contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a one-cycle registered MEM busy/accepted boundary for data-memory\\\\noperations. When a load or store enters MEM, capture whether it is waiting for\\\\ndmem_ready in a small state bit local to the MEM/hazard interface; while that\\\\nbit is set, hold EX/MEM and clear MEM/WB valid exactly as today, but drive the\\\\nglobal pipeline stalls from the registered busy state rather than directly\\\\nfrom io_dmemReady. Keep dmem_addr, dmem_wdata, dmem_wen, dmem_ren, RVFI memory\\\\nmasks/data, misalignment traps, and all in-order retirement behavior\\\\nunchanged. This deliberately accepts at most one extra cycle on the first\\\\nstal...\\n  Line 22: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r7s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Decode-only JAL redirect predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts for this core regressed because they added target\\\\nadders, tag compares, return-stack state, or predicted-PC muxing directly to\\\\nthe IF next-PC path. The current core still pays the EX redirect penalty for\\\\nevery unconditional JAL, and JAL needs no operand forwarding or direction\\\\nprediction once its instruction reaches decode.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts for this core regressed because they added target\\\\nadders, tag compares, return-stack state, or predicted-PC muxing directly to\\\\nthe IF next-PC path. The current core still pays the EX redirect penalty for\\\\nevery unconditional JAL, and JAL needs no operand forwarding or direction\\\\nprediction once its instruction reaches decode.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a decode-stage predictor for legal JAL instructions only. ID already has\\\\nthe instruction, PC, and immediate generator output, so it can compute the JAL\\\\ntarget and redirect fetch one cycle earlier than EX without adding any logic\\\\nto the IF PC increment path. Carry a small predicted-next-PC flag/value into\\\\nID/EX so EX suppresses its normal JAL redirect when the ID prediction matches,\\\\nwhile all conditional branches, JALR, illegal instructions, and mismatches use\\\\nthe existing EX recovery path. This avoids the failed fetch-side predictor\\\\nstructures while recovering one bubble on CoreMark calls and PC-relative jumps.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add minimal predicted-valid and predicted-next-PC metadata to the IF/ID and ID/EX bundles for decode-stage JAL prediction validation.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Detect legal JAL in decode, compute pc plus J-immediate, emit an early redirect, and latch the prediction metadata into ID/EX.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Wire the ID...\\n  Line 23: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r8s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Share M-unit multiplier hardware\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core already removed RV32M arithmetic from the ordinary EX\\\\nALU path, but m_unit still builds three separate 64-bit multiplier products\\\\nin parallel for MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU signedness variants. CoreMark uses\\\\nM operations rarely enough that throughput beyond the existing one-cycle\\\\nregistered M-unit is not the main limiter, while the extra DSP/LUT/routing\\\\npressure can still depress FPGA Fmax or perturb placement of the hot RV32I\\\\npipeline.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core already removed RV32M arithmetic from the ordinary EX\\\\nALU path, but m_unit still builds three separate 64-bit multiplier products\\\\nin parallel for MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU signedness variants. CoreMark uses\\\\nM operations rarely enough that throughput beyond the existing one-cycle\\\\nregistered M-unit is not the main limiter, while the extra DSP/LUT/routing\\\\npressure can still depress FPGA Fmax or perturb placement of the hot RV32I\\\\npipeline.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the parallel signed-signed, unsigned-unsigned, and signed-unsigned\\\\nproduct generation in m_unit with one shared signed multiplier fed by\\\\nsign/zero-extended operands selected from the M opcode. MUL can take the low\\\\nhalf of the same product because low 32 bits are independent of operand\\\\nsignedness; MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU select the high half with the appropriate\\\\noperand extension. Keep the existing start/valid/clear handshake, one-cycle\\\\nM latency, DIV/REM behavior, and RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions unchanged.\\\\nThis should trim multiplier area and routing without adding CPI, front-end\\\\nprediction logic, memory ordering state, or any RVFI-visible behavior change.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/m_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Use a single sign-controlled widened multiplier for MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU while pres...\\n  Line 24: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r8s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Register WB result at MEM boundary\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The recent structural attempts mostly lost fitness by adding control-flow\\\\nprediction, buffering, memory overlap, or deeper execute-stage interlocks.\\\\nA safer structural target is the existing MEM/WB-to-WB combinational path:\\\\nthe selected writeback value is still muxed after the MEM/WB register, then\\\\nfans out to the regfile write port, same-cycle regfile bypass, EX forwarding,\\\\nand RVFI rd_wdata. Registering that selected value at the retirement boundary\\\\nshould shorten a hot fanout path without changing CPI, branch timing, memory\\\\nordering, or the NRET=1 external contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The recent structural attempts mostly lost fitness by adding control-flow\\\\nprediction, buffering, memory overlap, or deeper execute-stage interlocks.\\\\nA safer structural target is the existing MEM/WB-to-WB combinational path:\\\\nthe selected writeback value is still muxed after the MEM/WB register, then\\\\nfans out to the regfile write port, same-cycle regfile bypass, EX forwarding,\\\\nand RVFI rd_wdata. Registering that selected value at the retirement boundary\\\\nshould shorten a hot fanout path without changing CPI, branch timing, memory\\\\nordering, or the NRET=1 external contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a preselected rd_wdata/writeback-result field to the MEM/WB pipeline\\\\nbundle and compute it inside mem_stage before the MEM/WB register captures.\\\\nFor loads this field captures the sign/zero-extended load result; for ALU,\\\\nJAL, JALR, AUIPC, LUI, and M-extension operations it captures the existing\\\\nEX/MEM alu_result. Then simplify wb_stage into a pass-through of the\\\\nregistered writeback value instead of a post-register mem_to_reg mux. This\\\\nstructural retiming preserves the same retirement boundary and RVFI data, but\\\\nremoves the load-vs-ALU mux from the next cycle's forwarding/regfile/RVFI\\\\nfanout cone, which may improve Tang Nano 20K Fmax with ess...\\n  Line 25: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r8s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Decode-stage backward branch predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts lost fitness when they added BTB, RAS, tag compare,\\\\ncounter, or target-adder logic to the IF next-PC path. The current core still\\\\npays a two-bubble EX redirect penalty on taken loop-closing branches, which\\\\nare the predictor opportunity most likely to matter for CoreMark. A predictor\\\\nthat waits until decode can use the already-fetched instruction and decoded\\\\nbranch control while keeping fetch as the current simple pc+4 path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts lost fitness when they added BTB, RAS, tag compare,\\\\ncounter, or target-adder logic to the IF next-PC path. The current core still\\\\npays a two-bubble EX redirect penalty on taken loop-closing branches, which\\\\nare the predictor opportunity most likely to matter for CoreMark. A predictor\\\\nthat waits until decode can use the already-fetched instruction and decoded\\\\nbranch control while keeping fetch as the current simple pc+4 path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a static decode-stage predictor for legal backward conditional branches\\\\nonly. ID uses the existing immediate generator and decoded is_branch signal to\\\\nrecognize negative B-type offsets, computes pc+imm, and redirects fetch one\\\\ncycle earlier than EX would. Carry predicted-valid and predicted-next-PC into\\\\nID/EX so EX suppresses the normal redirect when the branch is actually taken\\\\nto the predicted target, or redirects to pc+4 on the loop-exit not-taken\\\\nmisprediction. Forward branches, JAL, JALR, illegal encodings, and all memory\\\\nor RVFI behavior stay on the existing path. This targets loop bubbles without\\\\nreintroducing the failed IF-side predictor structures.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add minimal predicted-valid and predicted-next-PC fields to the ID/EX bundle for EX-stage branch predict...\\n  Line 26: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r9s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Narrow MEM/WB control state\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent micro-optimization wins came from removing always-present FPGA timing\\\\nand routing pressure, while attempts that added prediction, bypassing, or\\\\nstall-hiding control usually regressed. The current MEM/WB register still\\\\ncarries the full decode control bundle even though the writeback and RVFI\\\\nretirement logic only consume a few bits after the MEM stage. Pruning that\\\\ncold control state should reduce flop count, muxing, and routing without\\\\nchanging CoreMark CPI or touching the external NRET=1 contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent micro-optimization wins came from removing always-present FPGA timing\\\\nand routing pressure, while attempts that added prediction, bypassing, or\\\\nstall-hiding control usually regressed. The current MEM/WB register still\\\\ncarries the full decode control bundle even though the writeback and RVFI\\\\nretirement logic only consume a few bits after the MEM stage. Pruning that\\\\ncold control state should reduce flop count, muxing, and routing without\\\\nchanging CoreMark CPI or touching the external NRET=1 contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the full ctrl_t field in mem_wb_t with a narrow retirement/writeback\\\\ncontrol subset containing only reg_write, mem_to_reg, and is_illegal. Keep\\\\nthe existing full ctrl_t through ID/EX and EX/MEM, because the execute and\\\\nmemory stages still need branch, memory width, sign-extension, and M-op\\\\ncontrols. In mem_stage, collapse ctrl_with_trap into the narrow MEM/WB\\\\nfields after misalignment handling, and update wb_stage and core RVFI trap\\\\nreferences to use the narrow fields. This should trim unused MEM/WB control\\\\nflops and fanout while preserving all memory masks/data, load results,\\\\nwriteback behavior, RVFI order, and trap reporting.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Introduce a narrow M...\\n  Line 27: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r9s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Split RVFI shadow metadata from datapath\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The best recent gains came from removing broad arithmetic and register fanout from hot paths, while most structural attempts regressed by adding prediction, buffering, memory overlap, or extra execute-stage interlocks. The current pipeline still carries a wide all-purpose struct through EX/MEM and MEM/WB, mixing hot execution data with RVFI-only metadata such as original source values, source register numbers, instruction bits, memory masks, and retired PC fields. Even when those fields are not part of the architectural datapath, they share the same pipeline registers, reset/hold enables, and module boundaries as timing-critical forwarding, memory, and writeback signals.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The best recent gains came from removing broad arithmetic and register fanout from hot paths, while most structural attempts regressed by adding prediction, buffering, memory overlap, or extra execute-stage interlocks. The current pipeline still carries a wide all-purpose struct through EX/MEM and MEM/WB, mixing hot execution data with RVFI-only metadata such as original source values, source register numbers, instruction bits, memory masks, and retired PC fields. Even when those fields are not part of the architectural datapath, they share the same pipeline registers, reset/hold enables, and module boundaries as timing-critical forwarding, memory, and writeback signals.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Split the pipeline state into a narrow execution payload and a parallel RVFI shadow payload. Keep the hot EX/MEM and MEM/WB datapath bundles limited to control bits and values needed for forwarding, dmem, writeback, and stalls, while carrying RVFI-only fields in a separate shadow register chain that uses the same valid, flush, hold, trap, and redirect decisions. This should reduce register width, enable fanout, and routing pressure on the timing-critical da...\\n  Line 28: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r9s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Decode return target predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts for this core mostly regressed because they added\\\\ntarget adders, tag compares, counters, return-stack state, or predicted-PC\\\\nmuxes directly to the IF next-PC path. The current core still pays the EX\\\\nredirect penalty on every canonical function return, and CoreMark contains\\\\nenough call/return traffic that recovering one redirect cycle there may help\\\\niterations per cycle. A return predictor that operates only after decode can\\\\nuse the already-fetched instruction, regfile read data, and existing immediate\\\\npath while keeping fetch's common pc+4 path untouched.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts for this core mostly regressed because they added\\\\ntarget adders, tag compares, counters, return-stack state, or predicted-PC\\\\nmuxes directly to the IF next-PC path. The current core still pays the EX\\\\nredirect penalty on every canonical function return, and CoreMark contains\\\\nenough call/return traffic that recovering one redirect cycle there may help\\\\niterations per cycle. A return predictor that operates only after decode can\\\\nuse the already-fetched instruction, regfile read data, and existing immediate\\\\npath while keeping fetch's common pc+4 path untouched.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a decode-stage predictor for canonical returns only: legal JALR with\\\\nrd=x0, funct3=0, imm=0, and rs1=x1 or x5. ID computes the return target from\\\\nthe already-read link register value and redirects fetch one cycle before EX\\\\nwould, but only when the base register is not the destination of an unresolved\\\\nolder instruction in ID/EX or EX/MEM. Carry a minimal predicted-valid and\\\\npredicted-next-PC field into ID/EX so EX suppresses the normal JALR redirect\\\\non an exact match and uses the existing recovery redirect on mismatches. This\\\\navoids the failed IF-side RAS/BTB/BTFNT machinery while targeting a narrower\\\\ncontrol-flow pattern wit...\",\"metadata\":{\"matches\":28,\"truncated\":false},\"title\":\"category: micro_opt|micro_opt|hypothesis|title|fitness|outcome\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778696146821,\"end\":1778696146830}},\"metadata\":{\"openai\":{\"itemId\":\"fc_072aa58eff965527016a04bfd2b4848199a0e529e0cbea7483\"}},\"id\":\"prt_e228d4f830015XNsNjbOOZJdO6\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1dd72e607ffeznJ60Rvr51ABAB\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e228d1a37001szW1rugQOJEAxG\"}}\n./bench/gpt-5_5_medium/rep2/agent.log:5:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778696146835,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1dd72e607ffeznJ60Rvr51ABAB\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"read\",\"callID\":\"call_BdtMAXWVaYv7txPJv1JKn2xF\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"filePath\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_5_medium-rep2/cores/bench/LESSONS.md\",\"offset\":1,\"limit\":200},\"output\":\"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_5_medium-rep2/cores/bench/LESSONS.md</path>\\n<type>file</type>\\n<content>\\n1: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r1s0 (improvement, +32.85%): Moving RV32M multiply/divide out of the combinational EX ALU behind a multicycle handshake can substantially improve FPGA fitness because CoreMark rarely needs M-op throughput while the removed arithmetic cone dominates Fmax.\\n2: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r1s1 (broken, n/a): Moving branch/JALR resolution into ID needs exhaustive operand-valid stalling and forwarding for decode-stage compares/base operands; stale early redirects can let wrong-path state reach writeback, which riscv-formal's reg check catches.\\n3: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r1s2 (regression, +21.76%): IF-stage BTFNT/JAL prediction can regress CoreMark when instruction-bit immediate decode and target adders sit directly on the fetch PC path, so front-end bubble savings must be weighed against Fmax-critical next-PC logic.\\n4: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r2s0 (regression, -1.92%): Replacing MEM-stage variable shifts with explicit byte/halfword lane case muxes did not help FPGA fitness, suggesting the synthesizer already maps these narrow shifts well and the added mux/control structure can slightly worsen routing or LUT packing.\\n5: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r2s1 (regression, -7.79%): A two-entry IF skid buffer did not pay for CoreMark's random imem stalls; the added fetch-valid/control and redirect-flush machinery can cost more FPGA fitness than the front-end bubbles it hides.\\n6: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r2s2 (regression, -44.37%): A tiny IF return-address stack can still badly hurt FPGA fitness when canonical-return detection, RAS top muxing, and predicted-PC metadata outweigh the CoreMark return bubbles it removes.\\n7: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r3s0 (broken, n/a): Keep source-aware load-use interlocks separate from RVFI operand reporting changes; masking unused rs fields can break cosim's retire trace even when the intended stall reduction logic is local to hazard_unit.\\n8: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r3s1 (regression, -14.30%): MEM-to-EX load bypass can lose FPGA fitness when dmem-ready gating, load extraction, and an extra EX operand forwarding select lengthen the critical path more than CoreMark benefits from removing one-cycle load-use bubbles.\\n9: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r3s2 (regression, -20.54%): Even decode-stage JAL prediction can regress when the early_redirect priority path, target adder, and predicted-PC metadata still add enough IF/ID/EX control cost to outweigh saving CoreMark's unconditional-jump bubble.\\n10: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r4s1 (broken, n/a): A registered IF/ID boundary must pair imem backpressure with an ID consume/valid handshake; holding a valid IF/ID entry while ID keeps advancing can duplicate instructions during CoreMark stalls.\\n11: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r4s2 (regression, -9.29%): Even a resolved-target BTB can lose FPGA fitness when IF-side tag/valid/counter arrays, target muxing, and predicted-PC metadata lengthen the next-PC path more than CoreMark benefits from avoiding loop redirect bubbles.\\n12: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r5s0 (improvement, +1.93%): Dropping reset clears from the integer register file can improve FPGA fitness because pipeline-valid reset and x0 read forcing preserve architectural behavior while avoiding reset fanout and per-register muxing in an always-active datapath block.\\n13: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r5s1 (regression, -6.89%): A one-entry ordered store buffer can lose CoreMark fitness when store-order interlocks, MMIO/trap classification, and dmem muxing add more MEM/control-path cost than random store-stall overlap recovers.\\n14: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r5s2 (regression, -61.05%): Even a single-entry loop predictor can badly regress when predicted-PC metadata, IF PC/tag compare muxing, and EX mismatch redirect logic lengthen critical control paths more than repeated CoreMark loop-branch bubble removal helps.\\n15: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r6s0 (regression, -2.40%): Pruning unused EX/MEM branch_taken/branch_target metadata did not improve FPGA fitness, suggesting these dead-looking pipeline fields were not on the binding path or were already optimized well enough that layout perturbation outweighed the small flop reduction.\\n16: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r6s1 (regression, -88.09%): Splitting EX with an EX1 operand register can destroy CoreMark fitness when it delays branch/JALR redirects and forces broad dependent-ALU interlocks, so operand-cone retiming must preserve bypass/redirect latency to pay off.\\n17: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r6s2 (regression, -48.05%): A JAL-only single-entry target predictor still regressed without fetch-side immediate decode because the IF PC compare/target mux, predicted-PC pipeline fields, and EX mismatch redirect logic outweighed CoreMark's repeated unconditional-jump bubble savings.\\n18: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r7s0 (regression, -53.28%): Replacing cold RV32M DIV/REM combinational operators with an iterative divider can severely regress CoreMark when the busy/start handshake, divider state, and 32-cycle latency perturb the hot pipeline more than removing the rarely used divide cone helps Fmax.\\n19: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r7s1 (broken, n/a): Registering dmem backpressure needs a real ready/valid or skid boundary at MEM; a lone pending/wait bit can hide the first stalled cycle from upstream control and let younger CoreMark memory-state updates be dropped or reordered.\\n20: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r8s0 (improvement, +2.79%): Sharing one sign/zero-extended M-unit multiplier across MULH signedness variants can improve FPGA fitness because CoreMark rarely needs M throughput and parallel 64-bit products add DSP/LUT routing pressure.\\n21: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r8s1 (regression, -0.33%): Preselecting MEM/WB writeback data inside mem_stage did not improve fitness, suggesting the load-vs-ALU writeback mux is not a binding FPGA path and adding another pipeline field can slightly worsen packing/routing.\\n22: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r8s2 (regression, +1.88%): Moving backward-branch prediction from IF to ID still regressed because the ID target adder, redirect/stall arbitration, predicted-PC metadata, and EX mispredict compare/control outweighed CoreMark loop-bubble savings.\\n23: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r9s0 (regression, -14.30%): Narrowing MEM/WB from full ctrl_t to a tiny packed mem_wb_ctrl_t can hurt FPGA fitness despite fewer flops, suggesting late-stage control fields were already optimized away or well-packed and the new struct/mapping perturbed routing more than it saved.\\n24: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r9s1 (improvement, +3.53%): Splitting RVFI-only metadata into parallel shadow payloads can improve FPGA fitness because it removes wide instruction/source/PC/memory fields from hot EX/MEM and MEM/WB forwarding, dmem, and writeback bundles without changing pipeline timing.\\n25: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r9s2 (regression, -31.51%): Decode-stage return prediction still regressed because link-register hazard compares, early redirect arbitration, and predicted-PC fields added more hot control/datapath cost than CoreMark recovered from avoiding canonical JALR return bubbles.\\n\\n(End of file - total 25 lines)\\n</content>\",\"metadata\":{\"preview\":\"- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r1s0 (improvement, +32.85%): Moving RV32M multiply/divide out of the combinational EX ALU behind a multicycle handshake can substantially improve FPGA fitness because CoreMark rarely needs M-op throughput while the removed arithmetic cone dominates Fmax.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r1s1 (broken, n/a): Moving branch/JALR resolution into ID needs exhaustive operand-valid stalling and forwarding for decode-stage compares/base operands; stale early redirects can let wrong-path state reach writeback, which riscv-formal's reg check catches.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r1s2 (regression, +21.76%): IF-stage BTFNT/JAL prediction can regress CoreMark when instruction-bit immediate decode and target adders sit directly on the fetch PC path, so front-end bubble savings must be weighed against Fmax-critical next-PC logic.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r2s0 (regression, -1.92%): Replacing MEM-stage variable shifts with explicit byte/halfword lane case muxes did not help FPGA fitness, suggesting the synthesizer already maps these narrow shifts well and the added mux/control structure can slightly worsen routing or LUT packing.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r2s1 (regression, -7.79%): A two-entry IF skid buffer did not pay for CoreMark's random imem stalls; the added fetch-valid/control and redirect-flush machinery can cost more FPGA fitness than the front-end bubbles it hides.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r2s2 (regression, -44.37%): A tiny IF return-address stack can still badly hurt FPGA fitness when canonical-return detection, RAS top muxing, and predicted-PC metadata outweigh the CoreMark return bubbles it removes.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r3s0 (broken, n/a): Keep source-aware load-use interlocks separate from RVFI operand reporting changes; masking unused rs fields can break cosim's retire trace even when the intended stall reduction logic is local to hazard_unit.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r3s1 (regression, -14.30%): MEM-to-EX load bypass can lose FPGA fitness when dmem-ready gating, load extraction, and an extra EX operand forwarding select lengthen the critical path more than CoreMark benefits from removing one-cycle load-use bubbles.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r3s2 (regression, -20.54%): Even decode-stage JAL prediction can regress when the early_redirect priority path, target adder, and predicted-PC metadata still add enough IF/ID/EX control cost to outweigh saving CoreMark's unconditional-jump bubble.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r4s1 (broken, n/a): A registered IF/ID boundary must pair imem backpressure with an ID consume/valid handshake; holding a valid IF/ID entry while ID keeps advancing can duplicate instructions during CoreMark stalls.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r4s2 (regression, -9.29%): Even a resolved-target BTB can lose FPGA fitness when IF-side tag/valid/counter arrays, target muxing, and predicted-PC metadata lengthen the next-PC path more than CoreMark benefits from avoiding loop redirect bubbles.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r5s0 (improvement, +1.93%): Dropping reset clears from the integer register file can improve FPGA fitness because pipeline-valid reset and x0 read forcing preserve architectural behavior while avoiding reset fanout and per-register muxing in an always-active datapath block.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r5s1 (regression, -6.89%): A one-entry ordered store buffer can lose CoreMark fitness when store-order interlocks, MMIO/trap classification, and dmem muxing add more MEM/control-path cost than random store-stall overlap recovers.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r5s2 (regression, -61.05%): Even a single-entry loop predictor can badly regress when predicted-PC metadata, IF PC/tag compare muxing, and EX mismatch redirect logic lengthen critical control paths more than repeated CoreMark loop-branch bubble removal helps.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r6s0 (regression, -2.40%): Pruning unused EX/MEM branch_taken/branch_target metadata did not improve FPGA fitness, suggesting these dead-looking pipeline fields were not on the binding path or were already optimized well enough that layout perturbation outweighed the small flop reduction.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r6s1 (regression, -88.09%): Splitting EX with an EX1 operand register can destroy CoreMark fitness when it delays branch/JALR redirects and forces broad dependent-ALU interlocks, so operand-cone retiming must preserve bypass/redirect latency to pay off.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r6s2 (regression, -48.05%): A JAL-only single-entry target predictor still regressed without fetch-side immediate decode because the IF PC compare/target mux, predicted-PC pipeline fields, and EX mismatch redirect logic outweighed CoreMark's repeated unconditional-jump bubble savings.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r7s0 (regression, -53.28%): Replacing cold RV32M DIV/REM combinational operators with an iterative divider can severely regress CoreMark when the busy/start handshake, divider state, and 32-cycle latency perturb the hot pipeline more than removing the rarely used divide cone helps Fmax.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r7s1 (broken, n/a): Registering dmem backpressure needs a real ready/valid or skid boundary at MEM; a lone pending/wait bit can hide the first stalled cycle from upstream control and let younger CoreMark memory-state updates be dropped or reordered.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r8s0 (improvement, +2.79%): Sharing one sign/zero-extended M-unit multiplier across MULH signedness variants can improve FPGA fitness because CoreMark rarely needs M throughput and parallel 64-bit products add DSP/LUT routing pressure.\",\"truncated\":false,\"loaded\":[]},\"title\":\"cores/bench/LESSONS.md\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778696146818,\"end\":1778696146834}},\"metadata\":{\"openai\":{\"itemId\":\"fc_072aa58eff965527016a04bfd2b474819992ee554818fd04f8\"}},\"id\":\"prt_e228d4f78001sNor0WuCIy0A37\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1dd72e607ffeznJ60Rvr51ABAB\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e228d1a37001szW1rugQOJEAxG\"}}\n./bench/gpt-5_5_medium/rep2/agent.log:10:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778696161430,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1dd72e607ffeznJ60Rvr51ABAB\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"grep\",\"callID\":\"call_fFhw87b0fGWE2T4HP3dzKj12\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"pattern\":\"source-aware|Source-qualified|outcome|delta_pct\",\"path\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_5_medium-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\",\"include\":\"log.jsonl\"},\"output\":\"Found 28 matches\\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_5_medium-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:\\n  Line 1: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"baseline-bench-5327bba\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Baseline retest for cores/bench\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"improvement\\\", \\\"fitness\\\": 282.82, \\\"delta_pct\\\": 0.0, \\\"vs_baseline\\\": 0.0, \\\"fmax_mhz\\\": 127.03, \\\"ipc_coremark\\\": 2e-06, \\\"cycles\\\": 4491485, \\\"iterations\\\": 10, \\\"lut4\\\": 9563, \\\"ff\\\": 1866, \\\"seeds\\\": [128.72, 127.03, 123.02], \\\"formal_passed\\\": true, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": true, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"implementation_notes\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-05-13T13:08:01.185076Z\\\", \\\"round_id\\\": 0, \\\"slot\\\": 0}\\n\\n  Line 2: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r1s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Multicycle RV32M arithmetic unit\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current ALU computes every multiply and divide result combinationally in\\\\nthe EX stage, including signed and unsigned 64-bit products plus divider and\\\\nremainder operators. That wide arithmetic cone is a likely Fmax limiter on\\\\nTang Nano 20K, while CoreMark's hot path should not need single-cycle DIV/REM\\\\nthroughput. Trading rare M-extension latency for a shorter common EX critical\\\\npath should improve iterations/second through higher clock frequency.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current ALU computes every multiply and divide result combinationally in\\\\nthe EX stage, including signed and unsigned 64-bit products plus divider and\\\\nremainder operators. That wide arithmetic cone is a likely Fmax limiter on\\\\nTang Nano 20K, while CoreMark's hot path should not need single-cycle DIV/REM\\\\nthroughput. Trading rare M-extension latency for a shorter common EX critical\\\\npath should improve iterations/second through higher clock frequency.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Split RV32M operations out of the always-combinational ALU path behind a\\\\nsmall multicycle M-unit handshake in EX. Keep ADD/SUB/logic/branch/address\\\\noperations purely combinational, but when the decoded alu_op is MUL/DIV/REM,\\\\nhold ID/EX and EX/MEM until the iterative arithmetic result is ready, then\\\\nretire normally through the existing MEM/WB and RVFI path. This should remove\\\\ndivider and large multiplier logic from the ordinary EX-stage timing path;\\\\nthe added stalls only affect uncommon M instructions and should be outweighed\\\\nby improved Fmax on the FPGA fitness metric.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 6, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/alu.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Restrict the combinational ALU to fast RV32I operations and expose only lightweight M-op selection or helper logic needed by the new M-unit.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a multicycl...\\n  Line 3: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r1s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Resolve control flow in ID\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"CoreMark spends a significant fraction of its bracketed runtime in tight loops and conditional control flow. The current five-stage pipeline resolves branches and jumps in EX, then flushes both IF and ID, so every taken branch or jump pays roughly a two-cycle redirect penalty. Reducing that penalty improves iterations/cycle without increasing retirement width or touching the external NRET=1 contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"CoreMark spends a significant fraction of its bracketed runtime in tight loops and conditional control flow. The current five-stage pipeline resolves branches and jumps in EX, then flushes both IF and ID, so every taken branch or jump pays roughly a two-cycle redirect penalty. Reducing that penalty improves iterations/cycle without increasing retirement width or touching the external NRET=1 contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Move branch and JAL/JALR target/condition resolution from EX into the decode stage, with decode-stage operand forwarding from EX/MEM and MEM/WB for branch compares and JALR base operands. Keep the existing EX-stage trap/metadata path as the retirement source, but let ID drive redirect one cycle earlier when operands are available; stall only for unresolved load-to-branch/JALR dependencies. This should cut the common taken-control penalty from two bubbles to one while preserving RVFI order, trap reporting, and single-channel retirement.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 8, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Wire ID-stage early redirect signals into the IF and hazard path while preserving the existing NRET=1 RVFI port set.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add early branch/JAL/JALR resolution using decoded control, immediates, regfile operands, and forwarded compare/base operands.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/forward_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend forwarding ...\\n  Line 4: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r1s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Static BTFNT branch predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current five-stage core always fetches sequentially and resolves every branch or jump in EX, so each taken loop branch pays a two-instruction front-end flush. CoreMark has tight loops and many backward branches, making a small static predictor likely to improve iterations per cycle without adding much FPGA area or hurting the ALU critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current five-stage core always fetches sequentially and resolves every branch or jump in EX, so each taken loop branch pays a two-instruction front-end flush. CoreMark has tight loops and many backward branches, making a small static predictor likely to improve iterations per cycle without adding much FPGA area or hurting the ALU critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add an IF-stage static backward-taken/forward-not-taken predictor using the fetched instruction bits to compute B-type targets for negative branch immediates, and optionally early-predict JAL targets because they are unconditional PC-relative redirects. Carry the predicted next PC through IF/ID and ID/EX so EX redirects only when the resolved next PC differs from the prediction; mispredicted forward-taken branches, not-taken backward branches, and JALR still recover through the existing flush path. This should remove most loop-closing branch bubbles while preserving the single-retire RVFI contract because retirement remains unchanged at MEM/WB.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 8, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend IF/ID and ID/EX pipeline bundles with the predicted next PC needed for EX-stage mispredict detection.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Implement static BTFNT next-PC selection for backward conditional branches and early PC-relative JAL prediction from fetched instruction bits.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Lat...\\n  Line 5: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r2s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Simplify MEM load lane muxes\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"After the successful M-unit split, the remaining always-active timing paths are more likely to be ordinary RV32I datapath logic than rare multiply/divide arithmetic. The MEM stage currently uses variable shifts for load extraction and shifted masks for byte lanes, which can synthesize into wider mux/barrel-shifter logic on the FPGA load-to-WB path. CoreMark performs many byte, halfword, and word accesses, so a smaller MEM-stage cone should improve Fmax without changing CPI.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"After the successful M-unit split, the remaining always-active timing paths are more likely to be ordinary RV32I datapath logic than rare multiply/divide arithmetic. The MEM stage currently uses variable shifts for load extraction and shifted masks for byte lanes, which can synthesize into wider mux/barrel-shifter logic on the FPGA load-to-WB path. CoreMark performs many byte, halfword, and word accesses, so a smaller MEM-stage cone should improve Fmax without changing CPI.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Rewrite the MEM-stage byte-lane handling as explicit case muxes keyed by addr[1:0] instead of expressions like dmem_rdata >> (addr[1:0] * 8) and mask << addr[1:0]. Keep the same misalignment trap behavior, RVFI aligned mem_addr reporting, replicated store data convention, and MEM/WB retirement boundary. This should reduce LUT depth and routing on the common load-data path while preserving the existing five-stage control flow and single-retire NRET=1 contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace variable load-data shifts and shifted byte masks with explicit addr[1:0] case muxes for LB/LBU/LH/LHU/LW and store masks.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace variable load...\\n  Line 6: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r2s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Add fetch skid buffer\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current front end has no real IF/ID storage: the IF payload is combinational from the current PC and imem data, and an imem-not-ready cycle injects a bubble directly into decode. With the tournament cosim using roughly 22% random instruction stalls, CoreMark loses many cycles even when the back end is already stalled by load-use, dmem, or the multicycle M unit. A small fetch buffer is a structural way to recover iterations/cycle without revisiting the prior broken ID-stage branch-resolution path or adding fetch-path target adders that hurt Fmax.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current front end has no real IF/ID storage: the IF payload is combinational from the current PC and imem data, and an imem-not-ready cycle injects a bubble directly into decode. With the tournament cosim using roughly 22% random instruction stalls, CoreMark loses many cycles even when the back end is already stalled by load-use, dmem, or the multicycle M unit. A small fetch buffer is a structural way to recover iterations/cycle without revisiting the prior broken ID-stage branch-resolution path or adding fetch-path target adders that hurt Fmax.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Insert a one- or two-entry instruction skid buffer between IF and ID. The PC/imem side should continue fetching sequential instructions whenever the buffer has space, while the ID side consumes one buffered instruction only when ID/EX is ready. On redirect, flush the buffer and restart fetch at the EX-resolved target; on imem stalls, hold the outstanding fetch instead of emitting a synthetic NOP into decode. This should overlap random iStall cycles with existing back-end stalls and reduce front-end bubbles while preserving single-retire RVFI ordering and the existing EX-stage control-flow correctness.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 7, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"descr...\\n  Line 7: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r2s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Tiny return-address stack predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The prior static BTFNT predictor targeted loop branches but put branch immediate decode\\\\nand target adders directly on the IF PC-update path, regressing the CoreMark fitness\\\\ndespite saving some bubbles. CoreMark still has frequent function calls and returns,\\\\nand the current core pays the same EX-stage redirect penalty for every JALR return.\\\\nA tiny return predictor can attack those bubbles without adding branch-target arithmetic\\\\nto fetch.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The prior static BTFNT predictor targeted loop branches but put branch immediate decode\\\\nand target adders directly on the IF PC-update path, regressing the CoreMark fitness\\\\ndespite saving some bubbles. CoreMark still has frequent function calls and returns,\\\\nand the current core pays the same EX-stage redirect penalty for every JALR return.\\\\nA tiny return predictor can attack those bubbles without adding branch-target arithmetic\\\\nto fetch.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a small 4-entry return-address stack that predicts only canonical returns\\\\n(JALR x0, 0(x1/x5)) in IF. Push pc+4 for link-register calls when they resolve,\\\\npop/predict the stack top for returns, and carry the predicted next PC through the\\\\npipeline so EX redirects only on mismatch. This keeps ordinary branch fetch as pc+4,\\\\navoids B-type immediate decode and target adders in the IF critical path, and should\\\\nrecover part of the call/return control-flow penalty with modest LUT/FF cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add predicted-next-PC and return-predicted metadata to IF/ID, ID/EX, and EX/MEM bundles for mispredict detection and RAS bookkeeping.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Detect canonical return encodings in the fetched instruction and select the RAS top as the next PC only when the stack is nonempty....\\n  Line 8: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r3s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Source-aware load-use stalls\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current load-use hazard detector compares a load destination against the\\\\nraw rs1 and rs2 bitfields of the following instruction, regardless of whether\\\\nthat instruction actually consumes those operands. For I-type immediates,\\\\nloads, JALR, LUI, AUIPC, JAL, FENCE, and EBREAK, some of those fields are\\\\nimmediates or destination bits rather than true sources. Any accidental match\\\\nwith the preceding load rd inserts an unnecessary bubble, directly reducing\\\\nCoreMark iterations per cycle without improving correctness.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current load-use hazard detector compares a load destination against the\\\\nraw rs1 and rs2 bitfields of the following instruction, regardless of whether\\\\nthat instruction actually consumes those operands. For I-type immediates,\\\\nloads, JALR, LUI, AUIPC, JAL, FENCE, and EBREAK, some of those fields are\\\\nimmediates or destination bits rather than true sources. Any accidental match\\\\nwith the preceding load rd inserts an unnecessary bubble, directly reducing\\\\nCoreMark iterations per cycle without improving correctness.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Make the load-use interlock source-aware by qualifying the rd comparisons with\\\\nsimple decoded uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 signals for the instruction currently in\\\\nIF/ID. Keep the existing one-cycle stall for real load consumers, but avoid\\\\nstalling on unused source fields such as OP-IMM rs2 bits, U/J-type operand\\\\nfields, and SYSTEM/FENCE encodings. This should improve CPI under CoreMark's\\\\nload-heavy code while adding only small opcode decode logic off the main ALU\\\\nand memory datapaths, so Fmax should be largely preserved.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Derive or wire IF/ID source-use qualifiers into the hazard unit without changing the NRET=1 top-level RVFI interface.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl...\\n  Line 9: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r3s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Load-use MEM bypass\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The previous structural attempts spent LUTs and timing on front-end control-flow machinery, and the log shows those changes either broke correctness or regressed fitness. The current five-stage core still has the classic one-cycle load-use interlock even though the benchmark memory is zero-wait in FPGA and only randomly stalled in cosim. CoreMark has frequent dependent loads through pointer-heavy list, matrix, and state code, so removing this bubble can improve iterations per cycle without adding predictor target adders to the fetch critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The previous structural attempts spent LUTs and timing on front-end control-flow machinery, and the log shows those changes either broke correctness or regressed fitness. The current five-stage core still has the classic one-cycle load-use interlock even though the benchmark memory is zero-wait in FPGA and only randomly stalled in cosim. CoreMark has frequent dependent loads through pointer-heavy list, matrix, and state code, so removing this bubble can improve iterations per cycle without adding predictor target adders to the fetch critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a MEM-to-EX load-data bypass path that lets an instruction immediately behind a load proceed into EX when the load is in MEM and dmem is ready. Compute the existing load extraction result combinationally in MEM, expose it to the forwarding network, and add a forwarding select for EX operands that chooses the current MEM load result over the older EX/MEM ALU address. Keep the load-use stall only when the load will not have valid data that cycle, such as a dmem stall or a misaligned/trapping load. This structural bypass should remove many single-cycle bubbles while preserving the NRET=1 retirement boundary and RVFI order at MEM/WB.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 6, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\"...\\n  Line 10: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r3s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Decode-stage JAL predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The prior predictor attempts regressed because they placed branch target decode,\\\\ntarget adders, return detection, and prediction muxes directly on the IF PC path.\\\\nCoreMark still pays the existing EX redirect penalty for every unconditional JAL,\\\\nand JAL targets are PC-relative with no register dependency or branch condition.\\\\nPredicting only JAL in decode should recover one control-flow bubble for calls and\\\\nlong jumps while avoiding the risky fetch-path predictor logic that hurt Fmax.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The prior predictor attempts regressed because they placed branch target decode,\\\\ntarget adders, return detection, and prediction muxes directly on the IF PC path.\\\\nCoreMark still pays the existing EX redirect penalty for every unconditional JAL,\\\\nand JAL targets are PC-relative with no register dependency or branch condition.\\\\nPredicting only JAL in decode should recover one control-flow bubble for calls and\\\\nlong jumps while avoiding the risky fetch-path predictor logic that hurt Fmax.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a narrow decode-stage always-taken predictor for legal JAL instructions only.\\\\nID computes pc+imm for JAL, redirects fetch one cycle before EX would, and records\\\\nthe predicted next PC in the ID/EX bundle. EX then suppresses the normal JAL redirect\\\\nwhen the resolved jump target matches the predicted target, but retains the existing\\\\nEX recovery path for conditional branches, JALR, illegal instructions, and any target\\\\nmismatch. Because the predictor excludes branches and returns, it needs no B-type\\\\nimmediate decode in IF, no RAS state, and no operand forwarding, making it a smaller\\\\npredictor angle with a better chance of improving CoreMark iterations/cycle without\\\\nsacrificing the current high Fmax.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add p...\\n  Line 11: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r4s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Source-qualified load interlock\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current load-use detector compares the preceding load rd against both raw\\\\nsource bitfields of the following instruction, even when those bits are not\\\\narchitecturally consumed. CoreMark has many loads and immediate/control\\\\ninstructions, so accidental rd matches can inject bubbles that reduce\\\\niterations per cycle without protecting any real dependency.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current load-use detector compares the preceding load rd against both raw\\\\nsource bitfields of the following instruction, even when those bits are not\\\\narchitecturally consumed. CoreMark has many loads and immediate/control\\\\ninstructions, so accidental rd matches can inject bubbles that reduce\\\\niterations per cycle without protecting any real dependency.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a tiny source-use decode for the IF/ID instruction and qualify the existing\\\\nload-use interlock with uses_rs1 and uses_rs2. This is a narrower retry of the\\\\nearlier source-aware load-use idea: do not mask or reinterpret RVFI operand\\\\nreporting, and do not add MEM bypass selects or fetch prediction paths. The\\\\ncore should keep the one-cycle stall for true load consumers while avoiding\\\\nfalse stalls on OP-IMM rs2 fields, U/J-type immediates, FENCE, SYSTEM/EBREAK,\\\\nand other instructions that do not read both source registers.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Derive simple IF/ID uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 qualifiers from opcode/funct class and pass them only to the hazard unit.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Gate load-use rd comparisons with the source-use qualifiers while preserving existing dmem, imem, redirect, and M-unit stall priority.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Derive simple IF/ID use...\\n  Line 12: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r4s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Register IF/ID fetch boundary\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current front end feeds imem_data directly through decode, immediate\\\\ngeneration, regfile address decode, hazard checks, and into the ID/EX\\\\nregister in a single cycle. Prior structural attempts tried to hide stalls or\\\\nreduce bubbles with added prediction and bypass control, but those changes\\\\nregressed by lengthening already sensitive mux/control paths. A real IF/ID\\\\nregister is a simpler structural retiming that targets Fmax directly.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current front end feeds imem_data directly through decode, immediate\\\\ngeneration, regfile address decode, hazard checks, and into the ID/EX\\\\nregister in a single cycle. Prior structural attempts tried to hide stalls or\\\\nreduce bubbles with added prediction and bypass control, but those changes\\\\nregressed by lengthening already sensitive mux/control paths. A real IF/ID\\\\nregister is a simpler structural retiming that targets Fmax directly.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Insert a proper IF/ID pipeline register inside the fetch stage, so fetched\\\\ninstruction, PC, and valid are captured before decode sees them. The PC still\\\\nadvances only when the downstream ID stage can accept the entry, and redirect\\\\nflushes the registered IF/ID payload before wrong-path state can retire. This\\\\nadds one front-end cycle and may slightly increase taken-control penalty, but\\\\nit removes the imem-to-decoder combinational path and should improve FPGA\\\\nFmax enough to raise CoreMark iter/sec without changing the NRET=1 retirement\\\\ncontract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Keep the existing IF/ID bundle shape, but document and use it as a registered pipeline boundary rather than a combinational fetch payload.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add an IF/ID register for pc, instruction, and valid; hold it on deco...\\n  Line 13: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r4s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Tiny resolved-target BTB\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current core resolves all taken branches and jumps in EX, so tight CoreMark loops pay a two-bubble redirect penalty. Prior predictor attempts regressed because they decoded immediates, computed targets, or managed return-stack state on the front-end critical path. A small BTB trained from already-resolved EX redirects can target the same loop bubbles while avoiding fetch-stage target arithmetic.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current core resolves all taken branches and jumps in EX, so tight CoreMark loops pay a two-bubble redirect penalty. Prior predictor attempts regressed because they decoded immediates, computed targets, or managed return-stack state on the front-end critical path. A small BTB trained from already-resolved EX redirects can target the same loop bubbles while avoiding fetch-stage target arithmetic.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a tiny direct-mapped branch target buffer that stores resolved taken conditional-branch targets, preferably only backward loop branches, along with a compact PC tag and a 2-bit confidence counter. IF predicts to the stored target on a tag hit with a taken counter, otherwise it keeps the existing pc+4 path; EX updates the BTB after branch resolution and flushes on any predicted/resolved next-PC mismatch. Because the target is stored from the prior execution, the fetch path adds only a small tag compare and next-PC mux instead of B/J immediate decode and target adders, which should recover loop-closing branch bubbles with less Fmax damage than the previous predictor attempts.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Carry predicted next-PC metadata through IF/ID and ID/EX so EX can distinguish correct BTB predictions from mispredictions without changing the NRET=1 RVFI interface.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Imple...\\n  Line 14: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r5s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Drop regfile reset fanout\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current register file resets all 32 architectural registers with a for-loop,\\\\nwhich forces reset muxing or reset routing onto the whole small RF array. After\\\\nthe M-unit split, remaining FPGA Fmax is likely limited by ordinary datapath\\\\nand control fanout rather than rare arithmetic. CoreMark initializes the state\\\\nit uses, x0 is already hardwired on reads, and RVFI correctness depends on\\\\nretired architectural behavior rather than power-on values of unused registers.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current register file resets all 32 architectural registers with a for-loop,\\\\nwhich forces reset muxing or reset routing onto the whole small RF array. After\\\\nthe M-unit split, remaining FPGA Fmax is likely limited by ordinary datapath\\\\nand control fanout rather than rare arithmetic. CoreMark initializes the state\\\\nit uses, x0 is already hardwired on reads, and RVFI correctness depends on\\\\nretired architectural behavior rather than power-on values of unused registers.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Remove the explicit reset clear from the integer register file and keep only\\\\nthe synchronous write path for nonzero destination registers. Reads of x0 stay\\\\nforced to zero, same-cycle write-first bypass stays unchanged, and reset of the\\\\npipeline control/valid bits still prevents spurious retirement after reset.\\\\nThis should reduce reset fanout and per-register muxing in a timing-sensitive,\\\\nalways-active block without changing CPI, branch behavior, memory behavior, or\\\\nthe NRET=1 RVFI interface. The change is deliberately narrower than prior\\\\nfailed micro-opts: it does not touch hazard detection, RVFI operand reporting,\\\\nMEM load extraction, or any front-end prediction path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/reg_file.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove the reset-time loop that clears all 32 registers while preser...\\n  Line 15: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r5s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Ordered one-entry store buffer\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent structural attempts spent timing and LUTs on the front end or on load\\\\nbypass paths, and those changes either regressed fitness or broke stall\\\\nhandling. The current core still freezes the entire pipeline whenever a store\\\\nreaches MEM and dmem_ready is low, even though independent ALU, branch, and\\\\nregister-only instructions behind that store could execute while the store is\\\\nwaiting. CoreMark has frequent data stores and the apples-to-apples cosim\\\\ninjects random dmem stalls, so store backpressure is a promising structural\\\\ntarget that avoids fetch-path target adders and load-result critical muxes.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent structural attempts spent timing and LUTs on the front end or on load\\\\nbypass paths, and those changes either regressed fitness or broke stall\\\\nhandling. The current core still freezes the entire pipeline whenever a store\\\\nreaches MEM and dmem_ready is low, even though independent ALU, branch, and\\\\nregister-only instructions behind that store could execute while the store is\\\\nwaiting. CoreMark has frequent data stores and the apples-to-apples cosim\\\\ninjects random dmem stalls, so store backpressure is a promising structural\\\\ntarget that avoids fetch-path target adders and load-result critical muxes.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a single-entry ordered store buffer at the MEM boundary. A regular data\\\\nstore can retire into the buffer with its address, byte mask, write data, and\\\\nRVFI metadata captured, allowing the front of the pipeline to keep executing\\\\nnon-memory instructions while the buffer drives dmem until dmem_ready accepts\\\\nthe write. Preserve precise in-order behavior by stalling any younger load,\\\\nstore, MMIO access, FENCE, EBREAK, or illegal/trapping instruction until the\\\\nbuffer drains; MMIO marker/UART stores should bypass the buffer and keep the\\\\nexisting blocking behavior so CoreMark timing brackets re...\\n  Line 16: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r5s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Single-entry loop target predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current core fetches sequentially and pays the existing EX-stage redirect\\\\npenalty on every taken loop-closing branch. Earlier predictor attempts\\\\nregressed because they placed immediate decode, target adders, BTB arrays,\\\\ncounters, or RAS control on the fetch path. A predictor for this slot should\\\\ntherefore be much narrower: reuse targets already resolved in EX and add the\\\\nsmallest possible fetch-side structure.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current core fetches sequentially and pays the existing EX-stage redirect\\\\npenalty on every taken loop-closing branch. Earlier predictor attempts\\\\nregressed because they placed immediate decode, target adders, BTB arrays,\\\\ncounters, or RAS control on the fetch path. A predictor for this slot should\\\\ntherefore be much narrower: reuse targets already resolved in EX and add the\\\\nsmallest possible fetch-side structure.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a single-entry resolved-loop target predictor trained only by taken\\\\nbackward conditional branches. The entry stores one branch PC and its resolved\\\\ntarget; IF predicts taken only when the current PC equals that stored PC,\\\\notherwise it keeps the existing pc+4 behavior. Carry predicted-taken and\\\\npredicted-next-PC metadata to EX so the normal redirect path fires only on a\\\\nmismatch, and invalidate the entry when that same branch resolves not-taken\\\\nat loop exit. This should recover repeated inner-loop branch bubbles in\\\\nCoreMark while avoiding the fetch target adders, multi-entry tag arrays,\\\\nconfidence counters, and return-stack machinery that hurt prior predictors.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add minimal predicted-taken and predicted-next-PC metadata to IF/ID and ID/EX bundles for EX-stage mismatch detection.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Implement ...\\n  Line 17: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r6s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Prune dead EX/MEM branch metadata\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core has already won most of its fitness from removing broad\\\\ntiming/fanout costs, while recent attempts that add prediction, buffering,\\\\nbypassing, or memory-ordering control have regressed. The EX/MEM bundle still\\\\ncarries branch_taken and branch_target fields that are assigned every cycle\\\\nbut are not consumed by MEM, WB, RVFI, hazard, or forwarding logic. Those 33\\\\ndead flops and their input adders/mux routes sit in an always-active pipeline\\\\nboundary, so pruning them is a low-risk micro-optimization for LUT/FF count\\\\nand routing delay without changing CPI.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core has already won most of its fitness from removing broad\\\\ntiming/fanout costs, while recent attempts that add prediction, buffering,\\\\nbypassing, or memory-ordering control have regressed. The EX/MEM bundle still\\\\ncarries branch_taken and branch_target fields that are assigned every cycle\\\\nbut are not consumed by MEM, WB, RVFI, hazard, or forwarding logic. Those 33\\\\ndead flops and their input adders/mux routes sit in an always-active pipeline\\\\nboundary, so pruning them is a low-risk micro-optimization for LUT/FF count\\\\nand routing delay without changing CPI.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Remove the unused branch_taken and branch_target fields from ex_mem_t and stop\\\\nregistering them in ex_stage. Keep redirect and pc_next computation exactly as\\\\nthey are today, because pc_next is the RVFI-visible resolved next PC and the\\\\nredirect output is still the only live control-flow signal. This should trim\\\\ndead sequential state and reduce EX-stage fanout/routing pressure while\\\\npreserving the NRET=1 RVFI contract, trap behavior, memory behavior, and all\\\\nexisting stall/flush priorities.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 1, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove unused branch_taken and...\\n  Line 18: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r6s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Pipeline EX operand retiming\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent structural attempts that added fetch prediction, skid buffering, load bypassing,\\\\nor store buffering all paid too much control-path cost. The current core's remaining\\\\nhigh-fanout structural path is more basic: ID/EX values pass through forwarding muxes,\\\\nALU operand selection, ALU or branch compare/target logic, and into EX/MEM plus the\\\\nredirect path in one cycle. Retiming that boundary can target Fmax without adding a\\\\npredictor, memory ordering structure, or RVFI contract change.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent structural attempts that added fetch prediction, skid buffering, load bypassing,\\\\nor store buffering all paid too much control-path cost. The current core's remaining\\\\nhigh-fanout structural path is more basic: ID/EX values pass through forwarding muxes,\\\\nALU operand selection, ALU or branch compare/target logic, and into EX/MEM plus the\\\\nredirect path in one cycle. Retiming that boundary can target Fmax without adding a\\\\npredictor, memory ordering structure, or RVFI contract change.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Split the existing EX stage into a lightweight EX1 operand/metadata register and an EX2\\\\nexecute/redirect stage. EX1 captures the forwarded rs1/rs2 values, selected ALU operands,\\\\ndecoded control, immediate, PC, and RVFI source data; EX2 performs the ALU operation,\\\\nbranch/jump resolution, M-unit handshake, and drives the existing EX/MEM payload. Add the\\\\nminimal hazard/forwarding adjustments needed for the extra execute stage, preferring a\\\\none-cycle interlock on immediately dependent ALU consumers over wide new bypass muxes if\\\\nthat keeps timing short. This structural retiming should shorten the common forwarding-to-\\\\nALU-to-EX/MEM cone and may raise Tang Nano 20K Fmax enough to offset a small CPI cost,\\\\nwhile preserving NRET=1 retirement at MEM/WB and all RVFI ordering/trap behavior.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\"...\\n  Line 19: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r6s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Single-entry JAL target predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts regressed because they put immediate decode, target\\\\nadders, multi-entry tags, counters, or return-stack control on the fetch path.\\\\nCoreMark still pays the EX redirect penalty for repeated unconditional calls\\\\nand jumps, but JAL has no direction ambiguity once a target is known. A very\\\\nsmall learned JAL target entry can attack only that control-flow cost while\\\\navoiding conditional branch and return prediction complexity.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts regressed because they put immediate decode, target\\\\nadders, multi-entry tags, counters, or return-stack control on the fetch path.\\\\nCoreMark still pays the EX redirect penalty for repeated unconditional calls\\\\nand jumps, but JAL has no direction ambiguity once a target is known. A very\\\\nsmall learned JAL target entry can attack only that control-flow cost while\\\\navoiding conditional branch and return prediction complexity.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a single-entry predictor trained only by legal resolved JAL instructions\\\\nin EX. The entry stores the exact JAL PC and resolved target; IF predicts to\\\\nthe stored target only when the current PC equals that entry, otherwise it\\\\nkeeps the existing pc+4 path. Carry only predicted-valid and predicted-next-PC\\\\nmetadata to EX so the normal redirect path is suppressed on an exact correct\\\\nprediction and used for all misses or mismatches. This should recover repeated\\\\ncall/jump bubbles in CoreMark without fetch-side immediate decode, B-type\\\\nbranch prediction, return-stack state, confidence counters, or multi-entry BTB\\\\nrouting that hurt previous predictor hypotheses.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add minimal predicted-valid and predicted-next-PC fields to IF/ID and ID/EX bundles for EX-stage JAL prediction validation....\\n  Line 20: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r7s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Iterative M divider\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The largest proven fitness gain for this core came from moving RV32M arithmetic\\\\nout of the ordinary EX ALU path, but the current m_unit still contains wide\\\\ncombinational DIV/REM operators behind a one-cycle result register. Even if\\\\nthose operations are rare in CoreMark, the FPGA still has to place and time\\\\nthe divider/remainder cone, so it can cap Fmax or perturb routing for the hot\\\\nRV32I pipeline.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The largest proven fitness gain for this core came from moving RV32M arithmetic\\\\nout of the ordinary EX ALU path, but the current m_unit still contains wide\\\\ncombinational DIV/REM operators behind a one-cycle result register. Even if\\\\nthose operations are rare in CoreMark, the FPGA still has to place and time\\\\nthe divider/remainder cone, so it can cap Fmax or perturb routing for the hot\\\\nRV32I pipeline.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the one-cycle DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU implementation in m_unit with a small\\\\niterative restoring divider that runs only after a divide or remainder starts.\\\\nKeep MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU on the existing short registered path so any\\\\nmultiply use does not pay a 32-cycle penalty, and keep the existing EX-stage\\\\nhold/valid handshake as the only pipeline interface. This should remove the\\\\nexpensive combinational divider/remainder logic from the FPGA timing closure\\\\nproblem while adding CPI only for uncommon divide/remainder instructions.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/m_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Implement DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU with an iterative signed/unsigned divider state machine while preserving ALTOPS behavior under RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Adjust the existing M-unit busy/valid handshake only as needed so EX holds until multi-cycle DIV/REM completion and still clears completed results exactly once.\\\"}]}, \\\"expec...\\n  Line 21: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r7s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Register dmem stall boundary\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent structural attempts added front-end prediction, fetch buffering, load\\\\nbypassing, store buffering, or a deeper EX pipe, and each paid too much in\\\\nmux/control cost or correctness complexity. A smaller structural target is\\\\nthe current dmem stall path: io_dmemReady fans combinationally through the\\\\nhazard unit into PC hold, ID hold, EX/MEM hold, and MEM/WB valid clearing in\\\\nthe same cycle. CoreMark's apples-to-apples mode injects random dmem stalls,\\\\nso this control path is active during evaluation, and reducing its fanout may\\\\nimprove Fmax without changing the visible memory ordering contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent structural attempts added front-end prediction, fetch buffering, load\\\\nbypassing, store buffering, or a deeper EX pipe, and each paid too much in\\\\nmux/control cost or correctness complexity. A smaller structural target is\\\\nthe current dmem stall path: io_dmemReady fans combinationally through the\\\\nhazard unit into PC hold, ID hold, EX/MEM hold, and MEM/WB valid clearing in\\\\nthe same cycle. CoreMark's apples-to-apples mode injects random dmem stalls,\\\\nso this control path is active during evaluation, and reducing its fanout may\\\\nimprove Fmax without changing the visible memory ordering contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a one-cycle registered MEM busy/accepted boundary for data-memory\\\\noperations. When a load or store enters MEM, capture whether it is waiting for\\\\ndmem_ready in a small state bit local to the MEM/hazard interface; while that\\\\nbit is set, hold EX/MEM and clear MEM/WB valid exactly as today, but drive the\\\\nglobal pipeline stalls from the registered busy state rather than directly\\\\nfrom io_dmemReady. Keep dmem_addr, dmem_wdata, dmem_wen, dmem_ren, RVFI memory\\\\nmasks/data, misalignment traps, and all in-order retirement behavior\\\\nunchanged. This deliberately accepts at most one extra cycle on the first\\\\nstal...\\n  Line 22: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r7s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Decode-only JAL redirect predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts for this core regressed because they added target\\\\nadders, tag compares, return-stack state, or predicted-PC muxing directly to\\\\nthe IF next-PC path. The current core still pays the EX redirect penalty for\\\\nevery unconditional JAL, and JAL needs no operand forwarding or direction\\\\nprediction once its instruction reaches decode.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts for this core regressed because they added target\\\\nadders, tag compares, return-stack state, or predicted-PC muxing directly to\\\\nthe IF next-PC path. The current core still pays the EX redirect penalty for\\\\nevery unconditional JAL, and JAL needs no operand forwarding or direction\\\\nprediction once its instruction reaches decode.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a decode-stage predictor for legal JAL instructions only. ID already has\\\\nthe instruction, PC, and immediate generator output, so it can compute the JAL\\\\ntarget and redirect fetch one cycle earlier than EX without adding any logic\\\\nto the IF PC increment path. Carry a small predicted-next-PC flag/value into\\\\nID/EX so EX suppresses its normal JAL redirect when the ID prediction matches,\\\\nwhile all conditional branches, JALR, illegal instructions, and mismatches use\\\\nthe existing EX recovery path. This avoids the failed fetch-side predictor\\\\nstructures while recovering one bubble on CoreMark calls and PC-relative jumps.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add minimal predicted-valid and predicted-next-PC metadata to the IF/ID and ID/EX bundles for decode-stage JAL prediction validation.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Detect legal JAL in decode, compute pc plus J-immediate, emit an early redirect, and latch the prediction metadata into ID/EX.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Wire the ID...\\n  Line 23: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r8s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Share M-unit multiplier hardware\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core already removed RV32M arithmetic from the ordinary EX\\\\nALU path, but m_unit still builds three separate 64-bit multiplier products\\\\nin parallel for MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU signedness variants. CoreMark uses\\\\nM operations rarely enough that throughput beyond the existing one-cycle\\\\nregistered M-unit is not the main limiter, while the extra DSP/LUT/routing\\\\npressure can still depress FPGA Fmax or perturb placement of the hot RV32I\\\\npipeline.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core already removed RV32M arithmetic from the ordinary EX\\\\nALU path, but m_unit still builds three separate 64-bit multiplier products\\\\nin parallel for MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU signedness variants. CoreMark uses\\\\nM operations rarely enough that throughput beyond the existing one-cycle\\\\nregistered M-unit is not the main limiter, while the extra DSP/LUT/routing\\\\npressure can still depress FPGA Fmax or perturb placement of the hot RV32I\\\\npipeline.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the parallel signed-signed, unsigned-unsigned, and signed-unsigned\\\\nproduct generation in m_unit with one shared signed multiplier fed by\\\\nsign/zero-extended operands selected from the M opcode. MUL can take the low\\\\nhalf of the same product because low 32 bits are independent of operand\\\\nsignedness; MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU select the high half with the appropriate\\\\noperand extension. Keep the existing start/valid/clear handshake, one-cycle\\\\nM latency, DIV/REM behavior, and RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions unchanged.\\\\nThis should trim multiplier area and routing without adding CPI, front-end\\\\nprediction logic, memory ordering state, or any RVFI-visible behavior change.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/m_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Use a single sign-controlled widened multiplier for MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU while pres...\\n  Line 24: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r8s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Register WB result at MEM boundary\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The recent structural attempts mostly lost fitness by adding control-flow\\\\nprediction, buffering, memory overlap, or deeper execute-stage interlocks.\\\\nA safer structural target is the existing MEM/WB-to-WB combinational path:\\\\nthe selected writeback value is still muxed after the MEM/WB register, then\\\\nfans out to the regfile write port, same-cycle regfile bypass, EX forwarding,\\\\nand RVFI rd_wdata. Registering that selected value at the retirement boundary\\\\nshould shorten a hot fanout path without changing CPI, branch timing, memory\\\\nordering, or the NRET=1 external contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The recent structural attempts mostly lost fitness by adding control-flow\\\\nprediction, buffering, memory overlap, or deeper execute-stage interlocks.\\\\nA safer structural target is the existing MEM/WB-to-WB combinational path:\\\\nthe selected writeback value is still muxed after the MEM/WB register, then\\\\nfans out to the regfile write port, same-cycle regfile bypass, EX forwarding,\\\\nand RVFI rd_wdata. Registering that selected value at the retirement boundary\\\\nshould shorten a hot fanout path without changing CPI, branch timing, memory\\\\nordering, or the NRET=1 external contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a preselected rd_wdata/writeback-result field to the MEM/WB pipeline\\\\nbundle and compute it inside mem_stage before the MEM/WB register captures.\\\\nFor loads this field captures the sign/zero-extended load result; for ALU,\\\\nJAL, JALR, AUIPC, LUI, and M-extension operations it captures the existing\\\\nEX/MEM alu_result. Then simplify wb_stage into a pass-through of the\\\\nregistered writeback value instead of a post-register mem_to_reg mux. This\\\\nstructural retiming preserves the same retirement boundary and RVFI data, but\\\\nremoves the load-vs-ALU mux from the next cycle's forwarding/regfile/RVFI\\\\nfanout cone, which may improve Tang Nano 20K Fmax with ess...\\n  Line 25: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r8s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Decode-stage backward branch predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts lost fitness when they added BTB, RAS, tag compare,\\\\ncounter, or target-adder logic to the IF next-PC path. The current core still\\\\npays a two-bubble EX redirect penalty on taken loop-closing branches, which\\\\nare the predictor opportunity most likely to matter for CoreMark. A predictor\\\\nthat waits until decode can use the already-fetched instruction and decoded\\\\nbranch control while keeping fetch as the current simple pc+4 path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts lost fitness when they added BTB, RAS, tag compare,\\\\ncounter, or target-adder logic to the IF next-PC path. The current core still\\\\npays a two-bubble EX redirect penalty on taken loop-closing branches, which\\\\nare the predictor opportunity most likely to matter for CoreMark. A predictor\\\\nthat waits until decode can use the already-fetched instruction and decoded\\\\nbranch control while keeping fetch as the current simple pc+4 path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a static decode-stage predictor for legal backward conditional branches\\\\nonly. ID uses the existing immediate generator and decoded is_branch signal to\\\\nrecognize negative B-type offsets, computes pc+imm, and redirects fetch one\\\\ncycle earlier than EX would. Carry predicted-valid and predicted-next-PC into\\\\nID/EX so EX suppresses the normal redirect when the branch is actually taken\\\\nto the predicted target, or redirects to pc+4 on the loop-exit not-taken\\\\nmisprediction. Forward branches, JAL, JALR, illegal encodings, and all memory\\\\nor RVFI behavior stay on the existing path. This targets loop bubbles without\\\\nreintroducing the failed IF-side predictor structures.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add minimal predicted-valid and predicted-next-PC fields to the ID/EX bundle for EX-stage branch predict...\\n  Line 26: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r9s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Narrow MEM/WB control state\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent micro-optimization wins came from removing always-present FPGA timing\\\\nand routing pressure, while attempts that added prediction, bypassing, or\\\\nstall-hiding control usually regressed. The current MEM/WB register still\\\\ncarries the full decode control bundle even though the writeback and RVFI\\\\nretirement logic only consume a few bits after the MEM stage. Pruning that\\\\ncold control state should reduce flop count, muxing, and routing without\\\\nchanging CoreMark CPI or touching the external NRET=1 contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent micro-optimization wins came from removing always-present FPGA timing\\\\nand routing pressure, while attempts that added prediction, bypassing, or\\\\nstall-hiding control usually regressed. The current MEM/WB register still\\\\ncarries the full decode control bundle even though the writeback and RVFI\\\\nretirement logic only consume a few bits after the MEM stage. Pruning that\\\\ncold control state should reduce flop count, muxing, and routing without\\\\nchanging CoreMark CPI or touching the external NRET=1 contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the full ctrl_t field in mem_wb_t with a narrow retirement/writeback\\\\ncontrol subset containing only reg_write, mem_to_reg, and is_illegal. Keep\\\\nthe existing full ctrl_t through ID/EX and EX/MEM, because the execute and\\\\nmemory stages still need branch, memory width, sign-extension, and M-op\\\\ncontrols. In mem_stage, collapse ctrl_with_trap into the narrow MEM/WB\\\\nfields after misalignment handling, and update wb_stage and core RVFI trap\\\\nreferences to use the narrow fields. This should trim unused MEM/WB control\\\\nflops and fanout while preserving all memory masks/data, load results,\\\\nwriteback behavior, RVFI order, and trap reporting.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Introduce a narrow M...\\n  Line 27: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r9s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Split RVFI shadow metadata from datapath\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The best recent gains came from removing broad arithmetic and register fanout from hot paths, while most structural attempts regressed by adding prediction, buffering, memory overlap, or extra execute-stage interlocks. The current pipeline still carries a wide all-purpose struct through EX/MEM and MEM/WB, mixing hot execution data with RVFI-only metadata such as original source values, source register numbers, instruction bits, memory masks, and retired PC fields. Even when those fields are not part of the architectural datapath, they share the same pipeline registers, reset/hold enables, and module boundaries as timing-critical forwarding, memory, and writeback signals.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The best recent gains came from removing broad arithmetic and register fanout from hot paths, while most structural attempts regressed by adding prediction, buffering, memory overlap, or extra execute-stage interlocks. The current pipeline still carries a wide all-purpose struct through EX/MEM and MEM/WB, mixing hot execution data with RVFI-only metadata such as original source values, source register numbers, instruction bits, memory masks, and retired PC fields. Even when those fields are not part of the architectural datapath, they share the same pipeline registers, reset/hold enables, and module boundaries as timing-critical forwarding, memory, and writeback signals.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Split the pipeline state into a narrow execution payload and a parallel RVFI shadow payload. Keep the hot EX/MEM and MEM/WB datapath bundles limited to control bits and values needed for forwarding, dmem, writeback, and stalls, while carrying RVFI-only fields in a separate shadow register chain that uses the same valid, flush, hold, trap, and redirect decisions. This should reduce register width, enable fanout, and routing pressure on the timing-critical da...\\n  Line 28: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r9s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Decode return target predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts for this core mostly regressed because they added\\\\ntarget adders, tag compares, counters, return-stack state, or predicted-PC\\\\nmuxes directly to the IF next-PC path. The current core still pays the EX\\\\nredirect penalty on every canonical function return, and CoreMark contains\\\\nenough call/return traffic that recovering one redirect cycle there may help\\\\niterations per cycle. A return predictor that operates only after decode can\\\\nuse the already-fetched instruction, regfile read data, and existing immediate\\\\npath while keeping fetch's common pc+4 path untouched.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts for this core mostly regressed because they added\\\\ntarget adders, tag compares, counters, return-stack state, or predicted-PC\\\\nmuxes directly to the IF next-PC path. The current core still pays the EX\\\\nredirect penalty on every canonical function return, and CoreMark contains\\\\nenough call/return traffic that recovering one redirect cycle there may help\\\\niterations per cycle. A return predictor that operates only after decode can\\\\nuse the already-fetched instruction, regfile read data, and existing immediate\\\\npath while keeping fetch's common pc+4 path untouched.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a decode-stage predictor for canonical returns only: legal JALR with\\\\nrd=x0, funct3=0, imm=0, and rs1=x1 or x5. ID computes the return target from\\\\nthe already-read link register value and redirects fetch one cycle before EX\\\\nwould, but only when the base register is not the destination of an unresolved\\\\nolder instruction in ID/EX or EX/MEM. Carry a minimal predicted-valid and\\\\npredicted-next-PC field into ID/EX so EX suppresses the normal JALR redirect\\\\non an exact match and uses the existing recovery redirect on mismatches. This\\\\navoids the failed IF-side RAS/BTB/BTFNT machinery while targeting a narrower\\\\ncontrol-flow pattern wit...\",\"metadata\":{\"matches\":28,\"truncated\":false},\"title\":\"source-aware|Source-qualified|outcome|delta_pct\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778696161421,\"end\":1778696161429}},\"metadata\":{\"openai\":{\"itemId\":\"fc_07cdfe704e70ac60016a04bfe144ec8198beb3a9d93d0fc9b0\"}},\"id\":\"prt_e228d8871001EbG00G1aa6R8c1\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1dd72e607ffeznJ60Rvr51ABAB\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e228d4fde001fzsUndBPdvzkkz\"}}\n./bench/gpt-5_5_medium/rep2/agent.log:27:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778697087461,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1dd647e0effeZs5rb4D9DoknQl\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"read\",\"callID\":\"call_pskGQeEA9WFy488WkrQfcbiq\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"filePath\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_5_medium-rep2/cores/bench/LESSONS.md\",\"offset\":1,\"limit\":200},\"output\":\"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_5_medium-rep2/cores/bench/LESSONS.md</path>\\n<type>file</type>\\n<content>\\n1: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r1s0 (improvement, +32.85%): Moving RV32M multiply/divide out of the combinational EX ALU behind a multicycle handshake can substantially improve FPGA fitness because CoreMark rarely needs M-op throughput while the removed arithmetic cone dominates Fmax.\\n2: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r1s1 (broken, n/a): Moving branch/JALR resolution into ID needs exhaustive operand-valid stalling and forwarding for decode-stage compares/base operands; stale early redirects can let wrong-path state reach writeback, which riscv-formal's reg check catches.\\n3: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r1s2 (regression, +21.76%): IF-stage BTFNT/JAL prediction can regress CoreMark when instruction-bit immediate decode and target adders sit directly on the fetch PC path, so front-end bubble savings must be weighed against Fmax-critical next-PC logic.\\n4: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r2s0 (regression, -1.92%): Replacing MEM-stage variable shifts with explicit byte/halfword lane case muxes did not help FPGA fitness, suggesting the synthesizer already maps these narrow shifts well and the added mux/control structure can slightly worsen routing or LUT packing.\\n5: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r2s1 (regression, -7.79%): A two-entry IF skid buffer did not pay for CoreMark's random imem stalls; the added fetch-valid/control and redirect-flush machinery can cost more FPGA fitness than the front-end bubbles it hides.\\n6: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r2s2 (regression, -44.37%): A tiny IF return-address stack can still badly hurt FPGA fitness when canonical-return detection, RAS top muxing, and predicted-PC metadata outweigh the CoreMark return bubbles it removes.\\n7: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r3s0 (broken, n/a): Keep source-aware load-use interlocks separate from RVFI operand reporting changes; masking unused rs fields can break cosim's retire trace even when the intended stall reduction logic is local to hazard_unit.\\n8: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r3s1 (regression, -14.30%): MEM-to-EX load bypass can lose FPGA fitness when dmem-ready gating, load extraction, and an extra EX operand forwarding select lengthen the critical path more than CoreMark benefits from removing one-cycle load-use bubbles.\\n9: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r3s2 (regression, -20.54%): Even decode-stage JAL prediction can regress when the early_redirect priority path, target adder, and predicted-PC metadata still add enough IF/ID/EX control cost to outweigh saving CoreMark's unconditional-jump bubble.\\n10: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r4s1 (broken, n/a): A registered IF/ID boundary must pair imem backpressure with an ID consume/valid handshake; holding a valid IF/ID entry while ID keeps advancing can duplicate instructions during CoreMark stalls.\\n11: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r4s2 (regression, -9.29%): Even a resolved-target BTB can lose FPGA fitness when IF-side tag/valid/counter arrays, target muxing, and predicted-PC metadata lengthen the next-PC path more than CoreMark benefits from avoiding loop redirect bubbles.\\n12: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r5s0 (improvement, +1.93%): Dropping reset clears from the integer register file can improve FPGA fitness because pipeline-valid reset and x0 read forcing preserve architectural behavior while avoiding reset fanout and per-register muxing in an always-active datapath block.\\n13: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r5s1 (regression, -6.89%): A one-entry ordered store buffer can lose CoreMark fitness when store-order interlocks, MMIO/trap classification, and dmem muxing add more MEM/control-path cost than random store-stall overlap recovers.\\n14: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r5s2 (regression, -61.05%): Even a single-entry loop predictor can badly regress when predicted-PC metadata, IF PC/tag compare muxing, and EX mismatch redirect logic lengthen critical control paths more than repeated CoreMark loop-branch bubble removal helps.\\n15: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r6s0 (regression, -2.40%): Pruning unused EX/MEM branch_taken/branch_target metadata did not improve FPGA fitness, suggesting these dead-looking pipeline fields were not on the binding path or were already optimized well enough that layout perturbation outweighed the small flop reduction.\\n16: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r6s1 (regression, -88.09%): Splitting EX with an EX1 operand register can destroy CoreMark fitness when it delays branch/JALR redirects and forces broad dependent-ALU interlocks, so operand-cone retiming must preserve bypass/redirect latency to pay off.\\n17: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r6s2 (regression, -48.05%): A JAL-only single-entry target predictor still regressed without fetch-side immediate decode because the IF PC compare/target mux, predicted-PC pipeline fields, and EX mismatch redirect logic outweighed CoreMark's repeated unconditional-jump bubble savings.\\n18: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r7s0 (regression, -53.28%): Replacing cold RV32M DIV/REM combinational operators with an iterative divider can severely regress CoreMark when the busy/start handshake, divider state, and 32-cycle latency perturb the hot pipeline more than removing the rarely used divide cone helps Fmax.\\n19: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r7s1 (broken, n/a): Registering dmem backpressure needs a real ready/valid or skid boundary at MEM; a lone pending/wait bit can hide the first stalled cycle from upstream control and let younger CoreMark memory-state updates be dropped or reordered.\\n20: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r8s0 (improvement, +2.79%): Sharing one sign/zero-extended M-unit multiplier across MULH signedness variants can improve FPGA fitness because CoreMark rarely needs M throughput and parallel 64-bit products add DSP/LUT routing pressure.\\n21: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r8s1 (regression, -0.33%): Preselecting MEM/WB writeback data inside mem_stage did not improve fitness, suggesting the load-vs-ALU writeback mux is not a binding FPGA path and adding another pipeline field can slightly worsen packing/routing.\\n22: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r8s2 (regression, +1.88%): Moving backward-branch prediction from IF to ID still regressed because the ID target adder, redirect/stall arbitration, predicted-PC metadata, and EX mispredict compare/control outweighed CoreMark loop-bubble savings.\\n23: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r9s0 (regression, -14.30%): Narrowing MEM/WB from full ctrl_t to a tiny packed mem_wb_ctrl_t can hurt FPGA fitness despite fewer flops, suggesting late-stage control fields were already optimized away or well-packed and the new struct/mapping perturbed routing more than it saved.\\n24: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r9s1 (improvement, +3.53%): Splitting RVFI-only metadata into parallel shadow payloads can improve FPGA fitness because it removes wide instruction/source/PC/memory fields from hot EX/MEM and MEM/WB forwarding, dmem, and writeback bundles without changing pipeline timing.\\n25: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r9s2 (regression, -31.51%): Decode-stage return prediction still regressed because link-register hazard compares, early redirect arbitration, and predicted-PC fields added more hot control/datapath cost than CoreMark recovered from avoiding canonical JALR return bubbles.\\n26: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r10s1 (regression, -5.39%): Further splitting MEM RVFI memory sidebands after the broader RVFI-shadow win can regress when it duplicates byte-mask/write-data logic and adds another MEM/WB payload boundary without removing a binding dmem/load/writeback path.\\n27: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r10s2 (broken, n/a): EX-trained branch predictors must keep prediction-hit suppression, exit invalidation redirects, and RVFI pc_next reporting aligned with the next retired instruction; riscv-formal's pc_fwd check catches recovery paths that eventually fetch correctly but break the retirement PC chain.\\n\\n(End of file - total 27 lines)\\n</content>\",\"metadata\":{\"preview\":\"- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r1s0 (improvement, +32.85%): Moving RV32M multiply/divide out of the combinational EX ALU behind a multicycle handshake can substantially improve FPGA fitness because CoreMark rarely needs M-op throughput while the removed arithmetic cone dominates Fmax.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r1s1 (broken, n/a): Moving branch/JALR resolution into ID needs exhaustive operand-valid stalling and forwarding for decode-stage compares/base operands; stale early redirects can let wrong-path state reach writeback, which riscv-formal's reg check catches.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r1s2 (regression, +21.76%): IF-stage BTFNT/JAL prediction can regress CoreMark when instruction-bit immediate decode and target adders sit directly on the fetch PC path, so front-end bubble savings must be weighed against Fmax-critical next-PC logic.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r2s0 (regression, -1.92%): Replacing MEM-stage variable shifts with explicit byte/halfword lane case muxes did not help FPGA fitness, suggesting the synthesizer already maps these narrow shifts well and the added mux/control structure can slightly worsen routing or LUT packing.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r2s1 (regression, -7.79%): A two-entry IF skid buffer did not pay for CoreMark's random imem stalls; the added fetch-valid/control and redirect-flush machinery can cost more FPGA fitness than the front-end bubbles it hides.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r2s2 (regression, -44.37%): A tiny IF return-address stack can still badly hurt FPGA fitness when canonical-return detection, RAS top muxing, and predicted-PC metadata outweigh the CoreMark return bubbles it removes.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r3s0 (broken, n/a): Keep source-aware load-use interlocks separate from RVFI operand reporting changes; masking unused rs fields can break cosim's retire trace even when the intended stall reduction logic is local to hazard_unit.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r3s1 (regression, -14.30%): MEM-to-EX load bypass can lose FPGA fitness when dmem-ready gating, load extraction, and an extra EX operand forwarding select lengthen the critical path more than CoreMark benefits from removing one-cycle load-use bubbles.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r3s2 (regression, -20.54%): Even decode-stage JAL prediction can regress when the early_redirect priority path, target adder, and predicted-PC metadata still add enough IF/ID/EX control cost to outweigh saving CoreMark's unconditional-jump bubble.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r4s1 (broken, n/a): A registered IF/ID boundary must pair imem backpressure with an ID consume/valid handshake; holding a valid IF/ID entry while ID keeps advancing can duplicate instructions during CoreMark stalls.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r4s2 (regression, -9.29%): Even a resolved-target BTB can lose FPGA fitness when IF-side tag/valid/counter arrays, target muxing, and predicted-PC metadata lengthen the next-PC path more than CoreMark benefits from avoiding loop redirect bubbles.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r5s0 (improvement, +1.93%): Dropping reset clears from the integer register file can improve FPGA fitness because pipeline-valid reset and x0 read forcing preserve architectural behavior while avoiding reset fanout and per-register muxing in an always-active datapath block.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r5s1 (regression, -6.89%): A one-entry ordered store buffer can lose CoreMark fitness when store-order interlocks, MMIO/trap classification, and dmem muxing add more MEM/control-path cost than random store-stall overlap recovers.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r5s2 (regression, -61.05%): Even a single-entry loop predictor can badly regress when predicted-PC metadata, IF PC/tag compare muxing, and EX mismatch redirect logic lengthen critical control paths more than repeated CoreMark loop-branch bubble removal helps.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r6s0 (regression, -2.40%): Pruning unused EX/MEM branch_taken/branch_target metadata did not improve FPGA fitness, suggesting these dead-looking pipeline fields were not on the binding path or were already optimized well enough that layout perturbation outweighed the small flop reduction.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r6s1 (regression, -88.09%): Splitting EX with an EX1 operand register can destroy CoreMark fitness when it delays branch/JALR redirects and forces broad dependent-ALU interlocks, so operand-cone retiming must preserve bypass/redirect latency to pay off.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r6s2 (regression, -48.05%): A JAL-only single-entry target predictor still regressed without fetch-side immediate decode because the IF PC compare/target mux, predicted-PC pipeline fields, and EX mismatch redirect logic outweighed CoreMark's repeated unconditional-jump bubble savings.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r7s0 (regression, -53.28%): Replacing cold RV32M DIV/REM combinational operators with an iterative divider can severely regress CoreMark when the busy/start handshake, divider state, and 32-cycle latency perturb the hot pipeline more than removing the rarely used divide cone helps Fmax.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r7s1 (broken, n/a): Registering dmem backpressure needs a real ready/valid or skid boundary at MEM; a lone pending/wait bit can hide the first stalled cycle from upstream control and let younger CoreMark memory-state updates be dropped or reordered.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r8s0 (improvement, +2.79%): Sharing one sign/zero-extended M-unit multiplier across MULH signedness variants can improve FPGA fitness because CoreMark rarely needs M throughput and parallel 64-bit products add DSP/LUT routing pressure.\",\"truncated\":false,\"loaded\":[]},\"title\":\"cores/bench/LESSONS.md\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778697087451,\"end\":1778697087460}},\"metadata\":{\"openai\":{\"itemId\":\"fc_0e80768a67d4c345016a04c37f512c8199b5a9845bda9afc69\"}},\"id\":\"prt_e229ba9c60015I3k6DYBMgWPyw\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1dd647e0effeZs5rb4D9DoknQl\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e229b825c0012ZgpiPpsXJgC7J\"}}\n./bench/gpt-5_5_medium/rep2/agent.log:28:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778697087471,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1dd647e0effeZs5rb4D9DoknQl\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"grep\",\"callID\":\"call_UkJddlqThWBOOokYYa9xBQMW\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"pattern\":\"micro_opt|forward|hazard|critical|ALU|regfile|RVFI|shift|mux|Fmax\",\"path\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_5_medium-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\",\"include\":\"log.jsonl\"},\"output\":\"Found 31 matches\\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_5_medium-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:\\n  Line 1: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"baseline-bench-5327bba\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Baseline retest for cores/bench\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"improvement\\\", \\\"fitness\\\": 282.82, \\\"delta_pct\\\": 0.0, \\\"vs_baseline\\\": 0.0, \\\"fmax_mhz\\\": 127.03, \\\"ipc_coremark\\\": 2e-06, \\\"cycles\\\": 4491485, \\\"iterations\\\": 10, \\\"lut4\\\": 9563, \\\"ff\\\": 1866, \\\"seeds\\\": [128.72, 127.03, 123.02], \\\"formal_passed\\\": true, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": true, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"implementation_notes\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-05-13T13:08:01.185076Z\\\", \\\"round_id\\\": 0, \\\"slot\\\": 0}\\n\\n  Line 2: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r1s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Multicycle RV32M arithmetic unit\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current ALU computes every multiply and divide result combinationally in\\\\nthe EX stage, including signed and unsigned 64-bit products plus divider and\\\\nremainder operators. That wide arithmetic cone is a likely Fmax limiter on\\\\nTang Nano 20K, while CoreMark's hot path should not need single-cycle DIV/REM\\\\nthroughput. Trading rare M-extension latency for a shorter common EX critical\\\\npath should improve iterations/second through higher clock frequency.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current ALU computes every multiply and divide result combinationally in\\\\nthe EX stage, including signed and unsigned 64-bit products plus divider and\\\\nremainder operators. That wide arithmetic cone is a likely Fmax limiter on\\\\nTang Nano 20K, while CoreMark's hot path should not need single-cycle DIV/REM\\\\nthroughput. Trading rare M-extension latency for a shorter common EX critical\\\\npath should improve iterations/second through higher clock frequency.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Split RV32M operations out of the always-combinational ALU path behind a\\\\nsmall multicycle M-unit handshake in EX. Keep ADD/SUB/logic/branch/address\\\\noperations purely combinational, but when the decoded alu_op is MUL/DIV/REM,\\\\nhold ID/EX and EX/MEM until the iterative arithmetic result is ready, then\\\\nretire normally through the existing MEM/WB and RVFI path. This should remove\\\\ndivider and large multiplier logic from the ordinary EX-stage timing path;\\\\nthe added stalls only affect uncommon M instructions and should be outweighed\\\\nby improved Fmax on the FPGA fitness metric.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 6, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/alu.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Restrict the combinational ALU to fast RV32I operations and expose only lightweight M-op selection or helper logic needed by the new M-unit.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a multicycl...\\n  Line 3: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r1s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Resolve control flow in ID\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"CoreMark spends a significant fraction of its bracketed runtime in tight loops and conditional control flow. The current five-stage pipeline resolves branches and jumps in EX, then flushes both IF and ID, so every taken branch or jump pays roughly a two-cycle redirect penalty. Reducing that penalty improves iterations/cycle without increasing retirement width or touching the external NRET=1 contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"CoreMark spends a significant fraction of its bracketed runtime in tight loops and conditional control flow. The current five-stage pipeline resolves branches and jumps in EX, then flushes both IF and ID, so every taken branch or jump pays roughly a two-cycle redirect penalty. Reducing that penalty improves iterations/cycle without increasing retirement width or touching the external NRET=1 contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Move branch and JAL/JALR target/condition resolution from EX into the decode stage, with decode-stage operand forwarding from EX/MEM and MEM/WB for branch compares and JALR base operands. Keep the existing EX-stage trap/metadata path as the retirement source, but let ID drive redirect one cycle earlier when operands are available; stall only for unresolved load-to-branch/JALR dependencies. This should cut the common taken-control penalty from two bubbles to one while preserving RVFI order, trap reporting, and single-channel retirement.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 8, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Wire ID-stage early redirect signals into the IF and hazard path while preserving the existing NRET=1 RVFI port set.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add early branch/JAL/JALR resolution using decoded control, immediates, regfile operands, and forwarded compare/base operands.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/forward_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend forwarding ...\\n  Line 4: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r1s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Static BTFNT branch predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current five-stage core always fetches sequentially and resolves every branch or jump in EX, so each taken loop branch pays a two-instruction front-end flush. CoreMark has tight loops and many backward branches, making a small static predictor likely to improve iterations per cycle without adding much FPGA area or hurting the ALU critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current five-stage core always fetches sequentially and resolves every branch or jump in EX, so each taken loop branch pays a two-instruction front-end flush. CoreMark has tight loops and many backward branches, making a small static predictor likely to improve iterations per cycle without adding much FPGA area or hurting the ALU critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add an IF-stage static backward-taken/forward-not-taken predictor using the fetched instruction bits to compute B-type targets for negative branch immediates, and optionally early-predict JAL targets because they are unconditional PC-relative redirects. Carry the predicted next PC through IF/ID and ID/EX so EX redirects only when the resolved next PC differs from the prediction; mispredicted forward-taken branches, not-taken backward branches, and JALR still recover through the existing flush path. This should remove most loop-closing branch bubbles while preserving the single-retire RVFI contract because retirement remains unchanged at MEM/WB.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 8, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend IF/ID and ID/EX pipeline bundles with the predicted next PC needed for EX-stage mispredict detection.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Implement static BTFNT next-PC selection for backward conditional branches and early PC-relative JAL prediction from fetched instruction bits.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Lat...\\n  Line 5: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r2s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Simplify MEM load lane muxes\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"After the successful M-unit split, the remaining always-active timing paths are more likely to be ordinary RV32I datapath logic than rare multiply/divide arithmetic. The MEM stage currently uses variable shifts for load extraction and shifted masks for byte lanes, which can synthesize into wider mux/barrel-shifter logic on the FPGA load-to-WB path. CoreMark performs many byte, halfword, and word accesses, so a smaller MEM-stage cone should improve Fmax without changing CPI.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"After the successful M-unit split, the remaining always-active timing paths are more likely to be ordinary RV32I datapath logic than rare multiply/divide arithmetic. The MEM stage currently uses variable shifts for load extraction and shifted masks for byte lanes, which can synthesize into wider mux/barrel-shifter logic on the FPGA load-to-WB path. CoreMark performs many byte, halfword, and word accesses, so a smaller MEM-stage cone should improve Fmax without changing CPI.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Rewrite the MEM-stage byte-lane handling as explicit case muxes keyed by addr[1:0] instead of expressions like dmem_rdata >> (addr[1:0] * 8) and mask << addr[1:0]. Keep the same misalignment trap behavior, RVFI aligned mem_addr reporting, replicated store data convention, and MEM/WB retirement boundary. This should reduce LUT depth and routing on the common load-data path while preserving the existing five-stage control flow and single-retire NRET=1 contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace variable load-data shifts and shifted byte masks with explicit addr[1:0] case muxes for LB/LBU/LH/LHU/LW and store masks.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace variable load...\\n  Line 6: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r2s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Add fetch skid buffer\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current front end has no real IF/ID storage: the IF payload is combinational from the current PC and imem data, and an imem-not-ready cycle injects a bubble directly into decode. With the tournament cosim using roughly 22% random instruction stalls, CoreMark loses many cycles even when the back end is already stalled by load-use, dmem, or the multicycle M unit. A small fetch buffer is a structural way to recover iterations/cycle without revisiting the prior broken ID-stage branch-resolution path or adding fetch-path target adders that hurt Fmax.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current front end has no real IF/ID storage: the IF payload is combinational from the current PC and imem data, and an imem-not-ready cycle injects a bubble directly into decode. With the tournament cosim using roughly 22% random instruction stalls, CoreMark loses many cycles even when the back end is already stalled by load-use, dmem, or the multicycle M unit. A small fetch buffer is a structural way to recover iterations/cycle without revisiting the prior broken ID-stage branch-resolution path or adding fetch-path target adders that hurt Fmax.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Insert a one- or two-entry instruction skid buffer between IF and ID. The PC/imem side should continue fetching sequential instructions whenever the buffer has space, while the ID side consumes one buffered instruction only when ID/EX is ready. On redirect, flush the buffer and restart fetch at the EX-resolved target; on imem stalls, hold the outstanding fetch instead of emitting a synthetic NOP into decode. This should overlap random iStall cycles with existing back-end stalls and reduce front-end bubbles while preserving single-retire RVFI ordering and the existing EX-stage control-flow correctness.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 7, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"descr...\\n  Line 7: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r2s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Tiny return-address stack predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The prior static BTFNT predictor targeted loop branches but put branch immediate decode\\\\nand target adders directly on the IF PC-update path, regressing the CoreMark fitness\\\\ndespite saving some bubbles. CoreMark still has frequent function calls and returns,\\\\nand the current core pays the same EX-stage redirect penalty for every JALR return.\\\\nA tiny return predictor can attack those bubbles without adding branch-target arithmetic\\\\nto fetch.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The prior static BTFNT predictor targeted loop branches but put branch immediate decode\\\\nand target adders directly on the IF PC-update path, regressing the CoreMark fitness\\\\ndespite saving some bubbles. CoreMark still has frequent function calls and returns,\\\\nand the current core pays the same EX-stage redirect penalty for every JALR return.\\\\nA tiny return predictor can attack those bubbles without adding branch-target arithmetic\\\\nto fetch.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a small 4-entry return-address stack that predicts only canonical returns\\\\n(JALR x0, 0(x1/x5)) in IF. Push pc+4 for link-register calls when they resolve,\\\\npop/predict the stack top for returns, and carry the predicted next PC through the\\\\npipeline so EX redirects only on mismatch. This keeps ordinary branch fetch as pc+4,\\\\navoids B-type immediate decode and target adders in the IF critical path, and should\\\\nrecover part of the call/return control-flow penalty with modest LUT/FF cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add predicted-next-PC and return-predicted metadata to IF/ID, ID/EX, and EX/MEM bundles for mispredict detection and RAS bookkeeping.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Detect canonical return encodings in the fetched instruction and select the RAS top as the next PC only when the stack is nonempty....\\n  Line 8: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r3s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Source-aware load-use stalls\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current load-use hazard detector compares a load destination against the\\\\nraw rs1 and rs2 bitfields of the following instruction, regardless of whether\\\\nthat instruction actually consumes those operands. For I-type immediates,\\\\nloads, JALR, LUI, AUIPC, JAL, FENCE, and EBREAK, some of those fields are\\\\nimmediates or destination bits rather than true sources. Any accidental match\\\\nwith the preceding load rd inserts an unnecessary bubble, directly reducing\\\\nCoreMark iterations per cycle without improving correctness.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current load-use hazard detector compares a load destination against the\\\\nraw rs1 and rs2 bitfields of the following instruction, regardless of whether\\\\nthat instruction actually consumes those operands. For I-type immediates,\\\\nloads, JALR, LUI, AUIPC, JAL, FENCE, and EBREAK, some of those fields are\\\\nimmediates or destination bits rather than true sources. Any accidental match\\\\nwith the preceding load rd inserts an unnecessary bubble, directly reducing\\\\nCoreMark iterations per cycle without improving correctness.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Make the load-use interlock source-aware by qualifying the rd comparisons with\\\\nsimple decoded uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 signals for the instruction currently in\\\\nIF/ID. Keep the existing one-cycle stall for real load consumers, but avoid\\\\nstalling on unused source fields such as OP-IMM rs2 bits, U/J-type operand\\\\nfields, and SYSTEM/FENCE encodings. This should improve CPI under CoreMark's\\\\nload-heavy code while adding only small opcode decode logic off the main ALU\\\\nand memory datapaths, so Fmax should be largely preserved.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Derive or wire IF/ID source-use qualifiers into the hazard unit without changing the NRET=1 top-level RVFI interface.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl...\\n  Line 9: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r3s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Load-use MEM bypass\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The previous structural attempts spent LUTs and timing on front-end control-flow machinery, and the log shows those changes either broke correctness or regressed fitness. The current five-stage core still has the classic one-cycle load-use interlock even though the benchmark memory is zero-wait in FPGA and only randomly stalled in cosim. CoreMark has frequent dependent loads through pointer-heavy list, matrix, and state code, so removing this bubble can improve iterations per cycle without adding predictor target adders to the fetch critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The previous structural attempts spent LUTs and timing on front-end control-flow machinery, and the log shows those changes either broke correctness or regressed fitness. The current five-stage core still has the classic one-cycle load-use interlock even though the benchmark memory is zero-wait in FPGA and only randomly stalled in cosim. CoreMark has frequent dependent loads through pointer-heavy list, matrix, and state code, so removing this bubble can improve iterations per cycle without adding predictor target adders to the fetch critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a MEM-to-EX load-data bypass path that lets an instruction immediately behind a load proceed into EX when the load is in MEM and dmem is ready. Compute the existing load extraction result combinationally in MEM, expose it to the forwarding network, and add a forwarding select for EX operands that chooses the current MEM load result over the older EX/MEM ALU address. Keep the load-use stall only when the load will not have valid data that cycle, such as a dmem stall or a misaligned/trapping load. This structural bypass should remove many single-cycle bubbles while preserving the NRET=1 retirement boundary and RVFI order at MEM/WB.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 6, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\"...\\n  Line 10: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r3s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Decode-stage JAL predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The prior predictor attempts regressed because they placed branch target decode,\\\\ntarget adders, return detection, and prediction muxes directly on the IF PC path.\\\\nCoreMark still pays the existing EX redirect penalty for every unconditional JAL,\\\\nand JAL targets are PC-relative with no register dependency or branch condition.\\\\nPredicting only JAL in decode should recover one control-flow bubble for calls and\\\\nlong jumps while avoiding the risky fetch-path predictor logic that hurt Fmax.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The prior predictor attempts regressed because they placed branch target decode,\\\\ntarget adders, return detection, and prediction muxes directly on the IF PC path.\\\\nCoreMark still pays the existing EX redirect penalty for every unconditional JAL,\\\\nand JAL targets are PC-relative with no register dependency or branch condition.\\\\nPredicting only JAL in decode should recover one control-flow bubble for calls and\\\\nlong jumps while avoiding the risky fetch-path predictor logic that hurt Fmax.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a narrow decode-stage always-taken predictor for legal JAL instructions only.\\\\nID computes pc+imm for JAL, redirects fetch one cycle before EX would, and records\\\\nthe predicted next PC in the ID/EX bundle. EX then suppresses the normal JAL redirect\\\\nwhen the resolved jump target matches the predicted target, but retains the existing\\\\nEX recovery path for conditional branches, JALR, illegal instructions, and any target\\\\nmismatch. Because the predictor excludes branches and returns, it needs no B-type\\\\nimmediate decode in IF, no RAS state, and no operand forwarding, making it a smaller\\\\npredictor angle with a better chance of improving CoreMark iterations/cycle without\\\\nsacrificing the current high Fmax.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add p...\\n  Line 11: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r4s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Source-qualified load interlock\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current load-use detector compares the preceding load rd against both raw\\\\nsource bitfields of the following instruction, even when those bits are not\\\\narchitecturally consumed. CoreMark has many loads and immediate/control\\\\ninstructions, so accidental rd matches can inject bubbles that reduce\\\\niterations per cycle without protecting any real dependency.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current load-use detector compares the preceding load rd against both raw\\\\nsource bitfields of the following instruction, even when those bits are not\\\\narchitecturally consumed. CoreMark has many loads and immediate/control\\\\ninstructions, so accidental rd matches can inject bubbles that reduce\\\\niterations per cycle without protecting any real dependency.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a tiny source-use decode for the IF/ID instruction and qualify the existing\\\\nload-use interlock with uses_rs1 and uses_rs2. This is a narrower retry of the\\\\nearlier source-aware load-use idea: do not mask or reinterpret RVFI operand\\\\nreporting, and do not add MEM bypass selects or fetch prediction paths. The\\\\ncore should keep the one-cycle stall for true load consumers while avoiding\\\\nfalse stalls on OP-IMM rs2 fields, U/J-type immediates, FENCE, SYSTEM/EBREAK,\\\\nand other instructions that do not read both source registers.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Derive simple IF/ID uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 qualifiers from opcode/funct class and pass them only to the hazard unit.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Gate load-use rd comparisons with the source-use qualifiers while preserving existing dmem, imem, redirect, and M-unit stall priority.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Derive simple IF/ID use...\\n  Line 12: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r4s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Register IF/ID fetch boundary\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current front end feeds imem_data directly through decode, immediate\\\\ngeneration, regfile address decode, hazard checks, and into the ID/EX\\\\nregister in a single cycle. Prior structural attempts tried to hide stalls or\\\\nreduce bubbles with added prediction and bypass control, but those changes\\\\nregressed by lengthening already sensitive mux/control paths. A real IF/ID\\\\nregister is a simpler structural retiming that targets Fmax directly.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current front end feeds imem_data directly through decode, immediate\\\\ngeneration, regfile address decode, hazard checks, and into the ID/EX\\\\nregister in a single cycle. Prior structural attempts tried to hide stalls or\\\\nreduce bubbles with added prediction and bypass control, but those changes\\\\nregressed by lengthening already sensitive mux/control paths. A real IF/ID\\\\nregister is a simpler structural retiming that targets Fmax directly.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Insert a proper IF/ID pipeline register inside the fetch stage, so fetched\\\\ninstruction, PC, and valid are captured before decode sees them. The PC still\\\\nadvances only when the downstream ID stage can accept the entry, and redirect\\\\nflushes the registered IF/ID payload before wrong-path state can retire. This\\\\nadds one front-end cycle and may slightly increase taken-control penalty, but\\\\nit removes the imem-to-decoder combinational path and should improve FPGA\\\\nFmax enough to raise CoreMark iter/sec without changing the NRET=1 retirement\\\\ncontract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Keep the existing IF/ID bundle shape, but document and use it as a registered pipeline boundary rather than a combinational fetch payload.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add an IF/ID register for pc, instruction, and valid; hold it on deco...\\n  Line 13: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r4s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Tiny resolved-target BTB\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current core resolves all taken branches and jumps in EX, so tight CoreMark loops pay a two-bubble redirect penalty. Prior predictor attempts regressed because they decoded immediates, computed targets, or managed return-stack state on the front-end critical path. A small BTB trained from already-resolved EX redirects can target the same loop bubbles while avoiding fetch-stage target arithmetic.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current core resolves all taken branches and jumps in EX, so tight CoreMark loops pay a two-bubble redirect penalty. Prior predictor attempts regressed because they decoded immediates, computed targets, or managed return-stack state on the front-end critical path. A small BTB trained from already-resolved EX redirects can target the same loop bubbles while avoiding fetch-stage target arithmetic.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a tiny direct-mapped branch target buffer that stores resolved taken conditional-branch targets, preferably only backward loop branches, along with a compact PC tag and a 2-bit confidence counter. IF predicts to the stored target on a tag hit with a taken counter, otherwise it keeps the existing pc+4 path; EX updates the BTB after branch resolution and flushes on any predicted/resolved next-PC mismatch. Because the target is stored from the prior execution, the fetch path adds only a small tag compare and next-PC mux instead of B/J immediate decode and target adders, which should recover loop-closing branch bubbles with less Fmax damage than the previous predictor attempts.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Carry predicted next-PC metadata through IF/ID and ID/EX so EX can distinguish correct BTB predictions from mispredictions without changing the NRET=1 RVFI interface.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Imple...\\n  Line 14: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r5s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Drop regfile reset fanout\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current register file resets all 32 architectural registers with a for-loop,\\\\nwhich forces reset muxing or reset routing onto the whole small RF array. After\\\\nthe M-unit split, remaining FPGA Fmax is likely limited by ordinary datapath\\\\nand control fanout rather than rare arithmetic. CoreMark initializes the state\\\\nit uses, x0 is already hardwired on reads, and RVFI correctness depends on\\\\nretired architectural behavior rather than power-on values of unused registers.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current register file resets all 32 architectural registers with a for-loop,\\\\nwhich forces reset muxing or reset routing onto the whole small RF array. After\\\\nthe M-unit split, remaining FPGA Fmax is likely limited by ordinary datapath\\\\nand control fanout rather than rare arithmetic. CoreMark initializes the state\\\\nit uses, x0 is already hardwired on reads, and RVFI correctness depends on\\\\nretired architectural behavior rather than power-on values of unused registers.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Remove the explicit reset clear from the integer register file and keep only\\\\nthe synchronous write path for nonzero destination registers. Reads of x0 stay\\\\nforced to zero, same-cycle write-first bypass stays unchanged, and reset of the\\\\npipeline control/valid bits still prevents spurious retirement after reset.\\\\nThis should reduce reset fanout and per-register muxing in a timing-sensitive,\\\\nalways-active block without changing CPI, branch behavior, memory behavior, or\\\\nthe NRET=1 RVFI interface. The change is deliberately narrower than prior\\\\nfailed micro-opts: it does not touch hazard detection, RVFI operand reporting,\\\\nMEM load extraction, or any front-end prediction path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/reg_file.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove the reset-time loop that clears all 32 registers while preser...\\n  Line 15: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r5s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Ordered one-entry store buffer\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent structural attempts spent timing and LUTs on the front end or on load\\\\nbypass paths, and those changes either regressed fitness or broke stall\\\\nhandling. The current core still freezes the entire pipeline whenever a store\\\\nreaches MEM and dmem_ready is low, even though independent ALU, branch, and\\\\nregister-only instructions behind that store could execute while the store is\\\\nwaiting. CoreMark has frequent data stores and the apples-to-apples cosim\\\\ninjects random dmem stalls, so store backpressure is a promising structural\\\\ntarget that avoids fetch-path target adders and load-result critical muxes.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent structural attempts spent timing and LUTs on the front end or on load\\\\nbypass paths, and those changes either regressed fitness or broke stall\\\\nhandling. The current core still freezes the entire pipeline whenever a store\\\\nreaches MEM and dmem_ready is low, even though independent ALU, branch, and\\\\nregister-only instructions behind that store could execute while the store is\\\\nwaiting. CoreMark has frequent data stores and the apples-to-apples cosim\\\\ninjects random dmem stalls, so store backpressure is a promising structural\\\\ntarget that avoids fetch-path target adders and load-result critical muxes.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a single-entry ordered store buffer at the MEM boundary. A regular data\\\\nstore can retire into the buffer with its address, byte mask, write data, and\\\\nRVFI metadata captured, allowing the front of the pipeline to keep executing\\\\nnon-memory instructions while the buffer drives dmem until dmem_ready accepts\\\\nthe write. Preserve precise in-order behavior by stalling any younger load,\\\\nstore, MMIO access, FENCE, EBREAK, or illegal/trapping instruction until the\\\\nbuffer drains; MMIO marker/UART stores should bypass the buffer and keep the\\\\nexisting blocking behavior so CoreMark timing brackets re...\\n  Line 16: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r5s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Single-entry loop target predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current core fetches sequentially and pays the existing EX-stage redirect\\\\npenalty on every taken loop-closing branch. Earlier predictor attempts\\\\nregressed because they placed immediate decode, target adders, BTB arrays,\\\\ncounters, or RAS control on the fetch path. A predictor for this slot should\\\\ntherefore be much narrower: reuse targets already resolved in EX and add the\\\\nsmallest possible fetch-side structure.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current core fetches sequentially and pays the existing EX-stage redirect\\\\npenalty on every taken loop-closing branch. Earlier predictor attempts\\\\nregressed because they placed immediate decode, target adders, BTB arrays,\\\\ncounters, or RAS control on the fetch path. A predictor for this slot should\\\\ntherefore be much narrower: reuse targets already resolved in EX and add the\\\\nsmallest possible fetch-side structure.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a single-entry resolved-loop target predictor trained only by taken\\\\nbackward conditional branches. The entry stores one branch PC and its resolved\\\\ntarget; IF predicts taken only when the current PC equals that stored PC,\\\\notherwise it keeps the existing pc+4 behavior. Carry predicted-taken and\\\\npredicted-next-PC metadata to EX so the normal redirect path fires only on a\\\\nmismatch, and invalidate the entry when that same branch resolves not-taken\\\\nat loop exit. This should recover repeated inner-loop branch bubbles in\\\\nCoreMark while avoiding the fetch target adders, multi-entry tag arrays,\\\\nconfidence counters, and return-stack machinery that hurt prior predictors.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add minimal predicted-taken and predicted-next-PC metadata to IF/ID and ID/EX bundles for EX-stage mismatch detection.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Implement ...\\n  Line 17: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r6s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Prune dead EX/MEM branch metadata\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core has already won most of its fitness from removing broad\\\\ntiming/fanout costs, while recent attempts that add prediction, buffering,\\\\nbypassing, or memory-ordering control have regressed. The EX/MEM bundle still\\\\ncarries branch_taken and branch_target fields that are assigned every cycle\\\\nbut are not consumed by MEM, WB, RVFI, hazard, or forwarding logic. Those 33\\\\ndead flops and their input adders/mux routes sit in an always-active pipeline\\\\nboundary, so pruning them is a low-risk micro-optimization for LUT/FF count\\\\nand routing delay without changing CPI.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core has already won most of its fitness from removing broad\\\\ntiming/fanout costs, while recent attempts that add prediction, buffering,\\\\nbypassing, or memory-ordering control have regressed. The EX/MEM bundle still\\\\ncarries branch_taken and branch_target fields that are assigned every cycle\\\\nbut are not consumed by MEM, WB, RVFI, hazard, or forwarding logic. Those 33\\\\ndead flops and their input adders/mux routes sit in an always-active pipeline\\\\nboundary, so pruning them is a low-risk micro-optimization for LUT/FF count\\\\nand routing delay without changing CPI.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Remove the unused branch_taken and branch_target fields from ex_mem_t and stop\\\\nregistering them in ex_stage. Keep redirect and pc_next computation exactly as\\\\nthey are today, because pc_next is the RVFI-visible resolved next PC and the\\\\nredirect output is still the only live control-flow signal. This should trim\\\\ndead sequential state and reduce EX-stage fanout/routing pressure while\\\\npreserving the NRET=1 RVFI contract, trap behavior, memory behavior, and all\\\\nexisting stall/flush priorities.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 1, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove unused branch_taken and...\\n  Line 18: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r6s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Pipeline EX operand retiming\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent structural attempts that added fetch prediction, skid buffering, load bypassing,\\\\nor store buffering all paid too much control-path cost. The current core's remaining\\\\nhigh-fanout structural path is more basic: ID/EX values pass through forwarding muxes,\\\\nALU operand selection, ALU or branch compare/target logic, and into EX/MEM plus the\\\\nredirect path in one cycle. Retiming that boundary can target Fmax without adding a\\\\npredictor, memory ordering structure, or RVFI contract change.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent structural attempts that added fetch prediction, skid buffering, load bypassing,\\\\nor store buffering all paid too much control-path cost. The current core's remaining\\\\nhigh-fanout structural path is more basic: ID/EX values pass through forwarding muxes,\\\\nALU operand selection, ALU or branch compare/target logic, and into EX/MEM plus the\\\\nredirect path in one cycle. Retiming that boundary can target Fmax without adding a\\\\npredictor, memory ordering structure, or RVFI contract change.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Split the existing EX stage into a lightweight EX1 operand/metadata register and an EX2\\\\nexecute/redirect stage. EX1 captures the forwarded rs1/rs2 values, selected ALU operands,\\\\ndecoded control, immediate, PC, and RVFI source data; EX2 performs the ALU operation,\\\\nbranch/jump resolution, M-unit handshake, and drives the existing EX/MEM payload. Add the\\\\nminimal hazard/forwarding adjustments needed for the extra execute stage, preferring a\\\\none-cycle interlock on immediately dependent ALU consumers over wide new bypass muxes if\\\\nthat keeps timing short. This structural retiming should shorten the common forwarding-to-\\\\nALU-to-EX/MEM cone and may raise Tang Nano 20K Fmax enough to offset a small CPI cost,\\\\nwhile preserving NRET=1 retirement at MEM/WB and all RVFI ordering/trap behavior.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\"...\\n  Line 19: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r6s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Single-entry JAL target predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts regressed because they put immediate decode, target\\\\nadders, multi-entry tags, counters, or return-stack control on the fetch path.\\\\nCoreMark still pays the EX redirect penalty for repeated unconditional calls\\\\nand jumps, but JAL has no direction ambiguity once a target is known. A very\\\\nsmall learned JAL target entry can attack only that control-flow cost while\\\\navoiding conditional branch and return prediction complexity.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts regressed because they put immediate decode, target\\\\nadders, multi-entry tags, counters, or return-stack control on the fetch path.\\\\nCoreMark still pays the EX redirect penalty for repeated unconditional calls\\\\nand jumps, but JAL has no direction ambiguity once a target is known. A very\\\\nsmall learned JAL target entry can attack only that control-flow cost while\\\\navoiding conditional branch and return prediction complexity.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a single-entry predictor trained only by legal resolved JAL instructions\\\\nin EX. The entry stores the exact JAL PC and resolved target; IF predicts to\\\\nthe stored target only when the current PC equals that entry, otherwise it\\\\nkeeps the existing pc+4 path. Carry only predicted-valid and predicted-next-PC\\\\nmetadata to EX so the normal redirect path is suppressed on an exact correct\\\\nprediction and used for all misses or mismatches. This should recover repeated\\\\ncall/jump bubbles in CoreMark without fetch-side immediate decode, B-type\\\\nbranch prediction, return-stack state, confidence counters, or multi-entry BTB\\\\nrouting that hurt previous predictor hypotheses.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add minimal predicted-valid and predicted-next-PC fields to IF/ID and ID/EX bundles for EX-stage JAL prediction validation....\\n  Line 20: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r7s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Iterative M divider\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The largest proven fitness gain for this core came from moving RV32M arithmetic\\\\nout of the ordinary EX ALU path, but the current m_unit still contains wide\\\\ncombinational DIV/REM operators behind a one-cycle result register. Even if\\\\nthose operations are rare in CoreMark, the FPGA still has to place and time\\\\nthe divider/remainder cone, so it can cap Fmax or perturb routing for the hot\\\\nRV32I pipeline.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The largest proven fitness gain for this core came from moving RV32M arithmetic\\\\nout of the ordinary EX ALU path, but the current m_unit still contains wide\\\\ncombinational DIV/REM operators behind a one-cycle result register. Even if\\\\nthose operations are rare in CoreMark, the FPGA still has to place and time\\\\nthe divider/remainder cone, so it can cap Fmax or perturb routing for the hot\\\\nRV32I pipeline.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the one-cycle DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU implementation in m_unit with a small\\\\niterative restoring divider that runs only after a divide or remainder starts.\\\\nKeep MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU on the existing short registered path so any\\\\nmultiply use does not pay a 32-cycle penalty, and keep the existing EX-stage\\\\nhold/valid handshake as the only pipeline interface. This should remove the\\\\nexpensive combinational divider/remainder logic from the FPGA timing closure\\\\nproblem while adding CPI only for uncommon divide/remainder instructions.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/m_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Implement DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU with an iterative signed/unsigned divider state machine while preserving ALTOPS behavior under RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Adjust the existing M-unit busy/valid handshake only as needed so EX holds until multi-cycle DIV/REM completion and still clears completed results exactly once.\\\"}]}, \\\"expec...\\n  Line 21: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r7s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Register dmem stall boundary\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent structural attempts added front-end prediction, fetch buffering, load\\\\nbypassing, store buffering, or a deeper EX pipe, and each paid too much in\\\\nmux/control cost or correctness complexity. A smaller structural target is\\\\nthe current dmem stall path: io_dmemReady fans combinationally through the\\\\nhazard unit into PC hold, ID hold, EX/MEM hold, and MEM/WB valid clearing in\\\\nthe same cycle. CoreMark's apples-to-apples mode injects random dmem stalls,\\\\nso this control path is active during evaluation, and reducing its fanout may\\\\nimprove Fmax without changing the visible memory ordering contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent structural attempts added front-end prediction, fetch buffering, load\\\\nbypassing, store buffering, or a deeper EX pipe, and each paid too much in\\\\nmux/control cost or correctness complexity. A smaller structural target is\\\\nthe current dmem stall path: io_dmemReady fans combinationally through the\\\\nhazard unit into PC hold, ID hold, EX/MEM hold, and MEM/WB valid clearing in\\\\nthe same cycle. CoreMark's apples-to-apples mode injects random dmem stalls,\\\\nso this control path is active during evaluation, and reducing its fanout may\\\\nimprove Fmax without changing the visible memory ordering contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a one-cycle registered MEM busy/accepted boundary for data-memory\\\\noperations. When a load or store enters MEM, capture whether it is waiting for\\\\ndmem_ready in a small state bit local to the MEM/hazard interface; while that\\\\nbit is set, hold EX/MEM and clear MEM/WB valid exactly as today, but drive the\\\\nglobal pipeline stalls from the registered busy state rather than directly\\\\nfrom io_dmemReady. Keep dmem_addr, dmem_wdata, dmem_wen, dmem_ren, RVFI memory\\\\nmasks/data, misalignment traps, and all in-order retirement behavior\\\\nunchanged. This deliberately accepts at most one extra cycle on the first\\\\nstal...\\n  Line 22: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r7s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Decode-only JAL redirect predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts for this core regressed because they added target\\\\nadders, tag compares, return-stack state, or predicted-PC muxing directly to\\\\nthe IF next-PC path. The current core still pays the EX redirect penalty for\\\\nevery unconditional JAL, and JAL needs no operand forwarding or direction\\\\nprediction once its instruction reaches decode.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts for this core regressed because they added target\\\\nadders, tag compares, return-stack state, or predicted-PC muxing directly to\\\\nthe IF next-PC path. The current core still pays the EX redirect penalty for\\\\nevery unconditional JAL, and JAL needs no operand forwarding or direction\\\\nprediction once its instruction reaches decode.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a decode-stage predictor for legal JAL instructions only. ID already has\\\\nthe instruction, PC, and immediate generator output, so it can compute the JAL\\\\ntarget and redirect fetch one cycle earlier than EX without adding any logic\\\\nto the IF PC increment path. Carry a small predicted-next-PC flag/value into\\\\nID/EX so EX suppresses its normal JAL redirect when the ID prediction matches,\\\\nwhile all conditional branches, JALR, illegal instructions, and mismatches use\\\\nthe existing EX recovery path. This avoids the failed fetch-side predictor\\\\nstructures while recovering one bubble on CoreMark calls and PC-relative jumps.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add minimal predicted-valid and predicted-next-PC metadata to the IF/ID and ID/EX bundles for decode-stage JAL prediction validation.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Detect legal JAL in decode, compute pc plus J-immediate, emit an early redirect, and latch the prediction metadata into ID/EX.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Wire the ID...\\n  Line 23: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r8s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Share M-unit multiplier hardware\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core already removed RV32M arithmetic from the ordinary EX\\\\nALU path, but m_unit still builds three separate 64-bit multiplier products\\\\nin parallel for MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU signedness variants. CoreMark uses\\\\nM operations rarely enough that throughput beyond the existing one-cycle\\\\nregistered M-unit is not the main limiter, while the extra DSP/LUT/routing\\\\npressure can still depress FPGA Fmax or perturb placement of the hot RV32I\\\\npipeline.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core already removed RV32M arithmetic from the ordinary EX\\\\nALU path, but m_unit still builds three separate 64-bit multiplier products\\\\nin parallel for MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU signedness variants. CoreMark uses\\\\nM operations rarely enough that throughput beyond the existing one-cycle\\\\nregistered M-unit is not the main limiter, while the extra DSP/LUT/routing\\\\npressure can still depress FPGA Fmax or perturb placement of the hot RV32I\\\\npipeline.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the parallel signed-signed, unsigned-unsigned, and signed-unsigned\\\\nproduct generation in m_unit with one shared signed multiplier fed by\\\\nsign/zero-extended operands selected from the M opcode. MUL can take the low\\\\nhalf of the same product because low 32 bits are independent of operand\\\\nsignedness; MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU select the high half with the appropriate\\\\noperand extension. Keep the existing start/valid/clear handshake, one-cycle\\\\nM latency, DIV/REM behavior, and RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions unchanged.\\\\nThis should trim multiplier area and routing without adding CPI, front-end\\\\nprediction logic, memory ordering state, or any RVFI-visible behavior change.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/m_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Use a single sign-controlled widened multiplier for MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU while pres...\\n  Line 24: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r8s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Register WB result at MEM boundary\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The recent structural attempts mostly lost fitness by adding control-flow\\\\nprediction, buffering, memory overlap, or deeper execute-stage interlocks.\\\\nA safer structural target is the existing MEM/WB-to-WB combinational path:\\\\nthe selected writeback value is still muxed after the MEM/WB register, then\\\\nfans out to the regfile write port, same-cycle regfile bypass, EX forwarding,\\\\nand RVFI rd_wdata. Registering that selected value at the retirement boundary\\\\nshould shorten a hot fanout path without changing CPI, branch timing, memory\\\\nordering, or the NRET=1 external contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The recent structural attempts mostly lost fitness by adding control-flow\\\\nprediction, buffering, memory overlap, or deeper execute-stage interlocks.\\\\nA safer structural target is the existing MEM/WB-to-WB combinational path:\\\\nthe selected writeback value is still muxed after the MEM/WB register, then\\\\nfans out to the regfile write port, same-cycle regfile bypass, EX forwarding,\\\\nand RVFI rd_wdata. Registering that selected value at the retirement boundary\\\\nshould shorten a hot fanout path without changing CPI, branch timing, memory\\\\nordering, or the NRET=1 external contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a preselected rd_wdata/writeback-result field to the MEM/WB pipeline\\\\nbundle and compute it inside mem_stage before the MEM/WB register captures.\\\\nFor loads this field captures the sign/zero-extended load result; for ALU,\\\\nJAL, JALR, AUIPC, LUI, and M-extension operations it captures the existing\\\\nEX/MEM alu_result. Then simplify wb_stage into a pass-through of the\\\\nregistered writeback value instead of a post-register mem_to_reg mux. This\\\\nstructural retiming preserves the same retirement boundary and RVFI data, but\\\\nremoves the load-vs-ALU mux from the next cycle's forwarding/regfile/RVFI\\\\nfanout cone, which may improve Tang Nano 20K Fmax with ess...\\n  Line 25: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r8s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Decode-stage backward branch predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts lost fitness when they added BTB, RAS, tag compare,\\\\ncounter, or target-adder logic to the IF next-PC path. The current core still\\\\npays a two-bubble EX redirect penalty on taken loop-closing branches, which\\\\nare the predictor opportunity most likely to matter for CoreMark. A predictor\\\\nthat waits until decode can use the already-fetched instruction and decoded\\\\nbranch control while keeping fetch as the current simple pc+4 path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts lost fitness when they added BTB, RAS, tag compare,\\\\ncounter, or target-adder logic to the IF next-PC path. The current core still\\\\npays a two-bubble EX redirect penalty on taken loop-closing branches, which\\\\nare the predictor opportunity most likely to matter for CoreMark. A predictor\\\\nthat waits until decode can use the already-fetched instruction and decoded\\\\nbranch control while keeping fetch as the current simple pc+4 path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a static decode-stage predictor for legal backward conditional branches\\\\nonly. ID uses the existing immediate generator and decoded is_branch signal to\\\\nrecognize negative B-type offsets, computes pc+imm, and redirects fetch one\\\\ncycle earlier than EX would. Carry predicted-valid and predicted-next-PC into\\\\nID/EX so EX suppresses the normal redirect when the branch is actually taken\\\\nto the predicted target, or redirects to pc+4 on the loop-exit not-taken\\\\nmisprediction. Forward branches, JAL, JALR, illegal encodings, and all memory\\\\nor RVFI behavior stay on the existing path. This targets loop bubbles without\\\\nreintroducing the failed IF-side predictor structures.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add minimal predicted-valid and predicted-next-PC fields to the ID/EX bundle for EX-stage branch predict...\\n  Line 26: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r9s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Narrow MEM/WB control state\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent micro-optimization wins came from removing always-present FPGA timing\\\\nand routing pressure, while attempts that added prediction, bypassing, or\\\\nstall-hiding control usually regressed. The current MEM/WB register still\\\\ncarries the full decode control bundle even though the writeback and RVFI\\\\nretirement logic only consume a few bits after the MEM stage. Pruning that\\\\ncold control state should reduce flop count, muxing, and routing without\\\\nchanging CoreMark CPI or touching the external NRET=1 contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent micro-optimization wins came from removing always-present FPGA timing\\\\nand routing pressure, while attempts that added prediction, bypassing, or\\\\nstall-hiding control usually regressed. The current MEM/WB register still\\\\ncarries the full decode control bundle even though the writeback and RVFI\\\\nretirement logic only consume a few bits after the MEM stage. Pruning that\\\\ncold control state should reduce flop count, muxing, and routing without\\\\nchanging CoreMark CPI or touching the external NRET=1 contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the full ctrl_t field in mem_wb_t with a narrow retirement/writeback\\\\ncontrol subset containing only reg_write, mem_to_reg, and is_illegal. Keep\\\\nthe existing full ctrl_t through ID/EX and EX/MEM, because the execute and\\\\nmemory stages still need branch, memory width, sign-extension, and M-op\\\\ncontrols. In mem_stage, collapse ctrl_with_trap into the narrow MEM/WB\\\\nfields after misalignment handling, and update wb_stage and core RVFI trap\\\\nreferences to use the narrow fields. This should trim unused MEM/WB control\\\\nflops and fanout while preserving all memory masks/data, load results,\\\\nwriteback behavior, RVFI order, and trap reporting.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Introduce a narrow M...\\n  Line 27: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r9s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Split RVFI shadow metadata from datapath\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The best recent gains came from removing broad arithmetic and register fanout from hot paths, while most structural attempts regressed by adding prediction, buffering, memory overlap, or extra execute-stage interlocks. The current pipeline still carries a wide all-purpose struct through EX/MEM and MEM/WB, mixing hot execution data with RVFI-only metadata such as original source values, source register numbers, instruction bits, memory masks, and retired PC fields. Even when those fields are not part of the architectural datapath, they share the same pipeline registers, reset/hold enables, and module boundaries as timing-critical forwarding, memory, and writeback signals.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The best recent gains came from removing broad arithmetic and register fanout from hot paths, while most structural attempts regressed by adding prediction, buffering, memory overlap, or extra execute-stage interlocks. The current pipeline still carries a wide all-purpose struct through EX/MEM and MEM/WB, mixing hot execution data with RVFI-only metadata such as original source values, source register numbers, instruction bits, memory masks, and retired PC fields. Even when those fields are not part of the architectural datapath, they share the same pipeline registers, reset/hold enables, and module boundaries as timing-critical forwarding, memory, and writeback signals.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Split the pipeline state into a narrow execution payload and a parallel RVFI shadow payload. Keep the hot EX/MEM and MEM/WB datapath bundles limited to control bits and values needed for forwarding, dmem, writeback, and stalls, while carrying RVFI-only fields in a separate shadow register chain that uses the same valid, flush, hold, trap, and redirect decisions. This should reduce register width, enable fanout, and routing pressure on the timing-critical da...\\n  Line 28: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r9s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Decode return target predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts for this core mostly regressed because they added\\\\ntarget adders, tag compares, counters, return-stack state, or predicted-PC\\\\nmuxes directly to the IF next-PC path. The current core still pays the EX\\\\nredirect penalty on every canonical function return, and CoreMark contains\\\\nenough call/return traffic that recovering one redirect cycle there may help\\\\niterations per cycle. A return predictor that operates only after decode can\\\\nuse the already-fetched instruction, regfile read data, and existing immediate\\\\npath while keeping fetch's common pc+4 path untouched.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts for this core mostly regressed because they added\\\\ntarget adders, tag compares, counters, return-stack state, or predicted-PC\\\\nmuxes directly to the IF next-PC path. The current core still pays the EX\\\\nredirect penalty on every canonical function return, and CoreMark contains\\\\nenough call/return traffic that recovering one redirect cycle there may help\\\\niterations per cycle. A return predictor that operates only after decode can\\\\nuse the already-fetched instruction, regfile read data, and existing immediate\\\\npath while keeping fetch's common pc+4 path untouched.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a decode-stage predictor for canonical returns only: legal JALR with\\\\nrd=x0, funct3=0, imm=0, and rs1=x1 or x5. ID computes the return target from\\\\nthe already-read link register value and redirects fetch one cycle before EX\\\\nwould, but only when the base register is not the destination of an unresolved\\\\nolder instruction in ID/EX or EX/MEM. Carry a minimal predicted-valid and\\\\npredicted-next-PC field into ID/EX so EX suppresses the normal JALR redirect\\\\non an exact match and uses the existing recovery redirect on mismatches. This\\\\navoids the failed IF-side RAS/BTB/BTFNT machinery while targeting a narrower\\\\ncontrol-flow pattern wit...\\n  Line 29: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r10s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Prune unused LUI control bit\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent wins came from removing always-present arithmetic, reset, and RVFI\\\\nrouting pressure, while broader CPI-oriented changes and control prediction\\\\nusually regressed. The current control bundle still carries an is_lui bit\\\\nthrough decode and every pipeline stage even though LUI execution is already\\\\nfully encoded by alu_op=ALU_LUI and alu_src=1. That unused bit adds one more\\\\ndecoded signal, pipeline flop, reset/hold mux input, and routing load across\\\\nhot stage boundaries without contributing to architectural behavior.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent wins came from removing always-present arithmetic, reset, and RVFI\\\\nrouting pressure, while broader CPI-oriented changes and control prediction\\\\nusually regressed. The current control bundle still carries an is_lui bit\\\\nthrough decode and every pipeline stage even though LUI execution is already\\\\nfully encoded by alu_op=ALU_LUI and alu_src=1. That unused bit adds one more\\\\ndecoded signal, pipeline flop, reset/hold mux input, and routing load across\\\\nhot stage boundaries without contributing to architectural behavior.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Remove the redundant is_lui signal from the decoder interface and ctrl_t,\\\\nand delete the corresponding ID-stage temporary and bundle assignment. Keep\\\\nLUI behavior unchanged by continuing to set alu_op=ALU_LUI, alu_src=1, and\\\\nreg_write=1 in the existing validated LUI decode arm. This is a narrow\\\\nno-CPI micro-optimization that should slightly reduce control-bundle width,\\\\nregister enables, reset/hold muxing, and decode-to-EX routing pressure while\\\\npreserving the NRET=1 RVFI contract, trap discipline, memory behavior, and\\\\nall existing stall/forwarding logic.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 1, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove the unused is_lui field from ctrl_t so it no longer propagates throug...\\n  Line 30: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r10s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Split MEM RVFI sideband path\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The latest successful structural change split RVFI-only metadata away from the hot execution payloads and improved fitness without changing CPI. The remaining MEM stage still computes and registers RVFI memory sideband fields in the same combinational block and pipeline boundary that drives dmem, load extraction, writeback, and forwarding. A narrower structural separation in MEM is a safer follow-on than adding predictors, store buffers, load bypasses, or deeper execute retiming, all of which have regressed or broken previously.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The latest successful structural change split RVFI-only metadata away from the hot execution payloads and improved fitness without changing CPI. The remaining MEM stage still computes and registers RVFI memory sideband fields in the same combinational block and pipeline boundary that drives dmem, load extraction, writeback, and forwarding. A narrower structural separation in MEM is a safer follow-on than adding predictors, store buffers, load bypasses, or deeper execute retiming, all of which have regressed or broken previously.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Split the MEM-stage memory sideband work into a parallel RVFI-only path while keeping the execution path limited to dmem request signals, load_data, rd, compact control, and valid. The hot MEM/WB execution register should no longer share bundle assignment, hold, and mux fanout with RVFI-only mem_addr, mem_rmask, mem_wmask, mem_rdata, mem_wdata, pc, source values, and instruction fields. Preserve the existing single-retire MEM/WB retirement point, dmem stall behavior, misalignment traps, RVFI aligned-memory reporting, and NRET=1 port shape. This should reduce routing and enable fanout around the MEM boundary with little CPI risk, extending the proven RVFI-shadow split into the last stage where sideband metadata still sits next to timing-sensitive load/w...\\n  Line 31: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r10s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Single hot loop branch predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts for this core regressed because they added immediate\\\\ndecode, target adders, return-stack state, counters, or multi-entry tag logic\\\\nto the fetch/decode control paths. CoreMark still spends cycles on repeated\\\\ntaken loop-closing branches, but the predictor must be smaller than the prior\\\\nBTB, RAS, JAL, and decode-backward experiments to have any chance of paying\\\\non Tang Nano 20K fitness.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts for this core regressed because they added immediate\\\\ndecode, target adders, return-stack state, counters, or multi-entry tag logic\\\\nto the fetch/decode control paths. CoreMark still spends cycles on repeated\\\\ntaken loop-closing branches, but the predictor must be smaller than the prior\\\\nBTB, RAS, JAL, and decode-backward experiments to have any chance of paying\\\\non Tang Nano 20K fitness.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add the smallest possible EX-trained predictor for one hot taken conditional\\\\nbranch: store a single branch PC and its already-resolved target when EX sees\\\\na taken backward branch, predict that target only on an exact PC match, and\\\\ninvalidate the entry when the same branch later resolves not-taken. Unlike the\\\\nfailed BTFNT and BTB attempts, IF performs no instruction immediate decode and\\\\nno target addition; it only compares the current PC with one registered PC and\\\\nmuxes between pc+4 and one registered target. Carry a one-bit predicted flag\\\\nand predicted next PC to EX so correct predictions suppress the normal taken\\\\nredirect while mismatches use the existing recovery path. This targets the most\\\\ncommon loop bubble with minimal predictor state and avoids JAL/JALR/RAS logic.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 1, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add minimal predicted-valid and predicted-next-PC metadata to IF/ID and ID/EX b...\",\"metadata\":{\"matches\":31,\"truncated\":false},\"title\":\"micro_opt|forward|hazard|critical|ALU|regfile|RVFI|shift|mux|Fmax\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778697087462,\"end\":1778697087470}},\"metadata\":{\"openai\":{\"itemId\":\"fc_0e80768a67d4c345016a04c37f514481999151ec2f2c721c5a\"}},\"id\":\"prt_e229ba9dc001co20vWopdLvFiQ\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1dd647e0effeZs5rb4D9DoknQl\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e229b825c0012ZgpiPpsXJgC7J\"}}\n./bench/gpt-5_5_medium/rep2/agent.log:44:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778697729085,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1dd5ab9d1ffe2B2A54E2vq8V30\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"read\",\"callID\":\"call_wcSasLqAnaJytfGVIr2lsElz\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"filePath\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_5_medium-rep2/cores/bench/LESSONS.md\",\"offset\":1,\"limit\":200},\"output\":\"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_5_medium-rep2/cores/bench/LESSONS.md</path>\\n<type>file</type>\\n<content>\\n1: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r1s0 (improvement, +32.85%): Moving RV32M multiply/divide out of the combinational EX ALU behind a multicycle handshake can substantially improve FPGA fitness because CoreMark rarely needs M-op throughput while the removed arithmetic cone dominates Fmax.\\n2: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r1s1 (broken, n/a): Moving branch/JALR resolution into ID needs exhaustive operand-valid stalling and forwarding for decode-stage compares/base operands; stale early redirects can let wrong-path state reach writeback, which riscv-formal's reg check catches.\\n3: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r1s2 (regression, +21.76%): IF-stage BTFNT/JAL prediction can regress CoreMark when instruction-bit immediate decode and target adders sit directly on the fetch PC path, so front-end bubble savings must be weighed against Fmax-critical next-PC logic.\\n4: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r2s0 (regression, -1.92%): Replacing MEM-stage variable shifts with explicit byte/halfword lane case muxes did not help FPGA fitness, suggesting the synthesizer already maps these narrow shifts well and the added mux/control structure can slightly worsen routing or LUT packing.\\n5: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r2s1 (regression, -7.79%): A two-entry IF skid buffer did not pay for CoreMark's random imem stalls; the added fetch-valid/control and redirect-flush machinery can cost more FPGA fitness than the front-end bubbles it hides.\\n6: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r2s2 (regression, -44.37%): A tiny IF return-address stack can still badly hurt FPGA fitness when canonical-return detection, RAS top muxing, and predicted-PC metadata outweigh the CoreMark return bubbles it removes.\\n7: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r3s0 (broken, n/a): Keep source-aware load-use interlocks separate from RVFI operand reporting changes; masking unused rs fields can break cosim's retire trace even when the intended stall reduction logic is local to hazard_unit.\\n8: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r3s1 (regression, -14.30%): MEM-to-EX load bypass can lose FPGA fitness when dmem-ready gating, load extraction, and an extra EX operand forwarding select lengthen the critical path more than CoreMark benefits from removing one-cycle load-use bubbles.\\n9: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r3s2 (regression, -20.54%): Even decode-stage JAL prediction can regress when the early_redirect priority path, target adder, and predicted-PC metadata still add enough IF/ID/EX control cost to outweigh saving CoreMark's unconditional-jump bubble.\\n10: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r4s1 (broken, n/a): A registered IF/ID boundary must pair imem backpressure with an ID consume/valid handshake; holding a valid IF/ID entry while ID keeps advancing can duplicate instructions during CoreMark stalls.\\n11: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r4s2 (regression, -9.29%): Even a resolved-target BTB can lose FPGA fitness when IF-side tag/valid/counter arrays, target muxing, and predicted-PC metadata lengthen the next-PC path more than CoreMark benefits from avoiding loop redirect bubbles.\\n12: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r5s0 (improvement, +1.93%): Dropping reset clears from the integer register file can improve FPGA fitness because pipeline-valid reset and x0 read forcing preserve architectural behavior while avoiding reset fanout and per-register muxing in an always-active datapath block.\\n13: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r5s1 (regression, -6.89%): A one-entry ordered store buffer can lose CoreMark fitness when store-order interlocks, MMIO/trap classification, and dmem muxing add more MEM/control-path cost than random store-stall overlap recovers.\\n14: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r5s2 (regression, -61.05%): Even a single-entry loop predictor can badly regress when predicted-PC metadata, IF PC/tag compare muxing, and EX mismatch redirect logic lengthen critical control paths more than repeated CoreMark loop-branch bubble removal helps.\\n15: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r6s0 (regression, -2.40%): Pruning unused EX/MEM branch_taken/branch_target metadata did not improve FPGA fitness, suggesting these dead-looking pipeline fields were not on the binding path or were already optimized well enough that layout perturbation outweighed the small flop reduction.\\n16: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r6s1 (regression, -88.09%): Splitting EX with an EX1 operand register can destroy CoreMark fitness when it delays branch/JALR redirects and forces broad dependent-ALU interlocks, so operand-cone retiming must preserve bypass/redirect latency to pay off.\\n17: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r6s2 (regression, -48.05%): A JAL-only single-entry target predictor still regressed without fetch-side immediate decode because the IF PC compare/target mux, predicted-PC pipeline fields, and EX mismatch redirect logic outweighed CoreMark's repeated unconditional-jump bubble savings.\\n18: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r7s0 (regression, -53.28%): Replacing cold RV32M DIV/REM combinational operators with an iterative divider can severely regress CoreMark when the busy/start handshake, divider state, and 32-cycle latency perturb the hot pipeline more than removing the rarely used divide cone helps Fmax.\\n19: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r7s1 (broken, n/a): Registering dmem backpressure needs a real ready/valid or skid boundary at MEM; a lone pending/wait bit can hide the first stalled cycle from upstream control and let younger CoreMark memory-state updates be dropped or reordered.\\n20: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r8s0 (improvement, +2.79%): Sharing one sign/zero-extended M-unit multiplier across MULH signedness variants can improve FPGA fitness because CoreMark rarely needs M throughput and parallel 64-bit products add DSP/LUT routing pressure.\\n21: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r8s1 (regression, -0.33%): Preselecting MEM/WB writeback data inside mem_stage did not improve fitness, suggesting the load-vs-ALU writeback mux is not a binding FPGA path and adding another pipeline field can slightly worsen packing/routing.\\n22: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r8s2 (regression, +1.88%): Moving backward-branch prediction from IF to ID still regressed because the ID target adder, redirect/stall arbitration, predicted-PC metadata, and EX mispredict compare/control outweighed CoreMark loop-bubble savings.\\n23: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r9s0 (regression, -14.30%): Narrowing MEM/WB from full ctrl_t to a tiny packed mem_wb_ctrl_t can hurt FPGA fitness despite fewer flops, suggesting late-stage control fields were already optimized away or well-packed and the new struct/mapping perturbed routing more than it saved.\\n24: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r9s1 (improvement, +3.53%): Splitting RVFI-only metadata into parallel shadow payloads can improve FPGA fitness because it removes wide instruction/source/PC/memory fields from hot EX/MEM and MEM/WB forwarding, dmem, and writeback bundles without changing pipeline timing.\\n25: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r9s2 (regression, -31.51%): Decode-stage return prediction still regressed because link-register hazard compares, early redirect arbitration, and predicted-PC fields added more hot control/datapath cost than CoreMark recovered from avoiding canonical JALR return bubbles.\\n26: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r10s1 (regression, -5.39%): Further splitting MEM RVFI memory sidebands after the broader RVFI-shadow win can regress when it duplicates byte-mask/write-data logic and adds another MEM/WB payload boundary without removing a binding dmem/load/writeback path.\\n27: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r10s2 (broken, n/a): EX-trained branch predictors must keep prediction-hit suppression, exit invalidation redirects, and RVFI pc_next reporting aligned with the next retired instruction; riscv-formal's pc_fwd check catches recovery paths that eventually fetch correctly but break the retirement PC chain.\\n28: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r11s0 (regression, -5.83%): Narrowing EX/MEM and MEM/WB from full ctrl_t to post_ex_ctrl_t can still hurt FPGA fitness after RVFI-shadow splitting, implying the remaining control bits are not the binding path and the extra struct projection/packing perturbation outweighs small flop savings.\\n29: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r11s2 (regression, -12.97%): EX-side fall-through redirect elision can regress even without fetch predictor state because a resolved-target-vs-pc+4 comparator lengthens branch/jump control while CoreMark rarely benefits from target-equals-fall-through cases.\\n\\n(End of file - total 29 lines)\\n</content>\",\"metadata\":{\"preview\":\"- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r1s0 (improvement, +32.85%): Moving RV32M multiply/divide out of the combinational EX ALU behind a multicycle handshake can substantially improve FPGA fitness because CoreMark rarely needs M-op throughput while the removed arithmetic cone dominates Fmax.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r1s1 (broken, n/a): Moving branch/JALR resolution into ID needs exhaustive operand-valid stalling and forwarding for decode-stage compares/base operands; stale early redirects can let wrong-path state reach writeback, which riscv-formal's reg check catches.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r1s2 (regression, +21.76%): IF-stage BTFNT/JAL prediction can regress CoreMark when instruction-bit immediate decode and target adders sit directly on the fetch PC path, so front-end bubble savings must be weighed against Fmax-critical next-PC logic.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r2s0 (regression, -1.92%): Replacing MEM-stage variable shifts with explicit byte/halfword lane case muxes did not help FPGA fitness, suggesting the synthesizer already maps these narrow shifts well and the added mux/control structure can slightly worsen routing or LUT packing.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r2s1 (regression, -7.79%): A two-entry IF skid buffer did not pay for CoreMark's random imem stalls; the added fetch-valid/control and redirect-flush machinery can cost more FPGA fitness than the front-end bubbles it hides.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r2s2 (regression, -44.37%): A tiny IF return-address stack can still badly hurt FPGA fitness when canonical-return detection, RAS top muxing, and predicted-PC metadata outweigh the CoreMark return bubbles it removes.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r3s0 (broken, n/a): Keep source-aware load-use interlocks separate from RVFI operand reporting changes; masking unused rs fields can break cosim's retire trace even when the intended stall reduction logic is local to hazard_unit.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r3s1 (regression, -14.30%): MEM-to-EX load bypass can lose FPGA fitness when dmem-ready gating, load extraction, and an extra EX operand forwarding select lengthen the critical path more than CoreMark benefits from removing one-cycle load-use bubbles.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r3s2 (regression, -20.54%): Even decode-stage JAL prediction can regress when the early_redirect priority path, target adder, and predicted-PC metadata still add enough IF/ID/EX control cost to outweigh saving CoreMark's unconditional-jump bubble.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r4s1 (broken, n/a): A registered IF/ID boundary must pair imem backpressure with an ID consume/valid handshake; holding a valid IF/ID entry while ID keeps advancing can duplicate instructions during CoreMark stalls.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r4s2 (regression, -9.29%): Even a resolved-target BTB can lose FPGA fitness when IF-side tag/valid/counter arrays, target muxing, and predicted-PC metadata lengthen the next-PC path more than CoreMark benefits from avoiding loop redirect bubbles.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r5s0 (improvement, +1.93%): Dropping reset clears from the integer register file can improve FPGA fitness because pipeline-valid reset and x0 read forcing preserve architectural behavior while avoiding reset fanout and per-register muxing in an always-active datapath block.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r5s1 (regression, -6.89%): A one-entry ordered store buffer can lose CoreMark fitness when store-order interlocks, MMIO/trap classification, and dmem muxing add more MEM/control-path cost than random store-stall overlap recovers.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r5s2 (regression, -61.05%): Even a single-entry loop predictor can badly regress when predicted-PC metadata, IF PC/tag compare muxing, and EX mismatch redirect logic lengthen critical control paths more than repeated CoreMark loop-branch bubble removal helps.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r6s0 (regression, -2.40%): Pruning unused EX/MEM branch_taken/branch_target metadata did not improve FPGA fitness, suggesting these dead-looking pipeline fields were not on the binding path or were already optimized well enough that layout perturbation outweighed the small flop reduction.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r6s1 (regression, -88.09%): Splitting EX with an EX1 operand register can destroy CoreMark fitness when it delays branch/JALR redirects and forces broad dependent-ALU interlocks, so operand-cone retiming must preserve bypass/redirect latency to pay off.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r6s2 (regression, -48.05%): A JAL-only single-entry target predictor still regressed without fetch-side immediate decode because the IF PC compare/target mux, predicted-PC pipeline fields, and EX mismatch redirect logic outweighed CoreMark's repeated unconditional-jump bubble savings.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r7s0 (regression, -53.28%): Replacing cold RV32M DIV/REM combinational operators with an iterative divider can severely regress CoreMark when the busy/start handshake, divider state, and 32-cycle latency perturb the hot pipeline more than removing the rarely used divide cone helps Fmax.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r7s1 (broken, n/a): Registering dmem backpressure needs a real ready/valid or skid boundary at MEM; a lone pending/wait bit can hide the first stalled cycle from upstream control and let younger CoreMark memory-state updates be dropped or reordered.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r8s0 (improvement, +2.79%): Sharing one sign/zero-extended M-unit multiplier across MULH signedness variants can improve FPGA fitness because CoreMark rarely needs M throughput and parallel 64-bit products add DSP/LUT routing pressure.\",\"truncated\":false,\"loaded\":[]},\"title\":\"cores/bench/LESSONS.md\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778697729069,\"end\":1778697729082}},\"metadata\":{\"openai\":{\"itemId\":\"fc_06e10c426d2117f0016a04c600f18481998729927cb2411c9f\"}},\"id\":\"prt_e22a57427001LnosbuKhR100Gx\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1dd5ab9d1ffe2B2A54E2vq8V30\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e22a546c60015BuDFGSPL9BPSM\"}}\n./bench/gpt-5_5_medium/rep2/agent.log:45:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778697729098,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1dd5ab9d1ffe2B2A54E2vq8V30\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"grep\",\"callID\":\"call_fnJPeEkrL2AVxQpdhQILULek\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"pattern\":\"category: micro_opt|micro_opt|hyp-20260513-001-r5s0|hyp-20260513-001-r8s0|hyp-20260513-002-r9s1|forward|reg_file|decoder|alu\",\"path\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_5_medium-rep2/cores/bench/experiments\",\"include\":\"log.jsonl\"},\"output\":\"Found 26 matches\\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_5_medium-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:\\n  Line 1: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"baseline-bench-5327bba\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Baseline retest for cores/bench\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"improvement\\\", \\\"fitness\\\": 282.82, \\\"delta_pct\\\": 0.0, \\\"vs_baseline\\\": 0.0, \\\"fmax_mhz\\\": 127.03, \\\"ipc_coremark\\\": 2e-06, \\\"cycles\\\": 4491485, \\\"iterations\\\": 10, \\\"lut4\\\": 9563, \\\"ff\\\": 1866, \\\"seeds\\\": [128.72, 127.03, 123.02], \\\"formal_passed\\\": true, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": true, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"implementation_notes\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-05-13T13:08:01.185076Z\\\", \\\"round_id\\\": 0, \\\"slot\\\": 0}\\n\\n  Line 2: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r1s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Multicycle RV32M arithmetic unit\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current ALU computes every multiply and divide result combinationally in\\\\nthe EX stage, including signed and unsigned 64-bit products plus divider and\\\\nremainder operators. That wide arithmetic cone is a likely Fmax limiter on\\\\nTang Nano 20K, while CoreMark's hot path should not need single-cycle DIV/REM\\\\nthroughput. Trading rare M-extension latency for a shorter common EX critical\\\\npath should improve iterations/second through higher clock frequency.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current ALU computes every multiply and divide result combinationally in\\\\nthe EX stage, including signed and unsigned 64-bit products plus divider and\\\\nremainder operators. That wide arithmetic cone is a likely Fmax limiter on\\\\nTang Nano 20K, while CoreMark's hot path should not need single-cycle DIV/REM\\\\nthroughput. Trading rare M-extension latency for a shorter common EX critical\\\\npath should improve iterations/second through higher clock frequency.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Split RV32M operations out of the always-combinational ALU path behind a\\\\nsmall multicycle M-unit handshake in EX. Keep ADD/SUB/logic/branch/address\\\\noperations purely combinational, but when the decoded alu_op is MUL/DIV/REM,\\\\nhold ID/EX and EX/MEM until the iterative arithmetic result is ready, then\\\\nretire normally through the existing MEM/WB and RVFI path. This should remove\\\\ndivider and large multiplier logic from the ordinary EX-stage timing path;\\\\nthe added stalls only affect uncommon M instructions and should be outweighed\\\\nby improved Fmax on the FPGA fitness metric.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 6, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/alu.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Restrict the combinational ALU to fast RV32I operations and expose only lightweight M-op selection or helper logic needed by the new M-unit.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a multicycl...\\n  Line 3: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r1s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Resolve control flow in ID\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"CoreMark spends a significant fraction of its bracketed runtime in tight loops and conditional control flow. The current five-stage pipeline resolves branches and jumps in EX, then flushes both IF and ID, so every taken branch or jump pays roughly a two-cycle redirect penalty. Reducing that penalty improves iterations/cycle without increasing retirement width or touching the external NRET=1 contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"CoreMark spends a significant fraction of its bracketed runtime in tight loops and conditional control flow. The current five-stage pipeline resolves branches and jumps in EX, then flushes both IF and ID, so every taken branch or jump pays roughly a two-cycle redirect penalty. Reducing that penalty improves iterations/cycle without increasing retirement width or touching the external NRET=1 contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Move branch and JAL/JALR target/condition resolution from EX into the decode stage, with decode-stage operand forwarding from EX/MEM and MEM/WB for branch compares and JALR base operands. Keep the existing EX-stage trap/metadata path as the retirement source, but let ID drive redirect one cycle earlier when operands are available; stall only for unresolved load-to-branch/JALR dependencies. This should cut the common taken-control penalty from two bubbles to one while preserving RVFI order, trap reporting, and single-channel retirement.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 8, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Wire ID-stage early redirect signals into the IF and hazard path while preserving the existing NRET=1 RVFI port set.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add early branch/JAL/JALR resolution using decoded control, immediates, regfile operands, and forwarded compare/base operands.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/forward_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend forwarding ...\\n  Line 4: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r1s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Static BTFNT branch predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current five-stage core always fetches sequentially and resolves every branch or jump in EX, so each taken loop branch pays a two-instruction front-end flush. CoreMark has tight loops and many backward branches, making a small static predictor likely to improve iterations per cycle without adding much FPGA area or hurting the ALU critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current five-stage core always fetches sequentially and resolves every branch or jump in EX, so each taken loop branch pays a two-instruction front-end flush. CoreMark has tight loops and many backward branches, making a small static predictor likely to improve iterations per cycle without adding much FPGA area or hurting the ALU critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add an IF-stage static backward-taken/forward-not-taken predictor using the fetched instruction bits to compute B-type targets for negative branch immediates, and optionally early-predict JAL targets because they are unconditional PC-relative redirects. Carry the predicted next PC through IF/ID and ID/EX so EX redirects only when the resolved next PC differs from the prediction; mispredicted forward-taken branches, not-taken backward branches, and JALR still recover through the existing flush path. This should remove most loop-closing branch bubbles while preserving the single-retire RVFI contract because retirement remains unchanged at MEM/WB.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 8, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend IF/ID and ID/EX pipeline bundles with the predicted next PC needed for EX-stage mispredict detection.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Implement static BTFNT next-PC selection for backward conditional branches and early PC-relative JAL prediction from fetched instruction bits.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Lat...\\n  Line 5: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r2s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Simplify MEM load lane muxes\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"After the successful M-unit split, the remaining always-active timing paths are more likely to be ordinary RV32I datapath logic than rare multiply/divide arithmetic. The MEM stage currently uses variable shifts for load extraction and shifted masks for byte lanes, which can synthesize into wider mux/barrel-shifter logic on the FPGA load-to-WB path. CoreMark performs many byte, halfword, and word accesses, so a smaller MEM-stage cone should improve Fmax without changing CPI.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"After the successful M-unit split, the remaining always-active timing paths are more likely to be ordinary RV32I datapath logic than rare multiply/divide arithmetic. The MEM stage currently uses variable shifts for load extraction and shifted masks for byte lanes, which can synthesize into wider mux/barrel-shifter logic on the FPGA load-to-WB path. CoreMark performs many byte, halfword, and word accesses, so a smaller MEM-stage cone should improve Fmax without changing CPI.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Rewrite the MEM-stage byte-lane handling as explicit case muxes keyed by addr[1:0] instead of expressions like dmem_rdata >> (addr[1:0] * 8) and mask << addr[1:0]. Keep the same misalignment trap behavior, RVFI aligned mem_addr reporting, replicated store data convention, and MEM/WB retirement boundary. This should reduce LUT depth and routing on the common load-data path while preserving the existing five-stage control flow and single-retire NRET=1 contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace variable load-data shifts and shifted byte masks with explicit addr[1:0] case muxes for LB/LBU/LH/LHU/LW and store masks.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace variable load...\\n  Line 8: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r3s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Source-aware load-use stalls\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current load-use hazard detector compares a load destination against the\\\\nraw rs1 and rs2 bitfields of the following instruction, regardless of whether\\\\nthat instruction actually consumes those operands. For I-type immediates,\\\\nloads, JALR, LUI, AUIPC, JAL, FENCE, and EBREAK, some of those fields are\\\\nimmediates or destination bits rather than true sources. Any accidental match\\\\nwith the preceding load rd inserts an unnecessary bubble, directly reducing\\\\nCoreMark iterations per cycle without improving correctness.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current load-use hazard detector compares a load destination against the\\\\nraw rs1 and rs2 bitfields of the following instruction, regardless of whether\\\\nthat instruction actually consumes those operands. For I-type immediates,\\\\nloads, JALR, LUI, AUIPC, JAL, FENCE, and EBREAK, some of those fields are\\\\nimmediates or destination bits rather than true sources. Any accidental match\\\\nwith the preceding load rd inserts an unnecessary bubble, directly reducing\\\\nCoreMark iterations per cycle without improving correctness.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Make the load-use interlock source-aware by qualifying the rd comparisons with\\\\nsimple decoded uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 signals for the instruction currently in\\\\nIF/ID. Keep the existing one-cycle stall for real load consumers, but avoid\\\\nstalling on unused source fields such as OP-IMM rs2 bits, U/J-type operand\\\\nfields, and SYSTEM/FENCE encodings. This should improve CPI under CoreMark's\\\\nload-heavy code while adding only small opcode decode logic off the main ALU\\\\nand memory datapaths, so Fmax should be largely preserved.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Derive or wire IF/ID source-use qualifiers into the hazard unit without changing the NRET=1 top-level RVFI interface.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl...\\n  Line 9: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r3s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Load-use MEM bypass\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The previous structural attempts spent LUTs and timing on front-end control-flow machinery, and the log shows those changes either broke correctness or regressed fitness. The current five-stage core still has the classic one-cycle load-use interlock even though the benchmark memory is zero-wait in FPGA and only randomly stalled in cosim. CoreMark has frequent dependent loads through pointer-heavy list, matrix, and state code, so removing this bubble can improve iterations per cycle without adding predictor target adders to the fetch critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The previous structural attempts spent LUTs and timing on front-end control-flow machinery, and the log shows those changes either broke correctness or regressed fitness. The current five-stage core still has the classic one-cycle load-use interlock even though the benchmark memory is zero-wait in FPGA and only randomly stalled in cosim. CoreMark has frequent dependent loads through pointer-heavy list, matrix, and state code, so removing this bubble can improve iterations per cycle without adding predictor target adders to the fetch critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a MEM-to-EX load-data bypass path that lets an instruction immediately behind a load proceed into EX when the load is in MEM and dmem is ready. Compute the existing load extraction result combinationally in MEM, expose it to the forwarding network, and add a forwarding select for EX operands that chooses the current MEM load result over the older EX/MEM ALU address. Keep the load-use stall only when the load will not have valid data that cycle, such as a dmem stall or a misaligned/trapping load. This structural bypass should remove many single-cycle bubbles while preserving the NRET=1 retirement boundary and RVFI order at MEM/WB.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 6, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\"...\\n  Line 10: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r3s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Decode-stage JAL predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The prior predictor attempts regressed because they placed branch target decode,\\\\ntarget adders, return detection, and prediction muxes directly on the IF PC path.\\\\nCoreMark still pays the existing EX redirect penalty for every unconditional JAL,\\\\nand JAL targets are PC-relative with no register dependency or branch condition.\\\\nPredicting only JAL in decode should recover one control-flow bubble for calls and\\\\nlong jumps while avoiding the risky fetch-path predictor logic that hurt Fmax.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The prior predictor attempts regressed because they placed branch target decode,\\\\ntarget adders, return detection, and prediction muxes directly on the IF PC path.\\\\nCoreMark still pays the existing EX redirect penalty for every unconditional JAL,\\\\nand JAL targets are PC-relative with no register dependency or branch condition.\\\\nPredicting only JAL in decode should recover one control-flow bubble for calls and\\\\nlong jumps while avoiding the risky fetch-path predictor logic that hurt Fmax.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a narrow decode-stage always-taken predictor for legal JAL instructions only.\\\\nID computes pc+imm for JAL, redirects fetch one cycle before EX would, and records\\\\nthe predicted next PC in the ID/EX bundle. EX then suppresses the normal JAL redirect\\\\nwhen the resolved jump target matches the predicted target, but retains the existing\\\\nEX recovery path for conditional branches, JALR, illegal instructions, and any target\\\\nmismatch. Because the predictor excludes branches and returns, it needs no B-type\\\\nimmediate decode in IF, no RAS state, and no operand forwarding, making it a smaller\\\\npredictor angle with a better chance of improving CoreMark iterations/cycle without\\\\nsacrificing the current high Fmax.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add p...\\n  Line 11: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r4s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Source-qualified load interlock\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current load-use detector compares the preceding load rd against both raw\\\\nsource bitfields of the following instruction, even when those bits are not\\\\narchitecturally consumed. CoreMark has many loads and immediate/control\\\\ninstructions, so accidental rd matches can inject bubbles that reduce\\\\niterations per cycle without protecting any real dependency.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current load-use detector compares the preceding load rd against both raw\\\\nsource bitfields of the following instruction, even when those bits are not\\\\narchitecturally consumed. CoreMark has many loads and immediate/control\\\\ninstructions, so accidental rd matches can inject bubbles that reduce\\\\niterations per cycle without protecting any real dependency.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a tiny source-use decode for the IF/ID instruction and qualify the existing\\\\nload-use interlock with uses_rs1 and uses_rs2. This is a narrower retry of the\\\\nearlier source-aware load-use idea: do not mask or reinterpret RVFI operand\\\\nreporting, and do not add MEM bypass selects or fetch prediction paths. The\\\\ncore should keep the one-cycle stall for true load consumers while avoiding\\\\nfalse stalls on OP-IMM rs2 fields, U/J-type immediates, FENCE, SYSTEM/EBREAK,\\\\nand other instructions that do not read both source registers.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Derive simple IF/ID uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 qualifiers from opcode/funct class and pass them only to the hazard unit.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Gate load-use rd comparisons with the source-use qualifiers while preserving existing dmem, imem, redirect, and M-unit stall priority.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Derive simple IF/ID use...\\n  Line 12: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r4s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Register IF/ID fetch boundary\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current front end feeds imem_data directly through decode, immediate\\\\ngeneration, regfile address decode, hazard checks, and into the ID/EX\\\\nregister in a single cycle. Prior structural attempts tried to hide stalls or\\\\nreduce bubbles with added prediction and bypass control, but those changes\\\\nregressed by lengthening already sensitive mux/control paths. A real IF/ID\\\\nregister is a simpler structural retiming that targets Fmax directly.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current front end feeds imem_data directly through decode, immediate\\\\ngeneration, regfile address decode, hazard checks, and into the ID/EX\\\\nregister in a single cycle. Prior structural attempts tried to hide stalls or\\\\nreduce bubbles with added prediction and bypass control, but those changes\\\\nregressed by lengthening already sensitive mux/control paths. A real IF/ID\\\\nregister is a simpler structural retiming that targets Fmax directly.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Insert a proper IF/ID pipeline register inside the fetch stage, so fetched\\\\ninstruction, PC, and valid are captured before decode sees them. The PC still\\\\nadvances only when the downstream ID stage can accept the entry, and redirect\\\\nflushes the registered IF/ID payload before wrong-path state can retire. This\\\\nadds one front-end cycle and may slightly increase taken-control penalty, but\\\\nit removes the imem-to-decoder combinational path and should improve FPGA\\\\nFmax enough to raise CoreMark iter/sec without changing the NRET=1 retirement\\\\ncontract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Keep the existing IF/ID bundle shape, but document and use it as a registered pipeline boundary rather than a combinational fetch payload.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add an IF/ID register for pc, instruction, and valid; hold it on deco...\\n  Line 14: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r5s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Drop regfile reset fanout\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current register file resets all 32 architectural registers with a for-loop,\\\\nwhich forces reset muxing or reset routing onto the whole small RF array. After\\\\nthe M-unit split, remaining FPGA Fmax is likely limited by ordinary datapath\\\\nand control fanout rather than rare arithmetic. CoreMark initializes the state\\\\nit uses, x0 is already hardwired on reads, and RVFI correctness depends on\\\\nretired architectural behavior rather than power-on values of unused registers.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current register file resets all 32 architectural registers with a for-loop,\\\\nwhich forces reset muxing or reset routing onto the whole small RF array. After\\\\nthe M-unit split, remaining FPGA Fmax is likely limited by ordinary datapath\\\\nand control fanout rather than rare arithmetic. CoreMark initializes the state\\\\nit uses, x0 is already hardwired on reads, and RVFI correctness depends on\\\\nretired architectural behavior rather than power-on values of unused registers.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Remove the explicit reset clear from the integer register file and keep only\\\\nthe synchronous write path for nonzero destination registers. Reads of x0 stay\\\\nforced to zero, same-cycle write-first bypass stays unchanged, and reset of the\\\\npipeline control/valid bits still prevents spurious retirement after reset.\\\\nThis should reduce reset fanout and per-register muxing in a timing-sensitive,\\\\nalways-active block without changing CPI, branch behavior, memory behavior, or\\\\nthe NRET=1 RVFI interface. The change is deliberately narrower than prior\\\\nfailed micro-opts: it does not touch hazard detection, RVFI operand reporting,\\\\nMEM load extraction, or any front-end prediction path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/reg_file.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove the reset-time loop that clears all 32 registers while preser...\\n  Line 17: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r6s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Prune dead EX/MEM branch metadata\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core has already won most of its fitness from removing broad\\\\ntiming/fanout costs, while recent attempts that add prediction, buffering,\\\\nbypassing, or memory-ordering control have regressed. The EX/MEM bundle still\\\\ncarries branch_taken and branch_target fields that are assigned every cycle\\\\nbut are not consumed by MEM, WB, RVFI, hazard, or forwarding logic. Those 33\\\\ndead flops and their input adders/mux routes sit in an always-active pipeline\\\\nboundary, so pruning them is a low-risk micro-optimization for LUT/FF count\\\\nand routing delay without changing CPI.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core has already won most of its fitness from removing broad\\\\ntiming/fanout costs, while recent attempts that add prediction, buffering,\\\\nbypassing, or memory-ordering control have regressed. The EX/MEM bundle still\\\\ncarries branch_taken and branch_target fields that are assigned every cycle\\\\nbut are not consumed by MEM, WB, RVFI, hazard, or forwarding logic. Those 33\\\\ndead flops and their input adders/mux routes sit in an always-active pipeline\\\\nboundary, so pruning them is a low-risk micro-optimization for LUT/FF count\\\\nand routing delay without changing CPI.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Remove the unused branch_taken and branch_target fields from ex_mem_t and stop\\\\nregistering them in ex_stage. Keep redirect and pc_next computation exactly as\\\\nthey are today, because pc_next is the RVFI-visible resolved next PC and the\\\\nredirect output is still the only live control-flow signal. This should trim\\\\ndead sequential state and reduce EX-stage fanout/routing pressure while\\\\npreserving the NRET=1 RVFI contract, trap behavior, memory behavior, and all\\\\nexisting stall/flush priorities.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 1, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove unused branch_taken and...\\n  Line 18: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r6s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Pipeline EX operand retiming\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent structural attempts that added fetch prediction, skid buffering, load bypassing,\\\\nor store buffering all paid too much control-path cost. The current core's remaining\\\\nhigh-fanout structural path is more basic: ID/EX values pass through forwarding muxes,\\\\nALU operand selection, ALU or branch compare/target logic, and into EX/MEM plus the\\\\nredirect path in one cycle. Retiming that boundary can target Fmax without adding a\\\\npredictor, memory ordering structure, or RVFI contract change.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent structural attempts that added fetch prediction, skid buffering, load bypassing,\\\\nor store buffering all paid too much control-path cost. The current core's remaining\\\\nhigh-fanout structural path is more basic: ID/EX values pass through forwarding muxes,\\\\nALU operand selection, ALU or branch compare/target logic, and into EX/MEM plus the\\\\nredirect path in one cycle. Retiming that boundary can target Fmax without adding a\\\\npredictor, memory ordering structure, or RVFI contract change.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Split the existing EX stage into a lightweight EX1 operand/metadata register and an EX2\\\\nexecute/redirect stage. EX1 captures the forwarded rs1/rs2 values, selected ALU operands,\\\\ndecoded control, immediate, PC, and RVFI source data; EX2 performs the ALU operation,\\\\nbranch/jump resolution, M-unit handshake, and drives the existing EX/MEM payload. Add the\\\\nminimal hazard/forwarding adjustments needed for the extra execute stage, preferring a\\\\none-cycle interlock on immediately dependent ALU consumers over wide new bypass muxes if\\\\nthat keeps timing short. This structural retiming should shorten the common forwarding-to-\\\\nALU-to-EX/MEM cone and may raise Tang Nano 20K Fmax enough to offset a small CPI cost,\\\\nwhile preserving NRET=1 retirement at MEM/WB and all RVFI ordering/trap behavior.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\"...\\n  Line 20: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r7s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Iterative M divider\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The largest proven fitness gain for this core came from moving RV32M arithmetic\\\\nout of the ordinary EX ALU path, but the current m_unit still contains wide\\\\ncombinational DIV/REM operators behind a one-cycle result register. Even if\\\\nthose operations are rare in CoreMark, the FPGA still has to place and time\\\\nthe divider/remainder cone, so it can cap Fmax or perturb routing for the hot\\\\nRV32I pipeline.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The largest proven fitness gain for this core came from moving RV32M arithmetic\\\\nout of the ordinary EX ALU path, but the current m_unit still contains wide\\\\ncombinational DIV/REM operators behind a one-cycle result register. Even if\\\\nthose operations are rare in CoreMark, the FPGA still has to place and time\\\\nthe divider/remainder cone, so it can cap Fmax or perturb routing for the hot\\\\nRV32I pipeline.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the one-cycle DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU implementation in m_unit with a small\\\\niterative restoring divider that runs only after a divide or remainder starts.\\\\nKeep MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU on the existing short registered path so any\\\\nmultiply use does not pay a 32-cycle penalty, and keep the existing EX-stage\\\\nhold/valid handshake as the only pipeline interface. This should remove the\\\\nexpensive combinational divider/remainder logic from the FPGA timing closure\\\\nproblem while adding CPI only for uncommon divide/remainder instructions.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/m_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Implement DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU with an iterative signed/unsigned divider state machine while preserving ALTOPS behavior under RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Adjust the existing M-unit busy/valid handshake only as needed so EX holds until multi-cycle DIV/REM completion and still clears completed results exactly once.\\\"}]}, \\\"expec...\\n  Line 21: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r7s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Register dmem stall boundary\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent structural attempts added front-end prediction, fetch buffering, load\\\\nbypassing, store buffering, or a deeper EX pipe, and each paid too much in\\\\nmux/control cost or correctness complexity. A smaller structural target is\\\\nthe current dmem stall path: io_dmemReady fans combinationally through the\\\\nhazard unit into PC hold, ID hold, EX/MEM hold, and MEM/WB valid clearing in\\\\nthe same cycle. CoreMark's apples-to-apples mode injects random dmem stalls,\\\\nso this control path is active during evaluation, and reducing its fanout may\\\\nimprove Fmax without changing the visible memory ordering contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent structural attempts added front-end prediction, fetch buffering, load\\\\nbypassing, store buffering, or a deeper EX pipe, and each paid too much in\\\\nmux/control cost or correctness complexity. A smaller structural target is\\\\nthe current dmem stall path: io_dmemReady fans combinationally through the\\\\nhazard unit into PC hold, ID hold, EX/MEM hold, and MEM/WB valid clearing in\\\\nthe same cycle. CoreMark's apples-to-apples mode injects random dmem stalls,\\\\nso this control path is active during evaluation, and reducing its fanout may\\\\nimprove Fmax without changing the visible memory ordering contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a one-cycle registered MEM busy/accepted boundary for data-memory\\\\noperations. When a load or store enters MEM, capture whether it is waiting for\\\\ndmem_ready in a small state bit local to the MEM/hazard interface; while that\\\\nbit is set, hold EX/MEM and clear MEM/WB valid exactly as today, but drive the\\\\nglobal pipeline stalls from the registered busy state rather than directly\\\\nfrom io_dmemReady. Keep dmem_addr, dmem_wdata, dmem_wen, dmem_ren, RVFI memory\\\\nmasks/data, misalignment traps, and all in-order retirement behavior\\\\nunchanged. This deliberately accepts at most one extra cycle on the first\\\\nstal...\\n  Line 22: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r7s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Decode-only JAL redirect predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts for this core regressed because they added target\\\\nadders, tag compares, return-stack state, or predicted-PC muxing directly to\\\\nthe IF next-PC path. The current core still pays the EX redirect penalty for\\\\nevery unconditional JAL, and JAL needs no operand forwarding or direction\\\\nprediction once its instruction reaches decode.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts for this core regressed because they added target\\\\nadders, tag compares, return-stack state, or predicted-PC muxing directly to\\\\nthe IF next-PC path. The current core still pays the EX redirect penalty for\\\\nevery unconditional JAL, and JAL needs no operand forwarding or direction\\\\nprediction once its instruction reaches decode.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a decode-stage predictor for legal JAL instructions only. ID already has\\\\nthe instruction, PC, and immediate generator output, so it can compute the JAL\\\\ntarget and redirect fetch one cycle earlier than EX without adding any logic\\\\nto the IF PC increment path. Carry a small predicted-next-PC flag/value into\\\\nID/EX so EX suppresses its normal JAL redirect when the ID prediction matches,\\\\nwhile all conditional branches, JALR, illegal instructions, and mismatches use\\\\nthe existing EX recovery path. This avoids the failed fetch-side predictor\\\\nstructures while recovering one bubble on CoreMark calls and PC-relative jumps.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add minimal predicted-valid and predicted-next-PC metadata to the IF/ID and ID/EX bundles for decode-stage JAL prediction validation.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Detect legal JAL in decode, compute pc plus J-immediate, emit an early redirect, and latch the prediction metadata into ID/EX.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Wire the ID...\\n  Line 23: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r8s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Share M-unit multiplier hardware\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core already removed RV32M arithmetic from the ordinary EX\\\\nALU path, but m_unit still builds three separate 64-bit multiplier products\\\\nin parallel for MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU signedness variants. CoreMark uses\\\\nM operations rarely enough that throughput beyond the existing one-cycle\\\\nregistered M-unit is not the main limiter, while the extra DSP/LUT/routing\\\\npressure can still depress FPGA Fmax or perturb placement of the hot RV32I\\\\npipeline.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core already removed RV32M arithmetic from the ordinary EX\\\\nALU path, but m_unit still builds three separate 64-bit multiplier products\\\\nin parallel for MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU signedness variants. CoreMark uses\\\\nM operations rarely enough that throughput beyond the existing one-cycle\\\\nregistered M-unit is not the main limiter, while the extra DSP/LUT/routing\\\\npressure can still depress FPGA Fmax or perturb placement of the hot RV32I\\\\npipeline.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the parallel signed-signed, unsigned-unsigned, and signed-unsigned\\\\nproduct generation in m_unit with one shared signed multiplier fed by\\\\nsign/zero-extended operands selected from the M opcode. MUL can take the low\\\\nhalf of the same product because low 32 bits are independent of operand\\\\nsignedness; MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU select the high half with the appropriate\\\\noperand extension. Keep the existing start/valid/clear handshake, one-cycle\\\\nM latency, DIV/REM behavior, and RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions unchanged.\\\\nThis should trim multiplier area and routing without adding CPI, front-end\\\\nprediction logic, memory ordering state, or any RVFI-visible behavior change.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/m_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Use a single sign-controlled widened multiplier for MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU while pres...\\n  Line 24: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r8s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Register WB result at MEM boundary\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The recent structural attempts mostly lost fitness by adding control-flow\\\\nprediction, buffering, memory overlap, or deeper execute-stage interlocks.\\\\nA safer structural target is the existing MEM/WB-to-WB combinational path:\\\\nthe selected writeback value is still muxed after the MEM/WB register, then\\\\nfans out to the regfile write port, same-cycle regfile bypass, EX forwarding,\\\\nand RVFI rd_wdata. Registering that selected value at the retirement boundary\\\\nshould shorten a hot fanout path without changing CPI, branch timing, memory\\\\nordering, or the NRET=1 external contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The recent structural attempts mostly lost fitness by adding control-flow\\\\nprediction, buffering, memory overlap, or deeper execute-stage interlocks.\\\\nA safer structural target is the existing MEM/WB-to-WB combinational path:\\\\nthe selected writeback value is still muxed after the MEM/WB register, then\\\\nfans out to the regfile write port, same-cycle regfile bypass, EX forwarding,\\\\nand RVFI rd_wdata. Registering that selected value at the retirement boundary\\\\nshould shorten a hot fanout path without changing CPI, branch timing, memory\\\\nordering, or the NRET=1 external contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a preselected rd_wdata/writeback-result field to the MEM/WB pipeline\\\\nbundle and compute it inside mem_stage before the MEM/WB register captures.\\\\nFor loads this field captures the sign/zero-extended load result; for ALU,\\\\nJAL, JALR, AUIPC, LUI, and M-extension operations it captures the existing\\\\nEX/MEM alu_result. Then simplify wb_stage into a pass-through of the\\\\nregistered writeback value instead of a post-register mem_to_reg mux. This\\\\nstructural retiming preserves the same retirement boundary and RVFI data, but\\\\nremoves the load-vs-ALU mux from the next cycle's forwarding/regfile/RVFI\\\\nfanout cone, which may improve Tang Nano 20K Fmax with ess...\\n  Line 26: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r9s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Narrow MEM/WB control state\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent micro-optimization wins came from removing always-present FPGA timing\\\\nand routing pressure, while attempts that added prediction, bypassing, or\\\\nstall-hiding control usually regressed. The current MEM/WB register still\\\\ncarries the full decode control bundle even though the writeback and RVFI\\\\nretirement logic only consume a few bits after the MEM stage. Pruning that\\\\ncold control state should reduce flop count, muxing, and routing without\\\\nchanging CoreMark CPI or touching the external NRET=1 contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent micro-optimization wins came from removing always-present FPGA timing\\\\nand routing pressure, while attempts that added prediction, bypassing, or\\\\nstall-hiding control usually regressed. The current MEM/WB register still\\\\ncarries the full decode control bundle even though the writeback and RVFI\\\\nretirement logic only consume a few bits after the MEM stage. Pruning that\\\\ncold control state should reduce flop count, muxing, and routing without\\\\nchanging CoreMark CPI or touching the external NRET=1 contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the full ctrl_t field in mem_wb_t with a narrow retirement/writeback\\\\ncontrol subset containing only reg_write, mem_to_reg, and is_illegal. Keep\\\\nthe existing full ctrl_t through ID/EX and EX/MEM, because the execute and\\\\nmemory stages still need branch, memory width, sign-extension, and M-op\\\\ncontrols. In mem_stage, collapse ctrl_with_trap into the narrow MEM/WB\\\\nfields after misalignment handling, and update wb_stage and core RVFI trap\\\\nreferences to use the narrow fields. This should trim unused MEM/WB control\\\\nflops and fanout while preserving all memory masks/data, load results,\\\\nwriteback behavior, RVFI order, and trap reporting.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Introduce a narrow M...\\n  Line 27: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r9s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Split RVFI shadow metadata from datapath\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The best recent gains came from removing broad arithmetic and register fanout from hot paths, while most structural attempts regressed by adding prediction, buffering, memory overlap, or extra execute-stage interlocks. The current pipeline still carries a wide all-purpose struct through EX/MEM and MEM/WB, mixing hot execution data with RVFI-only metadata such as original source values, source register numbers, instruction bits, memory masks, and retired PC fields. Even when those fields are not part of the architectural datapath, they share the same pipeline registers, reset/hold enables, and module boundaries as timing-critical forwarding, memory, and writeback signals.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The best recent gains came from removing broad arithmetic and register fanout from hot paths, while most structural attempts regressed by adding prediction, buffering, memory overlap, or extra execute-stage interlocks. The current pipeline still carries a wide all-purpose struct through EX/MEM and MEM/WB, mixing hot execution data with RVFI-only metadata such as original source values, source register numbers, instruction bits, memory masks, and retired PC fields. Even when those fields are not part of the architectural datapath, they share the same pipeline registers, reset/hold enables, and module boundaries as timing-critical forwarding, memory, and writeback signals.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Split the pipeline state into a narrow execution payload and a parallel RVFI shadow payload. Keep the hot EX/MEM and MEM/WB datapath bundles limited to control bits and values needed for forwarding, dmem, writeback, and stalls, while carrying RVFI-only fields in a separate shadow register chain that uses the same valid, flush, hold, trap, and redirect decisions. This should reduce register width, enable fanout, and routing pressure on the timing-critical da...\\n  Line 28: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r9s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Decode return target predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts for this core mostly regressed because they added\\\\ntarget adders, tag compares, counters, return-stack state, or predicted-PC\\\\nmuxes directly to the IF next-PC path. The current core still pays the EX\\\\nredirect penalty on every canonical function return, and CoreMark contains\\\\nenough call/return traffic that recovering one redirect cycle there may help\\\\niterations per cycle. A return predictor that operates only after decode can\\\\nuse the already-fetched instruction, regfile read data, and existing immediate\\\\npath while keeping fetch's common pc+4 path untouched.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts for this core mostly regressed because they added\\\\ntarget adders, tag compares, counters, return-stack state, or predicted-PC\\\\nmuxes directly to the IF next-PC path. The current core still pays the EX\\\\nredirect penalty on every canonical function return, and CoreMark contains\\\\nenough call/return traffic that recovering one redirect cycle there may help\\\\niterations per cycle. A return predictor that operates only after decode can\\\\nuse the already-fetched instruction, regfile read data, and existing immediate\\\\npath while keeping fetch's common pc+4 path untouched.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a decode-stage predictor for canonical returns only: legal JALR with\\\\nrd=x0, funct3=0, imm=0, and rs1=x1 or x5. ID computes the return target from\\\\nthe already-read link register value and redirects fetch one cycle before EX\\\\nwould, but only when the base register is not the destination of an unresolved\\\\nolder instruction in ID/EX or EX/MEM. Carry a minimal predicted-valid and\\\\npredicted-next-PC field into ID/EX so EX suppresses the normal JALR redirect\\\\non an exact match and uses the existing recovery redirect on mismatches. This\\\\navoids the failed IF-side RAS/BTB/BTFNT machinery while targeting a narrower\\\\ncontrol-flow pattern wit...\\n  Line 29: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r10s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Prune unused LUI control bit\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent wins came from removing always-present arithmetic, reset, and RVFI\\\\nrouting pressure, while broader CPI-oriented changes and control prediction\\\\nusually regressed. The current control bundle still carries an is_lui bit\\\\nthrough decode and every pipeline stage even though LUI execution is already\\\\nfully encoded by alu_op=ALU_LUI and alu_src=1. That unused bit adds one more\\\\ndecoded signal, pipeline flop, reset/hold mux input, and routing load across\\\\nhot stage boundaries without contributing to architectural behavior.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent wins came from removing always-present arithmetic, reset, and RVFI\\\\nrouting pressure, while broader CPI-oriented changes and control prediction\\\\nusually regressed. The current control bundle still carries an is_lui bit\\\\nthrough decode and every pipeline stage even though LUI execution is already\\\\nfully encoded by alu_op=ALU_LUI and alu_src=1. That unused bit adds one more\\\\ndecoded signal, pipeline flop, reset/hold mux input, and routing load across\\\\nhot stage boundaries without contributing to architectural behavior.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Remove the redundant is_lui signal from the decoder interface and ctrl_t,\\\\nand delete the corresponding ID-stage temporary and bundle assignment. Keep\\\\nLUI behavior unchanged by continuing to set alu_op=ALU_LUI, alu_src=1, and\\\\nreg_write=1 in the existing validated LUI decode arm. This is a narrow\\\\nno-CPI micro-optimization that should slightly reduce control-bundle width,\\\\nregister enables, reset/hold muxing, and decode-to-EX routing pressure while\\\\npreserving the NRET=1 RVFI contract, trap discipline, memory behavior, and\\\\nall existing stall/forwarding logic.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 1, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove the unused is_lui field from ctrl_t so it no longer propagates throug...\\n  Line 30: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r10s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Split MEM RVFI sideband path\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The latest successful structural change split RVFI-only metadata away from the hot execution payloads and improved fitness without changing CPI. The remaining MEM stage still computes and registers RVFI memory sideband fields in the same combinational block and pipeline boundary that drives dmem, load extraction, writeback, and forwarding. A narrower structural separation in MEM is a safer follow-on than adding predictors, store buffers, load bypasses, or deeper execute retiming, all of which have regressed or broken previously.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The latest successful structural change split RVFI-only metadata away from the hot execution payloads and improved fitness without changing CPI. The remaining MEM stage still computes and registers RVFI memory sideband fields in the same combinational block and pipeline boundary that drives dmem, load extraction, writeback, and forwarding. A narrower structural separation in MEM is a safer follow-on than adding predictors, store buffers, load bypasses, or deeper execute retiming, all of which have regressed or broken previously.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Split the MEM-stage memory sideband work into a parallel RVFI-only path while keeping the execution path limited to dmem request signals, load_data, rd, compact control, and valid. The hot MEM/WB execution register should no longer share bundle assignment, hold, and mux fanout with RVFI-only mem_addr, mem_rmask, mem_wmask, mem_rdata, mem_wdata, pc, source values, and instruction fields. Preserve the existing single-retire MEM/WB retirement point, dmem stall behavior, misalignment traps, RVFI aligned-memory reporting, and NRET=1 port shape. This should reduce routing and enable fanout around the MEM boundary with little CPI risk, extending the proven RVFI-shadow split into the last stage where sideband metadata still sits next to timing-sensitive load/w...\\n  Line 31: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r10s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Single hot loop branch predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts for this core regressed because they added immediate\\\\ndecode, target adders, return-stack state, counters, or multi-entry tag logic\\\\nto the fetch/decode control paths. CoreMark still spends cycles on repeated\\\\ntaken loop-closing branches, but the predictor must be smaller than the prior\\\\nBTB, RAS, JAL, and decode-backward experiments to have any chance of paying\\\\non Tang Nano 20K fitness.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts for this core regressed because they added immediate\\\\ndecode, target adders, return-stack state, counters, or multi-entry tag logic\\\\nto the fetch/decode control paths. CoreMark still spends cycles on repeated\\\\ntaken loop-closing branches, but the predictor must be smaller than the prior\\\\nBTB, RAS, JAL, and decode-backward experiments to have any chance of paying\\\\non Tang Nano 20K fitness.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add the smallest possible EX-trained predictor for one hot taken conditional\\\\nbranch: store a single branch PC and its already-resolved target when EX sees\\\\na taken backward branch, predict that target only on an exact PC match, and\\\\ninvalidate the entry when the same branch later resolves not-taken. Unlike the\\\\nfailed BTFNT and BTB attempts, IF performs no instruction immediate decode and\\\\nno target addition; it only compares the current PC with one registered PC and\\\\nmuxes between pc+4 and one registered target. Carry a one-bit predicted flag\\\\nand predicted next PC to EX so correct predictions suppress the normal taken\\\\nredirect while mismatches use the existing recovery path. This targets the most\\\\ncommon loop bubble with minimal predictor state and avoids JAL/JALR/RAS logic.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 1, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add minimal predicted-valid and predicted-next-PC metadata to IF/ID and ID/EX b...\\n  Line 32: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r11s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Narrow EX/MEM control payload\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core already benefits from splitting RVFI shadow metadata away from hot execution payloads, but the EX/MEM execution register still carries the full decoded ctrl_t bundle after execute has consumed most of it. Branch, jump, operand-select, ALU-op, and AUIPC/LUI control bits are no longer needed once the EX result, write data, redirect, and RVFI pc_next have been produced. Trimming those cold bits at the EX/MEM boundary should reduce flop count, hold/reset muxing, and routing pressure on the memory, forwarding, and stall paths without changing CPI.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core already benefits from splitting RVFI shadow metadata away from hot execution payloads, but the EX/MEM execution register still carries the full decoded ctrl_t bundle after execute has consumed most of it. Branch, jump, operand-select, ALU-op, and AUIPC/LUI control bits are no longer needed once the EX result, write data, redirect, and RVFI pc_next have been produced. Trimming those cold bits at the EX/MEM boundary should reduce flop count, hold/reset muxing, and routing pressure on the memory, forwarding, and stall paths without changing CPI.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Introduce a compact post-EX control payload for EX/MEM and MEM-stage consumers containing only the bits still live after execute: mem_read, mem_write, mem_width, mem_sext, reg_write, mem_to_reg, and is_illegal. Keep the full ctrl_t through ID/EX and inside EX where decode, branch, jump, ALU, AUIPC, and M-op decisions are still needed, then collapse to the compact control when registering EX/MEM. This is narrower than prior failed prediction/bypass/stall-hiding changes and differs from the regressed MEM/WB-only pruning by removing unused control before the hotter dmem, hazard, forwarding, and MEM/WB fanout cone.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}...\\n  Line 33: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r11s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Handshake IFID register\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current front end feeds imem_data directly into decode, immediate generation,\\\\nregfile read-address selection, load-use hazard checks, and the ID/EX register in\\\\none cycle. Prior structural attempts that added prediction, bypassing, store\\\\nbuffering, or EX retiming regressed by adding hot mux/control paths. The earlier\\\\nIF/ID-register attempt broke because it lacked a real consume/valid handshake,\\\\nnot because the imem-to-decode retiming target was inherently invalid.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current front end feeds imem_data directly into decode, immediate generation,\\\\nregfile read-address selection, load-use hazard checks, and the ID/EX register in\\\\none cycle. Prior structural attempts that added prediction, bypassing, store\\\\nbuffering, or EX retiming regressed by adding hot mux/control paths. The earlier\\\\nIF/ID-register attempt broke because it lacked a real consume/valid handshake,\\\\nnot because the imem-to-decode retiming target was inherently invalid.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a proper registered IF/ID boundary with an explicit valid bit and ID consume\\\\nhandshake. IF captures a fetched instruction only when imem is ready and the IF/ID\\\\nslot is empty or ID is consuming it; ID consumes exactly one valid entry when the\\\\ndownstream ID/EX register is not held. Load-use, dmem, and M-unit stalls hold the\\\\nIF/ID entry without duplicating it, while EX redirects flush the registered entry\\\\nand update PC with redirect priority. This should remove the imem-data-to-decoder\\\\ncombinational path and improve Fmax, with only a small CPI risk and no change to\\\\nthe NRET=1 retirement/RVFI contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Keep the IF/ID bundle shape but treat it as a registered valid payload with explicit consume semantics documented for the implementation.\\\"},...\",\"metadata\":{\"matches\":26,\"truncated\":false},\"title\":\"category: micro_opt|micro_opt|hyp-20260513-001-r5s0|hyp-20260513-001-r8s0|hyp-20260513-002-r9s1|forward|reg_file|decoder|alu\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778697729079,\"end\":1778697729096}},\"metadata\":{\"openai\":{\"itemId\":\"fc_06e10c426d2117f0016a04c600f1948199b864d541c78139d9\"}},\"id\":\"prt_e22a57431001T4QoIzDNTDgAkN\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1dd5ab9d1ffe2B2A54E2vq8V30\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e22a546c60015BuDFGSPL9BPSM\"}}\n./bench/gpt-5_5_medium/rep2/agent.log:85:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778698719927,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1dd4b964cffeq9qZa6LGSGNehP\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"read\",\"callID\":\"call_EmzxiVHKKw3ZrhzeviUGuOLG\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"filePath\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_5_medium-rep2/cores/bench/LESSONS.md\",\"offset\":1,\"limit\":200},\"output\":\"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_5_medium-rep2/cores/bench/LESSONS.md</path>\\n<type>file</type>\\n<content>\\n1: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r1s0 (improvement, +32.85%): Moving RV32M multiply/divide out of the combinational EX ALU behind a multicycle handshake can substantially improve FPGA fitness because CoreMark rarely needs M-op throughput while the removed arithmetic cone dominates Fmax.\\n2: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r1s1 (broken, n/a): Moving branch/JALR resolution into ID needs exhaustive operand-valid stalling and forwarding for decode-stage compares/base operands; stale early redirects can let wrong-path state reach writeback, which riscv-formal's reg check catches.\\n3: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r1s2 (regression, +21.76%): IF-stage BTFNT/JAL prediction can regress CoreMark when instruction-bit immediate decode and target adders sit directly on the fetch PC path, so front-end bubble savings must be weighed against Fmax-critical next-PC logic.\\n4: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r2s0 (regression, -1.92%): Replacing MEM-stage variable shifts with explicit byte/halfword lane case muxes did not help FPGA fitness, suggesting the synthesizer already maps these narrow shifts well and the added mux/control structure can slightly worsen routing or LUT packing.\\n5: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r2s1 (regression, -7.79%): A two-entry IF skid buffer did not pay for CoreMark's random imem stalls; the added fetch-valid/control and redirect-flush machinery can cost more FPGA fitness than the front-end bubbles it hides.\\n6: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r2s2 (regression, -44.37%): A tiny IF return-address stack can still badly hurt FPGA fitness when canonical-return detection, RAS top muxing, and predicted-PC metadata outweigh the CoreMark return bubbles it removes.\\n7: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r3s0 (broken, n/a): Keep source-aware load-use interlocks separate from RVFI operand reporting changes; masking unused rs fields can break cosim's retire trace even when the intended stall reduction logic is local to hazard_unit.\\n8: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r3s1 (regression, -14.30%): MEM-to-EX load bypass can lose FPGA fitness when dmem-ready gating, load extraction, and an extra EX operand forwarding select lengthen the critical path more than CoreMark benefits from removing one-cycle load-use bubbles.\\n9: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r3s2 (regression, -20.54%): Even decode-stage JAL prediction can regress when the early_redirect priority path, target adder, and predicted-PC metadata still add enough IF/ID/EX control cost to outweigh saving CoreMark's unconditional-jump bubble.\\n10: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r4s1 (broken, n/a): A registered IF/ID boundary must pair imem backpressure with an ID consume/valid handshake; holding a valid IF/ID entry while ID keeps advancing can duplicate instructions during CoreMark stalls.\\n11: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r4s2 (regression, -9.29%): Even a resolved-target BTB can lose FPGA fitness when IF-side tag/valid/counter arrays, target muxing, and predicted-PC metadata lengthen the next-PC path more than CoreMark benefits from avoiding loop redirect bubbles.\\n12: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r5s0 (improvement, +1.93%): Dropping reset clears from the integer register file can improve FPGA fitness because pipeline-valid reset and x0 read forcing preserve architectural behavior while avoiding reset fanout and per-register muxing in an always-active datapath block.\\n13: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r5s1 (regression, -6.89%): A one-entry ordered store buffer can lose CoreMark fitness when store-order interlocks, MMIO/trap classification, and dmem muxing add more MEM/control-path cost than random store-stall overlap recovers.\\n14: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r5s2 (regression, -61.05%): Even a single-entry loop predictor can badly regress when predicted-PC metadata, IF PC/tag compare muxing, and EX mismatch redirect logic lengthen critical control paths more than repeated CoreMark loop-branch bubble removal helps.\\n15: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r6s0 (regression, -2.40%): Pruning unused EX/MEM branch_taken/branch_target metadata did not improve FPGA fitness, suggesting these dead-looking pipeline fields were not on the binding path or were already optimized well enough that layout perturbation outweighed the small flop reduction.\\n16: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r6s1 (regression, -88.09%): Splitting EX with an EX1 operand register can destroy CoreMark fitness when it delays branch/JALR redirects and forces broad dependent-ALU interlocks, so operand-cone retiming must preserve bypass/redirect latency to pay off.\\n17: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r6s2 (regression, -48.05%): A JAL-only single-entry target predictor still regressed without fetch-side immediate decode because the IF PC compare/target mux, predicted-PC pipeline fields, and EX mismatch redirect logic outweighed CoreMark's repeated unconditional-jump bubble savings.\\n18: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r7s0 (regression, -53.28%): Replacing cold RV32M DIV/REM combinational operators with an iterative divider can severely regress CoreMark when the busy/start handshake, divider state, and 32-cycle latency perturb the hot pipeline more than removing the rarely used divide cone helps Fmax.\\n19: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r7s1 (broken, n/a): Registering dmem backpressure needs a real ready/valid or skid boundary at MEM; a lone pending/wait bit can hide the first stalled cycle from upstream control and let younger CoreMark memory-state updates be dropped or reordered.\\n20: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r8s0 (improvement, +2.79%): Sharing one sign/zero-extended M-unit multiplier across MULH signedness variants can improve FPGA fitness because CoreMark rarely needs M throughput and parallel 64-bit products add DSP/LUT routing pressure.\\n21: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r8s1 (regression, -0.33%): Preselecting MEM/WB writeback data inside mem_stage did not improve fitness, suggesting the load-vs-ALU writeback mux is not a binding FPGA path and adding another pipeline field can slightly worsen packing/routing.\\n22: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r8s2 (regression, +1.88%): Moving backward-branch prediction from IF to ID still regressed because the ID target adder, redirect/stall arbitration, predicted-PC metadata, and EX mispredict compare/control outweighed CoreMark loop-bubble savings.\\n23: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r9s0 (regression, -14.30%): Narrowing MEM/WB from full ctrl_t to a tiny packed mem_wb_ctrl_t can hurt FPGA fitness despite fewer flops, suggesting late-stage control fields were already optimized away or well-packed and the new struct/mapping perturbed routing more than it saved.\\n24: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r9s1 (improvement, +3.53%): Splitting RVFI-only metadata into parallel shadow payloads can improve FPGA fitness because it removes wide instruction/source/PC/memory fields from hot EX/MEM and MEM/WB forwarding, dmem, and writeback bundles without changing pipeline timing.\\n25: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r9s2 (regression, -31.51%): Decode-stage return prediction still regressed because link-register hazard compares, early redirect arbitration, and predicted-PC fields added more hot control/datapath cost than CoreMark recovered from avoiding canonical JALR return bubbles.\\n26: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r10s1 (regression, -5.39%): Further splitting MEM RVFI memory sidebands after the broader RVFI-shadow win can regress when it duplicates byte-mask/write-data logic and adds another MEM/WB payload boundary without removing a binding dmem/load/writeback path.\\n27: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r10s2 (broken, n/a): EX-trained branch predictors must keep prediction-hit suppression, exit invalidation redirects, and RVFI pc_next reporting aligned with the next retired instruction; riscv-formal's pc_fwd check catches recovery paths that eventually fetch correctly but break the retirement PC chain.\\n28: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r11s0 (regression, -5.83%): Narrowing EX/MEM and MEM/WB from full ctrl_t to post_ex_ctrl_t can still hurt FPGA fitness after RVFI-shadow splitting, implying the remaining control bits are not the binding path and the extra struct projection/packing perturbation outweighs small flop savings.\\n29: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r11s2 (regression, -12.97%): EX-side fall-through redirect elision can regress even without fetch predictor state because a resolved-target-vs-pc+4 comparator lengthens branch/jump control while CoreMark rarely benefits from target-equals-fall-through cases.\\n30: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r12s0 (regression, -8.26%): Class-bit recoding of RV32M alu_op values can lose FPGA fitness when the old eight-way compare was not binding and the new high-bit fanout perturbs ALU/M-unit decode packing more than it simplifies EX control.\\n31: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r12s1 (regression, -4.78%): Extending RVFI-shadow splitting back to ID/EX can regress when source-register numbers still remain on the hot hazard/forwarding path, so the parallel shadow duplicates flops/routing without removing the binding decode-to-EX logic.\\n\\n(End of file - total 31 lines)\\n</content>\",\"metadata\":{\"preview\":\"- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r1s0 (improvement, +32.85%): Moving RV32M multiply/divide out of the combinational EX ALU behind a multicycle handshake can substantially improve FPGA fitness because CoreMark rarely needs M-op throughput while the removed arithmetic cone dominates Fmax.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r1s1 (broken, n/a): Moving branch/JALR resolution into ID needs exhaustive operand-valid stalling and forwarding for decode-stage compares/base operands; stale early redirects can let wrong-path state reach writeback, which riscv-formal's reg check catches.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r1s2 (regression, +21.76%): IF-stage BTFNT/JAL prediction can regress CoreMark when instruction-bit immediate decode and target adders sit directly on the fetch PC path, so front-end bubble savings must be weighed against Fmax-critical next-PC logic.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r2s0 (regression, -1.92%): Replacing MEM-stage variable shifts with explicit byte/halfword lane case muxes did not help FPGA fitness, suggesting the synthesizer already maps these narrow shifts well and the added mux/control structure can slightly worsen routing or LUT packing.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r2s1 (regression, -7.79%): A two-entry IF skid buffer did not pay for CoreMark's random imem stalls; the added fetch-valid/control and redirect-flush machinery can cost more FPGA fitness than the front-end bubbles it hides.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r2s2 (regression, -44.37%): A tiny IF return-address stack can still badly hurt FPGA fitness when canonical-return detection, RAS top muxing, and predicted-PC metadata outweigh the CoreMark return bubbles it removes.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r3s0 (broken, n/a): Keep source-aware load-use interlocks separate from RVFI operand reporting changes; masking unused rs fields can break cosim's retire trace even when the intended stall reduction logic is local to hazard_unit.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r3s1 (regression, -14.30%): MEM-to-EX load bypass can lose FPGA fitness when dmem-ready gating, load extraction, and an extra EX operand forwarding select lengthen the critical path more than CoreMark benefits from removing one-cycle load-use bubbles.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r3s2 (regression, -20.54%): Even decode-stage JAL prediction can regress when the early_redirect priority path, target adder, and predicted-PC metadata still add enough IF/ID/EX control cost to outweigh saving CoreMark's unconditional-jump bubble.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r4s1 (broken, n/a): A registered IF/ID boundary must pair imem backpressure with an ID consume/valid handshake; holding a valid IF/ID entry while ID keeps advancing can duplicate instructions during CoreMark stalls.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r4s2 (regression, -9.29%): Even a resolved-target BTB can lose FPGA fitness when IF-side tag/valid/counter arrays, target muxing, and predicted-PC metadata lengthen the next-PC path more than CoreMark benefits from avoiding loop redirect bubbles.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r5s0 (improvement, +1.93%): Dropping reset clears from the integer register file can improve FPGA fitness because pipeline-valid reset and x0 read forcing preserve architectural behavior while avoiding reset fanout and per-register muxing in an always-active datapath block.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r5s1 (regression, -6.89%): A one-entry ordered store buffer can lose CoreMark fitness when store-order interlocks, MMIO/trap classification, and dmem muxing add more MEM/control-path cost than random store-stall overlap recovers.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r5s2 (regression, -61.05%): Even a single-entry loop predictor can badly regress when predicted-PC metadata, IF PC/tag compare muxing, and EX mismatch redirect logic lengthen critical control paths more than repeated CoreMark loop-branch bubble removal helps.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r6s0 (regression, -2.40%): Pruning unused EX/MEM branch_taken/branch_target metadata did not improve FPGA fitness, suggesting these dead-looking pipeline fields were not on the binding path or were already optimized well enough that layout perturbation outweighed the small flop reduction.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r6s1 (regression, -88.09%): Splitting EX with an EX1 operand register can destroy CoreMark fitness when it delays branch/JALR redirects and forces broad dependent-ALU interlocks, so operand-cone retiming must preserve bypass/redirect latency to pay off.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r6s2 (regression, -48.05%): A JAL-only single-entry target predictor still regressed without fetch-side immediate decode because the IF PC compare/target mux, predicted-PC pipeline fields, and EX mismatch redirect logic outweighed CoreMark's repeated unconditional-jump bubble savings.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r7s0 (regression, -53.28%): Replacing cold RV32M DIV/REM combinational operators with an iterative divider can severely regress CoreMark when the busy/start handshake, divider state, and 32-cycle latency perturb the hot pipeline more than removing the rarely used divide cone helps Fmax.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r7s1 (broken, n/a): Registering dmem backpressure needs a real ready/valid or skid boundary at MEM; a lone pending/wait bit can hide the first stalled cycle from upstream control and let younger CoreMark memory-state updates be dropped or reordered.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r8s0 (improvement, +2.79%): Sharing one sign/zero-extended M-unit multiplier across MULH signedness variants can improve FPGA fitness because CoreMark rarely needs M throughput and parallel 64-bit products add DSP/LUT routing pressure.\",\"truncated\":false,\"loaded\":[]},\"title\":\"cores/bench/LESSONS.md\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778698719919,\"end\":1778698719926}},\"metadata\":{\"openai\":{\"itemId\":\"fc_05addd08f0f3deb3016a04c9dfc8c081998e89504c9a49b727\"}},\"id\":\"prt_e22b492ac001GFkEkfvzNuYGkj\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1dd4b964cffeq9qZa6LGSGNehP\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e22b46a1a001l8U15GfqDxNT74\"}}\n./bench/gpt-5_5_medium/rep2/agent.log:86:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778698720096,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1dd4b964cffeq9qZa6LGSGNehP\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"grep\",\"callID\":\"call_XssHk72I1abwu32NrsHHLJda\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"pattern\":\"category: micro_opt|\\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\"|micro_opt\",\"path\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_5_medium-rep2/cores/bench/experiments\",\"include\":\"log.jsonl\"},\"output\":\"Found 13 matches\\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_5_medium-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:\\n  Line 1: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"baseline-bench-5327bba\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Baseline retest for cores/bench\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"improvement\\\", \\\"fitness\\\": 282.82, \\\"delta_pct\\\": 0.0, \\\"vs_baseline\\\": 0.0, \\\"fmax_mhz\\\": 127.03, \\\"ipc_coremark\\\": 2e-06, \\\"cycles\\\": 4491485, \\\"iterations\\\": 10, \\\"lut4\\\": 9563, \\\"ff\\\": 1866, \\\"seeds\\\": [128.72, 127.03, 123.02], \\\"formal_passed\\\": true, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": true, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"implementation_notes\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-05-13T13:08:01.185076Z\\\", \\\"round_id\\\": 0, \\\"slot\\\": 0}\\n\\n  Line 2: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r1s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Multicycle RV32M arithmetic unit\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current ALU computes every multiply and divide result combinationally in\\\\nthe EX stage, including signed and unsigned 64-bit products plus divider and\\\\nremainder operators. That wide arithmetic cone is a likely Fmax limiter on\\\\nTang Nano 20K, while CoreMark's hot path should not need single-cycle DIV/REM\\\\nthroughput. Trading rare M-extension latency for a shorter common EX critical\\\\npath should improve iterations/second through higher clock frequency.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current ALU computes every multiply and divide result combinationally in\\\\nthe EX stage, including signed and unsigned 64-bit products plus divider and\\\\nremainder operators. That wide arithmetic cone is a likely Fmax limiter on\\\\nTang Nano 20K, while CoreMark's hot path should not need single-cycle DIV/REM\\\\nthroughput. Trading rare M-extension latency for a shorter common EX critical\\\\npath should improve iterations/second through higher clock frequency.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Split RV32M operations out of the always-combinational ALU path behind a\\\\nsmall multicycle M-unit handshake in EX. Keep ADD/SUB/logic/branch/address\\\\noperations purely combinational, but when the decoded alu_op is MUL/DIV/REM,\\\\nhold ID/EX and EX/MEM until the iterative arithmetic result is ready, then\\\\nretire normally through the existing MEM/WB and RVFI path. This should remove\\\\ndivider and large multiplier logic from the ordinary EX-stage timing path;\\\\nthe added stalls only affect uncommon M instructions and should be outweighed\\\\nby improved Fmax on the FPGA fitness metric.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 6, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/alu.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Restrict the combinational ALU to fast RV32I operations and expose only lightweight M-op selection or helper logic needed by the new M-unit.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a multicycl...\\n  Line 5: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r2s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Simplify MEM load lane muxes\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"After the successful M-unit split, the remaining always-active timing paths are more likely to be ordinary RV32I datapath logic than rare multiply/divide arithmetic. The MEM stage currently uses variable shifts for load extraction and shifted masks for byte lanes, which can synthesize into wider mux/barrel-shifter logic on the FPGA load-to-WB path. CoreMark performs many byte, halfword, and word accesses, so a smaller MEM-stage cone should improve Fmax without changing CPI.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"After the successful M-unit split, the remaining always-active timing paths are more likely to be ordinary RV32I datapath logic than rare multiply/divide arithmetic. The MEM stage currently uses variable shifts for load extraction and shifted masks for byte lanes, which can synthesize into wider mux/barrel-shifter logic on the FPGA load-to-WB path. CoreMark performs many byte, halfword, and word accesses, so a smaller MEM-stage cone should improve Fmax without changing CPI.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Rewrite the MEM-stage byte-lane handling as explicit case muxes keyed by addr[1:0] instead of expressions like dmem_rdata >> (addr[1:0] * 8) and mask << addr[1:0]. Keep the same misalignment trap behavior, RVFI aligned mem_addr reporting, replicated store data convention, and MEM/WB retirement boundary. This should reduce LUT depth and routing on the common load-data path while preserving the existing five-stage control flow and single-retire NRET=1 contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace variable load-data shifts and shifted byte masks with explicit addr[1:0] case muxes for LB/LBU/LH/LHU/LW and store masks.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace variable load...\\n  Line 8: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r3s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Source-aware load-use stalls\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current load-use hazard detector compares a load destination against the\\\\nraw rs1 and rs2 bitfields of the following instruction, regardless of whether\\\\nthat instruction actually consumes those operands. For I-type immediates,\\\\nloads, JALR, LUI, AUIPC, JAL, FENCE, and EBREAK, some of those fields are\\\\nimmediates or destination bits rather than true sources. Any accidental match\\\\nwith the preceding load rd inserts an unnecessary bubble, directly reducing\\\\nCoreMark iterations per cycle without improving correctness.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current load-use hazard detector compares a load destination against the\\\\nraw rs1 and rs2 bitfields of the following instruction, regardless of whether\\\\nthat instruction actually consumes those operands. For I-type immediates,\\\\nloads, JALR, LUI, AUIPC, JAL, FENCE, and EBREAK, some of those fields are\\\\nimmediates or destination bits rather than true sources. Any accidental match\\\\nwith the preceding load rd inserts an unnecessary bubble, directly reducing\\\\nCoreMark iterations per cycle without improving correctness.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Make the load-use interlock source-aware by qualifying the rd comparisons with\\\\nsimple decoded uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 signals for the instruction currently in\\\\nIF/ID. Keep the existing one-cycle stall for real load consumers, but avoid\\\\nstalling on unused source fields such as OP-IMM rs2 bits, U/J-type operand\\\\nfields, and SYSTEM/FENCE encodings. This should improve CPI under CoreMark's\\\\nload-heavy code while adding only small opcode decode logic off the main ALU\\\\nand memory datapaths, so Fmax should be largely preserved.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Derive or wire IF/ID source-use qualifiers into the hazard unit without changing the NRET=1 top-level RVFI interface.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl...\\n  Line 11: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r4s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Source-qualified load interlock\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current load-use detector compares the preceding load rd against both raw\\\\nsource bitfields of the following instruction, even when those bits are not\\\\narchitecturally consumed. CoreMark has many loads and immediate/control\\\\ninstructions, so accidental rd matches can inject bubbles that reduce\\\\niterations per cycle without protecting any real dependency.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current load-use detector compares the preceding load rd against both raw\\\\nsource bitfields of the following instruction, even when those bits are not\\\\narchitecturally consumed. CoreMark has many loads and immediate/control\\\\ninstructions, so accidental rd matches can inject bubbles that reduce\\\\niterations per cycle without protecting any real dependency.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a tiny source-use decode for the IF/ID instruction and qualify the existing\\\\nload-use interlock with uses_rs1 and uses_rs2. This is a narrower retry of the\\\\nearlier source-aware load-use idea: do not mask or reinterpret RVFI operand\\\\nreporting, and do not add MEM bypass selects or fetch prediction paths. The\\\\ncore should keep the one-cycle stall for true load consumers while avoiding\\\\nfalse stalls on OP-IMM rs2 fields, U/J-type immediates, FENCE, SYSTEM/EBREAK,\\\\nand other instructions that do not read both source registers.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Derive simple IF/ID uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 qualifiers from opcode/funct class and pass them only to the hazard unit.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Gate load-use rd comparisons with the source-use qualifiers while preserving existing dmem, imem, redirect, and M-unit stall priority.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Derive simple IF/ID use...\\n  Line 14: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r5s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Drop regfile reset fanout\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current register file resets all 32 architectural registers with a for-loop,\\\\nwhich forces reset muxing or reset routing onto the whole small RF array. After\\\\nthe M-unit split, remaining FPGA Fmax is likely limited by ordinary datapath\\\\nand control fanout rather than rare arithmetic. CoreMark initializes the state\\\\nit uses, x0 is already hardwired on reads, and RVFI correctness depends on\\\\nretired architectural behavior rather than power-on values of unused registers.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current register file resets all 32 architectural registers with a for-loop,\\\\nwhich forces reset muxing or reset routing onto the whole small RF array. After\\\\nthe M-unit split, remaining FPGA Fmax is likely limited by ordinary datapath\\\\nand control fanout rather than rare arithmetic. CoreMark initializes the state\\\\nit uses, x0 is already hardwired on reads, and RVFI correctness depends on\\\\nretired architectural behavior rather than power-on values of unused registers.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Remove the explicit reset clear from the integer register file and keep only\\\\nthe synchronous write path for nonzero destination registers. Reads of x0 stay\\\\nforced to zero, same-cycle write-first bypass stays unchanged, and reset of the\\\\npipeline control/valid bits still prevents spurious retirement after reset.\\\\nThis should reduce reset fanout and per-register muxing in a timing-sensitive,\\\\nalways-active block without changing CPI, branch behavior, memory behavior, or\\\\nthe NRET=1 RVFI interface. The change is deliberately narrower than prior\\\\nfailed micro-opts: it does not touch hazard detection, RVFI operand reporting,\\\\nMEM load extraction, or any front-end prediction path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/reg_file.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove the reset-time loop that clears all 32 registers while preser...\\n  Line 17: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r6s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Prune dead EX/MEM branch metadata\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core has already won most of its fitness from removing broad\\\\ntiming/fanout costs, while recent attempts that add prediction, buffering,\\\\nbypassing, or memory-ordering control have regressed. The EX/MEM bundle still\\\\ncarries branch_taken and branch_target fields that are assigned every cycle\\\\nbut are not consumed by MEM, WB, RVFI, hazard, or forwarding logic. Those 33\\\\ndead flops and their input adders/mux routes sit in an always-active pipeline\\\\nboundary, so pruning them is a low-risk micro-optimization for LUT/FF count\\\\nand routing delay without changing CPI.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core has already won most of its fitness from removing broad\\\\ntiming/fanout costs, while recent attempts that add prediction, buffering,\\\\nbypassing, or memory-ordering control have regressed. The EX/MEM bundle still\\\\ncarries branch_taken and branch_target fields that are assigned every cycle\\\\nbut are not consumed by MEM, WB, RVFI, hazard, or forwarding logic. Those 33\\\\ndead flops and their input adders/mux routes sit in an always-active pipeline\\\\nboundary, so pruning them is a low-risk micro-optimization for LUT/FF count\\\\nand routing delay without changing CPI.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Remove the unused branch_taken and branch_target fields from ex_mem_t and stop\\\\nregistering them in ex_stage. Keep redirect and pc_next computation exactly as\\\\nthey are today, because pc_next is the RVFI-visible resolved next PC and the\\\\nredirect output is still the only live control-flow signal. This should trim\\\\ndead sequential state and reduce EX-stage fanout/routing pressure while\\\\npreserving the NRET=1 RVFI contract, trap behavior, memory behavior, and all\\\\nexisting stall/flush priorities.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 1, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove unused branch_taken and...\\n  Line 20: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r7s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Iterative M divider\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The largest proven fitness gain for this core came from moving RV32M arithmetic\\\\nout of the ordinary EX ALU path, but the current m_unit still contains wide\\\\ncombinational DIV/REM operators behind a one-cycle result register. Even if\\\\nthose operations are rare in CoreMark, the FPGA still has to place and time\\\\nthe divider/remainder cone, so it can cap Fmax or perturb routing for the hot\\\\nRV32I pipeline.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The largest proven fitness gain for this core came from moving RV32M arithmetic\\\\nout of the ordinary EX ALU path, but the current m_unit still contains wide\\\\ncombinational DIV/REM operators behind a one-cycle result register. Even if\\\\nthose operations are rare in CoreMark, the FPGA still has to place and time\\\\nthe divider/remainder cone, so it can cap Fmax or perturb routing for the hot\\\\nRV32I pipeline.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the one-cycle DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU implementation in m_unit with a small\\\\niterative restoring divider that runs only after a divide or remainder starts.\\\\nKeep MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU on the existing short registered path so any\\\\nmultiply use does not pay a 32-cycle penalty, and keep the existing EX-stage\\\\nhold/valid handshake as the only pipeline interface. This should remove the\\\\nexpensive combinational divider/remainder logic from the FPGA timing closure\\\\nproblem while adding CPI only for uncommon divide/remainder instructions.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/m_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Implement DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU with an iterative signed/unsigned divider state machine while preserving ALTOPS behavior under RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Adjust the existing M-unit busy/valid handshake only as needed so EX holds until multi-cycle DIV/REM completion and still clears completed results exactly once.\\\"}]}, \\\"expec...\\n  Line 23: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r8s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Share M-unit multiplier hardware\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core already removed RV32M arithmetic from the ordinary EX\\\\nALU path, but m_unit still builds three separate 64-bit multiplier products\\\\nin parallel for MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU signedness variants. CoreMark uses\\\\nM operations rarely enough that throughput beyond the existing one-cycle\\\\nregistered M-unit is not the main limiter, while the extra DSP/LUT/routing\\\\npressure can still depress FPGA Fmax or perturb placement of the hot RV32I\\\\npipeline.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core already removed RV32M arithmetic from the ordinary EX\\\\nALU path, but m_unit still builds three separate 64-bit multiplier products\\\\nin parallel for MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU signedness variants. CoreMark uses\\\\nM operations rarely enough that throughput beyond the existing one-cycle\\\\nregistered M-unit is not the main limiter, while the extra DSP/LUT/routing\\\\npressure can still depress FPGA Fmax or perturb placement of the hot RV32I\\\\npipeline.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the parallel signed-signed, unsigned-unsigned, and signed-unsigned\\\\nproduct generation in m_unit with one shared signed multiplier fed by\\\\nsign/zero-extended operands selected from the M opcode. MUL can take the low\\\\nhalf of the same product because low 32 bits are independent of operand\\\\nsignedness; MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU select the high half with the appropriate\\\\noperand extension. Keep the existing start/valid/clear handshake, one-cycle\\\\nM latency, DIV/REM behavior, and RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions unchanged.\\\\nThis should trim multiplier area and routing without adding CPI, front-end\\\\nprediction logic, memory ordering state, or any RVFI-visible behavior change.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/m_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Use a single sign-controlled widened multiplier for MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU while pres...\\n  Line 26: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r9s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Narrow MEM/WB control state\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent micro-optimization wins came from removing always-present FPGA timing\\\\nand routing pressure, while attempts that added prediction, bypassing, or\\\\nstall-hiding control usually regressed. The current MEM/WB register still\\\\ncarries the full decode control bundle even though the writeback and RVFI\\\\nretirement logic only consume a few bits after the MEM stage. Pruning that\\\\ncold control state should reduce flop count, muxing, and routing without\\\\nchanging CoreMark CPI or touching the external NRET=1 contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent micro-optimization wins came from removing always-present FPGA timing\\\\nand routing pressure, while attempts that added prediction, bypassing, or\\\\nstall-hiding control usually regressed. The current MEM/WB register still\\\\ncarries the full decode control bundle even though the writeback and RVFI\\\\nretirement logic only consume a few bits after the MEM stage. Pruning that\\\\ncold control state should reduce flop count, muxing, and routing without\\\\nchanging CoreMark CPI or touching the external NRET=1 contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the full ctrl_t field in mem_wb_t with a narrow retirement/writeback\\\\ncontrol subset containing only reg_write, mem_to_reg, and is_illegal. Keep\\\\nthe existing full ctrl_t through ID/EX and EX/MEM, because the execute and\\\\nmemory stages still need branch, memory width, sign-extension, and M-op\\\\ncontrols. In mem_stage, collapse ctrl_with_trap into the narrow MEM/WB\\\\nfields after misalignment handling, and update wb_stage and core RVFI trap\\\\nreferences to use the narrow fields. This should trim unused MEM/WB control\\\\nflops and fanout while preserving all memory masks/data, load results,\\\\nwriteback behavior, RVFI order, and trap reporting.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Introduce a narrow M...\\n  Line 29: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r10s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Prune unused LUI control bit\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent wins came from removing always-present arithmetic, reset, and RVFI\\\\nrouting pressure, while broader CPI-oriented changes and control prediction\\\\nusually regressed. The current control bundle still carries an is_lui bit\\\\nthrough decode and every pipeline stage even though LUI execution is already\\\\nfully encoded by alu_op=ALU_LUI and alu_src=1. That unused bit adds one more\\\\ndecoded signal, pipeline flop, reset/hold mux input, and routing load across\\\\nhot stage boundaries without contributing to architectural behavior.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent wins came from removing always-present arithmetic, reset, and RVFI\\\\nrouting pressure, while broader CPI-oriented changes and control prediction\\\\nusually regressed. The current control bundle still carries an is_lui bit\\\\nthrough decode and every pipeline stage even though LUI execution is already\\\\nfully encoded by alu_op=ALU_LUI and alu_src=1. That unused bit adds one more\\\\ndecoded signal, pipeline flop, reset/hold mux input, and routing load across\\\\nhot stage boundaries without contributing to architectural behavior.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Remove the redundant is_lui signal from the decoder interface and ctrl_t,\\\\nand delete the corresponding ID-stage temporary and bundle assignment. Keep\\\\nLUI behavior unchanged by continuing to set alu_op=ALU_LUI, alu_src=1, and\\\\nreg_write=1 in the existing validated LUI decode arm. This is a narrow\\\\nno-CPI micro-optimization that should slightly reduce control-bundle width,\\\\nregister enables, reset/hold muxing, and decode-to-EX routing pressure while\\\\npreserving the NRET=1 RVFI contract, trap discipline, memory behavior, and\\\\nall existing stall/forwarding logic.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 1, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove the unused is_lui field from ctrl_t so it no longer propagates throug...\\n  Line 32: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r11s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Narrow EX/MEM control payload\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core already benefits from splitting RVFI shadow metadata away from hot execution payloads, but the EX/MEM execution register still carries the full decoded ctrl_t bundle after execute has consumed most of it. Branch, jump, operand-select, ALU-op, and AUIPC/LUI control bits are no longer needed once the EX result, write data, redirect, and RVFI pc_next have been produced. Trimming those cold bits at the EX/MEM boundary should reduce flop count, hold/reset muxing, and routing pressure on the memory, forwarding, and stall paths without changing CPI.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core already benefits from splitting RVFI shadow metadata away from hot execution payloads, but the EX/MEM execution register still carries the full decoded ctrl_t bundle after execute has consumed most of it. Branch, jump, operand-select, ALU-op, and AUIPC/LUI control bits are no longer needed once the EX result, write data, redirect, and RVFI pc_next have been produced. Trimming those cold bits at the EX/MEM boundary should reduce flop count, hold/reset muxing, and routing pressure on the memory, forwarding, and stall paths without changing CPI.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Introduce a compact post-EX control payload for EX/MEM and MEM-stage consumers containing only the bits still live after execute: mem_read, mem_write, mem_width, mem_sext, reg_write, mem_to_reg, and is_illegal. Keep the full ctrl_t through ID/EX and inside EX where decode, branch, jump, ALU, AUIPC, and M-op decisions are still needed, then collapse to the compact control when registering EX/MEM. This is narrower than prior failed prediction/bypass/stall-hiding changes and differs from the regressed MEM/WB-only pruning by removing unused control before the hotter dmem, hazard, forwarding, and MEM/WB fanout cone.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}...\\n  Line 35: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r12s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Class-bit M-op encoding\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent successful micro-optimizations improved fitness by removing always-active FPGA timing and routing pressure without adding new control-flow or memory machinery. The current EX stage still detects RV32M operations with eight separate alu_op equality comparisons, and that result feeds the M-unit start/busy path, redirect suppression, and EX/MEM bubble control. CoreMark rarely benefits from complex M-op detection logic being on this hot execute-control path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent successful micro-optimizations improved fitness by removing always-active FPGA timing and routing pressure without adding new control-flow or memory machinery. The current EX stage still detects RV32M operations with eight separate alu_op equality comparisons, and that result feeds the M-unit start/busy path, redirect suppression, and EX/MEM bubble control. CoreMark rarely benefits from complex M-op detection logic being on this hot execute-control path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Recode the internal ALU operation constants so every RV32M operation has alu_op[4]=1 while all ordinary RV32I operations keep alu_op[4]=0. Then replace the EX-stage is_m_op eight-way compare tree with a single in.valid && in.ctrl.alu_op[4] test. This preserves the decoder's legal-instruction checks, ALU and M-unit case behavior, RVFI retirement, and the existing one-cycle M-unit handshake, but should reduce LUT depth and fanout in the execute control path with no CPI cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Reassign ALU_MUL through ALU_REMU localparams into a contiguous high-bit class while leaving RV32I ALU ops below bit 4.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the explicit eight-operation is_m_op comparison with a single alu_op class-bit check.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delt...\",\"metadata\":{\"matches\":13,\"truncated\":false},\"title\":\"category: micro_opt|\\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\"|micro_opt\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778698720086,\"end\":1778698720095}},\"metadata\":{\"openai\":{\"itemId\":\"fc_05addd08f0f3deb3016a04c9dfc8d48199af742a80f45157c5\"}},\"id\":\"prt_e22b493540017d97VfssSEUAFX\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1dd4b964cffeq9qZa6LGSGNehP\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e22b46a1a001l8U15GfqDxNT74\"}}\n./bench/gpt-5_5_medium/rep2/agent.log:112:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778699818472,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1dd3adde4ffep5jPLBvr070Y0N\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"read\",\"callID\":\"call_twbje7dlcfBvjmuzVJFFTq19\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"filePath\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_5_medium-rep2/cores/bench/LESSONS.md\",\"offset\":1,\"limit\":220},\"output\":\"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_5_medium-rep2/cores/bench/LESSONS.md</path>\\n<type>file</type>\\n<content>\\n1: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r1s0 (improvement, +32.85%): Moving RV32M multiply/divide out of the combinational EX ALU behind a multicycle handshake can substantially improve FPGA fitness because CoreMark rarely needs M-op throughput while the removed arithmetic cone dominates Fmax.\\n2: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r1s1 (broken, n/a): Moving branch/JALR resolution into ID needs exhaustive operand-valid stalling and forwarding for decode-stage compares/base operands; stale early redirects can let wrong-path state reach writeback, which riscv-formal's reg check catches.\\n3: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r1s2 (regression, +21.76%): IF-stage BTFNT/JAL prediction can regress CoreMark when instruction-bit immediate decode and target adders sit directly on the fetch PC path, so front-end bubble savings must be weighed against Fmax-critical next-PC logic.\\n4: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r2s0 (regression, -1.92%): Replacing MEM-stage variable shifts with explicit byte/halfword lane case muxes did not help FPGA fitness, suggesting the synthesizer already maps these narrow shifts well and the added mux/control structure can slightly worsen routing or LUT packing.\\n5: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r2s1 (regression, -7.79%): A two-entry IF skid buffer did not pay for CoreMark's random imem stalls; the added fetch-valid/control and redirect-flush machinery can cost more FPGA fitness than the front-end bubbles it hides.\\n6: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r2s2 (regression, -44.37%): A tiny IF return-address stack can still badly hurt FPGA fitness when canonical-return detection, RAS top muxing, and predicted-PC metadata outweigh the CoreMark return bubbles it removes.\\n7: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r3s0 (broken, n/a): Keep source-aware load-use interlocks separate from RVFI operand reporting changes; masking unused rs fields can break cosim's retire trace even when the intended stall reduction logic is local to hazard_unit.\\n8: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r3s1 (regression, -14.30%): MEM-to-EX load bypass can lose FPGA fitness when dmem-ready gating, load extraction, and an extra EX operand forwarding select lengthen the critical path more than CoreMark benefits from removing one-cycle load-use bubbles.\\n9: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r3s2 (regression, -20.54%): Even decode-stage JAL prediction can regress when the early_redirect priority path, target adder, and predicted-PC metadata still add enough IF/ID/EX control cost to outweigh saving CoreMark's unconditional-jump bubble.\\n10: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r4s1 (broken, n/a): A registered IF/ID boundary must pair imem backpressure with an ID consume/valid handshake; holding a valid IF/ID entry while ID keeps advancing can duplicate instructions during CoreMark stalls.\\n11: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r4s2 (regression, -9.29%): Even a resolved-target BTB can lose FPGA fitness when IF-side tag/valid/counter arrays, target muxing, and predicted-PC metadata lengthen the next-PC path more than CoreMark benefits from avoiding loop redirect bubbles.\\n12: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r5s0 (improvement, +1.93%): Dropping reset clears from the integer register file can improve FPGA fitness because pipeline-valid reset and x0 read forcing preserve architectural behavior while avoiding reset fanout and per-register muxing in an always-active datapath block.\\n13: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r5s1 (regression, -6.89%): A one-entry ordered store buffer can lose CoreMark fitness when store-order interlocks, MMIO/trap classification, and dmem muxing add more MEM/control-path cost than random store-stall overlap recovers.\\n14: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r5s2 (regression, -61.05%): Even a single-entry loop predictor can badly regress when predicted-PC metadata, IF PC/tag compare muxing, and EX mismatch redirect logic lengthen critical control paths more than repeated CoreMark loop-branch bubble removal helps.\\n15: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r6s0 (regression, -2.40%): Pruning unused EX/MEM branch_taken/branch_target metadata did not improve FPGA fitness, suggesting these dead-looking pipeline fields were not on the binding path or were already optimized well enough that layout perturbation outweighed the small flop reduction.\\n16: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r6s1 (regression, -88.09%): Splitting EX with an EX1 operand register can destroy CoreMark fitness when it delays branch/JALR redirects and forces broad dependent-ALU interlocks, so operand-cone retiming must preserve bypass/redirect latency to pay off.\\n17: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r6s2 (regression, -48.05%): A JAL-only single-entry target predictor still regressed without fetch-side immediate decode because the IF PC compare/target mux, predicted-PC pipeline fields, and EX mismatch redirect logic outweighed CoreMark's repeated unconditional-jump bubble savings.\\n18: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r7s0 (regression, -53.28%): Replacing cold RV32M DIV/REM combinational operators with an iterative divider can severely regress CoreMark when the busy/start handshake, divider state, and 32-cycle latency perturb the hot pipeline more than removing the rarely used divide cone helps Fmax.\\n19: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r7s1 (broken, n/a): Registering dmem backpressure needs a real ready/valid or skid boundary at MEM; a lone pending/wait bit can hide the first stalled cycle from upstream control and let younger CoreMark memory-state updates be dropped or reordered.\\n20: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r8s0 (improvement, +2.79%): Sharing one sign/zero-extended M-unit multiplier across MULH signedness variants can improve FPGA fitness because CoreMark rarely needs M throughput and parallel 64-bit products add DSP/LUT routing pressure.\\n21: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r8s1 (regression, -0.33%): Preselecting MEM/WB writeback data inside mem_stage did not improve fitness, suggesting the load-vs-ALU writeback mux is not a binding FPGA path and adding another pipeline field can slightly worsen packing/routing.\\n22: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r8s2 (regression, +1.88%): Moving backward-branch prediction from IF to ID still regressed because the ID target adder, redirect/stall arbitration, predicted-PC metadata, and EX mispredict compare/control outweighed CoreMark loop-bubble savings.\\n23: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r9s0 (regression, -14.30%): Narrowing MEM/WB from full ctrl_t to a tiny packed mem_wb_ctrl_t can hurt FPGA fitness despite fewer flops, suggesting late-stage control fields were already optimized away or well-packed and the new struct/mapping perturbed routing more than it saved.\\n24: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r9s1 (improvement, +3.53%): Splitting RVFI-only metadata into parallel shadow payloads can improve FPGA fitness because it removes wide instruction/source/PC/memory fields from hot EX/MEM and MEM/WB forwarding, dmem, and writeback bundles without changing pipeline timing.\\n25: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r9s2 (regression, -31.51%): Decode-stage return prediction still regressed because link-register hazard compares, early redirect arbitration, and predicted-PC fields added more hot control/datapath cost than CoreMark recovered from avoiding canonical JALR return bubbles.\\n26: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r10s1 (regression, -5.39%): Further splitting MEM RVFI memory sidebands after the broader RVFI-shadow win can regress when it duplicates byte-mask/write-data logic and adds another MEM/WB payload boundary without removing a binding dmem/load/writeback path.\\n27: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r10s2 (broken, n/a): EX-trained branch predictors must keep prediction-hit suppression, exit invalidation redirects, and RVFI pc_next reporting aligned with the next retired instruction; riscv-formal's pc_fwd check catches recovery paths that eventually fetch correctly but break the retirement PC chain.\\n28: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r11s0 (regression, -5.83%): Narrowing EX/MEM and MEM/WB from full ctrl_t to post_ex_ctrl_t can still hurt FPGA fitness after RVFI-shadow splitting, implying the remaining control bits are not the binding path and the extra struct projection/packing perturbation outweighs small flop savings.\\n29: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r11s2 (regression, -12.97%): EX-side fall-through redirect elision can regress even without fetch predictor state because a resolved-target-vs-pc+4 comparator lengthens branch/jump control while CoreMark rarely benefits from target-equals-fall-through cases.\\n30: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r12s0 (regression, -8.26%): Class-bit recoding of RV32M alu_op values can lose FPGA fitness when the old eight-way compare was not binding and the new high-bit fanout perturbs ALU/M-unit decode packing more than it simplifies EX control.\\n31: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r12s1 (regression, -4.78%): Extending RVFI-shadow splitting back to ID/EX can regress when source-register numbers still remain on the hot hazard/forwarding path, so the parallel shadow duplicates flops/routing without removing the binding decode-to-EX logic.\\n32: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r13s0 (regression, -15.41%): Pruning a single unused `ctrl_t` bit like `is_lui` can still regress FPGA fitness because packed control-bundle layout and decode/ID register routing perturbation may dominate any tiny flop-width reduction.\\n33: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r13s1 (regression, -4.03%): Moving write-first WB bypass muxes from `reg_file` into `id_stage` can regress because the WB write port still fans into decode and lengthens the ID/EX capture path while the original regfile bypass was not the binding structure.\\n\\n(End of file - total 33 lines)\\n</content>\",\"metadata\":{\"preview\":\"- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r1s0 (improvement, +32.85%): Moving RV32M multiply/divide out of the combinational EX ALU behind a multicycle handshake can substantially improve FPGA fitness because CoreMark rarely needs M-op throughput while the removed arithmetic cone dominates Fmax.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r1s1 (broken, n/a): Moving branch/JALR resolution into ID needs exhaustive operand-valid stalling and forwarding for decode-stage compares/base operands; stale early redirects can let wrong-path state reach writeback, which riscv-formal's reg check catches.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r1s2 (regression, +21.76%): IF-stage BTFNT/JAL prediction can regress CoreMark when instruction-bit immediate decode and target adders sit directly on the fetch PC path, so front-end bubble savings must be weighed against Fmax-critical next-PC logic.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r2s0 (regression, -1.92%): Replacing MEM-stage variable shifts with explicit byte/halfword lane case muxes did not help FPGA fitness, suggesting the synthesizer already maps these narrow shifts well and the added mux/control structure can slightly worsen routing or LUT packing.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r2s1 (regression, -7.79%): A two-entry IF skid buffer did not pay for CoreMark's random imem stalls; the added fetch-valid/control and redirect-flush machinery can cost more FPGA fitness than the front-end bubbles it hides.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r2s2 (regression, -44.37%): A tiny IF return-address stack can still badly hurt FPGA fitness when canonical-return detection, RAS top muxing, and predicted-PC metadata outweigh the CoreMark return bubbles it removes.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r3s0 (broken, n/a): Keep source-aware load-use interlocks separate from RVFI operand reporting changes; masking unused rs fields can break cosim's retire trace even when the intended stall reduction logic is local to hazard_unit.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r3s1 (regression, -14.30%): MEM-to-EX load bypass can lose FPGA fitness when dmem-ready gating, load extraction, and an extra EX operand forwarding select lengthen the critical path more than CoreMark benefits from removing one-cycle load-use bubbles.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r3s2 (regression, -20.54%): Even decode-stage JAL prediction can regress when the early_redirect priority path, target adder, and predicted-PC metadata still add enough IF/ID/EX control cost to outweigh saving CoreMark's unconditional-jump bubble.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r4s1 (broken, n/a): A registered IF/ID boundary must pair imem backpressure with an ID consume/valid handshake; holding a valid IF/ID entry while ID keeps advancing can duplicate instructions during CoreMark stalls.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r4s2 (regression, -9.29%): Even a resolved-target BTB can lose FPGA fitness when IF-side tag/valid/counter arrays, target muxing, and predicted-PC metadata lengthen the next-PC path more than CoreMark benefits from avoiding loop redirect bubbles.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r5s0 (improvement, +1.93%): Dropping reset clears from the integer register file can improve FPGA fitness because pipeline-valid reset and x0 read forcing preserve architectural behavior while avoiding reset fanout and per-register muxing in an always-active datapath block.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r5s1 (regression, -6.89%): A one-entry ordered store buffer can lose CoreMark fitness when store-order interlocks, MMIO/trap classification, and dmem muxing add more MEM/control-path cost than random store-stall overlap recovers.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r5s2 (regression, -61.05%): Even a single-entry loop predictor can badly regress when predicted-PC metadata, IF PC/tag compare muxing, and EX mismatch redirect logic lengthen critical control paths more than repeated CoreMark loop-branch bubble removal helps.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r6s0 (regression, -2.40%): Pruning unused EX/MEM branch_taken/branch_target metadata did not improve FPGA fitness, suggesting these dead-looking pipeline fields were not on the binding path or were already optimized well enough that layout perturbation outweighed the small flop reduction.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r6s1 (regression, -88.09%): Splitting EX with an EX1 operand register can destroy CoreMark fitness when it delays branch/JALR redirects and forces broad dependent-ALU interlocks, so operand-cone retiming must preserve bypass/redirect latency to pay off.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r6s2 (regression, -48.05%): A JAL-only single-entry target predictor still regressed without fetch-side immediate decode because the IF PC compare/target mux, predicted-PC pipeline fields, and EX mismatch redirect logic outweighed CoreMark's repeated unconditional-jump bubble savings.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r7s0 (regression, -53.28%): Replacing cold RV32M DIV/REM combinational operators with an iterative divider can severely regress CoreMark when the busy/start handshake, divider state, and 32-cycle latency perturb the hot pipeline more than removing the rarely used divide cone helps Fmax.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r7s1 (broken, n/a): Registering dmem backpressure needs a real ready/valid or skid boundary at MEM; a lone pending/wait bit can hide the first stalled cycle from upstream control and let younger CoreMark memory-state updates be dropped or reordered.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r8s0 (improvement, +2.79%): Sharing one sign/zero-extended M-unit multiplier across MULH signedness variants can improve FPGA fitness because CoreMark rarely needs M throughput and parallel 64-bit products add DSP/LUT routing pressure.\",\"truncated\":false,\"loaded\":[]},\"title\":\"cores/bench/LESSONS.md\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778699818466,\"end\":1778699818471}},\"metadata\":{\"openai\":{\"itemId\":\"fc_0fc1f8544fd94f88016a04ce2a3d548198a8c3a74387fd5e6c\"}},\"id\":\"prt_e22c555df002a6abmKIUy1JSqr\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1dd3adde4ffep5jPLBvr070Y0N\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e22c52273001KQeLK0QIp7jPyP\"}}\n./bench/gpt-5_5_medium/rep2/agent.log:113:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778699818494,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1dd3adde4ffep5jPLBvr070Y0N\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"grep\",\"callID\":\"call_DjMIF07KJ7xl7YNplpyMko7c\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"pattern\":\"micro_opt|category: micro_opt|\\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\"|hyp-20260513-001-r14s0\",\"path\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_5_medium-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\",\"include\":\"log.jsonl\"},\"output\":\"Found 14 matches\\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_5_medium-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:\\n  Line 1: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"baseline-bench-5327bba\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Baseline retest for cores/bench\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"improvement\\\", \\\"fitness\\\": 282.82, \\\"delta_pct\\\": 0.0, \\\"vs_baseline\\\": 0.0, \\\"fmax_mhz\\\": 127.03, \\\"ipc_coremark\\\": 2e-06, \\\"cycles\\\": 4491485, \\\"iterations\\\": 10, \\\"lut4\\\": 9563, \\\"ff\\\": 1866, \\\"seeds\\\": [128.72, 127.03, 123.02], \\\"formal_passed\\\": true, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": true, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"implementation_notes\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-05-13T13:08:01.185076Z\\\", \\\"round_id\\\": 0, \\\"slot\\\": 0}\\n\\n  Line 2: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r1s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Multicycle RV32M arithmetic unit\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current ALU computes every multiply and divide result combinationally in\\\\nthe EX stage, including signed and unsigned 64-bit products plus divider and\\\\nremainder operators. That wide arithmetic cone is a likely Fmax limiter on\\\\nTang Nano 20K, while CoreMark's hot path should not need single-cycle DIV/REM\\\\nthroughput. Trading rare M-extension latency for a shorter common EX critical\\\\npath should improve iterations/second through higher clock frequency.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current ALU computes every multiply and divide result combinationally in\\\\nthe EX stage, including signed and unsigned 64-bit products plus divider and\\\\nremainder operators. That wide arithmetic cone is a likely Fmax limiter on\\\\nTang Nano 20K, while CoreMark's hot path should not need single-cycle DIV/REM\\\\nthroughput. Trading rare M-extension latency for a shorter common EX critical\\\\npath should improve iterations/second through higher clock frequency.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Split RV32M operations out of the always-combinational ALU path behind a\\\\nsmall multicycle M-unit handshake in EX. Keep ADD/SUB/logic/branch/address\\\\noperations purely combinational, but when the decoded alu_op is MUL/DIV/REM,\\\\nhold ID/EX and EX/MEM until the iterative arithmetic result is ready, then\\\\nretire normally through the existing MEM/WB and RVFI path. This should remove\\\\ndivider and large multiplier logic from the ordinary EX-stage timing path;\\\\nthe added stalls only affect uncommon M instructions and should be outweighed\\\\nby improved Fmax on the FPGA fitness metric.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 6, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/alu.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Restrict the combinational ALU to fast RV32I operations and expose only lightweight M-op selection or helper logic needed by the new M-unit.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a multicycl...\\n  Line 5: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r2s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Simplify MEM load lane muxes\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"After the successful M-unit split, the remaining always-active timing paths are more likely to be ordinary RV32I datapath logic than rare multiply/divide arithmetic. The MEM stage currently uses variable shifts for load extraction and shifted masks for byte lanes, which can synthesize into wider mux/barrel-shifter logic on the FPGA load-to-WB path. CoreMark performs many byte, halfword, and word accesses, so a smaller MEM-stage cone should improve Fmax without changing CPI.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"After the successful M-unit split, the remaining always-active timing paths are more likely to be ordinary RV32I datapath logic than rare multiply/divide arithmetic. The MEM stage currently uses variable shifts for load extraction and shifted masks for byte lanes, which can synthesize into wider mux/barrel-shifter logic on the FPGA load-to-WB path. CoreMark performs many byte, halfword, and word accesses, so a smaller MEM-stage cone should improve Fmax without changing CPI.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Rewrite the MEM-stage byte-lane handling as explicit case muxes keyed by addr[1:0] instead of expressions like dmem_rdata >> (addr[1:0] * 8) and mask << addr[1:0]. Keep the same misalignment trap behavior, RVFI aligned mem_addr reporting, replicated store data convention, and MEM/WB retirement boundary. This should reduce LUT depth and routing on the common load-data path while preserving the existing five-stage control flow and single-retire NRET=1 contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace variable load-data shifts and shifted byte masks with explicit addr[1:0] case muxes for LB/LBU/LH/LHU/LW and store masks.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace variable load...\\n  Line 8: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r3s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Source-aware load-use stalls\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current load-use hazard detector compares a load destination against the\\\\nraw rs1 and rs2 bitfields of the following instruction, regardless of whether\\\\nthat instruction actually consumes those operands. For I-type immediates,\\\\nloads, JALR, LUI, AUIPC, JAL, FENCE, and EBREAK, some of those fields are\\\\nimmediates or destination bits rather than true sources. Any accidental match\\\\nwith the preceding load rd inserts an unnecessary bubble, directly reducing\\\\nCoreMark iterations per cycle without improving correctness.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current load-use hazard detector compares a load destination against the\\\\nraw rs1 and rs2 bitfields of the following instruction, regardless of whether\\\\nthat instruction actually consumes those operands. For I-type immediates,\\\\nloads, JALR, LUI, AUIPC, JAL, FENCE, and EBREAK, some of those fields are\\\\nimmediates or destination bits rather than true sources. Any accidental match\\\\nwith the preceding load rd inserts an unnecessary bubble, directly reducing\\\\nCoreMark iterations per cycle without improving correctness.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Make the load-use interlock source-aware by qualifying the rd comparisons with\\\\nsimple decoded uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 signals for the instruction currently in\\\\nIF/ID. Keep the existing one-cycle stall for real load consumers, but avoid\\\\nstalling on unused source fields such as OP-IMM rs2 bits, U/J-type operand\\\\nfields, and SYSTEM/FENCE encodings. This should improve CPI under CoreMark's\\\\nload-heavy code while adding only small opcode decode logic off the main ALU\\\\nand memory datapaths, so Fmax should be largely preserved.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Derive or wire IF/ID source-use qualifiers into the hazard unit without changing the NRET=1 top-level RVFI interface.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl...\\n  Line 11: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r4s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Source-qualified load interlock\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current load-use detector compares the preceding load rd against both raw\\\\nsource bitfields of the following instruction, even when those bits are not\\\\narchitecturally consumed. CoreMark has many loads and immediate/control\\\\ninstructions, so accidental rd matches can inject bubbles that reduce\\\\niterations per cycle without protecting any real dependency.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current load-use detector compares the preceding load rd against both raw\\\\nsource bitfields of the following instruction, even when those bits are not\\\\narchitecturally consumed. CoreMark has many loads and immediate/control\\\\ninstructions, so accidental rd matches can inject bubbles that reduce\\\\niterations per cycle without protecting any real dependency.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a tiny source-use decode for the IF/ID instruction and qualify the existing\\\\nload-use interlock with uses_rs1 and uses_rs2. This is a narrower retry of the\\\\nearlier source-aware load-use idea: do not mask or reinterpret RVFI operand\\\\nreporting, and do not add MEM bypass selects or fetch prediction paths. The\\\\ncore should keep the one-cycle stall for true load consumers while avoiding\\\\nfalse stalls on OP-IMM rs2 fields, U/J-type immediates, FENCE, SYSTEM/EBREAK,\\\\nand other instructions that do not read both source registers.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Derive simple IF/ID uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 qualifiers from opcode/funct class and pass them only to the hazard unit.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Gate load-use rd comparisons with the source-use qualifiers while preserving existing dmem, imem, redirect, and M-unit stall priority.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Derive simple IF/ID use...\\n  Line 14: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r5s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Drop regfile reset fanout\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current register file resets all 32 architectural registers with a for-loop,\\\\nwhich forces reset muxing or reset routing onto the whole small RF array. After\\\\nthe M-unit split, remaining FPGA Fmax is likely limited by ordinary datapath\\\\nand control fanout rather than rare arithmetic. CoreMark initializes the state\\\\nit uses, x0 is already hardwired on reads, and RVFI correctness depends on\\\\nretired architectural behavior rather than power-on values of unused registers.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current register file resets all 32 architectural registers with a for-loop,\\\\nwhich forces reset muxing or reset routing onto the whole small RF array. After\\\\nthe M-unit split, remaining FPGA Fmax is likely limited by ordinary datapath\\\\nand control fanout rather than rare arithmetic. CoreMark initializes the state\\\\nit uses, x0 is already hardwired on reads, and RVFI correctness depends on\\\\nretired architectural behavior rather than power-on values of unused registers.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Remove the explicit reset clear from the integer register file and keep only\\\\nthe synchronous write path for nonzero destination registers. Reads of x0 stay\\\\nforced to zero, same-cycle write-first bypass stays unchanged, and reset of the\\\\npipeline control/valid bits still prevents spurious retirement after reset.\\\\nThis should reduce reset fanout and per-register muxing in a timing-sensitive,\\\\nalways-active block without changing CPI, branch behavior, memory behavior, or\\\\nthe NRET=1 RVFI interface. The change is deliberately narrower than prior\\\\nfailed micro-opts: it does not touch hazard detection, RVFI operand reporting,\\\\nMEM load extraction, or any front-end prediction path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/reg_file.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove the reset-time loop that clears all 32 registers while preser...\\n  Line 17: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r6s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Prune dead EX/MEM branch metadata\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core has already won most of its fitness from removing broad\\\\ntiming/fanout costs, while recent attempts that add prediction, buffering,\\\\nbypassing, or memory-ordering control have regressed. The EX/MEM bundle still\\\\ncarries branch_taken and branch_target fields that are assigned every cycle\\\\nbut are not consumed by MEM, WB, RVFI, hazard, or forwarding logic. Those 33\\\\ndead flops and their input adders/mux routes sit in an always-active pipeline\\\\nboundary, so pruning them is a low-risk micro-optimization for LUT/FF count\\\\nand routing delay without changing CPI.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core has already won most of its fitness from removing broad\\\\ntiming/fanout costs, while recent attempts that add prediction, buffering,\\\\nbypassing, or memory-ordering control have regressed. The EX/MEM bundle still\\\\ncarries branch_taken and branch_target fields that are assigned every cycle\\\\nbut are not consumed by MEM, WB, RVFI, hazard, or forwarding logic. Those 33\\\\ndead flops and their input adders/mux routes sit in an always-active pipeline\\\\nboundary, so pruning them is a low-risk micro-optimization for LUT/FF count\\\\nand routing delay without changing CPI.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Remove the unused branch_taken and branch_target fields from ex_mem_t and stop\\\\nregistering them in ex_stage. Keep redirect and pc_next computation exactly as\\\\nthey are today, because pc_next is the RVFI-visible resolved next PC and the\\\\nredirect output is still the only live control-flow signal. This should trim\\\\ndead sequential state and reduce EX-stage fanout/routing pressure while\\\\npreserving the NRET=1 RVFI contract, trap behavior, memory behavior, and all\\\\nexisting stall/flush priorities.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 1, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove unused branch_taken and...\\n  Line 20: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r7s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Iterative M divider\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The largest proven fitness gain for this core came from moving RV32M arithmetic\\\\nout of the ordinary EX ALU path, but the current m_unit still contains wide\\\\ncombinational DIV/REM operators behind a one-cycle result register. Even if\\\\nthose operations are rare in CoreMark, the FPGA still has to place and time\\\\nthe divider/remainder cone, so it can cap Fmax or perturb routing for the hot\\\\nRV32I pipeline.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The largest proven fitness gain for this core came from moving RV32M arithmetic\\\\nout of the ordinary EX ALU path, but the current m_unit still contains wide\\\\ncombinational DIV/REM operators behind a one-cycle result register. Even if\\\\nthose operations are rare in CoreMark, the FPGA still has to place and time\\\\nthe divider/remainder cone, so it can cap Fmax or perturb routing for the hot\\\\nRV32I pipeline.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the one-cycle DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU implementation in m_unit with a small\\\\niterative restoring divider that runs only after a divide or remainder starts.\\\\nKeep MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU on the existing short registered path so any\\\\nmultiply use does not pay a 32-cycle penalty, and keep the existing EX-stage\\\\nhold/valid handshake as the only pipeline interface. This should remove the\\\\nexpensive combinational divider/remainder logic from the FPGA timing closure\\\\nproblem while adding CPI only for uncommon divide/remainder instructions.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/m_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Implement DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU with an iterative signed/unsigned divider state machine while preserving ALTOPS behavior under RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Adjust the existing M-unit busy/valid handshake only as needed so EX holds until multi-cycle DIV/REM completion and still clears completed results exactly once.\\\"}]}, \\\"expec...\\n  Line 23: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r8s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Share M-unit multiplier hardware\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core already removed RV32M arithmetic from the ordinary EX\\\\nALU path, but m_unit still builds three separate 64-bit multiplier products\\\\nin parallel for MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU signedness variants. CoreMark uses\\\\nM operations rarely enough that throughput beyond the existing one-cycle\\\\nregistered M-unit is not the main limiter, while the extra DSP/LUT/routing\\\\npressure can still depress FPGA Fmax or perturb placement of the hot RV32I\\\\npipeline.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core already removed RV32M arithmetic from the ordinary EX\\\\nALU path, but m_unit still builds three separate 64-bit multiplier products\\\\nin parallel for MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU signedness variants. CoreMark uses\\\\nM operations rarely enough that throughput beyond the existing one-cycle\\\\nregistered M-unit is not the main limiter, while the extra DSP/LUT/routing\\\\npressure can still depress FPGA Fmax or perturb placement of the hot RV32I\\\\npipeline.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the parallel signed-signed, unsigned-unsigned, and signed-unsigned\\\\nproduct generation in m_unit with one shared signed multiplier fed by\\\\nsign/zero-extended operands selected from the M opcode. MUL can take the low\\\\nhalf of the same product because low 32 bits are independent of operand\\\\nsignedness; MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU select the high half with the appropriate\\\\noperand extension. Keep the existing start/valid/clear handshake, one-cycle\\\\nM latency, DIV/REM behavior, and RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions unchanged.\\\\nThis should trim multiplier area and routing without adding CPI, front-end\\\\nprediction logic, memory ordering state, or any RVFI-visible behavior change.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/m_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Use a single sign-controlled widened multiplier for MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU while pres...\\n  Line 26: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r9s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Narrow MEM/WB control state\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent micro-optimization wins came from removing always-present FPGA timing\\\\nand routing pressure, while attempts that added prediction, bypassing, or\\\\nstall-hiding control usually regressed. The current MEM/WB register still\\\\ncarries the full decode control bundle even though the writeback and RVFI\\\\nretirement logic only consume a few bits after the MEM stage. Pruning that\\\\ncold control state should reduce flop count, muxing, and routing without\\\\nchanging CoreMark CPI or touching the external NRET=1 contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent micro-optimization wins came from removing always-present FPGA timing\\\\nand routing pressure, while attempts that added prediction, bypassing, or\\\\nstall-hiding control usually regressed. The current MEM/WB register still\\\\ncarries the full decode control bundle even though the writeback and RVFI\\\\nretirement logic only consume a few bits after the MEM stage. Pruning that\\\\ncold control state should reduce flop count, muxing, and routing without\\\\nchanging CoreMark CPI or touching the external NRET=1 contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the full ctrl_t field in mem_wb_t with a narrow retirement/writeback\\\\ncontrol subset containing only reg_write, mem_to_reg, and is_illegal. Keep\\\\nthe existing full ctrl_t through ID/EX and EX/MEM, because the execute and\\\\nmemory stages still need branch, memory width, sign-extension, and M-op\\\\ncontrols. In mem_stage, collapse ctrl_with_trap into the narrow MEM/WB\\\\nfields after misalignment handling, and update wb_stage and core RVFI trap\\\\nreferences to use the narrow fields. This should trim unused MEM/WB control\\\\nflops and fanout while preserving all memory masks/data, load results,\\\\nwriteback behavior, RVFI order, and trap reporting.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Introduce a narrow M...\\n  Line 29: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r10s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Prune unused LUI control bit\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent wins came from removing always-present arithmetic, reset, and RVFI\\\\nrouting pressure, while broader CPI-oriented changes and control prediction\\\\nusually regressed. The current control bundle still carries an is_lui bit\\\\nthrough decode and every pipeline stage even though LUI execution is already\\\\nfully encoded by alu_op=ALU_LUI and alu_src=1. That unused bit adds one more\\\\ndecoded signal, pipeline flop, reset/hold mux input, and routing load across\\\\nhot stage boundaries without contributing to architectural behavior.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent wins came from removing always-present arithmetic, reset, and RVFI\\\\nrouting pressure, while broader CPI-oriented changes and control prediction\\\\nusually regressed. The current control bundle still carries an is_lui bit\\\\nthrough decode and every pipeline stage even though LUI execution is already\\\\nfully encoded by alu_op=ALU_LUI and alu_src=1. That unused bit adds one more\\\\ndecoded signal, pipeline flop, reset/hold mux input, and routing load across\\\\nhot stage boundaries without contributing to architectural behavior.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Remove the redundant is_lui signal from the decoder interface and ctrl_t,\\\\nand delete the corresponding ID-stage temporary and bundle assignment. Keep\\\\nLUI behavior unchanged by continuing to set alu_op=ALU_LUI, alu_src=1, and\\\\nreg_write=1 in the existing validated LUI decode arm. This is a narrow\\\\nno-CPI micro-optimization that should slightly reduce control-bundle width,\\\\nregister enables, reset/hold muxing, and decode-to-EX routing pressure while\\\\npreserving the NRET=1 RVFI contract, trap discipline, memory behavior, and\\\\nall existing stall/forwarding logic.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 1, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove the unused is_lui field from ctrl_t so it no longer propagates throug...\\n  Line 32: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r11s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Narrow EX/MEM control payload\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core already benefits from splitting RVFI shadow metadata away from hot execution payloads, but the EX/MEM execution register still carries the full decoded ctrl_t bundle after execute has consumed most of it. Branch, jump, operand-select, ALU-op, and AUIPC/LUI control bits are no longer needed once the EX result, write data, redirect, and RVFI pc_next have been produced. Trimming those cold bits at the EX/MEM boundary should reduce flop count, hold/reset muxing, and routing pressure on the memory, forwarding, and stall paths without changing CPI.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core already benefits from splitting RVFI shadow metadata away from hot execution payloads, but the EX/MEM execution register still carries the full decoded ctrl_t bundle after execute has consumed most of it. Branch, jump, operand-select, ALU-op, and AUIPC/LUI control bits are no longer needed once the EX result, write data, redirect, and RVFI pc_next have been produced. Trimming those cold bits at the EX/MEM boundary should reduce flop count, hold/reset muxing, and routing pressure on the memory, forwarding, and stall paths without changing CPI.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Introduce a compact post-EX control payload for EX/MEM and MEM-stage consumers containing only the bits still live after execute: mem_read, mem_write, mem_width, mem_sext, reg_write, mem_to_reg, and is_illegal. Keep the full ctrl_t through ID/EX and inside EX where decode, branch, jump, ALU, AUIPC, and M-op decisions are still needed, then collapse to the compact control when registering EX/MEM. This is narrower than prior failed prediction/bypass/stall-hiding changes and differs from the regressed MEM/WB-only pruning by removing unused control before the hotter dmem, hazard, forwarding, and MEM/WB fanout cone.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}...\\n  Line 35: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r12s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Class-bit M-op encoding\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent successful micro-optimizations improved fitness by removing always-active FPGA timing and routing pressure without adding new control-flow or memory machinery. The current EX stage still detects RV32M operations with eight separate alu_op equality comparisons, and that result feeds the M-unit start/busy path, redirect suppression, and EX/MEM bubble control. CoreMark rarely benefits from complex M-op detection logic being on this hot execute-control path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent successful micro-optimizations improved fitness by removing always-active FPGA timing and routing pressure without adding new control-flow or memory machinery. The current EX stage still detects RV32M operations with eight separate alu_op equality comparisons, and that result feeds the M-unit start/busy path, redirect suppression, and EX/MEM bubble control. CoreMark rarely benefits from complex M-op detection logic being on this hot execute-control path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Recode the internal ALU operation constants so every RV32M operation has alu_op[4]=1 while all ordinary RV32I operations keep alu_op[4]=0. Then replace the EX-stage is_m_op eight-way compare tree with a single in.valid && in.ctrl.alu_op[4] test. This preserves the decoder's legal-instruction checks, ALU and M-unit case behavior, RVFI retirement, and the existing one-cycle M-unit handshake, but should reduce LUT depth and fanout in the execute control path with no CPI cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Reassign ALU_MUL through ALU_REMU localparams into a contiguous high-bit class while leaving RV32I ALU ops below bit 4.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the explicit eight-operation is_m_op comparison with a single alu_op class-bit check.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delt...\\n  Line 38: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r13s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Prune redundant LUI control\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The strongest bench-core results have come from removing always-active FPGA\\\\nrouting pressure without adding new prediction, bypass, memory ordering, or\\\\nretiming machinery. The current control bundle still carries an is_lui bit\\\\nfrom decode through ID/EX, EX/MEM, and MEM/WB even though no live execute,\\\\nmemory, writeback, hazard, or RVFI logic consumes it. LUI behavior is already\\\\nfully represented by alu_op=ALU_LUI, alu_src=1, and reg_write=1.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The strongest bench-core results have come from removing always-active FPGA\\\\nrouting pressure without adding new prediction, bypass, memory ordering, or\\\\nretiming machinery. The current control bundle still carries an is_lui bit\\\\nfrom decode through ID/EX, EX/MEM, and MEM/WB even though no live execute,\\\\nmemory, writeback, hazard, or RVFI logic consumes it. LUI behavior is already\\\\nfully represented by alu_op=ALU_LUI, alu_src=1, and reg_write=1.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Remove the redundant is_lui signal from ctrl_t and from the decoder and ID\\\\nstage wiring, leaving the existing LUI decode arm to set ALU_LUI, alu_src,\\\\nand reg_write exactly as before. This should slightly reduce control-bundle\\\\nwidth, pipeline register muxing, and decode-to-execute routing fanout with no\\\\nCPI cost and no change to the NRET=1 top-level RVFI contract. The change is\\\\nintentionally smaller than the regressed control-payload splits and avoids\\\\nall front-end predictor or memory-bypass paths that have repeatedly lost\\\\nfitness on this FPGA.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 1, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove the unused is_lui field from ctrl_t so it no longer propagates through pipeline payloads.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/decoder.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Drop the is_lui output and keep the validated LUI arm encoded through ALU_LUI, alu_src, and reg_write.\\\"},...\",\"metadata\":{\"matches\":14,\"truncated\":false},\"title\":\"micro_opt|category: micro_opt|\\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\"|hyp-20260513-001-r14s0\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778699818486,\"end\":1778699818493}},\"metadata\":{\"openai\":{\"itemId\":\"fc_0fc1f8544fd94f88016a04ce2a3d68819888d7acc7d5f83492\"}},\"id\":\"prt_e22c555e4001Vsn5clPE36T6fq\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1dd3adde4ffep5jPLBvr070Y0N\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e22c52273001KQeLK0QIp7jPyP\"}}\n./bench/gpt-5_5_medium/rep2/agent.log:130:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778700495355,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1dd308545ffeS2tbmWRdSb0axR\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"read\",\"callID\":\"call_R52XU0T3FyK6brly3iikLQdI\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"filePath\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_5_medium-rep2/cores/bench/LESSONS.md\",\"offset\":1,\"limit\":200},\"output\":\"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_5_medium-rep2/cores/bench/LESSONS.md</path>\\n<type>file</type>\\n<content>\\n1: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r1s0 (improvement, +32.85%): Moving RV32M multiply/divide out of the combinational EX ALU behind a multicycle handshake can substantially improve FPGA fitness because CoreMark rarely needs M-op throughput while the removed arithmetic cone dominates Fmax.\\n2: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r1s1 (broken, n/a): Moving branch/JALR resolution into ID needs exhaustive operand-valid stalling and forwarding for decode-stage compares/base operands; stale early redirects can let wrong-path state reach writeback, which riscv-formal's reg check catches.\\n3: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r1s2 (regression, +21.76%): IF-stage BTFNT/JAL prediction can regress CoreMark when instruction-bit immediate decode and target adders sit directly on the fetch PC path, so front-end bubble savings must be weighed against Fmax-critical next-PC logic.\\n4: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r2s0 (regression, -1.92%): Replacing MEM-stage variable shifts with explicit byte/halfword lane case muxes did not help FPGA fitness, suggesting the synthesizer already maps these narrow shifts well and the added mux/control structure can slightly worsen routing or LUT packing.\\n5: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r2s1 (regression, -7.79%): A two-entry IF skid buffer did not pay for CoreMark's random imem stalls; the added fetch-valid/control and redirect-flush machinery can cost more FPGA fitness than the front-end bubbles it hides.\\n6: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r2s2 (regression, -44.37%): A tiny IF return-address stack can still badly hurt FPGA fitness when canonical-return detection, RAS top muxing, and predicted-PC metadata outweigh the CoreMark return bubbles it removes.\\n7: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r3s0 (broken, n/a): Keep source-aware load-use interlocks separate from RVFI operand reporting changes; masking unused rs fields can break cosim's retire trace even when the intended stall reduction logic is local to hazard_unit.\\n8: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r3s1 (regression, -14.30%): MEM-to-EX load bypass can lose FPGA fitness when dmem-ready gating, load extraction, and an extra EX operand forwarding select lengthen the critical path more than CoreMark benefits from removing one-cycle load-use bubbles.\\n9: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r3s2 (regression, -20.54%): Even decode-stage JAL prediction can regress when the early_redirect priority path, target adder, and predicted-PC metadata still add enough IF/ID/EX control cost to outweigh saving CoreMark's unconditional-jump bubble.\\n10: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r4s1 (broken, n/a): A registered IF/ID boundary must pair imem backpressure with an ID consume/valid handshake; holding a valid IF/ID entry while ID keeps advancing can duplicate instructions during CoreMark stalls.\\n11: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r4s2 (regression, -9.29%): Even a resolved-target BTB can lose FPGA fitness when IF-side tag/valid/counter arrays, target muxing, and predicted-PC metadata lengthen the next-PC path more than CoreMark benefits from avoiding loop redirect bubbles.\\n12: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r5s0 (improvement, +1.93%): Dropping reset clears from the integer register file can improve FPGA fitness because pipeline-valid reset and x0 read forcing preserve architectural behavior while avoiding reset fanout and per-register muxing in an always-active datapath block.\\n13: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r5s1 (regression, -6.89%): A one-entry ordered store buffer can lose CoreMark fitness when store-order interlocks, MMIO/trap classification, and dmem muxing add more MEM/control-path cost than random store-stall overlap recovers.\\n14: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r5s2 (regression, -61.05%): Even a single-entry loop predictor can badly regress when predicted-PC metadata, IF PC/tag compare muxing, and EX mismatch redirect logic lengthen critical control paths more than repeated CoreMark loop-branch bubble removal helps.\\n15: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r6s0 (regression, -2.40%): Pruning unused EX/MEM branch_taken/branch_target metadata did not improve FPGA fitness, suggesting these dead-looking pipeline fields were not on the binding path or were already optimized well enough that layout perturbation outweighed the small flop reduction.\\n16: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r6s1 (regression, -88.09%): Splitting EX with an EX1 operand register can destroy CoreMark fitness when it delays branch/JALR redirects and forces broad dependent-ALU interlocks, so operand-cone retiming must preserve bypass/redirect latency to pay off.\\n17: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r6s2 (regression, -48.05%): A JAL-only single-entry target predictor still regressed without fetch-side immediate decode because the IF PC compare/target mux, predicted-PC pipeline fields, and EX mismatch redirect logic outweighed CoreMark's repeated unconditional-jump bubble savings.\\n18: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r7s0 (regression, -53.28%): Replacing cold RV32M DIV/REM combinational operators with an iterative divider can severely regress CoreMark when the busy/start handshake, divider state, and 32-cycle latency perturb the hot pipeline more than removing the rarely used divide cone helps Fmax.\\n19: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r7s1 (broken, n/a): Registering dmem backpressure needs a real ready/valid or skid boundary at MEM; a lone pending/wait bit can hide the first stalled cycle from upstream control and let younger CoreMark memory-state updates be dropped or reordered.\\n20: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r8s0 (improvement, +2.79%): Sharing one sign/zero-extended M-unit multiplier across MULH signedness variants can improve FPGA fitness because CoreMark rarely needs M throughput and parallel 64-bit products add DSP/LUT routing pressure.\\n21: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r8s1 (regression, -0.33%): Preselecting MEM/WB writeback data inside mem_stage did not improve fitness, suggesting the load-vs-ALU writeback mux is not a binding FPGA path and adding another pipeline field can slightly worsen packing/routing.\\n22: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r8s2 (regression, +1.88%): Moving backward-branch prediction from IF to ID still regressed because the ID target adder, redirect/stall arbitration, predicted-PC metadata, and EX mispredict compare/control outweighed CoreMark loop-bubble savings.\\n23: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r9s0 (regression, -14.30%): Narrowing MEM/WB from full ctrl_t to a tiny packed mem_wb_ctrl_t can hurt FPGA fitness despite fewer flops, suggesting late-stage control fields were already optimized away or well-packed and the new struct/mapping perturbed routing more than it saved.\\n24: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r9s1 (improvement, +3.53%): Splitting RVFI-only metadata into parallel shadow payloads can improve FPGA fitness because it removes wide instruction/source/PC/memory fields from hot EX/MEM and MEM/WB forwarding, dmem, and writeback bundles without changing pipeline timing.\\n25: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r9s2 (regression, -31.51%): Decode-stage return prediction still regressed because link-register hazard compares, early redirect arbitration, and predicted-PC fields added more hot control/datapath cost than CoreMark recovered from avoiding canonical JALR return bubbles.\\n26: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r10s1 (regression, -5.39%): Further splitting MEM RVFI memory sidebands after the broader RVFI-shadow win can regress when it duplicates byte-mask/write-data logic and adds another MEM/WB payload boundary without removing a binding dmem/load/writeback path.\\n27: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r10s2 (broken, n/a): EX-trained branch predictors must keep prediction-hit suppression, exit invalidation redirects, and RVFI pc_next reporting aligned with the next retired instruction; riscv-formal's pc_fwd check catches recovery paths that eventually fetch correctly but break the retirement PC chain.\\n28: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r11s0 (regression, -5.83%): Narrowing EX/MEM and MEM/WB from full ctrl_t to post_ex_ctrl_t can still hurt FPGA fitness after RVFI-shadow splitting, implying the remaining control bits are not the binding path and the extra struct projection/packing perturbation outweighs small flop savings.\\n29: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r11s2 (regression, -12.97%): EX-side fall-through redirect elision can regress even without fetch predictor state because a resolved-target-vs-pc+4 comparator lengthens branch/jump control while CoreMark rarely benefits from target-equals-fall-through cases.\\n30: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r12s0 (regression, -8.26%): Class-bit recoding of RV32M alu_op values can lose FPGA fitness when the old eight-way compare was not binding and the new high-bit fanout perturbs ALU/M-unit decode packing more than it simplifies EX control.\\n31: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r12s1 (regression, -4.78%): Extending RVFI-shadow splitting back to ID/EX can regress when source-register numbers still remain on the hot hazard/forwarding path, so the parallel shadow duplicates flops/routing without removing the binding decode-to-EX logic.\\n32: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r13s0 (regression, -15.41%): Pruning a single unused `ctrl_t` bit like `is_lui` can still regress FPGA fitness because packed control-bundle layout and decode/ID register routing perturbation may dominate any tiny flop-width reduction.\\n33: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r13s1 (regression, -4.03%): Moving write-first WB bypass muxes from `reg_file` into `id_stage` can regress because the WB write port still fans into decode and lengthens the ID/EX capture path while the original regfile bypass was not the binding structure.\\n34: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r14s0 (regression, -62.33%): Adding a second registered operand/result phase inside `m_unit` can badly regress CoreMark because the extra `is_m_op && !m_valid` EX hold and added state/packing cost outweigh any relief from retiming an already-isolated rare RV32M arithmetic cone.\\n35: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r14s1 (broken, n/a): Source-aware load-use suppression must still keep RVFI raw rs1/rs2 register data architecturally current for unused bitfields; ad hoc MEM-stage load fixups can miss the formal reg-check contract even when execution operands are unaffected.\\n36: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r14s2 (regression, -27.94%): Gating the EX branch-target adder behind `branch_taken` can worsen FPGA fitness because the adder is still required for taken redirects and misalignment/RVFI reporting while the added `pc_plus4`/target muxing lengthens the branch control cone.\\n\\n(End of file - total 36 lines)\\n</content>\",\"metadata\":{\"preview\":\"- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r1s0 (improvement, +32.85%): Moving RV32M multiply/divide out of the combinational EX ALU behind a multicycle handshake can substantially improve FPGA fitness because CoreMark rarely needs M-op throughput while the removed arithmetic cone dominates Fmax.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r1s1 (broken, n/a): Moving branch/JALR resolution into ID needs exhaustive operand-valid stalling and forwarding for decode-stage compares/base operands; stale early redirects can let wrong-path state reach writeback, which riscv-formal's reg check catches.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r1s2 (regression, +21.76%): IF-stage BTFNT/JAL prediction can regress CoreMark when instruction-bit immediate decode and target adders sit directly on the fetch PC path, so front-end bubble savings must be weighed against Fmax-critical next-PC logic.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r2s0 (regression, -1.92%): Replacing MEM-stage variable shifts with explicit byte/halfword lane case muxes did not help FPGA fitness, suggesting the synthesizer already maps these narrow shifts well and the added mux/control structure can slightly worsen routing or LUT packing.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r2s1 (regression, -7.79%): A two-entry IF skid buffer did not pay for CoreMark's random imem stalls; the added fetch-valid/control and redirect-flush machinery can cost more FPGA fitness than the front-end bubbles it hides.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r2s2 (regression, -44.37%): A tiny IF return-address stack can still badly hurt FPGA fitness when canonical-return detection, RAS top muxing, and predicted-PC metadata outweigh the CoreMark return bubbles it removes.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r3s0 (broken, n/a): Keep source-aware load-use interlocks separate from RVFI operand reporting changes; masking unused rs fields can break cosim's retire trace even when the intended stall reduction logic is local to hazard_unit.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r3s1 (regression, -14.30%): MEM-to-EX load bypass can lose FPGA fitness when dmem-ready gating, load extraction, and an extra EX operand forwarding select lengthen the critical path more than CoreMark benefits from removing one-cycle load-use bubbles.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r3s2 (regression, -20.54%): Even decode-stage JAL prediction can regress when the early_redirect priority path, target adder, and predicted-PC metadata still add enough IF/ID/EX control cost to outweigh saving CoreMark's unconditional-jump bubble.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r4s1 (broken, n/a): A registered IF/ID boundary must pair imem backpressure with an ID consume/valid handshake; holding a valid IF/ID entry while ID keeps advancing can duplicate instructions during CoreMark stalls.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r4s2 (regression, -9.29%): Even a resolved-target BTB can lose FPGA fitness when IF-side tag/valid/counter arrays, target muxing, and predicted-PC metadata lengthen the next-PC path more than CoreMark benefits from avoiding loop redirect bubbles.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r5s0 (improvement, +1.93%): Dropping reset clears from the integer register file can improve FPGA fitness because pipeline-valid reset and x0 read forcing preserve architectural behavior while avoiding reset fanout and per-register muxing in an always-active datapath block.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r5s1 (regression, -6.89%): A one-entry ordered store buffer can lose CoreMark fitness when store-order interlocks, MMIO/trap classification, and dmem muxing add more MEM/control-path cost than random store-stall overlap recovers.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r5s2 (regression, -61.05%): Even a single-entry loop predictor can badly regress when predicted-PC metadata, IF PC/tag compare muxing, and EX mismatch redirect logic lengthen critical control paths more than repeated CoreMark loop-branch bubble removal helps.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r6s0 (regression, -2.40%): Pruning unused EX/MEM branch_taken/branch_target metadata did not improve FPGA fitness, suggesting these dead-looking pipeline fields were not on the binding path or were already optimized well enough that layout perturbation outweighed the small flop reduction.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r6s1 (regression, -88.09%): Splitting EX with an EX1 operand register can destroy CoreMark fitness when it delays branch/JALR redirects and forces broad dependent-ALU interlocks, so operand-cone retiming must preserve bypass/redirect latency to pay off.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r6s2 (regression, -48.05%): A JAL-only single-entry target predictor still regressed without fetch-side immediate decode because the IF PC compare/target mux, predicted-PC pipeline fields, and EX mismatch redirect logic outweighed CoreMark's repeated unconditional-jump bubble savings.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r7s0 (regression, -53.28%): Replacing cold RV32M DIV/REM combinational operators with an iterative divider can severely regress CoreMark when the busy/start handshake, divider state, and 32-cycle latency perturb the hot pipeline more than removing the rarely used divide cone helps Fmax.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r7s1 (broken, n/a): Registering dmem backpressure needs a real ready/valid or skid boundary at MEM; a lone pending/wait bit can hide the first stalled cycle from upstream control and let younger CoreMark memory-state updates be dropped or reordered.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r8s0 (improvement, +2.79%): Sharing one sign/zero-extended M-unit multiplier across MULH signedness variants can improve FPGA fitness because CoreMark rarely needs M throughput and parallel 64-bit products add DSP/LUT routing pressure.\",\"truncated\":false,\"loaded\":[]},\"title\":\"cores/bench/LESSONS.md\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778700495326,\"end\":1778700495354}},\"metadata\":{\"openai\":{\"itemId\":\"fc_09d3d947652c5be8016a04d0cf29cc819a9d48913f93220062\"}},\"id\":\"prt_e22cfa9d80010vyxc8y2Z27DeM\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1dd308545ffeS2tbmWRdSb0axR\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e22cf7b2b0011N5x08sNcBf1vY\"}}\n./bench/gpt-5_5_medium/rep2/agent.log:131:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778700495396,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1dd308545ffeS2tbmWRdSb0axR\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"grep\",\"callID\":\"call_UpS2CsuGrSVa84o3bfBaZKQB\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"pattern\":\"category.*micro_opt|micro_opt|Localize WB|RVFI|forward|branch-target|M-unit|reg_file|mem_stage|hazard_unit\",\"path\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_5_medium-rep2/cores/bench/experiments\",\"include\":\"log.jsonl\"},\"output\":\"Found 41 matches\\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_5_medium-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:\\n  Line 1: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"baseline-bench-5327bba\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Baseline retest for cores/bench\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"improvement\\\", \\\"fitness\\\": 282.82, \\\"delta_pct\\\": 0.0, \\\"vs_baseline\\\": 0.0, \\\"fmax_mhz\\\": 127.03, \\\"ipc_coremark\\\": 2e-06, \\\"cycles\\\": 4491485, \\\"iterations\\\": 10, \\\"lut4\\\": 9563, \\\"ff\\\": 1866, \\\"seeds\\\": [128.72, 127.03, 123.02], \\\"formal_passed\\\": true, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": true, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"implementation_notes\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-05-13T13:08:01.185076Z\\\", \\\"round_id\\\": 0, \\\"slot\\\": 0}\\n\\n  Line 2: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r1s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Multicycle RV32M arithmetic unit\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current ALU computes every multiply and divide result combinationally in\\\\nthe EX stage, including signed and unsigned 64-bit products plus divider and\\\\nremainder operators. That wide arithmetic cone is a likely Fmax limiter on\\\\nTang Nano 20K, while CoreMark's hot path should not need single-cycle DIV/REM\\\\nthroughput. Trading rare M-extension latency for a shorter common EX critical\\\\npath should improve iterations/second through higher clock frequency.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current ALU computes every multiply and divide result combinationally in\\\\nthe EX stage, including signed and unsigned 64-bit products plus divider and\\\\nremainder operators. That wide arithmetic cone is a likely Fmax limiter on\\\\nTang Nano 20K, while CoreMark's hot path should not need single-cycle DIV/REM\\\\nthroughput. Trading rare M-extension latency for a shorter common EX critical\\\\npath should improve iterations/second through higher clock frequency.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Split RV32M operations out of the always-combinational ALU path behind a\\\\nsmall multicycle M-unit handshake in EX. Keep ADD/SUB/logic/branch/address\\\\noperations purely combinational, but when the decoded alu_op is MUL/DIV/REM,\\\\nhold ID/EX and EX/MEM until the iterative arithmetic result is ready, then\\\\nretire normally through the existing MEM/WB and RVFI path. This should remove\\\\ndivider and large multiplier logic from the ordinary EX-stage timing path;\\\\nthe added stalls only affect uncommon M instructions and should be outweighed\\\\nby improved Fmax on the FPGA fitness metric.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 6, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/alu.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Restrict the combinational ALU to fast RV32I operations and expose only lightweight M-op selection or helper logic needed by the new M-unit.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a multicycl...\\n  Line 3: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r1s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Resolve control flow in ID\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"CoreMark spends a significant fraction of its bracketed runtime in tight loops and conditional control flow. The current five-stage pipeline resolves branches and jumps in EX, then flushes both IF and ID, so every taken branch or jump pays roughly a two-cycle redirect penalty. Reducing that penalty improves iterations/cycle without increasing retirement width or touching the external NRET=1 contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"CoreMark spends a significant fraction of its bracketed runtime in tight loops and conditional control flow. The current five-stage pipeline resolves branches and jumps in EX, then flushes both IF and ID, so every taken branch or jump pays roughly a two-cycle redirect penalty. Reducing that penalty improves iterations/cycle without increasing retirement width or touching the external NRET=1 contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Move branch and JAL/JALR target/condition resolution from EX into the decode stage, with decode-stage operand forwarding from EX/MEM and MEM/WB for branch compares and JALR base operands. Keep the existing EX-stage trap/metadata path as the retirement source, but let ID drive redirect one cycle earlier when operands are available; stall only for unresolved load-to-branch/JALR dependencies. This should cut the common taken-control penalty from two bubbles to one while preserving RVFI order, trap reporting, and single-channel retirement.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 8, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Wire ID-stage early redirect signals into the IF and hazard path while preserving the existing NRET=1 RVFI port set.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add early branch/JAL/JALR resolution using decoded control, immediates, regfile operands, and forwarded compare/base operands.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/forward_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend forwarding ...\\n  Line 4: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r1s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Static BTFNT branch predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current five-stage core always fetches sequentially and resolves every branch or jump in EX, so each taken loop branch pays a two-instruction front-end flush. CoreMark has tight loops and many backward branches, making a small static predictor likely to improve iterations per cycle without adding much FPGA area or hurting the ALU critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current five-stage core always fetches sequentially and resolves every branch or jump in EX, so each taken loop branch pays a two-instruction front-end flush. CoreMark has tight loops and many backward branches, making a small static predictor likely to improve iterations per cycle without adding much FPGA area or hurting the ALU critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add an IF-stage static backward-taken/forward-not-taken predictor using the fetched instruction bits to compute B-type targets for negative branch immediates, and optionally early-predict JAL targets because they are unconditional PC-relative redirects. Carry the predicted next PC through IF/ID and ID/EX so EX redirects only when the resolved next PC differs from the prediction; mispredicted forward-taken branches, not-taken backward branches, and JALR still recover through the existing flush path. This should remove most loop-closing branch bubbles while preserving the single-retire RVFI contract because retirement remains unchanged at MEM/WB.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 8, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend IF/ID and ID/EX pipeline bundles with the predicted next PC needed for EX-stage mispredict detection.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Implement static BTFNT next-PC selection for backward conditional branches and early PC-relative JAL prediction from fetched instruction bits.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Lat...\\n  Line 5: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r2s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Simplify MEM load lane muxes\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"After the successful M-unit split, the remaining always-active timing paths are more likely to be ordinary RV32I datapath logic than rare multiply/divide arithmetic. The MEM stage currently uses variable shifts for load extraction and shifted masks for byte lanes, which can synthesize into wider mux/barrel-shifter logic on the FPGA load-to-WB path. CoreMark performs many byte, halfword, and word accesses, so a smaller MEM-stage cone should improve Fmax without changing CPI.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"After the successful M-unit split, the remaining always-active timing paths are more likely to be ordinary RV32I datapath logic than rare multiply/divide arithmetic. The MEM stage currently uses variable shifts for load extraction and shifted masks for byte lanes, which can synthesize into wider mux/barrel-shifter logic on the FPGA load-to-WB path. CoreMark performs many byte, halfword, and word accesses, so a smaller MEM-stage cone should improve Fmax without changing CPI.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Rewrite the MEM-stage byte-lane handling as explicit case muxes keyed by addr[1:0] instead of expressions like dmem_rdata >> (addr[1:0] * 8) and mask << addr[1:0]. Keep the same misalignment trap behavior, RVFI aligned mem_addr reporting, replicated store data convention, and MEM/WB retirement boundary. This should reduce LUT depth and routing on the common load-data path while preserving the existing five-stage control flow and single-retire NRET=1 contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace variable load-data shifts and shifted byte masks with explicit addr[1:0] case muxes for LB/LBU/LH/LHU/LW and store masks.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace variable load...\\n  Line 6: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r2s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Add fetch skid buffer\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current front end has no real IF/ID storage: the IF payload is combinational from the current PC and imem data, and an imem-not-ready cycle injects a bubble directly into decode. With the tournament cosim using roughly 22% random instruction stalls, CoreMark loses many cycles even when the back end is already stalled by load-use, dmem, or the multicycle M unit. A small fetch buffer is a structural way to recover iterations/cycle without revisiting the prior broken ID-stage branch-resolution path or adding fetch-path target adders that hurt Fmax.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current front end has no real IF/ID storage: the IF payload is combinational from the current PC and imem data, and an imem-not-ready cycle injects a bubble directly into decode. With the tournament cosim using roughly 22% random instruction stalls, CoreMark loses many cycles even when the back end is already stalled by load-use, dmem, or the multicycle M unit. A small fetch buffer is a structural way to recover iterations/cycle without revisiting the prior broken ID-stage branch-resolution path or adding fetch-path target adders that hurt Fmax.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Insert a one- or two-entry instruction skid buffer between IF and ID. The PC/imem side should continue fetching sequential instructions whenever the buffer has space, while the ID side consumes one buffered instruction only when ID/EX is ready. On redirect, flush the buffer and restart fetch at the EX-resolved target; on imem stalls, hold the outstanding fetch instead of emitting a synthetic NOP into decode. This should overlap random iStall cycles with existing back-end stalls and reduce front-end bubbles while preserving single-retire RVFI ordering and the existing EX-stage control-flow correctness.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 7, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"descr...\\n  Line 7: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r2s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Tiny return-address stack predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The prior static BTFNT predictor targeted loop branches but put branch immediate decode\\\\nand target adders directly on the IF PC-update path, regressing the CoreMark fitness\\\\ndespite saving some bubbles. CoreMark still has frequent function calls and returns,\\\\nand the current core pays the same EX-stage redirect penalty for every JALR return.\\\\nA tiny return predictor can attack those bubbles without adding branch-target arithmetic\\\\nto fetch.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The prior static BTFNT predictor targeted loop branches but put branch immediate decode\\\\nand target adders directly on the IF PC-update path, regressing the CoreMark fitness\\\\ndespite saving some bubbles. CoreMark still has frequent function calls and returns,\\\\nand the current core pays the same EX-stage redirect penalty for every JALR return.\\\\nA tiny return predictor can attack those bubbles without adding branch-target arithmetic\\\\nto fetch.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a small 4-entry return-address stack that predicts only canonical returns\\\\n(JALR x0, 0(x1/x5)) in IF. Push pc+4 for link-register calls when they resolve,\\\\npop/predict the stack top for returns, and carry the predicted next PC through the\\\\npipeline so EX redirects only on mismatch. This keeps ordinary branch fetch as pc+4,\\\\navoids B-type immediate decode and target adders in the IF critical path, and should\\\\nrecover part of the call/return control-flow penalty with modest LUT/FF cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add predicted-next-PC and return-predicted metadata to IF/ID, ID/EX, and EX/MEM bundles for mispredict detection and RAS bookkeeping.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Detect canonical return encodings in the fetched instruction and select the RAS top as the next PC only when the stack is nonempty....\\n  Line 8: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r3s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Source-aware load-use stalls\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current load-use hazard detector compares a load destination against the\\\\nraw rs1 and rs2 bitfields of the following instruction, regardless of whether\\\\nthat instruction actually consumes those operands. For I-type immediates,\\\\nloads, JALR, LUI, AUIPC, JAL, FENCE, and EBREAK, some of those fields are\\\\nimmediates or destination bits rather than true sources. Any accidental match\\\\nwith the preceding load rd inserts an unnecessary bubble, directly reducing\\\\nCoreMark iterations per cycle without improving correctness.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current load-use hazard detector compares a load destination against the\\\\nraw rs1 and rs2 bitfields of the following instruction, regardless of whether\\\\nthat instruction actually consumes those operands. For I-type immediates,\\\\nloads, JALR, LUI, AUIPC, JAL, FENCE, and EBREAK, some of those fields are\\\\nimmediates or destination bits rather than true sources. Any accidental match\\\\nwith the preceding load rd inserts an unnecessary bubble, directly reducing\\\\nCoreMark iterations per cycle without improving correctness.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Make the load-use interlock source-aware by qualifying the rd comparisons with\\\\nsimple decoded uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 signals for the instruction currently in\\\\nIF/ID. Keep the existing one-cycle stall for real load consumers, but avoid\\\\nstalling on unused source fields such as OP-IMM rs2 bits, U/J-type operand\\\\nfields, and SYSTEM/FENCE encodings. This should improve CPI under CoreMark's\\\\nload-heavy code while adding only small opcode decode logic off the main ALU\\\\nand memory datapaths, so Fmax should be largely preserved.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Derive or wire IF/ID source-use qualifiers into the hazard unit without changing the NRET=1 top-level RVFI interface.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl...\\n  Line 9: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r3s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Load-use MEM bypass\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The previous structural attempts spent LUTs and timing on front-end control-flow machinery, and the log shows those changes either broke correctness or regressed fitness. The current five-stage core still has the classic one-cycle load-use interlock even though the benchmark memory is zero-wait in FPGA and only randomly stalled in cosim. CoreMark has frequent dependent loads through pointer-heavy list, matrix, and state code, so removing this bubble can improve iterations per cycle without adding predictor target adders to the fetch critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The previous structural attempts spent LUTs and timing on front-end control-flow machinery, and the log shows those changes either broke correctness or regressed fitness. The current five-stage core still has the classic one-cycle load-use interlock even though the benchmark memory is zero-wait in FPGA and only randomly stalled in cosim. CoreMark has frequent dependent loads through pointer-heavy list, matrix, and state code, so removing this bubble can improve iterations per cycle without adding predictor target adders to the fetch critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a MEM-to-EX load-data bypass path that lets an instruction immediately behind a load proceed into EX when the load is in MEM and dmem is ready. Compute the existing load extraction result combinationally in MEM, expose it to the forwarding network, and add a forwarding select for EX operands that chooses the current MEM load result over the older EX/MEM ALU address. Keep the load-use stall only when the load will not have valid data that cycle, such as a dmem stall or a misaligned/trapping load. This structural bypass should remove many single-cycle bubbles while preserving the NRET=1 retirement boundary and RVFI order at MEM/WB.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 6, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\"...\\n  Line 10: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r3s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Decode-stage JAL predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The prior predictor attempts regressed because they placed branch target decode,\\\\ntarget adders, return detection, and prediction muxes directly on the IF PC path.\\\\nCoreMark still pays the existing EX redirect penalty for every unconditional JAL,\\\\nand JAL targets are PC-relative with no register dependency or branch condition.\\\\nPredicting only JAL in decode should recover one control-flow bubble for calls and\\\\nlong jumps while avoiding the risky fetch-path predictor logic that hurt Fmax.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The prior predictor attempts regressed because they placed branch target decode,\\\\ntarget adders, return detection, and prediction muxes directly on the IF PC path.\\\\nCoreMark still pays the existing EX redirect penalty for every unconditional JAL,\\\\nand JAL targets are PC-relative with no register dependency or branch condition.\\\\nPredicting only JAL in decode should recover one control-flow bubble for calls and\\\\nlong jumps while avoiding the risky fetch-path predictor logic that hurt Fmax.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a narrow decode-stage always-taken predictor for legal JAL instructions only.\\\\nID computes pc+imm for JAL, redirects fetch one cycle before EX would, and records\\\\nthe predicted next PC in the ID/EX bundle. EX then suppresses the normal JAL redirect\\\\nwhen the resolved jump target matches the predicted target, but retains the existing\\\\nEX recovery path for conditional branches, JALR, illegal instructions, and any target\\\\nmismatch. Because the predictor excludes branches and returns, it needs no B-type\\\\nimmediate decode in IF, no RAS state, and no operand forwarding, making it a smaller\\\\npredictor angle with a better chance of improving CoreMark iterations/cycle without\\\\nsacrificing the current high Fmax.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add p...\\n  Line 11: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r4s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Source-qualified load interlock\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current load-use detector compares the preceding load rd against both raw\\\\nsource bitfields of the following instruction, even when those bits are not\\\\narchitecturally consumed. CoreMark has many loads and immediate/control\\\\ninstructions, so accidental rd matches can inject bubbles that reduce\\\\niterations per cycle without protecting any real dependency.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current load-use detector compares the preceding load rd against both raw\\\\nsource bitfields of the following instruction, even when those bits are not\\\\narchitecturally consumed. CoreMark has many loads and immediate/control\\\\ninstructions, so accidental rd matches can inject bubbles that reduce\\\\niterations per cycle without protecting any real dependency.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a tiny source-use decode for the IF/ID instruction and qualify the existing\\\\nload-use interlock with uses_rs1 and uses_rs2. This is a narrower retry of the\\\\nearlier source-aware load-use idea: do not mask or reinterpret RVFI operand\\\\nreporting, and do not add MEM bypass selects or fetch prediction paths. The\\\\ncore should keep the one-cycle stall for true load consumers while avoiding\\\\nfalse stalls on OP-IMM rs2 fields, U/J-type immediates, FENCE, SYSTEM/EBREAK,\\\\nand other instructions that do not read both source registers.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Derive simple IF/ID uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 qualifiers from opcode/funct class and pass them only to the hazard unit.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Gate load-use rd comparisons with the source-use qualifiers while preserving existing dmem, imem, redirect, and M-unit stall priority.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Derive simple IF/ID use...\\n  Line 12: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r4s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Register IF/ID fetch boundary\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current front end feeds imem_data directly through decode, immediate\\\\ngeneration, regfile address decode, hazard checks, and into the ID/EX\\\\nregister in a single cycle. Prior structural attempts tried to hide stalls or\\\\nreduce bubbles with added prediction and bypass control, but those changes\\\\nregressed by lengthening already sensitive mux/control paths. A real IF/ID\\\\nregister is a simpler structural retiming that targets Fmax directly.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current front end feeds imem_data directly through decode, immediate\\\\ngeneration, regfile address decode, hazard checks, and into the ID/EX\\\\nregister in a single cycle. Prior structural attempts tried to hide stalls or\\\\nreduce bubbles with added prediction and bypass control, but those changes\\\\nregressed by lengthening already sensitive mux/control paths. A real IF/ID\\\\nregister is a simpler structural retiming that targets Fmax directly.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Insert a proper IF/ID pipeline register inside the fetch stage, so fetched\\\\ninstruction, PC, and valid are captured before decode sees them. The PC still\\\\nadvances only when the downstream ID stage can accept the entry, and redirect\\\\nflushes the registered IF/ID payload before wrong-path state can retire. This\\\\nadds one front-end cycle and may slightly increase taken-control penalty, but\\\\nit removes the imem-to-decoder combinational path and should improve FPGA\\\\nFmax enough to raise CoreMark iter/sec without changing the NRET=1 retirement\\\\ncontract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Keep the existing IF/ID bundle shape, but document and use it as a registered pipeline boundary rather than a combinational fetch payload.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add an IF/ID register for pc, instruction, and valid; hold it on deco...\\n  Line 13: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r4s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Tiny resolved-target BTB\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current core resolves all taken branches and jumps in EX, so tight CoreMark loops pay a two-bubble redirect penalty. Prior predictor attempts regressed because they decoded immediates, computed targets, or managed return-stack state on the front-end critical path. A small BTB trained from already-resolved EX redirects can target the same loop bubbles while avoiding fetch-stage target arithmetic.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current core resolves all taken branches and jumps in EX, so tight CoreMark loops pay a two-bubble redirect penalty. Prior predictor attempts regressed because they decoded immediates, computed targets, or managed return-stack state on the front-end critical path. A small BTB trained from already-resolved EX redirects can target the same loop bubbles while avoiding fetch-stage target arithmetic.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a tiny direct-mapped branch target buffer that stores resolved taken conditional-branch targets, preferably only backward loop branches, along with a compact PC tag and a 2-bit confidence counter. IF predicts to the stored target on a tag hit with a taken counter, otherwise it keeps the existing pc+4 path; EX updates the BTB after branch resolution and flushes on any predicted/resolved next-PC mismatch. Because the target is stored from the prior execution, the fetch path adds only a small tag compare and next-PC mux instead of B/J immediate decode and target adders, which should recover loop-closing branch bubbles with less Fmax damage than the previous predictor attempts.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Carry predicted next-PC metadata through IF/ID and ID/EX so EX can distinguish correct BTB predictions from mispredictions without changing the NRET=1 RVFI interface.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Imple...\\n  Line 14: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r5s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Drop regfile reset fanout\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current register file resets all 32 architectural registers with a for-loop,\\\\nwhich forces reset muxing or reset routing onto the whole small RF array. After\\\\nthe M-unit split, remaining FPGA Fmax is likely limited by ordinary datapath\\\\nand control fanout rather than rare arithmetic. CoreMark initializes the state\\\\nit uses, x0 is already hardwired on reads, and RVFI correctness depends on\\\\nretired architectural behavior rather than power-on values of unused registers.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current register file resets all 32 architectural registers with a for-loop,\\\\nwhich forces reset muxing or reset routing onto the whole small RF array. After\\\\nthe M-unit split, remaining FPGA Fmax is likely limited by ordinary datapath\\\\nand control fanout rather than rare arithmetic. CoreMark initializes the state\\\\nit uses, x0 is already hardwired on reads, and RVFI correctness depends on\\\\nretired architectural behavior rather than power-on values of unused registers.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Remove the explicit reset clear from the integer register file and keep only\\\\nthe synchronous write path for nonzero destination registers. Reads of x0 stay\\\\nforced to zero, same-cycle write-first bypass stays unchanged, and reset of the\\\\npipeline control/valid bits still prevents spurious retirement after reset.\\\\nThis should reduce reset fanout and per-register muxing in a timing-sensitive,\\\\nalways-active block without changing CPI, branch behavior, memory behavior, or\\\\nthe NRET=1 RVFI interface. The change is deliberately narrower than prior\\\\nfailed micro-opts: it does not touch hazard detection, RVFI operand reporting,\\\\nMEM load extraction, or any front-end prediction path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/reg_file.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove the reset-time loop that clears all 32 registers while preser...\\n  Line 15: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r5s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Ordered one-entry store buffer\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent structural attempts spent timing and LUTs on the front end or on load\\\\nbypass paths, and those changes either regressed fitness or broke stall\\\\nhandling. The current core still freezes the entire pipeline whenever a store\\\\nreaches MEM and dmem_ready is low, even though independent ALU, branch, and\\\\nregister-only instructions behind that store could execute while the store is\\\\nwaiting. CoreMark has frequent data stores and the apples-to-apples cosim\\\\ninjects random dmem stalls, so store backpressure is a promising structural\\\\ntarget that avoids fetch-path target adders and load-result critical muxes.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent structural attempts spent timing and LUTs on the front end or on load\\\\nbypass paths, and those changes either regressed fitness or broke stall\\\\nhandling. The current core still freezes the entire pipeline whenever a store\\\\nreaches MEM and dmem_ready is low, even though independent ALU, branch, and\\\\nregister-only instructions behind that store could execute while the store is\\\\nwaiting. CoreMark has frequent data stores and the apples-to-apples cosim\\\\ninjects random dmem stalls, so store backpressure is a promising structural\\\\ntarget that avoids fetch-path target adders and load-result critical muxes.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a single-entry ordered store buffer at the MEM boundary. A regular data\\\\nstore can retire into the buffer with its address, byte mask, write data, and\\\\nRVFI metadata captured, allowing the front of the pipeline to keep executing\\\\nnon-memory instructions while the buffer drives dmem until dmem_ready accepts\\\\nthe write. Preserve precise in-order behavior by stalling any younger load,\\\\nstore, MMIO access, FENCE, EBREAK, or illegal/trapping instruction until the\\\\nbuffer drains; MMIO marker/UART stores should bypass the buffer and keep the\\\\nexisting blocking behavior so CoreMark timing brackets re...\\n  Line 16: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r5s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Single-entry loop target predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current core fetches sequentially and pays the existing EX-stage redirect\\\\npenalty on every taken loop-closing branch. Earlier predictor attempts\\\\nregressed because they placed immediate decode, target adders, BTB arrays,\\\\ncounters, or RAS control on the fetch path. A predictor for this slot should\\\\ntherefore be much narrower: reuse targets already resolved in EX and add the\\\\nsmallest possible fetch-side structure.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current core fetches sequentially and pays the existing EX-stage redirect\\\\npenalty on every taken loop-closing branch. Earlier predictor attempts\\\\nregressed because they placed immediate decode, target adders, BTB arrays,\\\\ncounters, or RAS control on the fetch path. A predictor for this slot should\\\\ntherefore be much narrower: reuse targets already resolved in EX and add the\\\\nsmallest possible fetch-side structure.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a single-entry resolved-loop target predictor trained only by taken\\\\nbackward conditional branches. The entry stores one branch PC and its resolved\\\\ntarget; IF predicts taken only when the current PC equals that stored PC,\\\\notherwise it keeps the existing pc+4 behavior. Carry predicted-taken and\\\\npredicted-next-PC metadata to EX so the normal redirect path fires only on a\\\\nmismatch, and invalidate the entry when that same branch resolves not-taken\\\\nat loop exit. This should recover repeated inner-loop branch bubbles in\\\\nCoreMark while avoiding the fetch target adders, multi-entry tag arrays,\\\\nconfidence counters, and return-stack machinery that hurt prior predictors.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add minimal predicted-taken and predicted-next-PC metadata to IF/ID and ID/EX bundles for EX-stage mismatch detection.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Implement ...\\n  Line 17: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r6s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Prune dead EX/MEM branch metadata\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core has already won most of its fitness from removing broad\\\\ntiming/fanout costs, while recent attempts that add prediction, buffering,\\\\nbypassing, or memory-ordering control have regressed. The EX/MEM bundle still\\\\ncarries branch_taken and branch_target fields that are assigned every cycle\\\\nbut are not consumed by MEM, WB, RVFI, hazard, or forwarding logic. Those 33\\\\ndead flops and their input adders/mux routes sit in an always-active pipeline\\\\nboundary, so pruning them is a low-risk micro-optimization for LUT/FF count\\\\nand routing delay without changing CPI.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core has already won most of its fitness from removing broad\\\\ntiming/fanout costs, while recent attempts that add prediction, buffering,\\\\nbypassing, or memory-ordering control have regressed. The EX/MEM bundle still\\\\ncarries branch_taken and branch_target fields that are assigned every cycle\\\\nbut are not consumed by MEM, WB, RVFI, hazard, or forwarding logic. Those 33\\\\ndead flops and their input adders/mux routes sit in an always-active pipeline\\\\nboundary, so pruning them is a low-risk micro-optimization for LUT/FF count\\\\nand routing delay without changing CPI.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Remove the unused branch_taken and branch_target fields from ex_mem_t and stop\\\\nregistering them in ex_stage. Keep redirect and pc_next computation exactly as\\\\nthey are today, because pc_next is the RVFI-visible resolved next PC and the\\\\nredirect output is still the only live control-flow signal. This should trim\\\\ndead sequential state and reduce EX-stage fanout/routing pressure while\\\\npreserving the NRET=1 RVFI contract, trap behavior, memory behavior, and all\\\\nexisting stall/flush priorities.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 1, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove unused branch_taken and...\\n  Line 18: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r6s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Pipeline EX operand retiming\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent structural attempts that added fetch prediction, skid buffering, load bypassing,\\\\nor store buffering all paid too much control-path cost. The current core's remaining\\\\nhigh-fanout structural path is more basic: ID/EX values pass through forwarding muxes,\\\\nALU operand selection, ALU or branch compare/target logic, and into EX/MEM plus the\\\\nredirect path in one cycle. Retiming that boundary can target Fmax without adding a\\\\npredictor, memory ordering structure, or RVFI contract change.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent structural attempts that added fetch prediction, skid buffering, load bypassing,\\\\nor store buffering all paid too much control-path cost. The current core's remaining\\\\nhigh-fanout structural path is more basic: ID/EX values pass through forwarding muxes,\\\\nALU operand selection, ALU or branch compare/target logic, and into EX/MEM plus the\\\\nredirect path in one cycle. Retiming that boundary can target Fmax without adding a\\\\npredictor, memory ordering structure, or RVFI contract change.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Split the existing EX stage into a lightweight EX1 operand/metadata register and an EX2\\\\nexecute/redirect stage. EX1 captures the forwarded rs1/rs2 values, selected ALU operands,\\\\ndecoded control, immediate, PC, and RVFI source data; EX2 performs the ALU operation,\\\\nbranch/jump resolution, M-unit handshake, and drives the existing EX/MEM payload. Add the\\\\nminimal hazard/forwarding adjustments needed for the extra execute stage, preferring a\\\\none-cycle interlock on immediately dependent ALU consumers over wide new bypass muxes if\\\\nthat keeps timing short. This structural retiming should shorten the common forwarding-to-\\\\nALU-to-EX/MEM cone and may raise Tang Nano 20K Fmax enough to offset a small CPI cost,\\\\nwhile preserving NRET=1 retirement at MEM/WB and all RVFI ordering/trap behavior.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\"...\\n  Line 19: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r6s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Single-entry JAL target predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts regressed because they put immediate decode, target\\\\nadders, multi-entry tags, counters, or return-stack control on the fetch path.\\\\nCoreMark still pays the EX redirect penalty for repeated unconditional calls\\\\nand jumps, but JAL has no direction ambiguity once a target is known. A very\\\\nsmall learned JAL target entry can attack only that control-flow cost while\\\\navoiding conditional branch and return prediction complexity.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts regressed because they put immediate decode, target\\\\nadders, multi-entry tags, counters, or return-stack control on the fetch path.\\\\nCoreMark still pays the EX redirect penalty for repeated unconditional calls\\\\nand jumps, but JAL has no direction ambiguity once a target is known. A very\\\\nsmall learned JAL target entry can attack only that control-flow cost while\\\\navoiding conditional branch and return prediction complexity.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a single-entry predictor trained only by legal resolved JAL instructions\\\\nin EX. The entry stores the exact JAL PC and resolved target; IF predicts to\\\\nthe stored target only when the current PC equals that entry, otherwise it\\\\nkeeps the existing pc+4 path. Carry only predicted-valid and predicted-next-PC\\\\nmetadata to EX so the normal redirect path is suppressed on an exact correct\\\\nprediction and used for all misses or mismatches. This should recover repeated\\\\ncall/jump bubbles in CoreMark without fetch-side immediate decode, B-type\\\\nbranch prediction, return-stack state, confidence counters, or multi-entry BTB\\\\nrouting that hurt previous predictor hypotheses.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add minimal predicted-valid and predicted-next-PC fields to IF/ID and ID/EX bundles for EX-stage JAL prediction validation....\\n  Line 20: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r7s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Iterative M divider\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The largest proven fitness gain for this core came from moving RV32M arithmetic\\\\nout of the ordinary EX ALU path, but the current m_unit still contains wide\\\\ncombinational DIV/REM operators behind a one-cycle result register. Even if\\\\nthose operations are rare in CoreMark, the FPGA still has to place and time\\\\nthe divider/remainder cone, so it can cap Fmax or perturb routing for the hot\\\\nRV32I pipeline.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The largest proven fitness gain for this core came from moving RV32M arithmetic\\\\nout of the ordinary EX ALU path, but the current m_unit still contains wide\\\\ncombinational DIV/REM operators behind a one-cycle result register. Even if\\\\nthose operations are rare in CoreMark, the FPGA still has to place and time\\\\nthe divider/remainder cone, so it can cap Fmax or perturb routing for the hot\\\\nRV32I pipeline.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the one-cycle DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU implementation in m_unit with a small\\\\niterative restoring divider that runs only after a divide or remainder starts.\\\\nKeep MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU on the existing short registered path so any\\\\nmultiply use does not pay a 32-cycle penalty, and keep the existing EX-stage\\\\nhold/valid handshake as the only pipeline interface. This should remove the\\\\nexpensive combinational divider/remainder logic from the FPGA timing closure\\\\nproblem while adding CPI only for uncommon divide/remainder instructions.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/m_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Implement DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU with an iterative signed/unsigned divider state machine while preserving ALTOPS behavior under RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Adjust the existing M-unit busy/valid handshake only as needed so EX holds until multi-cycle DIV/REM completion and still clears completed results exactly once.\\\"}]}, \\\"expec...\\n  Line 21: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r7s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Register dmem stall boundary\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent structural attempts added front-end prediction, fetch buffering, load\\\\nbypassing, store buffering, or a deeper EX pipe, and each paid too much in\\\\nmux/control cost or correctness complexity. A smaller structural target is\\\\nthe current dmem stall path: io_dmemReady fans combinationally through the\\\\nhazard unit into PC hold, ID hold, EX/MEM hold, and MEM/WB valid clearing in\\\\nthe same cycle. CoreMark's apples-to-apples mode injects random dmem stalls,\\\\nso this control path is active during evaluation, and reducing its fanout may\\\\nimprove Fmax without changing the visible memory ordering contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent structural attempts added front-end prediction, fetch buffering, load\\\\nbypassing, store buffering, or a deeper EX pipe, and each paid too much in\\\\nmux/control cost or correctness complexity. A smaller structural target is\\\\nthe current dmem stall path: io_dmemReady fans combinationally through the\\\\nhazard unit into PC hold, ID hold, EX/MEM hold, and MEM/WB valid clearing in\\\\nthe same cycle. CoreMark's apples-to-apples mode injects random dmem stalls,\\\\nso this control path is active during evaluation, and reducing its fanout may\\\\nimprove Fmax without changing the visible memory ordering contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a one-cycle registered MEM busy/accepted boundary for data-memory\\\\noperations. When a load or store enters MEM, capture whether it is waiting for\\\\ndmem_ready in a small state bit local to the MEM/hazard interface; while that\\\\nbit is set, hold EX/MEM and clear MEM/WB valid exactly as today, but drive the\\\\nglobal pipeline stalls from the registered busy state rather than directly\\\\nfrom io_dmemReady. Keep dmem_addr, dmem_wdata, dmem_wen, dmem_ren, RVFI memory\\\\nmasks/data, misalignment traps, and all in-order retirement behavior\\\\nunchanged. This deliberately accepts at most one extra cycle on the first\\\\nstal...\\n  Line 22: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r7s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Decode-only JAL redirect predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts for this core regressed because they added target\\\\nadders, tag compares, return-stack state, or predicted-PC muxing directly to\\\\nthe IF next-PC path. The current core still pays the EX redirect penalty for\\\\nevery unconditional JAL, and JAL needs no operand forwarding or direction\\\\nprediction once its instruction reaches decode.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts for this core regressed because they added target\\\\nadders, tag compares, return-stack state, or predicted-PC muxing directly to\\\\nthe IF next-PC path. The current core still pays the EX redirect penalty for\\\\nevery unconditional JAL, and JAL needs no operand forwarding or direction\\\\nprediction once its instruction reaches decode.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a decode-stage predictor for legal JAL instructions only. ID already has\\\\nthe instruction, PC, and immediate generator output, so it can compute the JAL\\\\ntarget and redirect fetch one cycle earlier than EX without adding any logic\\\\nto the IF PC increment path. Carry a small predicted-next-PC flag/value into\\\\nID/EX so EX suppresses its normal JAL redirect when the ID prediction matches,\\\\nwhile all conditional branches, JALR, illegal instructions, and mismatches use\\\\nthe existing EX recovery path. This avoids the failed fetch-side predictor\\\\nstructures while recovering one bubble on CoreMark calls and PC-relative jumps.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add minimal predicted-valid and predicted-next-PC metadata to the IF/ID and ID/EX bundles for decode-stage JAL prediction validation.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Detect legal JAL in decode, compute pc plus J-immediate, emit an early redirect, and latch the prediction metadata into ID/EX.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Wire the ID...\\n  Line 23: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r8s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Share M-unit multiplier hardware\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core already removed RV32M arithmetic from the ordinary EX\\\\nALU path, but m_unit still builds three separate 64-bit multiplier products\\\\nin parallel for MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU signedness variants. CoreMark uses\\\\nM operations rarely enough that throughput beyond the existing one-cycle\\\\nregistered M-unit is not the main limiter, while the extra DSP/LUT/routing\\\\npressure can still depress FPGA Fmax or perturb placement of the hot RV32I\\\\npipeline.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core already removed RV32M arithmetic from the ordinary EX\\\\nALU path, but m_unit still builds three separate 64-bit multiplier products\\\\nin parallel for MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU signedness variants. CoreMark uses\\\\nM operations rarely enough that throughput beyond the existing one-cycle\\\\nregistered M-unit is not the main limiter, while the extra DSP/LUT/routing\\\\npressure can still depress FPGA Fmax or perturb placement of the hot RV32I\\\\npipeline.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the parallel signed-signed, unsigned-unsigned, and signed-unsigned\\\\nproduct generation in m_unit with one shared signed multiplier fed by\\\\nsign/zero-extended operands selected from the M opcode. MUL can take the low\\\\nhalf of the same product because low 32 bits are independent of operand\\\\nsignedness; MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU select the high half with the appropriate\\\\noperand extension. Keep the existing start/valid/clear handshake, one-cycle\\\\nM latency, DIV/REM behavior, and RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions unchanged.\\\\nThis should trim multiplier area and routing without adding CPI, front-end\\\\nprediction logic, memory ordering state, or any RVFI-visible behavior change.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/m_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Use a single sign-controlled widened multiplier for MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU while pres...\\n  Line 24: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r8s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Register WB result at MEM boundary\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The recent structural attempts mostly lost fitness by adding control-flow\\\\nprediction, buffering, memory overlap, or deeper execute-stage interlocks.\\\\nA safer structural target is the existing MEM/WB-to-WB combinational path:\\\\nthe selected writeback value is still muxed after the MEM/WB register, then\\\\nfans out to the regfile write port, same-cycle regfile bypass, EX forwarding,\\\\nand RVFI rd_wdata. Registering that selected value at the retirement boundary\\\\nshould shorten a hot fanout path without changing CPI, branch timing, memory\\\\nordering, or the NRET=1 external contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The recent structural attempts mostly lost fitness by adding control-flow\\\\nprediction, buffering, memory overlap, or deeper execute-stage interlocks.\\\\nA safer structural target is the existing MEM/WB-to-WB combinational path:\\\\nthe selected writeback value is still muxed after the MEM/WB register, then\\\\nfans out to the regfile write port, same-cycle regfile bypass, EX forwarding,\\\\nand RVFI rd_wdata. Registering that selected value at the retirement boundary\\\\nshould shorten a hot fanout path without changing CPI, branch timing, memory\\\\nordering, or the NRET=1 external contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a preselected rd_wdata/writeback-result field to the MEM/WB pipeline\\\\nbundle and compute it inside mem_stage before the MEM/WB register captures.\\\\nFor loads this field captures the sign/zero-extended load result; for ALU,\\\\nJAL, JALR, AUIPC, LUI, and M-extension operations it captures the existing\\\\nEX/MEM alu_result. Then simplify wb_stage into a pass-through of the\\\\nregistered writeback value instead of a post-register mem_to_reg mux. This\\\\nstructural retiming preserves the same retirement boundary and RVFI data, but\\\\nremoves the load-vs-ALU mux from the next cycle's forwarding/regfile/RVFI\\\\nfanout cone, which may improve Tang Nano 20K Fmax with ess...\\n  Line 25: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r8s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Decode-stage backward branch predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts lost fitness when they added BTB, RAS, tag compare,\\\\ncounter, or target-adder logic to the IF next-PC path. The current core still\\\\npays a two-bubble EX redirect penalty on taken loop-closing branches, which\\\\nare the predictor opportunity most likely to matter for CoreMark. A predictor\\\\nthat waits until decode can use the already-fetched instruction and decoded\\\\nbranch control while keeping fetch as the current simple pc+4 path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts lost fitness when they added BTB, RAS, tag compare,\\\\ncounter, or target-adder logic to the IF next-PC path. The current core still\\\\npays a two-bubble EX redirect penalty on taken loop-closing branches, which\\\\nare the predictor opportunity most likely to matter for CoreMark. A predictor\\\\nthat waits until decode can use the already-fetched instruction and decoded\\\\nbranch control while keeping fetch as the current simple pc+4 path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a static decode-stage predictor for legal backward conditional branches\\\\nonly. ID uses the existing immediate generator and decoded is_branch signal to\\\\nrecognize negative B-type offsets, computes pc+imm, and redirects fetch one\\\\ncycle earlier than EX would. Carry predicted-valid and predicted-next-PC into\\\\nID/EX so EX suppresses the normal redirect when the branch is actually taken\\\\nto the predicted target, or redirects to pc+4 on the loop-exit not-taken\\\\nmisprediction. Forward branches, JAL, JALR, illegal encodings, and all memory\\\\nor RVFI behavior stay on the existing path. This targets loop bubbles without\\\\nreintroducing the failed IF-side predictor structures.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add minimal predicted-valid and predicted-next-PC fields to the ID/EX bundle for EX-stage branch predict...\\n  Line 26: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r9s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Narrow MEM/WB control state\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent micro-optimization wins came from removing always-present FPGA timing\\\\nand routing pressure, while attempts that added prediction, bypassing, or\\\\nstall-hiding control usually regressed. The current MEM/WB register still\\\\ncarries the full decode control bundle even though the writeback and RVFI\\\\nretirement logic only consume a few bits after the MEM stage. Pruning that\\\\ncold control state should reduce flop count, muxing, and routing without\\\\nchanging CoreMark CPI or touching the external NRET=1 contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent micro-optimization wins came from removing always-present FPGA timing\\\\nand routing pressure, while attempts that added prediction, bypassing, or\\\\nstall-hiding control usually regressed. The current MEM/WB register still\\\\ncarries the full decode control bundle even though the writeback and RVFI\\\\nretirement logic only consume a few bits after the MEM stage. Pruning that\\\\ncold control state should reduce flop count, muxing, and routing without\\\\nchanging CoreMark CPI or touching the external NRET=1 contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the full ctrl_t field in mem_wb_t with a narrow retirement/writeback\\\\ncontrol subset containing only reg_write, mem_to_reg, and is_illegal. Keep\\\\nthe existing full ctrl_t through ID/EX and EX/MEM, because the execute and\\\\nmemory stages still need branch, memory width, sign-extension, and M-op\\\\ncontrols. In mem_stage, collapse ctrl_with_trap into the narrow MEM/WB\\\\nfields after misalignment handling, and update wb_stage and core RVFI trap\\\\nreferences to use the narrow fields. This should trim unused MEM/WB control\\\\nflops and fanout while preserving all memory masks/data, load results,\\\\nwriteback behavior, RVFI order, and trap reporting.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Introduce a narrow M...\\n  Line 27: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r9s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Split RVFI shadow metadata from datapath\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The best recent gains came from removing broad arithmetic and register fanout from hot paths, while most structural attempts regressed by adding prediction, buffering, memory overlap, or extra execute-stage interlocks. The current pipeline still carries a wide all-purpose struct through EX/MEM and MEM/WB, mixing hot execution data with RVFI-only metadata such as original source values, source register numbers, instruction bits, memory masks, and retired PC fields. Even when those fields are not part of the architectural datapath, they share the same pipeline registers, reset/hold enables, and module boundaries as timing-critical forwarding, memory, and writeback signals.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The best recent gains came from removing broad arithmetic and register fanout from hot paths, while most structural attempts regressed by adding prediction, buffering, memory overlap, or extra execute-stage interlocks. The current pipeline still carries a wide all-purpose struct through EX/MEM and MEM/WB, mixing hot execution data with RVFI-only metadata such as original source values, source register numbers, instruction bits, memory masks, and retired PC fields. Even when those fields are not part of the architectural datapath, they share the same pipeline registers, reset/hold enables, and module boundaries as timing-critical forwarding, memory, and writeback signals.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Split the pipeline state into a narrow execution payload and a parallel RVFI shadow payload. Keep the hot EX/MEM and MEM/WB datapath bundles limited to control bits and values needed for forwarding, dmem, writeback, and stalls, while carrying RVFI-only fields in a separate shadow register chain that uses the same valid, flush, hold, trap, and redirect decisions. This should reduce register width, enable fanout, and routing pressure on the timing-critical da...\\n  Line 29: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r10s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Prune unused LUI control bit\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent wins came from removing always-present arithmetic, reset, and RVFI\\\\nrouting pressure, while broader CPI-oriented changes and control prediction\\\\nusually regressed. The current control bundle still carries an is_lui bit\\\\nthrough decode and every pipeline stage even though LUI execution is already\\\\nfully encoded by alu_op=ALU_LUI and alu_src=1. That unused bit adds one more\\\\ndecoded signal, pipeline flop, reset/hold mux input, and routing load across\\\\nhot stage boundaries without contributing to architectural behavior.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent wins came from removing always-present arithmetic, reset, and RVFI\\\\nrouting pressure, while broader CPI-oriented changes and control prediction\\\\nusually regressed. The current control bundle still carries an is_lui bit\\\\nthrough decode and every pipeline stage even though LUI execution is already\\\\nfully encoded by alu_op=ALU_LUI and alu_src=1. That unused bit adds one more\\\\ndecoded signal, pipeline flop, reset/hold mux input, and routing load across\\\\nhot stage boundaries without contributing to architectural behavior.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Remove the redundant is_lui signal from the decoder interface and ctrl_t,\\\\nand delete the corresponding ID-stage temporary and bundle assignment. Keep\\\\nLUI behavior unchanged by continuing to set alu_op=ALU_LUI, alu_src=1, and\\\\nreg_write=1 in the existing validated LUI decode arm. This is a narrow\\\\nno-CPI micro-optimization that should slightly reduce control-bundle width,\\\\nregister enables, reset/hold muxing, and decode-to-EX routing pressure while\\\\npreserving the NRET=1 RVFI contract, trap discipline, memory behavior, and\\\\nall existing stall/forwarding logic.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 1, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove the unused is_lui field from ctrl_t so it no longer propagates throug...\\n  Line 30: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r10s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Split MEM RVFI sideband path\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The latest successful structural change split RVFI-only metadata away from the hot execution payloads and improved fitness without changing CPI. The remaining MEM stage still computes and registers RVFI memory sideband fields in the same combinational block and pipeline boundary that drives dmem, load extraction, writeback, and forwarding. A narrower structural separation in MEM is a safer follow-on than adding predictors, store buffers, load bypasses, or deeper execute retiming, all of which have regressed or broken previously.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The latest successful structural change split RVFI-only metadata away from the hot execution payloads and improved fitness without changing CPI. The remaining MEM stage still computes and registers RVFI memory sideband fields in the same combinational block and pipeline boundary that drives dmem, load extraction, writeback, and forwarding. A narrower structural separation in MEM is a safer follow-on than adding predictors, store buffers, load bypasses, or deeper execute retiming, all of which have regressed or broken previously.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Split the MEM-stage memory sideband work into a parallel RVFI-only path while keeping the execution path limited to dmem request signals, load_data, rd, compact control, and valid. The hot MEM/WB execution register should no longer share bundle assignment, hold, and mux fanout with RVFI-only mem_addr, mem_rmask, mem_wmask, mem_rdata, mem_wdata, pc, source values, and instruction fields. Preserve the existing single-retire MEM/WB retirement point, dmem stall behavior, misalignment traps, RVFI aligned-memory reporting, and NRET=1 port shape. This should reduce routing and enable fanout around the MEM boundary with little CPI risk, extending the proven RVFI-shadow split into the last stage where sideband metadata still sits next to timing-sensitive load/w...\\n  Line 31: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r10s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Single hot loop branch predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts for this core regressed because they added immediate\\\\ndecode, target adders, return-stack state, counters, or multi-entry tag logic\\\\nto the fetch/decode control paths. CoreMark still spends cycles on repeated\\\\ntaken loop-closing branches, but the predictor must be smaller than the prior\\\\nBTB, RAS, JAL, and decode-backward experiments to have any chance of paying\\\\non Tang Nano 20K fitness.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts for this core regressed because they added immediate\\\\ndecode, target adders, return-stack state, counters, or multi-entry tag logic\\\\nto the fetch/decode control paths. CoreMark still spends cycles on repeated\\\\ntaken loop-closing branches, but the predictor must be smaller than the prior\\\\nBTB, RAS, JAL, and decode-backward experiments to have any chance of paying\\\\non Tang Nano 20K fitness.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add the smallest possible EX-trained predictor for one hot taken conditional\\\\nbranch: store a single branch PC and its already-resolved target when EX sees\\\\na taken backward branch, predict that target only on an exact PC match, and\\\\ninvalidate the entry when the same branch later resolves not-taken. Unlike the\\\\nfailed BTFNT and BTB attempts, IF performs no instruction immediate decode and\\\\nno target addition; it only compares the current PC with one registered PC and\\\\nmuxes between pc+4 and one registered target. Carry a one-bit predicted flag\\\\nand predicted next PC to EX so correct predictions suppress the normal taken\\\\nredirect while mismatches use the existing recovery path. This targets the most\\\\ncommon loop bubble with minimal predictor state and avoids JAL/JALR/RAS logic.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 1, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add minimal predicted-valid and predicted-next-PC metadata to IF/ID and ID/EX b...\\n  Line 32: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r11s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Narrow EX/MEM control payload\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core already benefits from splitting RVFI shadow metadata away from hot execution payloads, but the EX/MEM execution register still carries the full decoded ctrl_t bundle after execute has consumed most of it. Branch, jump, operand-select, ALU-op, and AUIPC/LUI control bits are no longer needed once the EX result, write data, redirect, and RVFI pc_next have been produced. Trimming those cold bits at the EX/MEM boundary should reduce flop count, hold/reset muxing, and routing pressure on the memory, forwarding, and stall paths without changing CPI.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core already benefits from splitting RVFI shadow metadata away from hot execution payloads, but the EX/MEM execution register still carries the full decoded ctrl_t bundle after execute has consumed most of it. Branch, jump, operand-select, ALU-op, and AUIPC/LUI control bits are no longer needed once the EX result, write data, redirect, and RVFI pc_next have been produced. Trimming those cold bits at the EX/MEM boundary should reduce flop count, hold/reset muxing, and routing pressure on the memory, forwarding, and stall paths without changing CPI.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Introduce a compact post-EX control payload for EX/MEM and MEM-stage consumers containing only the bits still live after execute: mem_read, mem_write, mem_width, mem_sext, reg_write, mem_to_reg, and is_illegal. Keep the full ctrl_t through ID/EX and inside EX where decode, branch, jump, ALU, AUIPC, and M-op decisions are still needed, then collapse to the compact control when registering EX/MEM. This is narrower than prior failed prediction/bypass/stall-hiding changes and differs from the regressed MEM/WB-only pruning by removing unused control before the hotter dmem, hazard, forwarding, and MEM/WB fanout cone.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}...\\n  Line 33: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r11s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Handshake IFID register\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current front end feeds imem_data directly into decode, immediate generation,\\\\nregfile read-address selection, load-use hazard checks, and the ID/EX register in\\\\none cycle. Prior structural attempts that added prediction, bypassing, store\\\\nbuffering, or EX retiming regressed by adding hot mux/control paths. The earlier\\\\nIF/ID-register attempt broke because it lacked a real consume/valid handshake,\\\\nnot because the imem-to-decode retiming target was inherently invalid.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current front end feeds imem_data directly into decode, immediate generation,\\\\nregfile read-address selection, load-use hazard checks, and the ID/EX register in\\\\none cycle. Prior structural attempts that added prediction, bypassing, store\\\\nbuffering, or EX retiming regressed by adding hot mux/control paths. The earlier\\\\nIF/ID-register attempt broke because it lacked a real consume/valid handshake,\\\\nnot because the imem-to-decode retiming target was inherently invalid.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a proper registered IF/ID boundary with an explicit valid bit and ID consume\\\\nhandshake. IF captures a fetched instruction only when imem is ready and the IF/ID\\\\nslot is empty or ID is consuming it; ID consumes exactly one valid entry when the\\\\ndownstream ID/EX register is not held. Load-use, dmem, and M-unit stalls hold the\\\\nIF/ID entry without duplicating it, while EX redirects flush the registered entry\\\\nand update PC with redirect priority. This should remove the imem-data-to-decoder\\\\ncombinational path and improve Fmax, with only a small CPI risk and no change to\\\\nthe NRET=1 retirement/RVFI contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Keep the IF/ID bundle shape but treat it as a registered valid payload with explicit consume semantics documented for the implementation.\\\"},...\\n  Line 35: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r12s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Class-bit M-op encoding\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent successful micro-optimizations improved fitness by removing always-active FPGA timing and routing pressure without adding new control-flow or memory machinery. The current EX stage still detects RV32M operations with eight separate alu_op equality comparisons, and that result feeds the M-unit start/busy path, redirect suppression, and EX/MEM bubble control. CoreMark rarely benefits from complex M-op detection logic being on this hot execute-control path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent successful micro-optimizations improved fitness by removing always-active FPGA timing and routing pressure without adding new control-flow or memory machinery. The current EX stage still detects RV32M operations with eight separate alu_op equality comparisons, and that result feeds the M-unit start/busy path, redirect suppression, and EX/MEM bubble control. CoreMark rarely benefits from complex M-op detection logic being on this hot execute-control path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Recode the internal ALU operation constants so every RV32M operation has alu_op[4]=1 while all ordinary RV32I operations keep alu_op[4]=0. Then replace the EX-stage is_m_op eight-way compare tree with a single in.valid && in.ctrl.alu_op[4] test. This preserves the decoder's legal-instruction checks, ALU and M-unit case behavior, RVFI retirement, and the existing one-cycle M-unit handshake, but should reduce LUT depth and fanout in the execute control path with no CPI cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Reassign ALU_MUL through ALU_REMU localparams into a contiguous high-bit class while leaving RV32I ALU ops below bit 4.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the explicit eight-operation is_m_op comparison with a single alu_op class-bit check.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delt...\\n  Line 36: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r12s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Split ID/EX RVFI shadow\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The only clearly successful structural change in the recent bench history was\\\\nsplitting RVFI-only metadata away from the hot EX/MEM and MEM/WB datapath\\\\npayloads. The current ID/EX register still mixes execution-critical operands,\\\\nimmediate/control, and forwarding source numbers with RVFI-only instruction\\\\nbits and retirement reporting fields. A similarly narrow split at the decode\\\\nboundary targets register width, reset/hold muxing, and routing pressure\\\\nwithout adding predictors, memory ordering structures, bypass selects, or an\\\\nextra execute stage.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The only clearly successful structural change in the recent bench history was\\\\nsplitting RVFI-only metadata away from the hot EX/MEM and MEM/WB datapath\\\\npayloads. The current ID/EX register still mixes execution-critical operands,\\\\nimmediate/control, and forwarding source numbers with RVFI-only instruction\\\\nbits and retirement reporting fields. A similarly narrow split at the decode\\\\nboundary targets register width, reset/hold muxing, and routing pressure\\\\nwithout adding predictors, memory ordering structures, bypass selects, or an\\\\nextra execute stage.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the single id_ex_t payload with a narrow execution payload and a\\\\nparallel ID/EX RVFI shadow payload. Keep the hot execution register limited\\\\nto PC, operands, immediate, rd, source register numbers needed by forwarding,\\\\ndecoded control, and valid; carry RVFI-only fields such as the original\\\\ninstruction and any retirement-only source metadata in a separate shadow\\\\nregister that follows the same stall and flush decisions. EX should consume\\\\nboth payloads and continue producing the existing split EX/MEM execution and\\\\nRVFI shadow outputs. This extends the proven RVFI-shadow structural pattern\\\\none stage earlier while preserving pipeline depth, redirect timing, load-use\\\\nbehavior, M-unit handshake, ...\\n  Line 37: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r12s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Guarded hot-loop predictor retry\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts for this core have consistently regressed or broken\\\\nbecause target adders, tags, counters, RAS state, or predicted-PC metadata\\\\nlengthened hot fetch/decode/control paths. The only predictor already present\\\\nand essentially free is static sequential fetch, but CoreMark still pays two\\\\nbubbles on every taken loop-closing branch. The one predictor angle still\\\\nworth testing is a corrected, extremely small hot-loop predictor that avoids\\\\ntarget arithmetic in fetch and explicitly fixes the prior pc_fwd failure.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts for this core have consistently regressed or broken\\\\nbecause target adders, tags, counters, RAS state, or predicted-PC metadata\\\\nlengthened hot fetch/decode/control paths. The only predictor already present\\\\nand essentially free is static sequential fetch, but CoreMark still pays two\\\\nbubbles on every taken loop-closing branch. The one predictor angle still\\\\nworth testing is a corrected, extremely small hot-loop predictor that avoids\\\\ntarget arithmetic in fetch and explicitly fixes the prior pc_fwd failure.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a single-entry EX-trained predictor for one taken backward conditional\\\\nbranch, but constrain the implementation around the last broken attempt's\\\\nfailure mode. IF only compares the current PC against one registered branch\\\\nPC and selects one registered target; it performs no instruction decode,\\\\ntarget addition, counters, or RAS work. Carry predicted_valid and\\\\npredicted_next_pc through IF/ID and ID/EX, and in EX suppress redirect only\\\\nwhen the branch is valid, actually taken, and its resolved target exactly\\\\nmatches predicted_next_pc. On predicted-taken loop exit, invalidate the entry\\\\nand redirect to pc+4 while reporting rvfi pc_next as pc+4 for that retiring\\\\nbranch. This narrow retry targets the most valuable CoreMark control hazard\\\\...\\n  Line 38: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r13s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Prune redundant LUI control\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The strongest bench-core results have come from removing always-active FPGA\\\\nrouting pressure without adding new prediction, bypass, memory ordering, or\\\\nretiming machinery. The current control bundle still carries an is_lui bit\\\\nfrom decode through ID/EX, EX/MEM, and MEM/WB even though no live execute,\\\\nmemory, writeback, hazard, or RVFI logic consumes it. LUI behavior is already\\\\nfully represented by alu_op=ALU_LUI, alu_src=1, and reg_write=1.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The strongest bench-core results have come from removing always-active FPGA\\\\nrouting pressure without adding new prediction, bypass, memory ordering, or\\\\nretiming machinery. The current control bundle still carries an is_lui bit\\\\nfrom decode through ID/EX, EX/MEM, and MEM/WB even though no live execute,\\\\nmemory, writeback, hazard, or RVFI logic consumes it. LUI behavior is already\\\\nfully represented by alu_op=ALU_LUI, alu_src=1, and reg_write=1.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Remove the redundant is_lui signal from ctrl_t and from the decoder and ID\\\\nstage wiring, leaving the existing LUI decode arm to set ALU_LUI, alu_src,\\\\nand reg_write exactly as before. This should slightly reduce control-bundle\\\\nwidth, pipeline register muxing, and decode-to-execute routing fanout with no\\\\nCPI cost and no change to the NRET=1 top-level RVFI contract. The change is\\\\nintentionally smaller than the regressed control-payload splits and avoids\\\\nall front-end predictor or memory-bypass paths that have repeatedly lost\\\\nfitness on this FPGA.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 1, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove the unused is_lui field from ctrl_t so it no longer propagates through pipeline payloads.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/decoder.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Drop the is_lui output and keep the validated LUI arm encoded through ALU_LUI, alu_src, and reg_write.\\\"},...\\n  Line 39: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r13s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Localize WB-to-ID bypass\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior structural attempts show that adding predictors, skid buffers, store buffers,\\\\nload bypasses, or deeper execute retiming usually costs more FPGA fitness than it\\\\nrecovers. The remaining promising structural direction is to reduce always-active\\\\nfanout and muxing without changing CPI or control-flow timing. The integer regfile\\\\ncurrently embeds write-first same-cycle bypass compares and 32-bit muxes on both\\\\ncombinational read ports, so WB write-enable/address/data fan out through the\\\\nregister file as well as to forwarding, RVFI, and writeback.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior structural attempts show that adding predictors, skid buffers, store buffers,\\\\nload bypasses, or deeper execute retiming usually costs more FPGA fitness than it\\\\nrecovers. The remaining promising structural direction is to reduce always-active\\\\nfanout and muxing without changing CPI or control-flow timing. The integer regfile\\\\ncurrently embeds write-first same-cycle bypass compares and 32-bit muxes on both\\\\ncombinational read ports, so WB write-enable/address/data fan out through the\\\\nregister file as well as to forwarding, RVFI, and writeback.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Move the same-cycle WB-to-ID bypass out of reg_file and into id_stage as two local\\\\nread-data selects after the raw register-file read. The reg_file would become a\\\\nsimpler 2-read/1-write array with x0 forcing only, while id_stage receives the WB\\\\nwrite port and selects wb_w_data for rs1/rs2 when the decoded source matches the\\\\nretiring destination. This preserves the existing architectural behavior needed\\\\nfor consumers that are too far from the writer to be covered by EX forwarding, but\\\\nlocalizes the write-first muxing near decode and may let synthesis pack the regfile\\\\narray and WB fanout better. It avoids changing pipeline depth, redirect timing,\\\\nmemory ordering, RVFI retirement, or the NRET=1 top-level con...\\n  Line 40: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r13s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Registered prebranch loop predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts mostly lost because the prediction decision sat on the\\\\nsame-cycle fetch next-PC path: immediate decode, target adders, tag compares,\\\\ncounters, RAS muxes, or current-PC BTB compares lengthened Fmax-critical logic.\\\\nCoreMark still pays repeated taken-loop redirect bubbles, so the remaining\\\\nuseful predictor angle is to predict one cycle before the loop branch reaches\\\\nfetch and keep the branch-cycle PC update path nearly as simple as today.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts mostly lost because the prediction decision sat on the\\\\nsame-cycle fetch next-PC path: immediate decode, target adders, tag compares,\\\\ncounters, RAS muxes, or current-PC BTB compares lengthened Fmax-critical logic.\\\\nCoreMark still pays repeated taken-loop redirect bubbles, so the remaining\\\\nuseful predictor angle is to predict one cycle before the loop branch reaches\\\\nfetch and keep the branch-cycle PC update path nearly as simple as today.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Train a single-entry loop predictor from resolved taken backward conditional\\\\nbranches in EX, but store both the branch PC and the preceding sequential PC\\\\n(branch_pc - 4) along with the resolved target. IF compares the current PC\\\\nagainst the stored prebranch PC and registers a pending prediction for the next\\\\ncycle; when the branch PC is fetched, the next-PC mux uses only that registered\\\\npending bit and stored target, not a same-cycle tag compare or target adder.\\\\nCarry minimal predicted-valid and predicted-next-PC metadata to EX so a correct\\\\nprediction suppresses the normal redirect, while loop exit invalidates the\\\\nentry and redirects to pc+4 with RVFI pc_next kept consistent. This targets the\\\\nhot backward-branch case while moving the predictor compare off the branch\\\\nfetch cycle, addressing the main Fmax failure mode of previous predictor tries.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact...\\n  Line 41: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r14s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Retimed M-unit operand stage\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The largest proven bench-core gains came from keeping rare RV32M arithmetic off the ordinary RV32I execute path, and the smaller multiplier-sharing win suggests M-unit placement and timing still matter on the Tang Nano 20K. The current M unit still evaluates multiply/divide/remainder results directly from EX operands into the result register on the start edge, so a wide arithmetic cone remains a possible global Fmax limiter even though CoreMark rarely needs M-op throughput.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The largest proven bench-core gains came from keeping rare RV32M arithmetic off the ordinary RV32I execute path, and the smaller multiplier-sharing win suggests M-unit placement and timing still matter on the Tang Nano 20K. The current M unit still evaluates multiply/divide/remainder results directly from EX operands into the result register on the start edge, so a wide arithmetic cone remains a possible global Fmax limiter even though CoreMark rarely needs M-op throughput.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a registered operand/opcode stage inside m_unit so start only captures the selected RV32M operation and operands, then compute and register the arithmetic result on the following cycle. Adjust the existing EX-stage hold logic to wait on the M unit's valid output rather than assuming exactly one bubble. This trades one extra cycle on rare MUL/DIV/REM instructions for a shorter start-to-register timing path, without touching branch prediction, memory bypassing, load-use behavior, RVFI fields, or the NRET=1 top-level interface.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/m_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Insert registered op/a/b operands ahead of the existing RV32M arithmetic and produce valid one cycle later while preserving RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS results.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Use the M-uni...\\n  Line 42: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r14s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Source-aware load interlock\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current five-stage pipeline still treats both raw rs bitfields of the\\\\ninstruction in IF/ID as real consumers when deciding whether a preceding load\\\\nmust insert a bubble. That is structurally conservative but overly broad:\\\\nOP-IMM, LOAD, JALR, LUI, AUIPC, JAL, FENCE, and EBREAK either ignore rs2 or\\\\nignore both source fields, so accidental matches against the load rd can\\\\ncreate false load-use stalls. CoreMark is load-heavy, and reducing bubbles\\\\nwithout adding predictors, MEM bypass muxes, store buffers, or deeper EX\\\\nstaging is one of the few remaining CPI improvements with modest timing risk.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current five-stage pipeline still treats both raw rs bitfields of the\\\\ninstruction in IF/ID as real consumers when deciding whether a preceding load\\\\nmust insert a bubble. That is structurally conservative but overly broad:\\\\nOP-IMM, LOAD, JALR, LUI, AUIPC, JAL, FENCE, and EBREAK either ignore rs2 or\\\\nignore both source fields, so accidental matches against the load rd can\\\\ncreate false load-use stalls. CoreMark is load-heavy, and reducing bubbles\\\\nwithout adding predictors, MEM bypass muxes, store buffers, or deeper EX\\\\nstaging is one of the few remaining CPI improvements with modest timing risk.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a tiny source-use classifier for the IF/ID instruction and feed its\\\\nuses_rs1 and uses_rs2 bits only into the load-use hazard comparison. Keep the\\\\nexisting one-cycle interlock for real consumers, but suppress the stall when\\\\nthe matching raw bitfield is actually an immediate, destination, or unused\\\\nSYSTEM/FENCE field. This should improve iterations/cycle by removing false\\\\nbubbles while leaving RVFI rs1/rs2 address and data reporting unchanged, which\\\\navoids the failure mode from earlier source-aware attempts. The added opcode\\\\ndecode is local to hazard control and should be cheaper than prior structural...\\n  Line 43: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r14s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Static not-taken fast path\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts for this core have consistently regressed or broken\\\\nbecause they added fetch-side target adders, tag compares, predicted-PC\\\\nmetadata, return-stack state, or decode-stage redirect arbitration. The only\\\\npredictor that has not hurt timing is the one already implicit in the core:\\\\nsequential static not-taken fetch for conditional branches. The remaining\\\\npredictor opportunity is therefore to make that static predictor cheaper in\\\\nEX rather than add new prediction state.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Prior predictor attempts for this core have consistently regressed or broken\\\\nbecause they added fetch-side target adders, tag compares, predicted-PC\\\\nmetadata, return-stack state, or decode-stage redirect arbitration. The only\\\\npredictor that has not hurt timing is the one already implicit in the core:\\\\nsequential static not-taken fetch for conditional branches. The remaining\\\\npredictor opportunity is therefore to make that static predictor cheaper in\\\\nEX rather than add new prediction state.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Restructure EX branch resolution around the static not-taken fast path. For\\\\nconditional branches, compute the resolved next PC as pc+4 by default and\\\\nonly feed the branch-target adder result into redirect and RVFI pc_next when\\\\nthe branch condition is true. Keep jumps, JALR, misalignment traps, RVFI\\\\nreporting, and the existing flush/stall policy unchanged. This does not save\\\\ntaken-branch bubbles, but it may shorten the common not-taken branch/control\\\\ncone and reduce target-adder fanout without any IF predictor mux, stored\\\\ntarget, predicted-PC payload, or new recovery path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 1, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Bias branch next-PC and redirect logic toward the existing static not-taken prediction, using the target adder only on taken ...\",\"metadata\":{\"matches\":41,\"truncated\":false},\"title\":\"category.*micro_opt|micro_opt|Localize WB|RVFI|forward|branch-target|M-unit|reg_file|mem_stage|hazard_unit\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778700495331,\"end\":1778700495394}},\"metadata\":{\"openai\":{\"itemId\":\"fc_09d3d947652c5be8016a04d0cf29dc819a9927bfc95b409e11\"}},\"id\":\"prt_e22cfa9e0001ZIfCw3AuyXQQ40\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1dd308545ffeS2tbmWRdSb0axR\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e22cf7b2b0011N5x08sNcBf1vY\"}}\n./bench/gpt-5_5_medium/rep2/agent.log:296:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778693403686,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1dd9d6cb0ffeTxEWWSpKre73Ol\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"apply_patch\",\"callID\":\"call_YNZtS7TLOeVRZCl6MzQGYGgF\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"patchText\":\"*** Begin Patch\\n*** Add File: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260513-001-r8s0.yaml\\n+id: hyp-20260513-001-r8s0\\n+title: Share M-unit multiplier hardware\\n+category: micro_opt\\n+motivation: |\\n+  The current best core already removed RV32M arithmetic from the ordinary EX\\n+  ALU path, but m_unit still builds three separate 64-bit multiplier products\\n+  in parallel for MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU signedness variants. CoreMark uses\\n+  M operations rarely enough that throughput beyond the existing one-cycle\\n+  registered M-unit is not the main limiter, while the extra DSP/LUT/routing\\n+  pressure can still depress FPGA Fmax or perturb placement of the hot RV32I\\n+  pipeline.\\n+hypothesis: |\\n+  Replace the parallel signed-signed, unsigned-unsigned, and signed-unsigned\\n+  product generation in m_unit with one shared signed multiplier fed by\\n+  sign/zero-extended operands selected from the M opcode. MUL can take the low\\n+  half of the same product because low 32 bits are independent of operand\\n+  signedness; MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU select the high half with the appropriate\\n+  operand extension. Keep the existing start/valid/clear handshake, one-cycle\\n+  M latency, DIV/REM behavior, and RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions unchanged.\\n+  This should trim multiplier area and routing without adding CPI, front-end\\n+  prediction logic, memory ordering state, or any RVFI-visible behavior change.\\n+expected_impact:\\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 2\\n+  confidence: medium\\n+changes:\\n+  - file: rtl/m_unit.sv\\n+    description: \\\"Use a single sign-controlled widened multiplier for MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU while preserving the current M-unit handshake and ALTOPS behavior.\\\"\\n*** End Patch\"},\"output\":\"Success. Updated the following files:\\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260513-001-r8s0.yaml\",\"metadata\":{\"diff\":\"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_5_medium-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260513-001-r8s0.yaml\\n===================================================================\\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_5_medium-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260513-001-r8s0.yaml\\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_5_medium-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260513-001-r8s0.yaml\\n@@ -0,0 +1,27 @@\\n+id: hyp-20260513-001-r8s0\\n+title: Share M-unit multiplier hardware\\n+category: micro_opt\\n+motivation: |\\n+  The current best core already removed RV32M arithmetic from the ordinary EX\\n+  ALU path, but m_unit still builds three separate 64-bit multiplier products\\n+  in parallel for MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU signedness variants. CoreMark uses\\n+  M operations rarely enough that throughput beyond the existing one-cycle\\n+  registered M-unit is not the main limiter, while the extra DSP/LUT/routing\\n+  pressure can still depress FPGA Fmax or perturb placement of the hot RV32I\\n+  pipeline.\\n+hypothesis: |\\n+  Replace the parallel signed-signed, unsigned-unsigned, and signed-unsigned\\n+  product generation in m_unit with one shared signed multiplier fed by\\n+  sign/zero-extended operands selected from the M opcode. MUL can take the low\\n+  half of the same product because low 32 bits are independent of operand\\n+  signedness; MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU select the high half with the appropriate\\n+  operand extension. Keep the existing start/valid/clear handshake, one-cycle\\n+  M latency, DIV/REM behavior, and RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions unchanged.\\n+  This should trim multiplier area and routing without adding CPI, front-end\\n+  prediction logic, memory ordering state, or any RVFI-visible behavior change.\\n+expected_impact:\\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 2\\n+  confidence: medium\\n+changes:\\n+  - file: rtl/m_unit.sv\\n+    description: \\\"Use a single sign-controlled widened multiplier for MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU while preserving the current M-unit handshake and ALTOPS behavior.\\\"\\n\\n\",\"files\":[{\"filePath\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_5_medium-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260513-001-r8s0.yaml\",\"relativePath\":\"cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260513-001-r8s0.yaml\",\"type\":\"add\",\"patch\":\"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_5_medium-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260513-001-r8s0.yaml\\n===================================================================\\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_5_medium-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260513-001-r8s0.yaml\\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_5_medium-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260513-001-r8s0.yaml\\n@@ -0,0 +1,27 @@\\n+id: hyp-20260513-001-r8s0\\n+title: Share M-unit multiplier hardware\\n+category: micro_opt\\n+motivation: |\\n+  The current best core already removed RV32M arithmetic from the ordinary EX\\n+  ALU path, but m_unit still builds three separate 64-bit multiplier products\\n+  in parallel for MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU signedness variants. CoreMark uses\\n+  M operations rarely enough that throughput beyond the existing one-cycle\\n+  registered M-unit is not the main limiter, while the extra DSP/LUT/routing\\n+  pressure can still depress FPGA Fmax or perturb placement of the hot RV32I\\n+  pipeline.\\n+hypothesis: |\\n+  Replace the parallel signed-signed, unsigned-unsigned, and signed-unsigned\\n+  product generation in m_unit with one shared signed multiplier fed by\\n+  sign/zero-extended operands selected from the M opcode. MUL can take the low\\n+  half of the same product because low 32 bits are independent of operand\\n+  signedness; MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU select the high half with the appropriate\\n+  operand extension. Keep the existing start/valid/clear handshake, one-cycle\\n+  M latency, DIV/REM behavior, and RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions unchanged.\\n+  This should trim multiplier area and routing without adding CPI, front-end\\n+  prediction logic, memory ordering state, or any RVFI-visible behavior change.\\n+expected_impact:\\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 2\\n+  confidence: medium\\n+changes:\\n+  - file: rtl/m_unit.sv\\n+    description: \\\"Use a single sign-controlled widened multiplier for MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU while preserving the current M-unit handshake and ALTOPS behavior.\\\"\\n\",\"additions\":27,\"deletions\":0}],\"diagnostics\":{},\"truncated\":false},\"title\":\"Success. Updated the following files:\\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260513-001-r8s0.yaml\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778693403679,\"end\":1778693403685}},\"metadata\":{\"openai\":{\"itemId\":\"fc_0489bf2edb773fff016a04b50b8d1881999edbf204e8a91a8b\"}},\"id\":\"prt_e2263358600141XaOfu1E25O7n\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1dd9d6cb0ffeTxEWWSpKre73Ol\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e2262cf610018x5LVMiKRwNUT0\"}}\n./bench/gpt-5_5_medium/rep2/agent.log:305:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778695238059,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1dd80c37dffeA0edlSklrIPTbJ\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"read\",\"callID\":\"call_rZHyeMg5mqrdgTKXspD13JVx\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"filePath\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_5_medium-rep2/cores/bench/LESSONS.md\",\"offset\":1,\"limit\":200},\"output\":\"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_5_medium-rep2/cores/bench/LESSONS.md</path>\\n<type>file</type>\\n<content>\\n1: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r1s0 (improvement, +32.85%): Moving RV32M multiply/divide out of the combinational EX ALU behind a multicycle handshake can substantially improve FPGA fitness because CoreMark rarely needs M-op throughput while the removed arithmetic cone dominates Fmax.\\n2: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r1s1 (broken, n/a): Moving branch/JALR resolution into ID needs exhaustive operand-valid stalling and forwarding for decode-stage compares/base operands; stale early redirects can let wrong-path state reach writeback, which riscv-formal's reg check catches.\\n3: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r1s2 (regression, +21.76%): IF-stage BTFNT/JAL prediction can regress CoreMark when instruction-bit immediate decode and target adders sit directly on the fetch PC path, so front-end bubble savings must be weighed against Fmax-critical next-PC logic.\\n4: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r2s0 (regression, -1.92%): Replacing MEM-stage variable shifts with explicit byte/halfword lane case muxes did not help FPGA fitness, suggesting the synthesizer already maps these narrow shifts well and the added mux/control structure can slightly worsen routing or LUT packing.\\n5: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r2s1 (regression, -7.79%): A two-entry IF skid buffer did not pay for CoreMark's random imem stalls; the added fetch-valid/control and redirect-flush machinery can cost more FPGA fitness than the front-end bubbles it hides.\\n6: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r2s2 (regression, -44.37%): A tiny IF return-address stack can still badly hurt FPGA fitness when canonical-return detection, RAS top muxing, and predicted-PC metadata outweigh the CoreMark return bubbles it removes.\\n7: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r3s0 (broken, n/a): Keep source-aware load-use interlocks separate from RVFI operand reporting changes; masking unused rs fields can break cosim's retire trace even when the intended stall reduction logic is local to hazard_unit.\\n8: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r3s1 (regression, -14.30%): MEM-to-EX load bypass can lose FPGA fitness when dmem-ready gating, load extraction, and an extra EX operand forwarding select lengthen the critical path more than CoreMark benefits from removing one-cycle load-use bubbles.\\n9: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r3s2 (regression, -20.54%): Even decode-stage JAL prediction can regress when the early_redirect priority path, target adder, and predicted-PC metadata still add enough IF/ID/EX control cost to outweigh saving CoreMark's unconditional-jump bubble.\\n10: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r4s1 (broken, n/a): A registered IF/ID boundary must pair imem backpressure with an ID consume/valid handshake; holding a valid IF/ID entry while ID keeps advancing can duplicate instructions during CoreMark stalls.\\n11: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r4s2 (regression, -9.29%): Even a resolved-target BTB can lose FPGA fitness when IF-side tag/valid/counter arrays, target muxing, and predicted-PC metadata lengthen the next-PC path more than CoreMark benefits from avoiding loop redirect bubbles.\\n12: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r5s0 (improvement, +1.93%): Dropping reset clears from the integer register file can improve FPGA fitness because pipeline-valid reset and x0 read forcing preserve architectural behavior while avoiding reset fanout and per-register muxing in an always-active datapath block.\\n13: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r5s1 (regression, -6.89%): A one-entry ordered store buffer can lose CoreMark fitness when store-order interlocks, MMIO/trap classification, and dmem muxing add more MEM/control-path cost than random store-stall overlap recovers.\\n14: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r5s2 (regression, -61.05%): Even a single-entry loop predictor can badly regress when predicted-PC metadata, IF PC/tag compare muxing, and EX mismatch redirect logic lengthen critical control paths more than repeated CoreMark loop-branch bubble removal helps.\\n15: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r6s0 (regression, -2.40%): Pruning unused EX/MEM branch_taken/branch_target metadata did not improve FPGA fitness, suggesting these dead-looking pipeline fields were not on the binding path or were already optimized well enough that layout perturbation outweighed the small flop reduction.\\n16: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r6s1 (regression, -88.09%): Splitting EX with an EX1 operand register can destroy CoreMark fitness when it delays branch/JALR redirects and forces broad dependent-ALU interlocks, so operand-cone retiming must preserve bypass/redirect latency to pay off.\\n17: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r6s2 (regression, -48.05%): A JAL-only single-entry target predictor still regressed without fetch-side immediate decode because the IF PC compare/target mux, predicted-PC pipeline fields, and EX mismatch redirect logic outweighed CoreMark's repeated unconditional-jump bubble savings.\\n18: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r7s0 (regression, -53.28%): Replacing cold RV32M DIV/REM combinational operators with an iterative divider can severely regress CoreMark when the busy/start handshake, divider state, and 32-cycle latency perturb the hot pipeline more than removing the rarely used divide cone helps Fmax.\\n19: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r7s1 (broken, n/a): Registering dmem backpressure needs a real ready/valid or skid boundary at MEM; a lone pending/wait bit can hide the first stalled cycle from upstream control and let younger CoreMark memory-state updates be dropped or reordered.\\n20: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r8s0 (improvement, +2.79%): Sharing one sign/zero-extended M-unit multiplier across MULH signedness variants can improve FPGA fitness because CoreMark rarely needs M throughput and parallel 64-bit products add DSP/LUT routing pressure.\\n21: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r8s1 (regression, -0.33%): Preselecting MEM/WB writeback data inside mem_stage did not improve fitness, suggesting the load-vs-ALU writeback mux is not a binding FPGA path and adding another pipeline field can slightly worsen packing/routing.\\n22: - 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r8s2 (regression, +1.88%): Moving backward-branch prediction from IF to ID still regressed because the ID target adder, redirect/stall arbitration, predicted-PC metadata, and EX mispredict compare/control outweighed CoreMark loop-bubble savings.\\n\\n(End of file - total 22 lines)\\n</content>\",\"metadata\":{\"preview\":\"- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r1s0 (improvement, +32.85%): Moving RV32M multiply/divide out of the combinational EX ALU behind a multicycle handshake can substantially improve FPGA fitness because CoreMark rarely needs M-op throughput while the removed arithmetic cone dominates Fmax.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r1s1 (broken, n/a): Moving branch/JALR resolution into ID needs exhaustive operand-valid stalling and forwarding for decode-stage compares/base operands; stale early redirects can let wrong-path state reach writeback, which riscv-formal's reg check catches.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r1s2 (regression, +21.76%): IF-stage BTFNT/JAL prediction can regress CoreMark when instruction-bit immediate decode and target adders sit directly on the fetch PC path, so front-end bubble savings must be weighed against Fmax-critical next-PC logic.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r2s0 (regression, -1.92%): Replacing MEM-stage variable shifts with explicit byte/halfword lane case muxes did not help FPGA fitness, suggesting the synthesizer already maps these narrow shifts well and the added mux/control structure can slightly worsen routing or LUT packing.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r2s1 (regression, -7.79%): A two-entry IF skid buffer did not pay for CoreMark's random imem stalls; the added fetch-valid/control and redirect-flush machinery can cost more FPGA fitness than the front-end bubbles it hides.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r2s2 (regression, -44.37%): A tiny IF return-address stack can still badly hurt FPGA fitness when canonical-return detection, RAS top muxing, and predicted-PC metadata outweigh the CoreMark return bubbles it removes.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r3s0 (broken, n/a): Keep source-aware load-use interlocks separate from RVFI operand reporting changes; masking unused rs fields can break cosim's retire trace even when the intended stall reduction logic is local to hazard_unit.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r3s1 (regression, -14.30%): MEM-to-EX load bypass can lose FPGA fitness when dmem-ready gating, load extraction, and an extra EX operand forwarding select lengthen the critical path more than CoreMark benefits from removing one-cycle load-use bubbles.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r3s2 (regression, -20.54%): Even decode-stage JAL prediction can regress when the early_redirect priority path, target adder, and predicted-PC metadata still add enough IF/ID/EX control cost to outweigh saving CoreMark's unconditional-jump bubble.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r4s1 (broken, n/a): A registered IF/ID boundary must pair imem backpressure with an ID consume/valid handshake; holding a valid IF/ID entry while ID keeps advancing can duplicate instructions during CoreMark stalls.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r4s2 (regression, -9.29%): Even a resolved-target BTB can lose FPGA fitness when IF-side tag/valid/counter arrays, target muxing, and predicted-PC metadata lengthen the next-PC path more than CoreMark benefits from avoiding loop redirect bubbles.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r5s0 (improvement, +1.93%): Dropping reset clears from the integer register file can improve FPGA fitness because pipeline-valid reset and x0 read forcing preserve architectural behavior while avoiding reset fanout and per-register muxing in an always-active datapath block.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r5s1 (regression, -6.89%): A one-entry ordered store buffer can lose CoreMark fitness when store-order interlocks, MMIO/trap classification, and dmem muxing add more MEM/control-path cost than random store-stall overlap recovers.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r5s2 (regression, -61.05%): Even a single-entry loop predictor can badly regress when predicted-PC metadata, IF PC/tag compare muxing, and EX mismatch redirect logic lengthen critical control paths more than repeated CoreMark loop-branch bubble removal helps.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r6s0 (regression, -2.40%): Pruning unused EX/MEM branch_taken/branch_target metadata did not improve FPGA fitness, suggesting these dead-looking pipeline fields were not on the binding path or were already optimized well enough that layout perturbation outweighed the small flop reduction.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r6s1 (regression, -88.09%): Splitting EX with an EX1 operand register can destroy CoreMark fitness when it delays branch/JALR redirects and forces broad dependent-ALU interlocks, so operand-cone retiming must preserve bypass/redirect latency to pay off.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-003-r6s2 (regression, -48.05%): A JAL-only single-entry target predictor still regressed without fetch-side immediate decode because the IF PC compare/target mux, predicted-PC pipeline fields, and EX mismatch redirect logic outweighed CoreMark's repeated unconditional-jump bubble savings.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r7s0 (regression, -53.28%): Replacing cold RV32M DIV/REM combinational operators with an iterative divider can severely regress CoreMark when the busy/start handshake, divider state, and 32-cycle latency perturb the hot pipeline more than removing the rarely used divide cone helps Fmax.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-002-r7s1 (broken, n/a): Registering dmem backpressure needs a real ready/valid or skid boundary at MEM; a lone pending/wait bit can hide the first stalled cycle from upstream control and let younger CoreMark memory-state updates be dropped or reordered.\\n- 2026-05-13 hyp-20260513-001-r8s0 (improvement, +2.79%): Sharing one sign/zero-extended M-unit multiplier across MULH signedness variants can improve FPGA fitness because CoreMark rarely needs M throughput and parallel 64-bit products add DSP/LUT routing pressure.\",\"truncated\":false,\"loaded\":[]},\"title\":\"cores/bench/LESSONS.md\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778695238050,\"end\":1778695238058}},\"metadata\":{\"openai\":{\"itemId\":\"fc_098f252b08c14eb4016a04bc45e114819abe9fdd43e68ca76d\"}},\"id\":\"prt_e227f718400166InL7CSGSHZ3C\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1dd80c37dffeA0edlSklrIPTbJ\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e227f3cc5001SNL03vP88B8YIj\"}}\n./bench/gpt-5_5_medium/rep2/agent.log:306:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778695238096,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1dd80c37dffeA0edlSklrIPTbJ\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"grep\",\"callID\":\"call_xekRKUFzxf2KWcgGf6hCwddA\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"pattern\":\"micro_opt|hyp-20260513-001-r8s0|forward|reset|ALU|hazard|m_unit|reg_file|critical|Fmax\",\"path\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_5_medium-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\",\"include\":\"log.jsonl\"},\"output\":\"Found 24 matches\\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_5_medium-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:\\n  Line 1: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"baseline-bench-5327bba\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Baseline retest for cores/bench\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"improvement\\\", \\\"fitness\\\": 282.82, \\\"delta_pct\\\": 0.0, \\\"vs_baseline\\\": 0.0, \\\"fmax_mhz\\\": 127.03, \\\"ipc_coremark\\\": 2e-06, \\\"cycles\\\": 4491485, \\\"iterations\\\": 10, \\\"lut4\\\": 9563, \\\"ff\\\": 1866, \\\"seeds\\\": [128.72, 127.03, 123.02], \\\"formal_passed\\\": true, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": true, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"implementation_notes\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-05-13T13:08:01.185076Z\\\", \\\"round_id\\\": 0, \\\"slot\\\": 0}\\n\\n  Line 2: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r1s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Multicycle RV32M arithmetic unit\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current ALU computes every multiply and divide result combinationally in\\\\nthe EX stage, including signed and unsigned 64-bit products plus divider and\\\\nremainder operators. That wide arithmetic cone is a likely Fmax limiter on\\\\nTang Nano 20K, while CoreMark's hot path should not need single-cycle DIV/REM\\\\nthroughput. Trading rare M-extension latency for a shorter common EX critical\\\\npath should improve iterations/second through higher clock frequency.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current ALU computes every multiply and divide result combinationally in\\\\nthe EX stage, including signed and unsigned 64-bit products plus divider and\\\\nremainder operators. That wide arithmetic cone is a likely Fmax limiter on\\\\nTang Nano 20K, while CoreMark's hot path should not need single-cycle DIV/REM\\\\nthroughput. Trading rare M-extension latency for a shorter common EX critical\\\\npath should improve iterations/second through higher clock frequency.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Split RV32M operations out of the always-combinational ALU path behind a\\\\nsmall multicycle M-unit handshake in EX. Keep ADD/SUB/logic/branch/address\\\\noperations purely combinational, but when the decoded alu_op is MUL/DIV/REM,\\\\nhold ID/EX and EX/MEM until the iterative arithmetic result is ready, then\\\\nretire normally through the existing MEM/WB and RVFI path. This should remove\\\\ndivider and large multiplier logic from the ordinary EX-stage timing path;\\\\nthe added stalls only affect uncommon M instructions and should be outweighed\\\\nby improved Fmax on the FPGA fitness metric.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 6, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/alu.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Restrict the combinational ALU to fast RV32I operations and expose only lightweight M-op selection or helper logic needed by the new M-unit.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a multicycl...\\n  Line 3: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r1s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Resolve control flow in ID\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"CoreMark spends a significant fraction of its bracketed runtime in tight loops and conditional control flow. The current five-stage pipeline resolves branches and jumps in EX, then flushes both IF and ID, so every taken branch or jump pays roughly a two-cycle redirect penalty. Reducing that penalty improves iterations/cycle without increasing retirement width or touching the external NRET=1 contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"CoreMark spends a significant fraction of its bracketed runtime in tight loops and conditional control flow. The current five-stage pipeline resolves branches and jumps in EX, then flushes both IF and ID, so every taken branch or jump pays roughly a two-cycle redirect penalty. Reducing that penalty improves iterations/cycle without increasing retirement width or touching the external NRET=1 contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Move branch and JAL/JALR target/condition resolution from EX into the decode stage, with decode-stage operand forwarding from EX/MEM and MEM/WB for branch compares and JALR base operands. Keep the existing EX-stage trap/metadata path as the retirement source, but let ID drive redirect one cycle earlier when operands are available; stall only for unresolved load-to-branch/JALR dependencies. This should cut the common taken-control penalty from two bubbles to one while preserving RVFI order, trap reporting, and single-channel retirement.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 8, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Wire ID-stage early redirect signals into the IF and hazard path while preserving the existing NRET=1 RVFI port set.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add early branch/JAL/JALR resolution using decoded control, immediates, regfile operands, and forwarded compare/base operands.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/forward_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend forwarding ...\\n  Line 4: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r1s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Static BTFNT branch predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current five-stage core always fetches sequentially and resolves every branch or jump in EX, so each taken loop branch pays a two-instruction front-end flush. CoreMark has tight loops and many backward branches, making a small static predictor likely to improve iterations per cycle without adding much FPGA area or hurting the ALU critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current five-stage core always fetches sequentially and resolves every branch or jump in EX, so each taken loop branch pays a two-instruction front-end flush. CoreMark has tight loops and many backward branches, making a small static predictor likely to improve iterations per cycle without adding much FPGA area or hurting the ALU critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add an IF-stage static backward-taken/forward-not-taken predictor using the fetched instruction bits to compute B-type targets for negative branch immediates, and optionally early-predict JAL targets because they are unconditional PC-relative redirects. Carry the predicted next PC through IF/ID and ID/EX so EX redirects only when the resolved next PC differs from the prediction; mispredicted forward-taken branches, not-taken backward branches, and JALR still recover through the existing flush path. This should remove most loop-closing branch bubbles while preserving the single-retire RVFI contract because retirement remains unchanged at MEM/WB.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 8, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend IF/ID and ID/EX pipeline bundles with the predicted next PC needed for EX-stage mispredict detection.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Implement static BTFNT next-PC selection for backward conditional branches and early PC-relative JAL prediction from fetched instruction bits.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Lat...\\n  Line 5: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r2s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Simplify MEM load lane muxes\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"After the successful M-unit split, the remaining always-active timing paths are more likely to be ordinary RV32I datapath logic than rare multiply/divide arithmetic. The MEM stage currently uses variable shifts for load extraction and shifted masks for byte lanes, which can synthesize into wider mux/barrel-shifter logic on the FPGA load-to-WB path. CoreMark performs many byte, halfword, and word accesses, so a smaller MEM-stage cone should improve Fmax without changing CPI.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"After the successful M-unit split, the remaining always-active timing paths are more likely to be ordinary RV32I datapath logic than rare multiply/divide arithmetic. The MEM stage currently uses variable shifts for load extraction and shifted masks for byte lanes, which can synthesize into wider mux/barrel-shifter logic on the FPGA load-to-WB path. CoreMark performs many byte, halfword, and word accesses, so a smaller MEM-stage cone should improve Fmax without changing CPI.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Rewrite the MEM-stage byte-lane handling as explicit case muxes keyed by addr[1:0] instead of expressions like dmem_rdata >> (addr[1:0] * 8) and mask << addr[1:0]. Keep the same misalignment trap behavior, RVFI aligned mem_addr reporting, replicated store data convention, and MEM/WB retirement boundary. This should reduce LUT depth and routing on the common load-data path while preserving the existing five-stage control flow and single-retire NRET=1 contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace variable load-data shifts and shifted byte masks with explicit addr[1:0] case muxes for LB/LBU/LH/LHU/LW and store masks.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace variable load...\\n  Line 6: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r2s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Add fetch skid buffer\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current front end has no real IF/ID storage: the IF payload is combinational from the current PC and imem data, and an imem-not-ready cycle injects a bubble directly into decode. With the tournament cosim using roughly 22% random instruction stalls, CoreMark loses many cycles even when the back end is already stalled by load-use, dmem, or the multicycle M unit. A small fetch buffer is a structural way to recover iterations/cycle without revisiting the prior broken ID-stage branch-resolution path or adding fetch-path target adders that hurt Fmax.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current front end has no real IF/ID storage: the IF payload is combinational from the current PC and imem data, and an imem-not-ready cycle injects a bubble directly into decode. With the tournament cosim using roughly 22% random instruction stalls, CoreMark loses many cycles even when the back end is already stalled by load-use, dmem, or the multicycle M unit. A small fetch buffer is a structural way to recover iterations/cycle without revisiting the prior broken ID-stage branch-resolution path or adding fetch-path target adders that hurt Fmax.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Insert a one- or two-entry instruction skid buffer between IF and ID. The PC/imem side should continue fetching sequential instructions whenever the buffer has space, while the ID side consumes one buffered instruction only when ID/EX is ready. On redirect, flush the buffer and restart fetch at the EX-resolved target; on imem stalls, hold the outstanding fetch instead of emitting a synthetic NOP into decode. This should overlap random iStall cycles with existing back-end stalls and reduce front-end bubbles while preserving single-retire RVFI ordering and the existing EX-stage control-flow correctness.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 7, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"descr...\\n  Line 7: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r2s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Tiny return-address stack predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The prior static BTFNT predictor targeted loop branches but put branch immediate decode\\\\nand target adders directly on the IF PC-update path, regressing the CoreMark fitness\\\\ndespite saving some bubbles. CoreMark still has frequent function calls and returns,\\\\nand the current core pays the same EX-stage redirect penalty for every JALR return.\\\\nA tiny return predictor can attack those bubbles without adding branch-target arithmetic\\\\nto fetch.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The prior static BTFNT predictor targeted loop branches but put branch immediate decode\\\\nand target adders directly on the IF PC-update path, regressing the CoreMark fitness\\\\ndespite saving some bubbles. CoreMark still has frequent function calls and returns,\\\\nand the current core pays the same EX-stage redirect penalty for every JALR return.\\\\nA tiny return predictor can attack those bubbles without adding branch-target arithmetic\\\\nto fetch.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a small 4-entry return-address stack that predicts only canonical returns\\\\n(JALR x0, 0(x1/x5)) in IF. Push pc+4 for link-register calls when they resolve,\\\\npop/predict the stack top for returns, and carry the predicted next PC through the\\\\npipeline so EX redirects only on mismatch. This keeps ordinary branch fetch as pc+4,\\\\navoids B-type immediate decode and target adders in the IF critical path, and should\\\\nrecover part of the call/return control-flow penalty with modest LUT/FF cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add predicted-next-PC and return-predicted metadata to IF/ID, ID/EX, and EX/MEM bundles for mispredict detection and RAS bookkeeping.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Detect canonical return encodings in the fetched instruction and select the RAS top as the next PC only when the stack is nonempty....\\n  Line 8: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r3s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Source-aware load-use stalls\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current load-use hazard detector compares a load destination against the\\\\nraw rs1 and rs2 bitfields of the following instruction, regardless of whether\\\\nthat instruction actually consumes those operands. For I-type immediates,\\\\nloads, JALR, LUI, AUIPC, JAL, FENCE, and EBREAK, some of those fields are\\\\nimmediates or destination bits rather than true sources. Any accidental match\\\\nwith the preceding load rd inserts an unnecessary bubble, directly reducing\\\\nCoreMark iterations per cycle without improving correctness.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current load-use hazard detector compares a load destination against the\\\\nraw rs1 and rs2 bitfields of the following instruction, regardless of whether\\\\nthat instruction actually consumes those operands. For I-type immediates,\\\\nloads, JALR, LUI, AUIPC, JAL, FENCE, and EBREAK, some of those fields are\\\\nimmediates or destination bits rather than true sources. Any accidental match\\\\nwith the preceding load rd inserts an unnecessary bubble, directly reducing\\\\nCoreMark iterations per cycle without improving correctness.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Make the load-use interlock source-aware by qualifying the rd comparisons with\\\\nsimple decoded uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 signals for the instruction currently in\\\\nIF/ID. Keep the existing one-cycle stall for real load consumers, but avoid\\\\nstalling on unused source fields such as OP-IMM rs2 bits, U/J-type operand\\\\nfields, and SYSTEM/FENCE encodings. This should improve CPI under CoreMark's\\\\nload-heavy code while adding only small opcode decode logic off the main ALU\\\\nand memory datapaths, so Fmax should be largely preserved.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Derive or wire IF/ID source-use qualifiers into the hazard unit without changing the NRET=1 top-level RVFI interface.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl...\\n  Line 9: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r3s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Load-use MEM bypass\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The previous structural attempts spent LUTs and timing on front-end control-flow machinery, and the log shows those changes either broke correctness or regressed fitness. The current five-stage core still has the classic one-cycle load-use interlock even though the benchmark memory is zero-wait in FPGA and only randomly stalled in cosim. CoreMark has frequent dependent loads through pointer-heavy list, matrix, and state code, so removing this bubble can improve iterations per cycle without adding predictor target adders to the fetch critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The previous structural attempts spent LUTs and timing on front-end control-flow machinery, and the log shows those changes either broke correctness or regressed fitness. The current five-stage core still has the classic one-cycle load-use interlock even though the benchmark memory is zero-wait in FPGA and only randomly stalled in cosim. CoreMark has frequent dependent loads through pointer-heavy list, matrix, and state code, so removing this bubble can improve iterations per cycle without adding predictor target adders to the fetch critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a MEM-to-EX load-data bypass path that lets an instruction immediately behind a load proceed into EX when the load is in MEM and dmem is ready. Compute the existing load extraction result combinationally in MEM, expose it to the forwarding network, and add a forwarding select for EX operands that chooses the current MEM load result over the older EX/MEM ALU address. Keep the load-use stall only when the load will not have valid data that cycle, such as a dmem stall or a misaligned/trapping load. This structural bypass should remove many single-cycle bubbles while preserving the NRET=1 retirement boundary and RVFI order at MEM/WB.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 6, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\"...\\n  Line 10: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r3s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Decode-stage JAL predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The prior predictor attempts regressed because they placed branch target decode,\\\\ntarget adders, return detection, and prediction muxes directly on the IF PC path.\\\\nCoreMark still pays the existing EX redirect penalty for every unconditional JAL,\\\\nand JAL targets are PC-relative with no register dependency or branch condition.\\\\nPredicting only JAL in decode should recover one control-flow bubble for calls and\\\\nlong jumps while avoiding the risky fetch-path predictor logic that hurt Fmax.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The prior predictor attempts regressed because they placed branch target decode,\\\\ntarget adders, return detection, and prediction muxes directly on the IF PC path.\\\\nCoreMark still pays the existing EX redirect penalty for every unconditional JAL,\\\\nand JAL targets are PC-relative with no register dependency or branch condition.\\\\nPredicting only JAL in decode should recover one control-flow bubble for calls and\\\\nlong jumps while avoiding the risky fetch-path predictor logic that hurt Fmax.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a narrow decode-stage always-taken predictor for legal JAL instructions only.\\\\nID computes pc+imm for JAL, redirects fetch one cycle before EX would, and records\\\\nthe predicted next PC in the ID/EX bundle. EX then suppresses the normal JAL redirect\\\\nwhen the resolved jump target matches the predicted target, but retains the existing\\\\nEX recovery path for conditional branches, JALR, illegal instructions, and any target\\\\nmismatch. Because the predictor excludes branches and returns, it needs no B-type\\\\nimmediate decode in IF, no RAS state, and no operand forwarding, making it a smaller\\\\npredictor angle with a better chance of improving CoreMark iterations/cycle without\\\\nsacrificing the current high Fmax.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add p...\\n  Line 11: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r4s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Source-qualified load interlock\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current load-use detector compares the preceding load rd against both raw\\\\nsource bitfields of the following instruction, even when those bits are not\\\\narchitecturally consumed. CoreMark has many loads and immediate/control\\\\ninstructions, so accidental rd matches can inject bubbles that reduce\\\\niterations per cycle without protecting any real dependency.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current load-use detector compares the preceding load rd against both raw\\\\nsource bitfields of the following instruction, even when those bits are not\\\\narchitecturally consumed. CoreMark has many loads and immediate/control\\\\ninstructions, so accidental rd matches can inject bubbles that reduce\\\\niterations per cycle without protecting any real dependency.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a tiny source-use decode for the IF/ID instruction and qualify the existing\\\\nload-use interlock with uses_rs1 and uses_rs2. This is a narrower retry of the\\\\nearlier source-aware load-use idea: do not mask or reinterpret RVFI operand\\\\nreporting, and do not add MEM bypass selects or fetch prediction paths. The\\\\ncore should keep the one-cycle stall for true load consumers while avoiding\\\\nfalse stalls on OP-IMM rs2 fields, U/J-type immediates, FENCE, SYSTEM/EBREAK,\\\\nand other instructions that do not read both source registers.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Derive simple IF/ID uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 qualifiers from opcode/funct class and pass them only to the hazard unit.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Gate load-use rd comparisons with the source-use qualifiers while preserving existing dmem, imem, redirect, and M-unit stall priority.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Derive simple IF/ID use...\\n  Line 12: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r4s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Register IF/ID fetch boundary\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current front end feeds imem_data directly through decode, immediate\\\\ngeneration, regfile address decode, hazard checks, and into the ID/EX\\\\nregister in a single cycle. Prior structural attempts tried to hide stalls or\\\\nreduce bubbles with added prediction and bypass control, but those changes\\\\nregressed by lengthening already sensitive mux/control paths. A real IF/ID\\\\nregister is a simpler structural retiming that targets Fmax directly.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current front end feeds imem_data directly through decode, immediate\\\\ngeneration, regfile address decode, hazard checks, and into the ID/EX\\\\nregister in a single cycle. Prior structural attempts tried to hide stalls or\\\\nreduce bubbles with added prediction and bypass control, but those changes\\\\nregressed by lengthening already sensitive mux/control paths. A real IF/ID\\\\nregister is a simpler structural retiming that targets Fmax directly.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Insert a proper IF/ID pipeline register inside the fetch stage, so fetched\\\\ninstruction, PC, and valid are captured before decode sees them. The PC still\\\\nadvances only when the downstream ID stage can accept the entry, and redirect\\\\nflushes the registered IF/ID payload before wrong-path state can retire. This\\\\nadds one front-end cycle and may slightly increase taken-control penalty, but\\\\nit removes the imem-to-decoder combinational path and should improve FPGA\\\\nFmax enough to raise CoreMark iter/sec without changing the NRET=1 retirement\\\\ncontract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Keep the existing IF/ID bundle shape, but document and use it as a registered pipeline boundary rather than a combinational fetch payload.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add an IF/ID register for pc, instruction, and valid; hold it on deco...\\n  Line 13: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r4s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Tiny resolved-target BTB\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current core resolves all taken branches and jumps in EX, so tight CoreMark loops pay a two-bubble redirect penalty. Prior predictor attempts regressed because they decoded immediates, computed targets, or managed return-stack state on the front-end critical path. A small BTB trained from already-resolved EX redirects can target the same loop bubbles while avoiding fetch-stage target arithmetic.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current core resolves all taken branches and jumps in EX, so tight CoreMark loops pay a two-bubble redirect penalty. Prior predictor attempts regressed because they decoded immediates, computed targets, or managed return-stack state on the front-end critical path. A small BTB trained from already-resolved EX redirects can target the same loop bubbles while avoiding fetch-stage target arithmetic.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a tiny direct-mapped branch target buffer that stores resolved taken conditional-branch targets, preferably only backward loop branches, along with a compact PC tag and a 2-bit confidence counter. IF predicts to the stored target on a tag hit with a taken counter, otherwise it keeps the existing pc+4 path; EX updates the BTB after branch resolution and flushes on any predicted/resolved next-PC mismatch. Because the target is stored from the prior execution, the fetch path adds only a small tag compare and next-PC mux instead of B/J immediate decode and target adders, which should recover loop-closing branch bubbles with less Fmax damage than the previous predictor attempts.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Carry predicted next-PC metadata through IF/ID and ID/EX so EX can distinguish correct BTB predictions from mispredictions without changing the NRET=1 RVFI interface.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Imple...\\n  Line 14: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r5s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Drop regfile reset fanout\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current register file resets all 32 architectural registers with a for-loop,\\\\nwhich forces reset muxing or reset routing onto the whole small RF array. After\\\\nthe M-unit split, remaining FPGA Fmax is likely limited by ordinary datapath\\\\nand control fanout rather than rare arithmetic. CoreMark initializes the state\\\\nit uses, x0 is already hardwired on reads, and RVFI correctness depends on\\\\nretired architectural behavior rather than power-on values of unused registers.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current register file resets all 32 architectural registers with a for-loop,\\\\nwhich forces reset muxing or reset routing onto the whole small RF array. After\\\\nthe M-unit split, remaining FPGA Fmax is likely limited by ordinary datapath\\\\nand control fanout rather than rare arithmetic. CoreMark initializes the state\\\\nit uses, x0 is already hardwired on reads, and RVFI correctness depends on\\\\nretired architectural behavior rather than power-on values of unused registers.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Remove the explicit reset clear from the integer register file and keep only\\\\nthe synchronous write path for nonzero destination registers. Reads of x0 stay\\\\nforced to zero, same-cycle write-first bypass stays unchanged, and reset of the\\\\npipeline control/valid bits still prevents spurious retirement after reset.\\\\nThis should reduce reset fanout and per-register muxing in a timing-sensitive,\\\\nalways-active block without changing CPI, branch behavior, memory behavior, or\\\\nthe NRET=1 RVFI interface. The change is deliberately narrower than prior\\\\nfailed micro-opts: it does not touch hazard detection, RVFI operand reporting,\\\\nMEM load extraction, or any front-end prediction path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/reg_file.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove the reset-time loop that clears all 32 registers while preser...\\n  Line 15: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r5s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Ordered one-entry store buffer\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent structural attempts spent timing and LUTs on the front end or on load\\\\nbypass paths, and those changes either regressed fitness or broke stall\\\\nhandling. The current core still freezes the entire pipeline whenever a store\\\\nreaches MEM and dmem_ready is low, even though independent ALU, branch, and\\\\nregister-only instructions behind that store could execute while the store is\\\\nwaiting. CoreMark has frequent data stores and the apples-to-apples cosim\\\\ninjects random dmem stalls, so store backpressure is a promising structural\\\\ntarget that avoids fetch-path target adders and load-result critical muxes.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent structural attempts spent timing and LUTs on the front end or on load\\\\nbypass paths, and those changes either regressed fitness or broke stall\\\\nhandling. The current core still freezes the entire pipeline whenever a store\\\\nreaches MEM and dmem_ready is low, even though independent ALU, branch, and\\\\nregister-only instructions behind that store could execute while the store is\\\\nwaiting. CoreMark has frequent data stores and the apples-to-apples cosim\\\\ninjects random dmem stalls, so store backpressure is a promising structural\\\\ntarget that avoids fetch-path target adders and load-result critical muxes.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a single-entry ordered store buffer at the MEM boundary. A regular data\\\\nstore can retire into the buffer with its address, byte mask, write data, and\\\\nRVFI metadata captured, allowing the front of the pipeline to keep executing\\\\nnon-memory instructions while the buffer drives dmem until dmem_ready accepts\\\\nthe write. Preserve precise in-order behavior by stalling any younger load,\\\\nstore, MMIO access, FENCE, EBREAK, or illegal/trapping instruction until the\\\\nbuffer drains; MMIO marker/UART stores should bypass the buffer and keep the\\\\nexisting blocking behavior so CoreMark timing brackets re...\\n  Line 16: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-003-r5s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Single-entry loop target predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current core fetches sequentially and pays the existing EX-stage redirect\\\\npenalty on every taken loop-closing branch. Earlier predictor attempts\\\\nregressed because they placed immediate decode, target adders, BTB arrays,\\\\ncounters, or RAS control on the fetch path. A predictor for this slot should\\\\ntherefore be much narrower: reuse targets already resolved in EX and add the\\\\nsmallest possible fetch-side structure.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current core fetches sequentially and pays the existing EX-stage redirect\\\\npenalty on every taken loop-closing branch. Earlier predictor attempts\\\\nregressed because they placed immediate decode, target adders, BTB arrays,\\\\ncounters, or RAS control on the fetch path. A predictor for this slot should\\\\ntherefore be much narrower: reuse targets already resolved in EX and add the\\\\nsmallest possible fetch-side structure.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a single-entry resolved-loop target predictor trained only by taken\\\\nbackward conditional branches. The entry stores one branch PC and its resolved\\\\ntarget; IF predicts taken only when the current PC equals that stored PC,\\\\notherwise it keeps the existing pc+4 behavior. Carry predicted-taken and\\\\npredicted-next-PC metadata to EX so the normal redirect path fires only on a\\\\nmismatch, and invalidate the entry when that same branch resolves not-taken\\\\nat loop exit. This should recover repeated inner-loop branch bubbles in\\\\nCoreMark while avoiding the fetch target adders, multi-entry tag arrays,\\\\nconfidence counters, and return-stack machinery that hurt prior predictors.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add minimal predicted-taken and predicted-next-PC metadata to IF/ID and ID/EX bundles for EX-stage mismatch detection.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Implement ...\\n  Line 17: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r6s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Prune dead EX/MEM branch metadata\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core has already won most of its fitness from removing broad\\\\ntiming/fanout costs, while recent attempts that add prediction, buffering,\\\\nbypassing, or memory-ordering control have regressed. The EX/MEM bundle still\\\\ncarries branch_taken and branch_target fields that are assigned every cycle\\\\nbut are not consumed by MEM, WB, RVFI, hazard, or forwarding logic. Those 33\\\\ndead flops and their input adders/mux routes sit in an always-active pipeline\\\\nboundary, so pruning them is a low-risk micro-optimization for LUT/FF count\\\\nand routing delay without changing CPI.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core has already won most of its fitness from removing broad\\\\ntiming/fanout costs, while recent attempts that add prediction, buffering,\\\\nbypassing, or memory-ordering control have regressed. The EX/MEM bundle still\\\\ncarries branch_taken and branch_target fields that are assigned every cycle\\\\nbut are not consumed by MEM, WB, RVFI, hazard, or forwarding logic. Those 33\\\\ndead flops and their input adders/mux routes sit in an always-active pipeline\\\\nboundary, so pruning them is a low-risk micro-optimization for LUT/FF count\\\\nand routing delay without changing CPI.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Remove the unused branch_taken and branch_target fields from ex_mem_t and stop\\\\nregistering them in ex_stage. Keep redirect and pc_next computation exactly as\\\\nthey are today, because pc_next is the RVFI-visible resolved next PC and the\\\\nredirect output is still the only live control-flow signal. This should trim\\\\ndead sequential state and reduce EX-stage fanout/routing pressure while\\\\npreserving the NRET=1 RVFI contract, trap behavior, memory behavior, and all\\\\nexisting stall/flush priorities.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 1, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove unused branch_taken and...\\n  Line 18: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-002-r6s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Pipeline EX operand retiming\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent structural attempts that added fetch prediction, skid buffering, load bypassing,\\\\nor store buffering all paid too much control-path cost. The current core's remaining\\\\nhigh-fanout structural path is more basic: ID/EX values pass through forwarding muxes,\\\\nALU operand selection, ALU or branch compare/target logic, and into EX/MEM plus the\\\\nredirect path in one cycle. Retiming that boundary can target Fmax without adding a\\\\npredictor, memory ordering structure, or RVFI contract change.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Recent structural attempts that added fetch prediction, skid buffering, load bypassing,\\\\nor store buffering all paid too much control-path cost. The current core's remaining\\\\nhigh-fanout structural path is more basic: ID/EX values pass through forwarding muxes,\\\\nALU operand selection, ALU or branch compare/target logic, and into EX/MEM plus the\\\\nredirect path in one cycle. Retiming that boundary can target Fmax without adding a\\\\npredictor, memory ordering structure, or RVFI contract change.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Split the existing EX stage into a lightweight EX1 operand/metadata register and an EX2\\\\nexecute/redirect stage. EX1 captures the forwarded rs1/rs2 values, selected ALU operands,\\\\ndecoded control, immediate, PC, and RVFI source data; EX2 performs the ALU operation,\\\\nbranch/jump resolution, M-unit handshake, and drives the existing EX/MEM payload. Add the\\\\nminimal hazard/forwarding adjustments needed for the extra execute stage, preferring a\\\\none-cycle interlock on immediately dependent ALU consumers over wide new bypass muxes if\\\\nthat keeps timing short. This structural retiming should shorten the common forwarding-to-\\\\nALU-to-EX/MEM cone and may raise Tang Nano 20K Fmax enough to offset a small CPI cost,\\\\nwhile preserving NRET=1 retirement at MEM/WB and all RVFI ordering/trap behavior.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\"...\\n  Line 20: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260513-001-r7s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Iterative M divider\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The largest proven fitness gain for this core came from moving RV32M arithmetic\\\\nout of the ordinary EX ALU path, but the current m_unit still contains wide\\\\ncombinational DIV/REM operators behind a one-cycle result register. Even if\\\\nthose operations are rare in CoreMark, the FPGA still has to place and time\\\\nthe divider/remainder cone, so it can cap Fmax or perturb routing for the hot\\\\nRV32I \",\"id\":\"tool_write_2sFRKuuHMfwbIhCHvM5O\",\"format\":\"google-gemini-v1\",\"index\":0}]}},\"id\":\"prt_e261c5932001f6qWjP70pXbFcx\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d9e6089affeJtK3COrYIc8epr\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e2619f79f001fo2AXlfVLjI61k\"}}\n./bench/gemini-3_1-pro/rep1/agent.log:193:{\"type\":\"text\",\"timestamp\":1778755861774,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d9e6089affeJtK3COrYIc8epr\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_e261c6d82001SrqFGo1YASndkH\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e261c59a20012BGtLr3WXYEmNq\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d9e6089affeJtK3COrYIc8epr\",\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"I have analyzed the current microarchitecture and identified three distinct but highly synergistic optimizations that tackle both the critical path (Fmax) and pipeline stalls (IPC). I have bundled them into a single `micro_opt` hypothesis and written the YAML to the specified path.\\n\\n### The Optimizations\\n1. **ALU Multiplier Consolidation**: The current `alu.sv` unconditionally evaluates three 64x64 multipliers (`mul_ss`, `mul_uu`, `mul_su`) before the `case` statement. While Yosys removes these under `ALTOPS`, the standard Verilator/FPGA builds instantiate massive multipliers. By replacing them with a single 33x33 signed multiplier preceded by an operand mux, we drastically reduce DSP/LUT utilization, easing routing congestion and boosting Fmax.\\n2. **EX Stage Target Adder Reuse**: The `ex_stage.sv` currently uses separate adders for `branch_target` (`in.pc + in.imm`) and `jalr_sum` (`rs1 + in.imm`). They can be cleanly merged into one `target_sum = (is_jalr ? rs1 : in.pc) + in.imm`, saving a full 32-bit adder and further reducing area.\\n3. **Precise Load-Use Hazards**: The `hazard_unit` conservatively stalls if `id_ex_rd` matches `instr[19:15]` or `instr[24:20]`, regardless of whether the incoming instruction actually reads those registers. Instructions like `LUI`, `AUIPC`, and `JAL` trigger false load-use stalls because their immediate fields randomly overlap with active registers. Passing the opcode to the hazard unit and decoding actual `rs1_en`/`rs2_en` will surgically remove these false stalls, improving CoreMark IPC.\\n\\nThe hypothesis is now written to `cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260514-001-r2s0.yaml` and is ready for the orchestrator implementation phase!\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778755857794,\"end\":1778755861773}}}\n./bench/gemini-3_1-pro/rep1/agent.log:201:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778757218877,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d9cfa9a2ffeMT2uPdw9AaYBmZ\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"bash\",\"callID\":\"tool_bash_Wr8aaVgUe7iFxankdGo5\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"command\":\"cat cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | grep hyp-20260514-001-r2s0\",\"description\":\"Check log for the failed hypothesis\"},\"output\":\"{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-001-r2s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"ALU multiplier consolidation, EX adder reuse, and precise load-use hazard\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The ALU currently infers three 64x64 multipliers, wasting LUTs. The EX stage duplicates 32-bit target adders. The hazard unit conservatively stalls on all opcodes, reducing IPC.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The ALU currently infers three 64x64 multipliers, wasting LUTs. The EX stage duplicates 32-bit target adders. The hazard unit conservatively stalls on all opcodes, reducing IPC.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Consolidating ALU multipliers to a single 33x33 signed block and EX adders into one will improve Fmax by reducing LUT utilization and routing congestion. Passing the IF/ID opcode to the hazard unit to stall only when rs1/rs2 are actually read will increase iterations/cycle.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 10, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"high\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/alu.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Refactor mul_ss, mul_uu, mul_su into a single 33x33 signed multiplier driven by an input mux based on ALU op.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace branch_target and jalr_sum adders with a single target_base + in.imm adder.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Decode opcode from if_id_instr to qualify load_use_hazard with rs1_en and rs2_en.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Update hazard_unit instantiation to pass if_id_w.instr instead of separated rs1/rs2.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 10, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"high\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/alu.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Refactor mul_ss, mul_uu, mul_su into a single 33x33 signed multiplier driven by an input mux based on ALU op.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace branch_target and jalr_sum adders with a single target_base + in.imm adder.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Decode opcode from if_id_instr to qualify load_use_hazard with rs1_en and rs2_en.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Update hazard_unit instantiation to pass if_id_w.instr instead of separated rs1/rs2.\\\"}], \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"broken\\\", \\\"formal_passed\\\": false, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": false, \\\"error\\\": \\\"cosim_failed: selftest.elf\\\", \\\"slot\\\": 0, \\\"round_id\\\": 2, \\\"scribe_skipped\\\": \\\"TimeoutError: scribe timed out after 240s\\\"}\\n\",\"metadata\":{\"output\":\"{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-001-r2s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"ALU multiplier consolidation, EX adder reuse, and precise load-use hazard\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The ALU currently infers three 64x64 multipliers, wasting LUTs. The EX stage duplicates 32-bit target adders. The hazard unit conservatively stalls on all opcodes, reducing IPC.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The ALU currently infers three 64x64 multipliers, wasting LUTs. The EX stage duplicates 32-bit target adders. The hazard unit conservatively stalls on all opcodes, reducing IPC.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Consolidating ALU multipliers to a single 33x33 signed block and EX adders into one will improve Fmax by reducing LUT utilization and routing congestion. Passing the IF/ID opcode to the hazard unit to stall only when rs1/rs2 are actually read will increase iterations/cycle.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 10, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"high\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/alu.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Refactor mul_ss, mul_uu, mul_su into a single 33x33 signed multiplier driven by an input mux based on ALU op.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace branch_target and jalr_sum adders with a single target_base + in.imm adder.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Decode opcode from if_id_instr to qualify load_use_hazard with rs1_en and rs2_en.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Update hazard_unit instantiation to pass if_id_w.instr instead of separated rs1/rs2.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 10, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"high\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/alu.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Refactor mul_ss, mul_uu, mul_su into a single 33x33 signed multiplier driven by an input mux based on ALU op.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace branch_target and jalr_sum adders with a single target_base + in.imm adder.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Decode opcode from if_id_instr to qualify load_use_hazard with rs1_en and rs2_en.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Update hazard_unit instantiation to pass if_id_w.instr instead of separated rs1/rs2.\\\"}], \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"broken\\\", \\\"formal_passed\\\": false, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": false, \\\"error\\\": \\\"cosim_failed: selftest.elf\\\", \\\"slot\\\": 0, \\\"round_id\\\": 2, \\\"scribe_skipped\\\": \\\"TimeoutError: scribe timed out after 240s\\\"}\\n\",\"exit\":0,\"description\":\"Check log for the failed hypothesis\",\"truncated\":false},\"title\":\"Check log for the failed hypothesis\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778757218873,\"end\":1778757218876}},\"metadata\":{\"openrouter\":{\"reasoning_details\":[{\"type\":\"reasoning.text\",\"text\":\"**Refining Load-Use Hazard**\\n\\nI'm currently re-examining the precise load-use hazard, suspecting it might be the culprit behind the cosim failure. The multiplier consolidation was also a possibility, but the load-use logic feels more likely to be the source of error given the selftest.elf result.\\n\\n**Detecting Spurious Stalls**\\n\\nI've identified a spurious load-use stall where the hazard unit incorrectly stalls on instructions that don't actually use source registers. This occurs because it blindly checks instruction fields rather than their actual usage.\\n\\n**Investigating Stall Logic**\\n\\nI'm now focusing on a spurious load-use stall. The hazard unit incorrectly stalls for instructions that don't actually read source registers, as it checks instruction fields blindly. This is likely due to U-type and J-type instructions, and some I-types, not using `rs1` or `rs2`.\\n\\n**Fixing Hazard Unit**\\n\\nI've pinpointed a spurious load-use hazard stall. The unit was incorrectly stalling on instructions that don't actually read source registers, simply checking instruction fields. This fix addresses that by more accurately identifying true dependencies.\\n\\n**Proposing Multiplier Consolidation**\\n\\nI'm now focused on a different optimization: consolidating the three 64-bit multipliers into a single 33x33 signed multiplier to improve Fmax and resource utilization. This is a significant change that could resolve performance bottlenecks.\\n\\n**Optimizing Multiplier and Adder Usage**\\n\\nI'm proposing two micro-optimizations: consolidating the three 64-bit multipliers into a single 33x33 signed multiplier to reduce resource usage and potentially improve Fmax, and sharing an adder in the EX stage for branch and jump target calculations to cut down on logic.\\n\\n**Proposing Multiplier Consolidation**\\n\\nI'm now focused on optimizing the ALU's multiplier implementation. The current design infers three separate 64-bit multipliers, which is inefficient for the Tang Nano 20K. I propose consolidating these into a single, more resource-friendly 33x33 signed multiplier. This should significantly improve Fmax and reduce LUT/DSP usage.\\n\\n**Refining Hazard Detection**\\n\\nI am now focusing on improving the accuracy of load-use hazard detection. Previously, I considered the possibility of spurious stalls and am now implementing a fix to prevent unnecessary stalls on instructions that do not actually read source registers.\\n\\n**Fixing Spurious Stalls**\\n\\nI'm now refining load-use hazard detection. Previously, I identified that the hazard unit unnecessarily stalls when subsequent instructions, like U-type or J-type, don't actually read source registers. I'm implementing a fix to accurately identify true dependencies, preventing these spurious stalls.\\n\\n**Fixing Load-Use Hazards**\\n\\nI'm now refining the load-use hazard detection. Previously, I noted that the hazard unit incorrectly stalls on instructions that don't actually read source registers. I'm implementing a fix to accurately identify true dependencies by passing the instruction itself to the hazard unit, allowing it to determine if `rs1` or `rs2` are actually used.\\n\\n**Refining Hazard Logic**\\n\\nI'm now refining the load-use hazard detection logic. My analysis reveals that the hazard unit was incorrectly stalling the pipeline for instructions that don't actually utilize source registers, such as U-type and J-type instructions. This was due to a simplistic check of instruction fields rather than their actual usage. I am implementing a fix to accurately identify true dependencies, preventing these spurious stalls and improving pipeline efficiency.\\n\\n**Optimizing Multiplier Logic**\\n\\nI'm now focusing on enhancing the ALU's multiplier implementation. The current design infers three separate 64-bit multipliers, which is highly inefficient for the Tang Nano 20K. I propose consolidating these into a single, resource-friendly 33x33 signed multiplier. This should significantly improve Fmax and reduce LUT/DSP usage.\\n\\n**Refining Hazard Logic**\\n\\nI'm now refining the load-use hazard detection logic. My analysis reveals that the hazard unit was incorrectly stalling the pipeline for instructions that don't actually utilize source registers, such as U-type and J-type instructions. This was due to a simplistic check of instruction fields rather than their actual usage. I am implementing a fix to accurately identify true dependencies, preventing these spurious stalls and improving pipeline efficiency.\\n\\n**Implementing Hazard Unit Refinements**\\n\\nI'm now refining the load-use hazard logic within the hazard unit. My analysis revealed that the unit was incorrectly stalling the pipeline for instructions that don't actually utilize source registers, such as U-type and J-type instructions. This was due to a simplistic check of instruction fields rather than their actual usage. I am implementing a fix to accurately identify true dependencies, preventing these spurious stalls and improving pipeline efficiency.\\n\\n**Improving Hazard Detection**\\n\\nI am refining the load-use hazard logic. My analysis revealed the hazard unit was incorrectly stalling for instructions that don't actually utilize source registers, like U-type and J-type instructions, due to a simplistic check of instruction fields. I'm implementing a fix to accurately identify true dependencies, preventing these spurious stalls.\\n\\n\",\"format\":\"google-gemini-v1\",\"index\":0},{\"type\":\"reasoning.encrypted\",\"data\":\"\",\"id\":\"tool_bash_Wr8aaVgUe7iFxankdGo5\",\"format\":\"google-gemini-v1\",\"index\":0}]}},\"id\":\"prt_e263132220014TKuTg6np1Kt2i\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d9cfa9a2ffeMT2uPdw9AaYBmZ\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e26307876001qOQJMPmHN56PZD\"}}\n./bench/gemini-3_1-pro/rep1/agent.log:204:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778757244277,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d9cfa9a2ffeMT2uPdw9AaYBmZ\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"write\",\"callID\":\"tool_write_jTioZUP48fW4mXg8ZPKR\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"content\":\"id: hyp-20260514-001-r3s0\\ntitle: \\\"Precise Load-Use Hazard & 33x33 Multiplier Consolidation\\\"\\ncategory: micro_opt\\nmotivation: |\\n  The hazard unit currently stalls blindly on all instructions if their arbitrary rs1/rs2 fields match a load destination, reducing IPC. Additionally, the ALU infers three separate 64x64 multipliers, wasting LUTs and DSPs, which creates routing congestion and lowers Fmax. Consolidating to a single 33x33 multiplier and decoding rs1_read/rs2_read will improve both iter/cycle and Fmax.\\nhypothesis: |\\n  By partially decoding the opcode in the hazard unit to check if rs1 and rs2 are actually read, we eliminate spurious load-use stalls (e.g. for LUI/JAL/OP-IMM). By replacing `mul_ss`, `mul_uu`, and `mul_su` in the ALU with a single signed 33x33 multiplier, we drastically reduce area and critical path delay.\\nexpected_impact:\\n  fitness_delta_pct: 7\\n  confidence: medium\\nchanges:\\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\n    description: \\\"Add an input `if_id_instr` [31:0] (replacing `if_id_rs1` and `if_id_rs2`). Decode its opcode to generate `rs1_en` and `rs2_en` (e.g. LUI/AUIPC/JAL clear both, OP-IMM/LOAD/JALR/SYSTEM clear rs2_en). Qualify the load-use hazard check with these enable bits.\\\"\\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\\n    description: \\\"Update the `hazard_unit` instantiation: remove `.if_id_rs1` and `.if_id_rs2`, and connect `.if_id_instr (if_id_w.instr)` instead.\\\"\\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\\n    description: \\\"Replace `mul_ss`, `mul_uu`, `mul_su` with a single `logic signed [65:0] mul_res`. Drive it via `logic signed [32:0] mul_a` and `mul_b`. Set `a_is_signed = (op == ALU_MULH || op == ALU_MULHSU)` and `b_is_signed = (op == ALU_MULH)`. Assign `mul_a = $signed({a_is_signed & a[31], a})` and same for `mul_b`. Update the `ALU_MUL*` output cases to extract from `mul_res`. Make sure to keep the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS branch identical.\\\"\\n\",\"filePath\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gemini-3_1-pro-rep1/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260514-001-r3s0.yaml\"},\"output\":\"Wrote file successfully.\",\"metadata\":{\"diagnostics\":{},\"filepath\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gemini-3_1-pro-rep1/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260514-001-r3s0.yaml\",\"exists\":false,\"truncated\":false},\"title\":\"cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260514-001-r3s0.yaml\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778757244265,\"end\":1778757244276}},\"metadata\":{\"openrouter\":{\"reasoning_details\":[{\"type\":\"reasoning.text\",\"text\":\"**Exploring Hazard and Sharing**\\n\\nI'm now focusing on a precise load-use hazard and target adder sharing, deliberately bypassing the multiplier to refine my understanding. This focused approach aims to isolate potential bottlenecks.\\n\\n**Refining Instruction Decoding**\\n\\nI'm now zeroing in on precisely identifying which instructions don't access `rs1` and `rs2`, a critical step for optimizing hazard detection and load-use stalls. This detailed analysis is key to unlocking performance gains.\\n\\n**Refining Instruction Decoding**\\n\\nMy current focus is on precisely identifying instructions that do not access `rs1` and `rs2`. This detailed opcode analysis, implemented via a SystemVerilog `case` statement, directly targets the refinement of load-use hazard detection and potential load-use stalls.\\n\\n**Optimizing Multiplier Logic**\\n\\nI am now exploring significant Fmax optimizations by simplifying the multiplier logic. The current approach instantiates three 64x64 multipliers; I aim to replace this with a more streamlined implementation that handles signed and unsigned multiplication more efficiently, reducing critical path and area overhead.\\n\\n**Refining Instruction Checks**\\n\\nI'm now meticulously identifying instructions that bypass `rs1` and `rs2` reads using a SystemVerilog `case` statement. This precise opcode analysis directly targets the refinement of load-use hazard detection and the reduction of potential load-use stalls, which should significantly boost IPC.\\n\\n**Refining Instruction Decoding**\\n\\nI am now precisely identifying instructions that bypass `rs1` and `rs2` reads via a SystemVerilog `case` statement. This detailed opcode analysis directly targets the refinement of load-use hazard detection and the reduction of potential load-use stalls.\\n\\n**Optimizing Multiplier Logic**\\n\\nI'm exploring Fmax optimizations by streamlining the multiplier logic. Currently, three 64x64 multipliers are instantiated; I aim to replace this with a more efficient implementation for signed and unsigned multiplication.\\n\\n**Sharing Target Adder**\\n\\nI'm investigating sharing an adder for PC-relative immediate calculations for branches and jumps, plus PC+immediate for JALR, to reduce critical path and area. The current implementation calculates `pc + imm` twice, which can be optimized.\\n\\n**Implementing Hazard and Sharing**\\n\\nI'm now focusing on precisely identifying instructions that bypass `rs1` and `rs2` reads to refine load-use hazard detection and reduce stalls. Additionally, I'm investigating sharing an adder for PC-relative immediate calculations for branches and jumps, which will reduce critical path and area.\\n\\n\",\"format\":\"google-gemini-v1\",\"index\":0},{\"type\":\"reasoning.encrypted\",\"data\":\"\",\"id\":\"tool_write_jTioZUP48fW4mXg8ZPKR\",\"format\":\"google-gemini-v1\",\"index\":0}]}},\"id\":\"prt_e263195630018pRZ9Aoqh0TCK9\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d9cfa9a2ffeMT2uPdw9AaYBmZ\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e263132c8002P6oDvtrSE7plxe\"}}\n./bench/gemini-3_1-pro/rep1/agent.log:207:{\"type\":\"text\",\"timestamp\":1778757258492,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d9cfa9a2ffeMT2uPdw9AaYBmZ\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_e2631c060001TRdTml81c2Gq0M\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e263196ce001aLOR2ylRCwm4rm\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d9cfa9a2ffeMT2uPdw9AaYBmZ\",\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"I have proposed the micro-architectural optimization hypothesis `hyp-20260514-001-r3s0` directly in the requested location (`cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260514-001-r3s0.yaml`). \\n\\n### Hypothesis Summary\\nThis hypothesis combines two proven micro-optimizations that directly target the components of the fitness metric (CoreMark iter/sec = iter/cycle × Fmax):\\n\\n1. **Precise Load-Use Hazards (Improves iter/cycle):** The baseline hazard unit stalls any time the arbitrary `rs1`/`rs2` fields of the IF/ID instruction match a preceding LOAD's `rd` (even if the instruction doesn't read registers, like `LUI`, `AUIPC`, `JAL`, or `OP-IMM` which only reads `rs1`). By decoding the opcode in the hazard unit to yield `rs1_en` and `rs2_en`, we eliminate spurious bubbles.\\n2. **ALU Multiplier Consolidation (Improves Fmax & Area):** The baseline infers three separate full-width 64x64 multipliers (`mul_ss`, `mul_uu`, `mul_su`). Tang Nano 20K DSP blocks are 18x18, making 64-bit inference extremely taxing on area and routing. Consolidating this into a single signed 33x33 multiplier via dynamic sign-extension bits dramatically reduces LUT/DSP utilization and shortens the critical path.\\n\\nThe hypothesis is highly specific in its `changes` section to ensure the implementing agent does not stumble on cosim/formal regressions (like preserving `RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS` behavior).\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778757255264,\"end\":1778757258491}}}\n./bench/gemini-3_1-pro/rep1/agent.log:212:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778758071378,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d9c21a76ffexANSgE0u3qKLpH\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"bash\",\"callID\":\"tool_bash_0pt50gDBtO8uv0ZUm7MS\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"error\",\"input\":{\"command\":\"grep -A 2 -B 2 \\\"Static Branch/JAL Predictor in ID\\\" experiments/log.jsonl\"},\"error\":\"The bash tool was called with invalid arguments: SchemaError(Missing key\\n  at [\\\"description\\\"]).\\nPlease rewrite the input so it satisfies the expected schema.\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778758071374,\"end\":1778758071377}},\"metadata\":{\"openrouter\":{\"reasoning_details\":[{\"type\":\"reasoning.text\",\"text\":\"**Proposing Microarchitecture Optimization**\\n\\nI'm currently focused on developing a hypothesis for a `micro_opt` within the `cores/bench/` target. The aim is to improve performance beyond the current baseline of a 5-stage in-order RV32IM core, which achieves 282.82 iter/sec.\\n\\n**Proposing Microarchitecture Optimization**\\n\\nI'm currently investigating potential microarchitecture optimizations for the `cores/bench/` target. My focus is on addressing the current 2-cycle branch misprediction penalty inherent in the existing 5-stage in-order RV32IM core. I am examining the `ex_stage.sv` and `alu.sv` to understand how branch resolution and ALU operations are handled, looking for areas to improve performance.\\n\\n**Analyzing ALU Bottlenecks**\\n\\nI'm delving into the `alu.sv` and `ex_stage.sv` files. I've identified that both 64-bit multiplication and division are implemented as combinational logic, which is likely creating a significant critical path. I need to investigate if this is impacting performance and explore potential optimizations.\\n\\n**Analyzing ALU and Pipeline Depth**\\n\\nI'm currently dissecting the `alu.sv` and `ex_stage.sv` modules, focusing on the combinational implementations of 64-bit multiplication and division. My hypothesis is that these operations, especially the multiplier's unconditional computation of all three variants, are creating a significant critical path. I'm also re-evaluating the impact of the 2-cycle branch misprediction penalty within the current 5-stage pipeline.\\n\\n**Exploring ALU and Pipeline Improvements**\\n\\nI'm currently scrutinizing the combinational implementations of multiplication and division within `alu.sv`. My hypothesis is that these operations are forming a significant critical path. I'm also reassessing the impact of the 2-cycle branch misprediction penalty and examining the ALU's role in branch condition evaluation for potential optimization.\\n\\n**Investigating Branch Prediction**\\n\\nI'm analyzing the impact of the 2-cycle branch misprediction penalty. My current focus is on whether resolving JAL instructions earlier, potentially in the ID stage, could reduce this penalty and improve performance. I'm re-examining the \\\"Static Branch/JAL Predictor in ID\\\" regression to understand its implications.\\n\\n\",\"format\":\"google-gemini-v1\",\"index\":0},{\"type\":\"reasoning.encrypted\",\"data\":\"\",\"id\":\"tool_bash_0pt50gDBtO8uv0ZUm7MS\",\"format\":\"google-gemini-v1\",\"in