'm currently investigating the subtle IPC changes that occurred.\\n\\n**Investigating Performance Discrepancy**\\n\\nI've confirmed the branch predictor met baseline performance exactly on CoreMark, with no IPC or Fmax delta. I'm now probing deeper to understand why cycle counts weren't reduced, suspecting pipeline flushes related to specific hazard conditions might be the cause.\\n\\n**Investigating Prediction Accuracy**\\n\\nI've confirmed the ID branch predictor perfectly matched baseline performance on CoreMark, achieving zero delta in IPC and Fmax. I am now exploring why cycle counts weren't reduced, suspecting residual pipeline flushes from specific hazard conditions might be the culprit.\\n\\n**Optimizing ALU Operations**\\n\\nI've identified a significant optimization opportunity within the ALU. We can replace the current three parallel multipliers with a single signed 33x33 multiplier to compute all necessary signed and unsigned multiplication results.\\n\\n**Analyzing Branch Predictor Impact**\\n\\nI've confirmed the ID branch predictor perfectly matched baseline CoreMark performance, showing zero delta in IPC and Fmax. I am now investigating why cycle counts were not reduced, suspecting residual pipeline flushes due to specific hazard conditions might be the cause.\\n\\n**Optimizing ALU Multiplier**\\n\\nI've identified a key optimization for the ALU. We can replace the current three parallel multipliers with a single signed 33x33 multiplier to efficiently compute all required signed and unsigned multiplication results.\\n\\n**Optimizing ALU Multiplier**\\n\\nI've identified a key optimization for the ALU. We can replace the current three parallel multipliers with a single signed 33x33 multiplier to efficiently compute all required signed and unsigned multiplication results.\\n\\n**Optimizing ALU Multiplier**\\n\\nI've identified a critical optimization in the ALU. The current implementation utilizes three separate 64x64 multipliers for signed and unsigned operations. I propose replacing this with a single signed 33x33 multiplier, which can compute all necessary signed and unsigned results more efficiently.\\n\\n**Optimizing ALU Multiplication**\\n\\nI've identified a significant opportunity to optimize the ALU's multiplication logic. The current implementation uses three parallel 64x64 multipliers, which is excessively resource-intensive. I propose replacing this with a single signed 33x33 multiplier that can efficiently compute all required signed and unsigned multiplication results.\\n\\n\",\"format\":\"google-gemini-v1\",\"index\":0},{\"type\":\"reasoning.encrypted\",\"data\":\"\",\"id\":\"tool_bash_jEAGR2hXsi5vkGgMAvxL\",\"format\":\"google-gemini-v1\",\"index\":0}]}},\"id\":\"prt_e263f1d53001s4Ky7ZmGtCPJRc\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d9c21a76ffexANSgE0u3qKLpH\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e263ea8a2001khwo4MWbQc1gas\"}}\n./bench/gemini-3_1-pro/rep1/agent.log:224:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778758146092,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d9c21a76ffexANSgE0u3qKLpH\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"bash\",\"callID\":\"tool_bash_WH5lCP9GaON0LcoMNNBb\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"command\":\"cat << 'EOF' > test_mul_one.sv\\nmodule test_mul_one(\\n  input logic [4:0] op,\\n  input logic [31:0] a, b,\\n  output logic [63:0] mul_res\\n);\\n  logic signed [32:0] a_ext;\\n  logic signed [32:0] b_ext;\\n  logic signed [65:0] res;\\n  \\n  always_comb begin\\n    if (op == 5'd11 || op == 5'd13) begin // MUL, MULHU\\n      a_ext = {1'b0, a};\\n      b_ext = {1'b0, b};\\n    end else if (op == 5'd14) begin // MULHSU\\n      a_ext = {a[31], a};\\n      b_ext = {1'b0, b};\\n    end else begin\\n      a_ext = {a[31], a};\\n      b_ext = {b[31], b};\\n    end\\n    res = a_ext * b_ext;\\n    mul_res = res[63:0];\\n  end\\nendmodule\\nEOF\\nyosys -p \\\"read_verilog -sv test_mul_one.sv; synth_gowin -family gw2a\\\"\\n\",\"description\":\"Test single multiplier synthesis\"},\"output\":\"\\n /----------------------------