{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"jq -r 'select(.category == \\\"structural\\\") | [.id, .title, .outcome, (.fitness // \\\"\\\"), (.delta_pct // \\\"\\\"), (.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis | tostring | gsub(\\\"\\\\n\\\"; \\\" \\\"))] | @tsv' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"hyp-20260710-002-r1s1\tDecode-stage branch resolution\tbroken\t\t\tRestructure the front end so conditional branches resolve in decode using the register-file read values and explicit bypasses from EX/MEM and WB. Redirect the PC and squash only the fetched wrong-path instruction in the same decode cycle; retain EX resolution for JALR and preserve a targeted load-to-branch interlock until load data is available. This removes one recovery cycle for the common backward conditional branch while retaining single-retire RVFI ordering and the existing memory interface. \nhyp-20260710-002-r2s1\tDecoupled iterative divide unit\timprovement\t405.36\t32.31\tReplace the combinational RV32M divide/remainder implementation with a blocking iterative divide unit that accepts an EX-stage divide instruction, holds front-end issue while it runs, then injects the correctly signed quotient or remainder into the existing EX/MEM path. Keep add/logic/shift and the shared multiplier entirely on the one-cycle EX path. This structural separation should shorten normal-cycle timing enough to raise Fmax, while the rare multi-cycle divide latency has little CoreMark IPC cost. \nhyp-20260710-002-r3s1\tDecoupled sequential fetch queue\tbroken\t\t\tReplace the single combinational fetch payload with a small sequential instruction FIFO and an independent fetch PC. While decode or execute is stalled, the front end continues to fill unused FIFO entries whenever imem is ready; while imem is temporarily not ready, decode consumes buffered instructions. A redirect atomically invalidates queued wrong-path entries and restarts fetching at the resolved target. This preserves the existing EX-stage branch decision and single-retire RVFI boundary while converting available slack into tolerance of the benchmark's random instruction stalls. \nhyp-20260710-002-r4s1\tProcedural decoupled instruction queue\tregression\t330.11\t-18.56\tReplace the direct combinational IF-to-ID path with a four-entry instruction queue driven by a separate fetch PC. Implement its storage as a normal packed array indexed procedurally by runtime read/write pointers, never as generated hierarchy. A ready-qualified fill may run while downstream is interlocked, and decode consumes an already buffered instruction during temporary imem stalls. On an EX redirect, atomically discard all queued entries, reset the pointers and count, and restart the fetch PC at the architectural target; no pre-redirect instruction may reach ID. This converts available front-end slack into instruction-stall coverage without putting prediction logic on the PC feedback path or changing the single-retire RVFI boundary. \nhyp-20260710-002-r5s1\tCold iterative high-half multiply unit\tregression\t349.82\t-13.7\tKeep architectural MUL as a one-cycle unsigned low-32-bit product, whose result is independent of operand signedness, and dispatch MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU to a blocking iterative high-product unit. The unit snapshots its operands and signedness, performs a shift/add product over multiple cycles, then injects the required high half through the normal EX/MEM path while younger issue is interlocked. This removes the 33-bit signed product and high-half selection logic from every ordinary EX cycle; the occasional multi-cycle high multiply should be amortized by the improved Fmax. \nhyp-20260710-002-r6s1\tOrdered one-entry store buffer\tbroken\t\t\tRestructure the memory side around a one-entry ordered store buffer. A store entering MEM snapshots its aligned address, replicated write data, and byte mask and may retire once accepted into the buffer; the buffer alone drives the dmem write request until io_dmemReady acknowledges it. While that pending write drains, younger non-memory instructions continue through the pipe. A load, a second store when the entry is occupied, and any operation requiring memory ordering interlock until the entry has drained, so the externally visible memory order and RVFI store payload remain architectural. This avoids the queue and predictor front-end costs that regressed, while directly covering a distinct source of the benchmark's injected dmem stalls. \nhyp-20260710-002-r7s1\tMEM-to-EX load forwarding\tregression\t364.7\t-11.37\tRestructure the load-result path so the combinationally extracted MEM-stage load value can bypass directly to the simultaneously executing dependent instruction in EX. Remove the ordinary one-cycle load-use bubble, but only permit that bypass when the older load is in EX/MEM and dmem is ready; on a dmem stall, retain the existing pipeline hold until the response is valid. The normal MEM/WB forwarding remains as the fallback for older loads, and RVFI continues to retire the original load and dependent instruction in program order. This should improve CoreMark iterations per cycle without adding predictor state or changing the external memory or RVFI contracts. \nhyp-20260710-002-r8s1\tDecode-accepted direct JAL redirect\tregression\t161.77\t-62.42\tResolve only direct JAL in decode when that instruction is accepted into ID/EX. Form its immediate target from the fetched instruction and update the fetch PC at the same edge, while carrying an already-redirected bit so EX preserves the JAL RVFI pc_wdata and return-address result without issuing a second redirect. Leave conditional branches and JALR in EX, avoiding the costly decode-stage comparator and operand-forwarding network that made the earlier full branch-resolution attempt fragile. Gate the early redirect on ID acceptance and give an older EX redirect priority across all stalls. \nhyp-20260710-002-r9s1\tIn-order memory completion queue\tbroken\t\t\tReplace the single coupled MEM/WB boundary with a two-entry in-order completion queue. A memory operation occupies the head entry until its dmem handshake completes; its RVFI record and architectural retirement occur only after that completion. While the head is waiting, one younger non-memory EX result may enter the second entry, but cannot retire early. Loads and stores behind an unresolved memory head remain interlocked, and store data/masks stay stable until acknowledgement. This isolates short dmem waits from EX and decode, preserves strictly ordered RVFI retirement and memory visibility, and avoids putting a dmem-return forwarding cone on the EX path. \nhyp-20260711-002-r10s1\tElastic registered fetch boundary\tbroken\t\t\tInsert a single-entry elastic IF/ID register between instruction memory and decode. A separate fetch PC fills the entry only when imemReady is high; decode consumes its registered PC/instruction only when ID/EX can accept. Permit simultaneous dequeue/refill, retain the entry across downstream interlocks, and give an older EX redirect priority to invalidate the entry and restart fetch at the architectural target. This adds only a compact ready/valid boundary, breaks the imem-to-decode critical path, and can absorb one instruction response while the back end is stalled without changing in-order execution or the RVFI retirement boundary. \nhyp-20260711-002-r11s1\tTwo-stage partial-product multiplier\tregression\t355.48\t-17.42\tReplace the one-cycle 33x33 signed multiplier with a blocking, two-stage partial-product unit. Stage one captures forwarded operands and computes four 16/17-bit partial products; stage two recombines their shifted sum, selects the RV32M high or low word, and inserts the result once into the existing EX/MEM path. Hold younger issue only for the added multiply stage, while older MEM/WB retirement continues normally. This removes the wide multiplier from the normal ALU cone and should raise Fmax enough to offset CoreMark's sparse one-cycle multiply interlocks. \nhyp-20260711-002-r12s1\tDecode-resolved direct JAL path\tbroken\t\t\tAdd a decode-stage redirect path for validated JAL only. Form pc+J-immediate from the current IF/ID payload and update the fetch PC when that payload advances into ID/EX, while preserving the JAL payload for normal EX/MEM/WB retirement and PC+4 writeback. Give an older EX redirect priority, keep conditional branches and JALR entirely EX-resolved, and distinguish the decode redirect from an EX flush so the issuing JAL is never suppressed. This removes one control-recovery cycle for direct jumps without a branch comparator, register bypass network, prediction table, or imem-to-PC path. \nhyp-20260711-002-r13s1\tDSP-backed one-cycle multiplier unit\tregression\t337.88\t-21.51\tReplace the inferred fabric multiply inside the general ALU with a dedicated, one-cycle RV32M multiply execution unit explicitly structured for Gowin DSP inference or primitive mapping. The unit will sign-extend operands once, compose the full product from DSP-sized terms, and select MUL or the required high half before the existing EX/MEM register. It remains combinational at the architectural interface, so dependent instructions, retirement timing, and RVFI payloads are unchanged; only the implementation topology moves the multiplier off LUT/carry fabric and out of the normal ALU cone. \n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
