"formal_passed\\\": true, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": true, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"implementation_notes\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-07-11T02:21:20.670665Z\\\", \\\"slot\\\": 0, \\\"round_id\\\": 6, \\\"lesson\\\": \\\"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r6s0 (improvement, +1.51%): Encoding BEQ/BNE and signed/unsigned relational branches from one 33-bit subtract's zero, sign, and borrow flags shares a carry-chain-friendly EX cone, modestly improving CoreMark loop-branch timing without changing redirect behavior.\\\"}\\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:18:{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260710-002-r6s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Ordered one-entry store buffer\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The random dmem-ready deassertions currently hold the entire pipeline for\\\\nevery store, even though a store has no destination register and younger\\\\nnon-memory instructions cannot observe its completion. CoreMark performs\\\\nfrequent stores in its list and matrix kernels, so these otherwise avoidable\\\\nfull-pipeline waits lower useful instruction throughput.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The random dmem-ready deassertions currently hold the entire pipeline for\\\\nevery store, even though a store has no destination register and younger\\\\nnon-memory instructions cannot observe its completion. CoreMark performs\\\\nfrequent stores in its list and matrix kernels, so these otherwise avoidable\\\\nfull-pipeline waits lower useful instruction throughput.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Restructure the memory side around a one-entry ordered store buffer. A store\\\\nentering MEM snapshots its aligned address, replicated write data, and byte\\\\nmask and may retire once accepted into the buffer; the buffer alone drives\\\\nthe dmem write request until io_dmemReady acknowledges it. While that pending\\\\nwrite drains, younger non-memory instructions continue through the pipe. A\\\\nload, a second store when the entry is occupied, and any operation requiring\\\\nmemory ordering interlock until the entry has drained, so the externally\\\\nvisible memory order and RVFI store payload remain architectural. This avoids\\\\nthe queue and predictor front-end costs that regressed, while directly\\\\ncovering a distinct source of the benchmark's injected dmem stalls.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a one-entry store-buffer state machine that snapshots stores, drives pending write requests, accepts a store without waiting for dmemReady, and preserves its RVFI memory fields at retirement.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add the packed store-buffer request payload and explicit memory-issue status signals needed to distinguish buffered stores from ordered loads.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the blanket dmem stall with ordering-aware interlocks: permit non-memory work behind a pending buffered store, but hold loads and a second store until the buffer drains.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Wire memory-stage buffer occupancy and memory-order backpressure into pipeline control without changing the nret=1 external interface.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_mem_stage.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add directed tests for delayed store acknowledgment, continued non-memory retirement, store-to-load ordering, consecutive stores, byte masks, and RVFI store payload stability.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a one-entry store-buffer state machine that snapshots stores, drives pending write requests, accepts a store without waiting for dmemReady, and preserves its RVFI mem