{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_2\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"jq -r 'select(.category == \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"predictor\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\") | [.id, .title, .outcome, (.fitness // \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"n/a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tostring), (.fmax_mhz // \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"n/a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tostring), (.cycles // \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"n/a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tostring), (.hypothesis.hypothesis // \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\")] | @tsv' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl && test -e cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260711-003-r11s2.yaml && echo exists || true && sed -n '1,240p' cores/bench/LESSONS.md\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"hyp-20260710-003-r1s2\\\\tBackward-taken fetch branch prediction\\\\tregression\\\\t252.17\\\\t108.67\\\\t4309463\\\\tAdd a combinational backward-taken, forward-not-taken (BTFNT) predictor in\\\\\\\\nIF. For a valid BRANCH encoding with a negative B immediate, fetch will\\\\\\\\nselect the statically computed target instead of PC+4 and carry the\\\\\\\\npredicted next PC with the instruction. EX will compare that prediction with\\\\\\\\nits resolved next PC, redirect and squash only on disagreement. This removes\\\\\\\\nthe recurring loop-back recovery bubbles without adding a RAM-based BTB or a\\\\\\\\npredictor-table timing path.\\\\\\\\n\\\\nhyp-20260710-003-r2s2\\\\tPC-indexed dynamic loop predictor\\\\tregression\\\\t308.5\\\\t131.46\\\\t4261280\\\\tAdd a small direct-mapped branch target buffer with a two-bit saturating\\\\\\\\ncounter per entry. Fetch indexes it solely with the registered PC; a valid\\\\\\\\ntag match and taken counter select a previously learned target, otherwise\\\\\\\\nfetch falls through. EX continues to resolve every branch and updates the\\\\\\\\nentry with the resolved direction and target, then redirects only on a\\\\\\\\npredicted-next-PC mismatch. This warms up after one taken loop edge while\\\\\\\\navoiding any io_imemData-to-PC combinational dependency, preserving timing\\\\\\\\nbetter than BTFNT while removing recurring loop-back recovery bubbles.\\\\\\\\n\\\\nhyp-20260710-003-r3s2\\\\tLookahead registered BTB predictor\\\\tbroken\\\\tn/a\\\\tn/a\\\\tn/a\\\\tQuery a small tagged BTB one sequential instruction ahead, using fetch PC+4,\\\\\\\\nand register the hit, direction, target, and queried PC. On the following\\\\\\\\ncycle that registered response belongs to the current sequentially fetched\\\\\\\\ninstruction; if it is a taken branch prediction, IF advances to the saved\\\\\\\\ntarget while still delivering the branch itself to ID. Carry the predicted\\\\\\\\nnext PC to EX and redirect only when architectural resolution disagrees.\\\\\\\\nThis predicts hot loop-closing branches without imem-data decode, BTB tag\\\\\\\\ncompare, counter read, or target selection on the PC-to-PC timing path.\\\\\\\\n\\\\nhyp-20260710-003-r4s2\\\\tReturn-address-stack fetch predictor\\\\tregression\\\\t364.95\\\\t163.43\\\\t4478102\\\\tAdd a shallow committed return-address stack and predict only canonical\\\\\\\\nreturns (`jalr x0, x1/x5, 0`) in IF. The prediction is just an instruction\\\\\\\\nfield match and a mux of the registered RAS top, avoiding branch-immediate\\\\\\\\nextraction, target addition, tags, and counter RAM on the PC path. Train\\\\\\\\npushes and pops only when the corresponding JAL/JALR has resolved in EX;\\\\\\\\ncarry the predicted next PC to EX and retain existing redirect recovery for\\\\\\\\nany mismatch. Correct returns therefore avoid the existing two younger\\\\\\\\nwrong-path instructions without changing conditional-branch behavior.\\\\\\\\n\\\\nhyp-20260710-003-r5s2\\\\tValidated lookahead BTB predictor\\\\tbroken\\\\tn/a\\\\tn/a\\\\tn/a\\\\tImplement a small direct-mapped conditional-branch BTB whose lookup is made\\\\\\\\nfor sequential PC+4 and registered before it can influence fetch. A response\\\\\\\\nmay redirect only when its recorded query PC exactly equals the current fetch\\\\\\\\nPC; otherwise fetch deterministically falls through and invalidates the\\\\\\\\nstale response. EX trains only resolved valid branches and carries the exact\\\\\\\\npredicted next PC for mismatch recovery. Redirects, instruction stalls, and\\\\\\\\ndownstream holds invalidate or preserve the response according to whether\\\\\\\\nthe matching fetched instruction was actually accepted. This removes hot\\\\\\\\nloop recovery bubbles while keeping tag comparison, counter read, and target\\\\\\\\nselection out of the PC-to-PC feedback path.\\\\\\\\n\\\\nhyp-20260710-003-r6s2\\\\tOne-entry loop target predictor\\\\tbroken\\\\tn/a\\\\tn/a\\\\tn/a\\\\tAdd a one-entry last-resolved conditional-branch predictor. It stores only\\\\\\\\nthe PC, resolved target, and last direction of the most recently resolved\\\\\\\\nvalid conditional branch; fetch predicts taken only when the registered PC\\\\\\\\nexactly matches that entry and the stored direction is taken. This replaces\\\\\\\\na table lookup, tag array, and saturating-counter logic with a single\\\\\\\\nequality check and target mux, while preserving EX as the sole architectural\\\\\\\\nresolver. The fetched instruction carries an explicit predicted-next-PC\\\\\\\\ntransaction; EX redirects only on a mismatch and trains the entry only when\\\\\\\\nthat branch actually advances, with redirect and all front-end holds keeping\\\\\\\\nprediction metadata paired with its accepted instruction.\\\\\\\\n\\\\nhyp-20260710-003-r7s2\\\\tSpeculation-safe loop target predictor\\\\tregression\\\\t167.49\\\\t73.5\\\\t4388269\\\\tAdd a one-entry last-resolved conditional-branch predictor, but make its\\\\\\\\nprediction a transaction carried by every fetched instruction rather than a\\\\\\\\nbranch-only promise. The entry stores branch PC, target, and last direction;\\\\\\\\nits small registered-PC equality and target mux remain the only logic added\\\\\\\\nto fetch. IF/ID and ID/EX carry predicted_next_pc for all accepted fetches.\\\\\\\\nIn EX, compare that value against the architectural next PC for every valid\\\\\\\\ninstruction: non-control instructions resolve to PC+4, branches and jumps\\\\\\\\nresolve normally. Any mismatch redirects and flushes, including a predictor\\\\\\\\nhit whose symbolic imem word is no longer a branch. Train the entry only\\\\\\\\nwhen a valid resolved conditional branch advances from EX, and bind prediction\\\\\\\\nmetadata to its fetch PC across imem/dmem stalls and redirects. This retains\\\\\\\\nthe timing advantage of a one-entry PC-only predictor while making all stale\\\\\\\\nor aliased hits recoverable under RVFI.\\\\\\\\n\\\\nhyp-20260710-003-r8s2\\\\tSignature-qualified loop predictor\\\\tbroken\\\\tn/a\\\\tn/a\\\\tn/a\\\\tAdd a one-entry last-taken conditional-branch predictor that stores branch\\\\\\\\nPC, target, direction, and the resolved instruction word as a signature.\\\\\\\\nIF uses only registered PC equality, direction, and target muxing to select\\\\\\\\nthe speculative successor; the instruction signature is carried as\\\\\\\\nsideband validation metadata and is not placed in the PC-select cone. EX\\\\\\\\nretains architectural branch resolution and redirects only for a predicted\\\\\\\\ncontrol instruction whose resolved successor differs, or for a predicted\\\\\\\\nhit that resolves as a non-branch (including a symbolic-imem signature\\\\\\\\nmismatch), explicitly restoring PC+4. This avoids assuming PC identity\\\\\\\\nproves instruction identity, while replacing the prior universal 32-bit\\\\\\\\nnext-PC compare with narrow prediction-valid/control-qualified recovery.\\\\\\\\n\\\\nhyp-20260710-003-r9s2\\\\tEpoch-bound registered loop predictor\\\\tregression\\\\t343.92\\\\t148.59\\\\t4320468\\\\tAdd a one-entry last-taken conditional-branch predictor whose lookup is\\\\\\\\nissued only for the sequential candidate PC+4 and whose response is\\\\\\\\nregistered at the same edge that advances the fetch PC to that candidate.\\\\\\\\nThe response carries its queried PC and a fetch epoch; it may select its\\\\\\\\nstored target only for that exact next accepted fetch, then is consumed.\\\\\\\\nRedirects increment the epoch and invalidate the response, while imem,\\\\\\\\ndmem, and divide holds retain or discard it according to whether its query\\\\\\\\nPC was actually accepted. EX carries the prediction transaction with every\\\\\\\\nfetched instruction and compares the architectural successor for every\\\\\\\\nvalid instruction, recovering to PC+4 for symbolic-imem non-branch alias\\\\\\\\nhits as well as normal direction/target mispredictions. This saves one\\\\\\\\nhot-loop recovery cycle without putting BTB lookup, target selection, or\\\\\\\\ninstruction decode in the PC-to-PC combinational path.\\\\\\\\n\\\\nhyp-20260711-003-r10s2\\\\tRegistered decode BTFNT predictor\\\\tbroken\\\\tn/a\\\\tn/a\\\\tn/a\\\\tAdd a real registered IF/ID payload and apply static backward-taken,\\\\\\\\nforward-not-taken prediction in decode only for a decoded, valid conditional\\\\\\\\nbranch with a negative B immediate. Decode supplies the predicted target to\\\\\\\\nthe PC register for the next fetch, so instruction-memory data, branch\\\\\\\\ndecode, immediate extraction, and target addition are separated across the\\\\\\\\nIF/ID register instead of one PC-to-PC timing cone. Carry a small\\\\\\\\npredicted-branch/predicted-taken bit with the instruction; EX performs its\\\\\\\\nexisting architectural branch resolution and recovers only for that\\\\\\\\nprediction's direction/target mismatch. Thus non-branch instructions avoid\\\\\\\\nthe universal 32-bit successor comparison that made the epoch predictor\\\\\\\\nslow, while symbolic instruction memory is safe because prediction is\\\\\\\\nqualified from the registered instruction itself.\\\\\\\\n\\\\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r1s0 (improvement, +8.33%): RV32M multiply variants should share a sign-selected 33x33 product and select the required 32-bit half, since parallel signed/unsigned multiplier cones waste LUTs even though each instruction consumes only one result.\\\\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r1s2 (regression, -10.84%): A combinational BTFNT predictor that derives the PC update from imem_data puts instruction fetch, branch decode, immediate extraction, and target addition on the IF-to-PC feedback path, so it must be pipelined or decoupled when its Fmax loss exceeds CoreMark's saved redirect bubbles.\\\\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r2s0 (regression, +20.73%): Replacing a shared 33x33 signed multiplier with a 32x32 unsigned product can regress when MULH/MULHSU require chained conditional 32-bit high-half corrections, because the added subtractor network outweighs any saved multiplier logic on the CoreMark-critical ALU path.\\\\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r2s1 (improvement, +32.31%): Moving DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU into a blocking iterative unit removes wide divide/remainder cones from the common EX path, and CoreMark's sparse division use lets the Fmax gain outweigh the rare multi-cycle front-end stalls.\\\\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r2s2 (regression, +0.69%): Removing imem_data from branch prediction is insufficient when a combinational PC-indexed BTB tag match, counter read, and next-PC mux remain on the fetch feedback path; CoreMark's saved loop-back bubbles did not amortize that added front-end cost.\\\\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r3s0 (regression, -27.02%): Registering a blocking 33x33 multiplier without splitting its arithmetic only moves the full-cycle product into a new register-to-register cone while adding EX ownership and interlock logic, so it cannot deliver the divide unit's Fmax benefit without real pipelining or iteration.\\\\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r3s1 (broken, n/a): A sequential fetch FIFO must index its storage procedurally with runtime read/write pointers; generate-block hierarchy and prefixes are elaboration-time constructs and reject dynamic queue indices before synthesis.\\\\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r4s0 (broken, n/a): Decode-qualified load-use detection must classify every architectural source in the same valid/timing domain as operand capture, because suppressing a real dependency lets stale register data reach EX and is caught by RVFI register checks.\\\\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r4s1 (regression, -18.56%): A four-entry procedural IF queue can lose CoreMark fitness when its pointer/count, dequeue arbitration, and redirect-flush control cost more than the transient imem stalls that prefetch can cover, so front-end decoupling needs measured slack rather than assumed stall hiding.\\\\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r5s2 (broken, n/a): A registered lookahead BTB must treat its response PC and predicted next PC as a fetch-acceptance transaction, invalidating or holding them consistently across redirects and stalls, or stale predictions can misassociate with conditional branches and violate RVFI control-flow checks.\\\\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r6s0 (improvement, +1.51%): Encoding BEQ/BNE and signed/unsigned relational branches from one 33-bit subtract's zero, sign, and borrow flags shares a carry-chain-friendly EX cone, modestly improving CoreMark loop-branch timing without changing redirect behavior.\\\\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r6s1 (broken, n/a): A write-behind store buffer must make buffer admission, external write acknowledgement, and RVFI retirement one ordered transaction boundary, since retiring a store while its request can outlive or be overwritten by younger MEM traffic violates architectural load/store ordering and register-state checks.\\\\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r6s2 (broken, n/a): A PC-only branch predictor must validate the fetched instruction's branch identity or rely on immutable instruction memory, because formal's per-cycle symbolic imem can reuse a trained PC for a non-branch and make the predictor skip its required PC+4.\\\\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r7s0 (improvement, +4.61%): On an FPGA MEM path, replacing variable 32-bit load shifts and shifted store masks with fixed addr-indexed byte/halfword lane muxes removes a barrel-mux cone after dmem and improves Fmax when alignment trapping already restricts halfword selection.\\\\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r7s1 (regression, -11.37%): Zero-wait MEM-to-EX forwarding creates a dmem-return-to-load-format-to-operand-mux-to-ALU critical path, so CoreMark's saved load-use bubbles can be outweighed by Fmax loss unless the load response is timing-isolated.\\\\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r8s2 (broken, n/a): A signature-mismatched conditional-branch prediction must redirect to the resolved PC+4 even when the branch is not taken, because treating it as a cold not-taken branch without recovery leaves the already-applied stale taken target on the fetch path and violates RVFI PC-forward progress.\\\\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r9s1 (broken, n/a): A decoupled in-order completion queue must make dequeue an atomic retirement event for RVFI payload/order, register-file writeback, and PC-forward state, because separate retire and commit views can misalign architectural instruction state across queue transitions.\\\\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260710-003-r9s2 (regression, -20.10%): An epoch-safe registered loop predictor can still lose badly when every EX instruction computes and 32-bit-compares an architectural successor against carried prediction metadata, because that recovery logic extends the hot EX redirect cone more than CoreMark amortizes the saved loop-back bubble.\\\\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r10s0 (regression, -4.08%): Sharing RV32I shifts through a 33-bit arithmetic-right barrel with pre/post bit-reversal can hurt CoreMark because the direction/sign-control muxing and reversal routing extend the hot EX shift-result path more than the removed parallel shift cones save.\\\\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r10s1 (broken, n/a): A one-entry elastic IF/ID boundary must make fetch fill/dequeue and ID/EX advancement a single valid-ready transaction, because consuming or refilling an entry out of step with decode can misassociate instruction state and break RVFI PC-forward, register, and liveness checks.\\\\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r10s2 (broken, n/a): A decode-stage branch prediction redirect must atomically invalidate or suppress the already-issued sequential fetch payload, because otherwise a fall-through instruction can survive alongside the predicted target and widespread conditional-branch RVFI PC checks fail.\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_3\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"agent_message\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"The measured predictors all paid for fetch-path logic or broad EX recovery. I’m selecting the unmeasured-but-correctable variant: an atomic, registered decode-stage BTFNT path, with the sequential fetch payload explicitly invalidated on the prediction redirect. This keeps branch qualification off the PC feedback path and avoids the costly “compare every instruction” recovery scheme.\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.started\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_4\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"file_change\\\",\\\"changes\\\":[{\\\"path\\\":\\\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_6-terra-rep1/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260711-003-r11s2.yaml\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"add\\\"}],\\\"status\\\":\\\"in_progress\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_4\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"file_change\\\",\\\"changes\\\":[{\\\"path\\\":\\\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_6-terra-rep1/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260711-003-r11s2.yaml\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"add\\\"}],\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.started\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_5\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"ruby -e 'require \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"yaml\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"; p YAML.load_file(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260711-003-r11s2.yaml\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\")' && git diff --check -- cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260711-003-r11s2.yaml && git status --short -- cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260711-003-r11s2.yaml\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":null,\\\"status\\\":\\\"in_progress\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_5\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"ruby -e 'require \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"yaml\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"; p YAML.load_file(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260711-003-r11s2.yaml\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\")' && git diff --check -- cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260711-003-r11s2.yaml && git status --short -- cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260711-003-r11s2.yaml\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\"=>\\\\\\\"hyp-20260711-003-r11s2\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"title\\\\\\\"=>\\\\\\\"Atomic registered BTFNT front end\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"category\\\\\\\"=>\\\\\\\"predictor\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"motivation\\\\\\\"=>\\\\\\\"CoreMark repeatedly takes backward conditional loop branches, while the\\\\\\\\ncurrent EX-stage resolver fetches two sequential wrong-path instructions.\\\\\\\\nEarlier fetch predictors hurt Fmax by extending the PC feedback path, and\\\\\\\\nthe prior registered decode predictor failed because its sequential fetch\\\\\\\\npayload was not invalidated atomically with a predicted redirect.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"hypothesis\\\\\\\"=>\\\\\\\"Insert one stall-aware registered IF/ID fetch payload and perform static\\\\\\\\nbackward-taken, forward-not-taken prediction only when that registered,\\\\\\\\nvalid instruction decodes as a conditional branch with a negative B\\\\\\\\nimmediate. Treat prediction as an atomic valid-ready transaction: when\\\\\\\\ndecode advances the