h instructions.\\nEarlier predictors either placed decode and target addition on the fetch\\nfeedback path or allowed a sequential fetch payload to survive a redirect.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark repeatedly executes backward conditional loop branches, yet the\\ncurrent EX-stage redirect admits two sequential wrong-path instructions.\\nEarlier predictors either placed decode and target addition on the fetch\\nfeedback path or allowed a sequential fetch payload to survive a redirect.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a single-ahead registered fetch payload and use static\\nbackward-taken/forward-not-taken prediction only when that payload is\\naccepted by decode as a valid conditional branch. The fetch address always\\nrequests the sequential successor of the registered payload, so normal\\ninstructions retain one-instruction-per-cycle delivery. On an accepted\\npredicted branch, atomically discard that already-fetched sequential word,\\ninstall an invalid payload, and issue the recorded backward target next;\\nthus the target reaches decode as the branch reaches EX without a decode or\\ntarget-adder cone on the PC feedback path. Carry prediction metadata only\\nwith predicted branches and make EX redirect only for that branch's\\ndirection/target mismatch. Give EX recovery priority over predictor\\nrefill, and gate every fill/consume transition with imem readiness and the\\nexisting downstream hold conditions, preserving a one-to-one PC,\\ninstruction, and prediction transaction under stalls.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add fetch-payload validity, PC, and branch-only predicted-successor fields to keep each prediction bound to its decoded instruction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement the single-ahead registered payload, sequential lookahead fetch, atomic predicted-branch discard, and priority-ordered EX recovery.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Recognize valid backward conditional branches from the registered payload and emit a prediction only on an actual ID/EX acceptance.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry branch-only prediction metadata and compare resolved direction and target only for a predicted branch before requesting recovery.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Define acceptance, hold, and flush precedence for imem/dmem stalls, load-use stalls, divider ownership, predicted discard, and EX recovery.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire fetch acceptance, branch prediction metadata, and qualified recovery without changing the nret=1 top-level interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed taken-loop, final-exit, forward-branch, imem-stall, dmem-stall, and divider-hold tests for atomic sequential-payload discard and RVFI PC order.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add fetch-payload validity, PC, and branch-only predicted-successor fields to keep each prediction bound to its decoded instruction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement the single-ahead registered payload, sequential lookahead fetch, atomic predicted-branch discard, and priority-ordered EX recovery.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Recognize valid backward conditional branches from the registered payload and emit a prediction only on an actual ID/EX acceptance.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry branch-only prediction metadata and compare resolved direction and target only for a predicted branch before requesting recovery.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Define acceptance, hold, and flush precedence for imem/dmem stalls, load-use stalls, divider ownership, predicted discard, and EX recovery.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire fetch acceptance, branch prediction metadata, and qualified recovery without changing the nret=1 top-level interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed taken-loop, final-exit, forward-branch, imem-stall, dmem-stall, and divider-hold tests for atomic sequential-payload discard and RVFI PC order.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 358.51, \"delta_pct\": -16.71, \"vs_baseline\": 26.76, \"fmax_mhz\": 167.56, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4673734, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5976, \"ff\": 2334, \"seeds\": [172.83, 167.56, 166.94], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T03:34:09.779966Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 12}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:38:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r13s0\", \"title\": \"Share PC-relative target adder\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current EX control logic forms pc + immediate for branch_target and\\nseparately expresses the same addition as the direct-JAL arm of\\njump_target. This leaves two equivalent wide arithmetic expressions in the\\nhot control cone, despite both consumers requiring the identical result.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current EX control logic forms pc + immediate for branch_target and\\nseparately expresses the same addition as the direct-JAL arm of\\njump_target. This leaves two equivalent wide arithmetic expressions in the\\nhot control cone, despite both consumers requiring the identical result.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Materialize one pc-relative target wire in EX and use it for both conditional\\nbranch targets and non-JALR jump targets, including the EX/MEM branch-target\\nmetadata. Keep JALR on its independent forwarded rs1 + immediate path and\\nretain the existing misalignment and redirect semantics. Making this common\\nsubexpression explicit should force one carry-chain-friendly adder rather\\nthan duplicate arithmetic and routing, improving Fmax without changing IPC\\nor moving any control decision earlier in the pipeline.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Introduce one explicit pc-relative target adder and route branch, JAL, redirect, EX/MEM metadata, and alignment checks through that shared result; preserve the separate JALR adder and all trap behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed branch and JAL target cases, including positive and negative offsets plus misaligned targets, to verify shared-target control flow and RVFI PC results.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Introduce one explicit pc-relative target adder and route branch, JAL, redirect, EX/MEM metadata, and alignment checks through that shared result; preserve the separate JALR adder and all trap behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed branch and JAL target cases, including positive and negative offsets plus misaligned targets, to verify shared-target control flow and RVFI PC results.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 380.36, \"delta_pct\": -11.64, \"vs_baseline\": 34.49, \"fmax_mhz\": 170.85, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491817, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5791, \"ff\": 2221, \"seeds\": [170.85, 177.4, 170.39], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T03:45:51.301622Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 13, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r13s0 (regression, -11.64%): Explicitly sharing the PC-relative branch/JAL adder can reduce Fmax when the single result must fan out through EX redirect, alignment, and EX/MEM metadata logic, since local duplicated adders may route more cheaply than a high-fanout shared control-cone signal.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:40:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-003-r13s2\", \"title\": \"Partial-tag backward loop predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark spends much of its time on repeatedly taken backward loop branches,\\nbut full BTBs and BTFNT decode have reduced Fmax by making the fetch feedback\\ncone too wide. A much smaller predictor can retain the useful loop behavior\\nwithout placing instruction decode or a full PC tag comparison on that path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark spends much of its time on repeatedly taken backward loop branches,\\nbut full BTBs and BTFNT decode have reduced Fmax by making the fetch feedback\\ncone too wide. A much smaller predictor can retain the useful loop behavior\\nwithout placing instruction decode or a full PC tag comparison on that path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a four-entry, direct-mapped backward-loop target cache trained only by\\nresolved, valid, taken conditional branches whose target is below their PC.\\nEach entry holds a valid bit, a short PC fragment tag, and its target; fetch\\npredicts taken only on the narrow index-plus-fragment match, selecting the\\nregistered target rather than PC+4. Carry a prediction-valid bit and the\\nselected successor with the fetched instruction. EX remains the sole\\narchitectural resolver and recovers to PC+4 for a predicted alias that is\\nnot a branch, or to the resolved successor for a branch direction/target\\nmismatch. This deliberately permits harmless partial-tag aliases but makes\\ntheir recovery explicit, preserving correctness under symbolic imem while\\navoiding a full tag, counter RAM, or imem-data-to-PC path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a four-entry direct-mapped cache of resolved backward taken branch targets using a narrow PC-fragment tag and EX-only training.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry prediction-valid and predicted-successor metadata from IF through ID/EX for branch-qualified recovery.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select the target-cache successor from registered-PC predictor outputs without inspecting io_imemData in the PC feedback path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch fetch prediction metadata atomically with the instruction payload and preserve it across downstream holds.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Resolve every control instruction architecturally, train valid backward taken branches, and redirect predicted non-branches or direction/target mismatches.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Treat predictor recovery as an EX redirect with existing bus-stall, load-use, and divider-hold precedence.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate and wire predictor lookup, EX training, metadata, and recovery while retaining the nret=1 top-level contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Test loop warm-up, final exit, fragment-tag alias to a non-branch, branch-target collision, and imem/dmem stall recovery with RVFI PC order.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a four-entry direct-mapped cache of resolved backward taken branch targets using a narrow PC-fragment tag and EX-only training.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry prediction-valid and predicted-successor metadata from IF through ID/EX for branch-qualified recovery.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select the target-cache successor from registered-PC predictor outputs without inspecting io_imemData in the PC feedback path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch fetch prediction metadata atomically with the instruction payload and preserve it across downstream holds.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Resolve every control instruction architecturally, train valid backward taken branches, and redirect predicted non-branches or direction/target mismatches.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Treat predictor recovery as an EX redirect with existing bus-stall, load-use, and divider-hold precedence.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate and wire predictor lookup, EX training, metadata, and recovery while retaining the nret=1 top-level contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Test loop warm-up, final exit, fragment-tag alias to a non-branch, branch-target collision, and imem/dmem stall recovery with RVFI PC order.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 404.91, \"delta_pct\": -5.93, \"vs_baseline\": 43.17, \"fmax_mhz\": 179.12, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4423715, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 6264, \"ff\": 2399, \"seeds\": [179.12, 176.68, 188.64], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T03:48:36.935256Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 13}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' cores/bench/LESSONS.md && jq -r 'select(.category == \\\"predictor\\\") | [.id, .title, .outcome, (.fitness // \\\"n/a\\\"), (.delta_pct // \\\"n/a\\\")] | @tsv' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' cores/bench/LESSONS.md && jq -r 'select(.category == \\\"predictor\\\") | [.id, .title, .outcome, (.fitness // \\\"n/a\\\"), (.delta_pct // \\\"n/a\\\")] | @tsv' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r1s0 (improvement, +8.33%): RV32M multiply variants should share a sign-selected 33x33 product and select the required 32-bit half, since parallel signed/unsigned multiplier cones waste LUTs even though each instruction consumes only one result.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r1s2 (regression, -10.84%): A combinational BTFNT predictor that derives the PC update from imem_data puts instruction fetch, branch decode, immediate extraction, and target addition on the IF-to-PC feedback path, so it must be pipelined or decoupled when its Fmax loss exceeds CoreMark's saved redirect bubbles.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r2s0 (regression, +20.73%): Replacing a shared 33x33 signed multiplier with a 32x32 unsigned product can regress when MULH/MULHSU require chained conditional 32-bit high-half corrections, because the added subtractor network outweighs any saved multiplier logic on the CoreMark-critical ALU path.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r2s1 (improvement, +32.31%): Moving DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU into a blocking iterative unit removes wide divide/remainder cones from the common EX path, and CoreMark's sparse division use lets the Fmax gain outweigh the rare multi-cycle front-end stalls.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r2s2 (regression, +0.69%): Removing imem_data from branch prediction is insufficient when a combinational PC-indexed BTB tag match, counter read, and next-PC mux remain on the fetch feedback path; CoreMark's saved loop-back bubbles did not amortize that added front-end cost.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r3s0 (regression, -27.02%): Registering a blocking 33x33 multiplier without splitting its arithmetic only moves the full-cycle product into a new register-to-register cone while adding EX ownership and interlock logic, so it cannot deliver the divide unit's Fmax benefit without real pipelining or iteration.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r3s1 (broken, n/a): A sequential fetch FIFO must index its storage procedurally with runtime read/write pointers; generate-block hierarchy and prefixes are elaboration-time constructs and reject dynamic queue indices before synthesis.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r4s0 (broken, n/a): Decode-qualified load-use detection must classify every architectural source in the same valid/timing domain as operand capture, because suppressing a real dependency lets stale register data reach EX and is caught by RVFI register checks.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r4s1 (regression, -18.56%): A four-entry procedural IF queue can lose CoreMark fitness when its pointer/count, dequeue arbitration, and redirect-flush control cost more than the transient imem stalls that prefetch can cover, so front-end decoupling needs measured slack rather than assumed stall hiding.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r5s2 (broken, n/a): A registered lookahead BTB must treat its response PC and predicted next PC as a fetch-acceptance transaction, invalidating or holding them consistently across redirects and stalls, or stale predictions can misassociate with conditional branches and violate RVFI control-flow checks.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r6s0 (improvement, +1.51%): Encoding BEQ/BNE and signed/unsigned relational branches from one 33-bit subtract's zero, sign, and borrow flags shares a carry-chain-friendly EX cone, modestly improving CoreMark loop-branch timing without changing redirect behavior.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r6s1 (broken, n/a): A write-behind store buffer must make buffer admission, external write acknowledgement, and RVFI retirement one ordered transaction boundary, since retiring a store while its request can outlive or be overwritten by younger MEM traffic violates architectural load/store ordering and register-state checks.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r6s2 (broken, n/a): A PC-only branch predictor must validate the fetched instruction's branch identity or rely on immutable instruction memory, because formal's per-cycle symbolic imem can reuse a trained PC for a non-branch and make the predictor skip its required PC+4.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r7s0 (improvement, +4.61%): On an FPGA MEM path, replacing variable 32-bit load shifts and shifted store masks with fixed addr-indexed byte/halfword lane muxes removes a barrel-mux cone after dmem and improves Fmax when alignment trapping already restricts halfword selection.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r7s1 (regression, -11.37%): Zero-wait MEM-to-EX forwarding creates a dmem-return-to-load-format-to-operand-mux-to-ALU critical path, so CoreMark's saved load-use bubbles can be outweighed by Fmax loss unless the load response is timing-isolated.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r8s2 (broken, n/a): A signature-mismatched conditional-branch prediction must redirect to the resolved PC+4 even when the branch is not taken, because treating it as a cold not-taken branch without recovery leaves the already-applied stale taken target on the fetch path and violates RVFI PC-forward progress.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r9s1 (broken, n/a): A decoupled in-order completion queue must make dequeue an atomic retirement event for RVFI payload/order, register-file writeback, and PC-forward state, because separate retire and commit views can misalign architectural instruction state across queue transitions.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260710-003-r9s2 (regression, -20.10%): An epoch-safe registered loop predictor can still lose badly when every EX instruction computes and 32-bit-compares an architectural successor against carried prediction metadata, because that recovery logic extends the hot EX redirect cone more than CoreMark amortizes the saved loop-back bubble.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r10s0 (regression, -4.08%): Sharing RV32I shifts through a 33-bit arithmetic-right barrel with pre/post bit-reversal can hurt CoreMark because the direction/sign-control muxing and reversal routing extend the hot EX shift-result path more than the removed parallel shift cones save.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r10s1 (broken, n/a): A one-entry elastic IF/ID boundary must make fetch fill/dequeue and ID/EX advancement a single valid-ready transaction, because consuming or refilling an entry out of step with decode can misassociate instruction state and break RVFI PC-forward, register, and liveness checks.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r10s2 (broken, n/a): A decode-stage branch prediction redirect must atomically invalidate or suppress the already-issued sequential fetch payload, because otherwise a fall-through instruction can survive alongside the predicted target and widespread conditional-branch RVFI PC checks fail.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r11s1 (regression, -17.42%): Splitting RV32M multiplication into parallel partial products and a blocking recombination stage can still regress CoreMark when the extra EX interlock and wide shifted-sum reduction cost more than removing the monolithic multiplier from the normal ALU cone, especially for sparse multiplies.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r12s0 (regression, -12.36%): Replacing resettable register-file data with a valid-masked LUTRAM array can hurt Fmax when the valid-bitmap lookup and invalid-to-zero mux are added to each asynchronous decode read port, outweighing any reset-fanout or storage-mapping savings.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r13s0 (regression, -11.64%): Explicitly sharing the PC-relative branch/JAL adder can reduce Fmax when the single result must fan out through EX redirect, alignment, and EX/MEM metadata logic, since local duplicated adders may route more cheaply than a high-fanout shared control-cone signal.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r13s1 (regression, -21.51%): Moving RV32M multiplication into a separate combinational `mul_dsp` module does not shorten the EX-to-EX/MEM path by itself; without verified DSP mapping, the full product plus result-selection routing can cost more than the original shared ALU cone.\nhyp-20260710-003-r1s2\tBackward-taken fetch branch prediction\tregression\t252.17\t-10.84\nhyp-20260710-003-r2s2\tPC-indexed dynamic loop predictor\tregression\t308.5\t0.69\nhyp-20260710-003-r3s2\tLookahead registered BTB predictor\tbroken\tn/a\tn/a\nhyp-20260710-003-r4s2\tReturn-address-stack fetch predictor\tregression\t364.95\t-9.97\nhyp-20260710-003-r5s2\tValidated lookahead BTB predictor\tbroken\tn/a\tn/a\nhyp-20260710-003-r6s2\tOne-entry loop target predictor\tbroken\tn/a\tn/a\nhyp-20260710-003-r7s2\tSpeculation-safe loop target predictor\tregression\t167.49\t-59.3\nhyp-20260710-003-r8s2\tSignature-qualified loop predictor\tbroken\tn/a\tn/a\nhyp-20260710-003-r9s2\tEpoch-bound registered loop predictor\tregression\t343.92\t-20.1\nhyp-20260711-003-r10s2\tRegistered decode BTFNT predictor\tbroken\tn/a\tn/a\nhyp-20260711-003-r11s2\tAtomic registered BTFNT front end\tbroken\tn/a\tn/a\nhyp-20260711-003-r12s2\tAtomic single-ahead BTFNT predictor\tregression\t358.51\t-16.71\nhyp-20260711-003-r13s2\tPartial-tag backward loop predictor\tregression\t404.91\t-5.93\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"jq 'select(.id == \\\"hyp-20260710-003-r4s2\\\" or .id == \\\"hyp-20260710-003-r7s2\\\" or .id == \\\"hyp-20260710-003-r9s2\\\" or .id == \\\"hyp-20260711-003-r13s2\\\") | {id,title,hypothesis:(.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis),changes,outcome,fitness,delta_pct,fmax_mhz,cycles,lut4,ff,implementation_notes,error}' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"jq 'select(.id == \\\"hyp-20260710-003-r4s2\\\" or .id == \\\"hyp-20260710-003-r7s2\\\" or .id == \\\"hyp-20260710-003-r9s2\\\" or .id == \\\"hyp-20260711-003-r13s2\\\") | {id,title,hypothesis:(.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis),changes,outcome,fitness,delta_pct,fmax_mhz,cycles,lut4,ff,implementation_notes,error}' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"{\n  \"id\": \"hyp-20260710-003-r4s2\",\n  \"title\": \"Return-address-stack fetch predictor\",\n  \"hypothesis\": \"Add a shallow committed return-address stack and predict only canonical\\nreturns (`jalr x0, x1/x5, 0`) in IF. The prediction is just an instruction\\nfield match and a mux of the registered RAS top, avoiding branch-immediate\\nextraction, target addition, tags, and counter RAM on the PC path. Train\\npushes and pops only when the corresponding JAL/JALR has resolved in EX;\\ncarry the predicted next PC to EX and retain existing redirect recovery for\\nany mismatch. Correct returns therefore avoid the existing two younger\\nwrong-path instructions without changing conditional-branch behavior.\\n\",\n  \"changes\": [\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/return_predictor.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Add a small registered RAS with resolved-call push and resolved-return pop inputs, plus a combinational valid/top-address query interface.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Extend fetch and ID/EX payloads with the predicted next-PC metadata needed to validate a return prediction at EX.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Recognize only canonical ra/t0 JALR returns and select the registered RAS top as their predicted next PC; all other instructions keep PC+4.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Latch the fetch packet's predicted next PC beside the decoded instruction.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Compare resolved control-flow next PC with the carried prediction, redirect only on mismatch, and emit ordered call/return RAS update events after validation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Treat a control-flow prediction mismatch as the flush condition while retaining existing bus, load-use, and divide interlock priorities.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Wire the RAS predictor, fetch metadata, EX validation, and update events without changing the nret=1 top-level interface.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\",\n      \"description\": \"Add directed nested-call/return, empty-stack, noncanonical-JALR, misprediction-recovery, and imem-stall tests with RVFI PC-order checks.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"outcome\": \"regression\",\n  \"fitness\": 364.95,\n  \"delta_pct\": -9.97,\n  \"fmax_mhz\": 163.43,\n  \"cycles\": 4478102,\n  \"lut4\": 6228,\n  \"ff\": 2486,\n  \"implementation_notes\": \"\",\n  \"error\": null\n}\n{\n  \"id\": \"hyp-20260710-003-r7s2\",\n  \"title\": \"Speculation-safe loop target predictor\",\n  \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry last-resolved conditional-branch predictor, but make its\\nprediction a transaction carried by every fetched instruction rather than a\\nbranch-only promise. The entry stores branch PC, target, and last direction;\\nits small registered-PC equality and target mux remain the only logic added\\nto fetch. IF/ID and ID/EX carry predicted_next_pc for all accepted fetches.\\nIn EX, compare that value against the architectural next PC for every valid\\ninstruction: non-control instructions resolve to PC+4, branches and jumps\\nresolve normally. Any mismatch redirects and flushes, including a predictor\\nhit whose symbolic imem word is no longer a branch. Train the entry only\\nwhen a valid resolved conditional branch advances from EX, and bind prediction\\nmetadata to its fetch PC across imem/dmem stalls and redirects. This retains\\nthe timing advantage of a one-entry PC-only predictor while making all stale\\nor aliased hits recoverable under RVFI.\\n\",\n  \"changes\": [\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/last_branch_predictor.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Implement a one-entry PC/target/direction predictor with reset state and an EX-advance-qualified conditional-branch training interface.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Add fetch-PC and predicted-next-PC fields to the IF/ID and ID/EX payloads so prediction remains paired with the instruction that accepted it.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Use the one-entry predictor only to choose the fetch successor, and emit the exact predicted-next-PC transaction for every fetched instruction.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Latch prediction metadata atomically with decoded instruction state while preserving existing flush and hold semantics.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Compute architectural next PC for every valid instruction, redirect on any predicted-versus-actual mismatch, and expose precisely qualified conditional-branch training signals.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Integrate universal misprediction recovery with redirect, load-use, long-operation, and memory-stall priorities without advancing stale fetch metadata.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Wire predictor lookup, EX training, transaction metadata, and recovery while retaining the nret=1 external and RVFI contracts.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\",\n      \"description\": \"Add directed tests for loop warm-up and exit, a trained-PC non-branch instruction replacement, redirects, and imem/dmem stall transaction ownership.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"outcome\": \"regression\",\n  \"fitness\": 167.49,\n  \"delta_pct\": -59.3,\n  \"fmax_mhz\": 73.5,\n  \"cycles\": 4388269,\n  \"lut4\": 5875,\n  \"ff\": 2286,\n  \"implementation_notes\": \"\",\n  \"error\": null\n}\n{\n  \"id\": \"hyp-20260710-003-r9s2\",\n  \"title\": \"Epoch-bound registered loop predictor\",\n  \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry last-taken conditional-branch predictor whose lookup is\\nissued only for the sequential candidate PC+4 and whose response is\\nregistered at the same edge that advances the fetch PC to that candidate.\\nThe response carries its queried PC and a fetch epoch; it may select its\\nstored target only for that exact next accepted fetch, then is consumed.\\nRedirects increment the epoch and invalidate the response, while imem,\\ndmem, and divide holds retain or discard it according to whether its query\\nPC was actually accepted. EX carries the prediction transaction with every\\nfetched instruction and compares the architectural successor for every\\nvalid instruction, recovering to PC+4 for symbolic-imem non-branch alias\\nhits as well as normal direction/target mispredictions. This saves one\\nhot-loop recovery cycle without putting BTB lookup, target selection, or\\ninstruction decode in the PC-to-PC combinational path.\\n\",\n  \"changes\": [\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/lookahead_loop_predictor.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Implement a resettable one-entry last-taken branch record plus a registered PC+4 lookup response tagged with query PC and fetch epoch.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Add accepted-fetch prediction transaction fields, including queried PC, predicted successor, valid bit, and epoch, to the pipeline payloads.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Capture a registered lookahead response only on a sequential fetch acceptance, consume it only for its exact tagged PC and epoch, and prioritize older EX recovery.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Latch the predictor transaction atomically with the fetched instruction so ordinary holds and flushes cannot detach metadata.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Compare each valid instruction's architectural successor against its carried prediction, recover every mismatch including non-branch alias hits, and emit advance-qualified branch training.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Define fetch-acceptance and redirect/hold precedence so predictor response creation, consumption, and invalidation match pipeline progress.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Wire predictor lookup, epoch management, accepted-fetch metadata, EX recovery, and branch training while preserving the nret=1 top-level RVFI contract.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\",\n      \"description\": \"Add directed warm-up, repeated-loop, final-exit, redirect, imem/dmem-stall, divide-hold, and symbolic-PC-alias recovery tests with RVFI PC-order checks.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"outcome\": \"regression\",\n  \"fitness\": 343.92,\n  \"delta_pct\": -20.1,\n  \"fmax_mhz\": 148.59,\n  \"cycles\": 4320468,\n  \"lut4\": 5975,\n  \"ff\": 2379,\n  \"implementation_notes\": \"\",\n  \"error\": null\n}\n{\n  \"id\": \"hyp-20260711-003-r13s2\",\n  \"title\": \"Partial-tag backward loop predictor\",\n  \"hypothesis\": \"Add a four-entry, direct-mapped backward-loop target cache trained only by\\nresolved, valid, taken conditional branches whose target is below their PC.\\nEach entry holds a valid bit, a short PC fragment tag, and its target; fetch\\npredicts taken only on the narrow index-plus-fragment match, selecting the\\nregistered target rather than PC+4. Carry a prediction-valid bit and the\\nselected successor with the fetched instruction. EX remains the sole\\narchitectural resolver and recovers to PC+4 for a predicted alias that is\\nnot a branch, or to the resolved successor for a branch direction/target\\nmismatch. This deliberately permits harmless partial-tag aliases but makes\\ntheir recovery explicit, preserving correctness under symbolic imem while\\navoiding a full tag, counter RAM, or imem-data-to-PC path.\\n\",\n  \"changes\": [\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Add a four-entry direct-mapped cache of resolved backward taken branch targets using a narrow PC-fragment tag and EX-only training.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Carry prediction-valid and predicted-successor metadata from IF through ID/EX for branch-qualified recovery.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Select the target-cache successor from registered-PC predictor outputs without inspecting io_imemData in the PC feedback path.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Latch fetch prediction metadata atomically with the instruction payload and preserve it across downstream holds.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Resolve every control instruction architecturally, train valid backward taken branches, and redirect predicted non-branches or direction/target mismatches.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Treat predictor recovery as an EX redirect with existing bus-stall, load-use, and divider-hold precedence.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Instantiate and wire predictor lookup, EX training, metadata, and recovery while retaining the nret=1 top-level contract.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\",\n      \"description\": \"Test loop warm-up, final exit, fragment-tag alias to a non-branch, branch-target collision, and imem/dmem stall recovery with RVFI PC order.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"outcome\": \"regression\",\n  \"fitness\": 404.91,\n  \"delta_pct\": -5.93,\n  \"fmax_mhz\": 179.12,\n  \"cycles\": 4423715,\n  \"lut4\": 6264,\n  \"ff\": 2399,\n  \"implementation_notes\": \"\",\n  \"error\": null\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
