ath. Hold younger issue only for the added multiply stage,\\nwhile older MEM/WB retirement continues normally. This removes the wide\\nmultiplier from the normal ALU cone and should raise Fmax enough to offset\\nCoreMark's sparse one-cycle multiply interlocks.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the real 33x33 product from the fast ALU and retain only non-multiply operations plus matching RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mul_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a two-stage signedness-selected partial-product multiplier with operand/result registers, RV32M high/low selection, and a start/busy/done handshake.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Dispatch all MUL-family operations to the new unit, snapshot forwarded sources and instruction metadata, and emit the completed result into EX/MEM exactly once.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the long-operation interlock for the multiplier's two-stage lifecycle while preserving load-use, redirect, divide, and dmem-stall priorities.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire multiplier busy/completion control into execute and global stall signals without changing the nret=1 interface or RVFI retirement boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Add exact RV32M multiply high/low corner and randomized vectors, including dependent consumers and consecutive multiply retirement ordering.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the real 33x33 product from the fast ALU and retain only non-multiply operations plus matching RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mul_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a two-stage signedness-selected partial-product multiplier with operand/result registers, RV32M high/low selection, and a start/busy/done handshake.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Dispatch all MUL-family operations to the new unit, snapshot forwarded sources and instruction metadata, and emit the completed result into EX/MEM exactly once.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the long-operation interlock for the multiplier's two-stage lifecycle while preserving load-use, redirect, divide, and dmem-stall priorities.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire multiplier busy/completion control into execute and global stall signals without changing the nret=1 interface or RVFI retirement boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Add exact RV32M multiply high/low corner and randomized vectors, including dependent consumers and consecutive multiply retirement ordering.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 355.48, \"delta_pct\": -17.42, \"vs_baseline\": 25.69, \"fmax_mhz\": 166.39, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4680666, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 6224, \"ff\": 2556, \"seeds\": [177.3, 166.39, 160.15], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T03:22:53.733150Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 11, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r11s1 (regression, -17.42%): Splitting RV32M multiplication into parallel partial products and a blocking recombination stage can still regress CoreMark when the extra EX interlock and wide shifted-sum reduction cost more than removing the monolithic multiplier from the normal ALU cone, especially for sparse multiplies.\"}\n34:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-003-r11s2\", \"title\": \"Atomic registered BTFNT front end\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark repeatedly takes backward conditional loop branches, while the\\ncurrent EX-stage resolver fetches two sequential wrong-path instructions.\\nEarlier fetch predictors hurt Fmax by extending the PC feedback path, and\\nthe prior registered decode predictor failed because its sequential fetch\\npayload was not invalidated atomically with a predicted redirect.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark repeatedly takes backward conditional loop branches, while the\\ncurrent EX-stage resolver fetches two sequential wrong-path instructions.\\nEarlier fetch predictors hurt Fmax by extending the PC feedback path, and\\nthe prior registered decode predictor failed because its sequential fetch\\npayload was not invalidated atomically with a predicted redirect.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Insert one stall-aware registered IF/ID fetch payload and perform static\\nbackward-taken, forward-not-taken prediction only when that registered,\\nvalid instruction decodes as a conditional branch with a negative B\\nimmediate. Treat prediction as an atomic valid-ready transaction: when\\ndecode advances the predicted branch into ID/EX, replace the concurrently\\nissued sequential fetch payload with an invalid entry and redirect the PC\\nto the predicted target. Carry branch-only prediction metadata to EX;\\nEX recovers on a direction or target mismatch, while unpredicted\\ninstructions retain normal PC+4 behavior without a universal successor\\ncomparator. This removes hot loop-back recovery work while keeping\\ninstruction decode and target formation behind the fetch register.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add IF/ID registered-payload and branch-only prediction metadata fields, including the predicted successor.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement a one-entry stall-aware IF/ID register whose fill, dequeue, predicted redirect, EX recovery, and invalidation follow one atomic acceptance protocol.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Detect valid backward conditional branches from the registered payload, form their target, and emit prediction only when the payload advances into ID/EX.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry qualified branch prediction metadata and recover only a resolved predicted branch whose direction or target disagrees.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Define ready/hold and redirect precedence so load-use, bus stalls, divider ownership, prediction, and EX recovery cannot consume or retain a wrong-path fetch entry.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the atomic fetch transaction, prediction metadata, and EX recovery while preserving the existing single-retire RVFI interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed loop, final-exit, forward-branch, imem/dmem-stall, and divide-hold tests that verify no sequential payload retires after a predicted redirect.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add IF/ID registered-payload and branch-only prediction metadata fields, including the predicted successor.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement a one-entry stall-aware IF/ID register whose fill, dequeue, predicted redirect, EX recovery, and invalidation follow one atomic acceptance protocol.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Detect valid backward conditional branches from the registered payload, form their target, and emit prediction only when the payload advances into ID/EX.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry qualified branch prediction metadata and recover only a resolved predicted branch whose direction or target disagrees.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Define ready/hold and redirect precedence so load-use, bus stalls, divider ownership, prediction, and EX recovery cannot consume or retain a wrong-path fetch entry.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the atomic fetch transaction, prediction metadata, and EX recovery while preserving the existing single-retire RVFI interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed loop, final-exit, forward-branch, imem/dmem-stall, and divide-hold tests that verify no sequential payload retires after a predicted redirect.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"cosim_failed: coremark.elf\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 11}\n35:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r12s0\", \"title\": \"Validity-masked LUTRAM register file\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The 32 by 32 register file currently resets every data bit, forcing a\\nresettable flip-flop implementation and placing a large asynchronous read\\nmux on the decode-to-execute path. Tang Nano LUTRAM can implement the data\\narray more compactly when the array itself is not reset.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The 32 by 32 register file currently resets every data bit, forcing a\\nresettable flip-flop implementation and placing a large asynchronous read\\nmux on the decode-to-execute path. Tang Nano LUTRAM can implement the data\\narray more compactly when the array itself is not reset.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep architectural reset semantics with a resettable 32-bit valid bitmap,\\nwhile leaving the 32-bit register-data array unreset and marking it for\\ndistributed RAM inference. Reads from invalid entries return zero, and a\\nsame-cycle write remains the highest-priority bypass, so x0, reset, and\\nwrite-first behavior remain identical. This removes reset fanout and should\\nmap the two asynchronous read ports to a shallower LUTRAM implementation,\\nimproving Fmax without adding pipeline latency or changing RVFI state.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/reg_file.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace resettable register-data flops with a distributed-RAM-friendly unreset array plus resettable valid bitmap, retaining x0 masking and write-first dual-read semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_reg_file.py\", \"description\": \"Add reset-after-write, invalid-entry zero-read, and same-cycle write-first tests to prove the valid-mask implementation preserves architectural register-file behavior.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/reg_file.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace resettable register-data flops with a distributed-RAM-friendly unreset array plus resettable valid bitmap, retaining x0 masking and write-first dual-read semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_reg_file.py\", \"description\": \"Add reset-after-write, invalid-entry zero-read, and same-cycle write-first tests to prove the valid-mask implementation preserves architectural register-file behavior.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 377.26, \"delta_pct\": -12.36, \"vs_baseline\": 33.39, \"fmax_mhz\": 169.46, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491817, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 3415, \"ff\": 1426, \"seeds\": [180.28, 157.06, 169.46], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T03:30:04.131083Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 12, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r12s0 (regression, -12.36%): Replacing resettable register-file data with a valid-masked LUTRAM array can hurt Fmax when the valid-bitmap lookup and invalid-to-zero mux are added to each asynchronous decode read port, outweighing any reset-fanout or storage-mapping savings.\"}\n36:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r12s1\", \"title\": \"Decode-resolved direct JAL path\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Direct JAL has a fully encoded PC-relative target and no register operands,\\nyet the current pipeline waits until EX to redirect it. Calls, returns from\\ncompiler veneers, and unconditional loop transfers therefore pay the same\\ntwo-younger-instruction recovery cost as a data-dependent control transfer.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Direct JAL has a fully encoded PC-relative target and no register operands,\\nyet the current pipeline waits until EX to redirect it. Calls, returns from\\ncompiler veneers, and unconditional loop transfers therefore pay the same\\ntwo-younger-instruction recovery cost as a data-dependent control transfer.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a decode-stage redirect path for validated JAL only. Form pc+J-immediate\\nfrom the current IF/ID payload and update the fetch PC when that payload\\nadvances into ID/EX, while preserving the JAL payload for normal EX/MEM/WB\\nretirement and PC+4 writeback. Give an older EX redirect priority, keep\\nconditional branches and JALR entirely EX-resolved, and distinguish the\\ndecode redirect from an EX flush so the issuing JAL is never suppressed.\\nThis removes one control-recovery cycle for direct jumps without a branch\\ncomparator, register bypass network, prediction table, or imem-to-PC path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Arbitrate decode-stage JAL and older EX redirects, and preserve the single-retire RVFI boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Decode validated JAL, form its PC-relative target, and emit a ready-qualified direct-redirect request.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Accept a distinct decode redirect without bubbling the currently issued JAL; continue to flush only EX-resolved wrong-path fetches.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Suppress duplicate EX redirect generation for JAL while retaining its PC+4 result and RVFI pc_next payload.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Define load, bus, long-operation, decode-redirect, and older EX-redirect precedence so a JAL advances exactly once.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed JAL, consecutive JAL, JAL-after-stall, and older-EX-redirect collision tests checking PC order, link values, and RVFI retirement.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Arbitrate decode-stage JAL and older EX redirects, and preserve the single-retire RVFI boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Decode validated JAL, form its PC-relative target, and emit a ready-qualified direct-redirect request.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Accept a distinct decode redirect without bubbling the currently issued JAL; continue to flush only EX-resolved wrong-path fetches.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Suppress duplicate EX redirect generation for JAL while retaining its PC+4 result and RVFI pc_next payload.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Define load, bus, long-operation, decode-redirect, and older EX-redirect precedence so a JAL advances exactly once.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed JAL, consecutive JAL, JAL-after-stall, and older-EX-redirect collision tests checking PC order, link values, and RVFI retirement.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"formal_failed: reg_ch0\\nSBY  0:29:48 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:37  Writing trace to Verilog testbench: engine_0/trace_tb.v\\nSBY  0:29:48 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:37  Writing trace to constraints file: engine_0/trace.smtc\\nSBY  0:29:48 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:37  Writing trace to Yosys witness file: engine_0/trace.yw\\nSBY  0:29:48 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:37  Status: failed\\nSBY  0:29:48 [reg_ch0] engine_0: finished (returncode=1)\\nSBY  0:29:48 [reg_ch0] engine_0: Status returned by engine: FAIL\\nSBY  0:29:48 [reg_ch0] summary: Elapse